er in Chapter 24, right, the 4D numbers. Yes, just as a hint of how much deeper this rabbit hole goes. If you try to build quantum mechanics with quaternions, which have three different imaginary axes, the book proves a mathematical bound, showing that quaternionic, mutually unbiased bases can outnumber complex ones by a factor of roughly two. Specifically, a formula of 2D plus 1 instead of the standard D plus 1. Which implies what? It suggests that there are even more exotic, highly symmetric structures that are mathematically admissible under the bare rules of probability, even though we haven't found a reason for nature to use them yet. It means the space of possible physics is much larger than the physics we actually observe. Okay, I need to take a breath. Let's bring this all back home for the listener. We have been on quite a journey today. We started with the absolute bare bones of classical probability, just shuffling decks of cards and looking at points inside triangles. We watched classical real numbers mathematically fail to glue together in the closed loops of our Escher staircase. We saw complex numbers perfectly balance the ledgers of composite systems, while real numbers tried to hide extra dimensions behind cryptographic keys. We realized that whether a particle is deeply entangled or just classically correlated might depend entirely on the mathematical goggles you were wearing when you look at it. We watched the entire standard textbook of quantum mechanics, the Born rule, Schrodinger's equation, fall out as the inevitable result of just four basic operational assumptions about time and measurement. And then, just when we thought the complex numbers had won, the Amplit Wist and the 2026 literature pulled the rug out from under us entirely, showing that standard quantum mechanics is just one dialect, one way to write down a geometric truth that can equally be expressed entirely with real numbers if you were just willing to change how systems compose. It is a stunning display of what theoretical physics should be. Stripping away the dogma, showing exactly what our assumptions cost, and carefully separating the structural reality of the universe from the human conventions we use to write it down on a whiteboard. It forces us to distinguish the territory from the map. Which leaves me with a final thought for you, the listener, to mull over. If the absolute necessity of imaginary numbers and quantum mechanics, something that Nobel laureates have sworn by for a hundred years, was partially just a byproduct of how we chose to write down the multiplication of two systems, what else about our universe is an illusion of our language? That is the ultimate question for the next century of physics. When you look at the fundamental laws of reality, when you see a complex equation explaining the birth of a star or the spin of an electron, how much of what you are seeing is the true, raw, geometric universe, and how much is just the human mathematical lens you are looking through, something to chew on the next time you hear a physicist confidently declare that the universe is made of math. Thanks for joining us on this deep dive.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 14.3, "text": " What if the deepest mystery in all of quantum mechanics, like the underlying reason why physicists have been forced to use imaginary numbers to describe the universe for the last century, is actually just a math typo we made back in the 1920s?", "tokens": [50365, 708, 498, 264, 28288, 11422, 294, 439, 295, 13018, 12939, 11, 411, 264, 14217, 1778, 983, 48716, 362, 668, 7579, 281, 764, 26164, 3547, 281, 6786, 264, 6445, 337, 264, 1036, 4901, 11, 307, 767, 445, 257, 5221, 2125, 78, 321, 1027, 646, 294, 264, 22003, 82, 30, 51080], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.04257570780240572, "compression_ratio": 1.396551724137931, "no_speech_prob": 8.037523012600911e-13}, {"id": 1, "seek": 1430, "start": 14.3, "end": 34.18, "text": " I mean, it sounds borderline heretical when you put it exactly like that. But that is genuinely the frontier of theoretical physics right now. Because for 100 years, the imaginary unit, you know, the square root of negative one, which we denote with the letter I, has just been sitting right there in the core equations of reality.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 914, 11, 309, 3263, 7838, 1889, 720, 27800, 562, 291, 829, 309, 2293, 411, 300, 13, 583, 300, 307, 17839, 264, 35853, 295, 20864, 10649, 558, 586, 13, 1436, 337, 2319, 924, 11, 264, 26164, 4985, 11, 291, 458, 11, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 3671, 472, 11, 597, 321, 45708, 365, 264, 5063, 286, 11, 575, 445, 668, 3798, 558, 456, 294, 264, 4965, 11787, 295, 4103, 13, 51359], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07400304977207968, "compression_ratio": 1.5395348837209302, "no_speech_prob": 7.121258877723635e-13}, {"id": 2, "seek": 3418, "start": 34.18, "end": 35.82, "text": " Right, like in Schrodinger's equation.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 11, 411, 294, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 13, 50447], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07759228829414613, "compression_ratio": 1.5993975903614457, "no_speech_prob": 1.1423778589758626e-12}, {"id": 3, "seek": 3418, "start": 36.06, "end": 44.0, "text": " Exactly. It's how we calculate the fundamental states of particles. And for a century, physicists have largely just, well, shrugged and accepted it.", "tokens": [50459, 7587, 13, 467, 311, 577, 321, 8873, 264, 8088, 4368, 295, 10007, 13, 400, 337, 257, 4901, 11, 48716, 362, 11611, 445, 11, 731, 11, 9884, 697, 3004, 293, 9035, 309, 13, 50856], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07759228829414613, "compression_ratio": 1.5993975903614457, "no_speech_prob": 1.1423778589758626e-12}, {"id": 4, "seek": 3418, "start": 44.0, "end": 51.06, "text": " Because the math works, right? The predictions are accurate to 10 decimal places, so why question the alphabet we use to write the equation?", "tokens": [50856, 1436, 264, 5221, 1985, 11, 558, 30,