 {"id": 8, "seek": 6418, "start": 81.2, "end": 81.28, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51216, 1779, 13, 51220], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13390632503288835, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.4384707617762227e-12}, {"id": 9, "seek": 6418, "start": 81.5, "end": 89.74000000000001, "text": " And crucially, doing it without assuming any of the textbook rules are true to begin with. Like, this isn't about taking Schrodinger's equation as a given.", "tokens": [51231, 400, 5140, 1909, 11, 884, 309, 1553, 11926, 604, 295, 264, 25591, 4474, 366, 2074, 281, 1841, 365, 13, 1743, 11, 341, 1943, 380, 466, 1940, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 382, 257, 2212, 13, 51643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13390632503288835, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.4384707617762227e-12}, {"id": 10, "seek": 6418, "start": 89.92000000000002, "end": 93.30000000000001, "text": " So we're not just accepting the standard textbook as gospel here.", "tokens": [51652, 407, 321, 434, 406, 445, 17391, 264, 3832, 25591, 382, 14943, 510, 13, 51821], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13390632503288835, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.4384707617762227e-12}, {"id": 11, "seek": 9330, "start": 93.3, "end": 103.17999999999999, "text": " Not at all. The methodology here is to start with the most basic, undeniable, logical rules of how probability works, and then aggressively push those rules until they break.", "tokens": [50365, 1726, 412, 439, 13, 440, 24850, 510, 307, 281, 722, 365, 264, 881, 3875, 11, 674, 268, 9364, 11, 14978, 4474, 295, 577, 8482, 1985, 11, 293, 550, 32024, 2944, 729, 4474, 1826, 436, 1821, 13, 50859], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05254534931925984, "compression_ratio": 1.5521739130434782, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355763977401791e-12}, {"id": 12, "seek": 9330, "start": 103.62, "end": 111.58, "text": " Because, you know, we are looking for the exact moment where classical math shatters. That fracture line is where the quantum structure of the universe is basically forced to emerge.", "tokens": [50881, 1436, 11, 291, 458, 11, 321, 366, 1237, 337, 264, 1900, 1623, 689, 13735, 5221, 402, 37690, 13, 663, 36877, 1622, 307, 689, 264, 13018, 3877, 295, 264, 6445, 307, 1936, 7579, 281, 21511, 13, 51279], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.05254534931925984, "compression_ratio": 1.5521739130434782, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355763977401791e-12}, {"id": 13, "seek": 11158, "start": 111.58, "end": 114.96, "text": " Okay, so we have a clear blueprint for where we're taking you today.", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 370, 321, 362, 257, 1850, 35868, 337, 689, 321, 434, 1940, 291, 965, 13, 50534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15161832641152775, "compression_ratio": 1.4489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.981067870512887e-12}, {"id": 14, "seek": 11158, "start": 115.78, "end": 122.03999999999999, "text": " We'll start in a completely classical world, just simple statistics, absolutely no quantum weirdness at all.", "tokens": [50575, 492, 603, 722, 294, 257, 2584, 13735, 1002, 11, 445, 2199, 12523, 11, 3122, 572, 13018, 3657, 1287, 412, 439, 13, 50888], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15161832641152775, "compression_ratio": 1.4489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.981067870512887e-12}, {"id": 15, "seek": 11158, "start": 122.56, "end": 126.36, "text": " Then we are going to push that system until we find a mathematical contradiction.", "tokens": [50914, 1396, 321, 366, 516, 281, 2944, 300, 1185, 1826, 321, 915, 257, 18894, 34937, 13, 51104], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15161832641152775, "compression_ratio": 1.4489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.981067870512887e-12}, {"id": 16, "seek": 11158, "start": 126.84, "end": 128.0, "text": " Yep, the breaking point.", "tokens": [51128, 7010, 11, 264, 7697, 935, 13, 51186], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15161832641152775, "compression_ratio": 1.4489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.981067870512887e-12}, {"id": 17, "seek": 12800, "start": 128.0, "end": 137.06, "text": " Right. We'll explore why complex numbers seem to perfectly patch that contradiction, especially when two different systems try to, you know, talk to each other.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 492, 603, 6839, 983, 3997, 3547, 1643, 281, 6239, 9972, 300, 34937, 11, 2318, 562, 732, 819, 3652, 853, 281, 11, 291, 458, 11, 751, 281, 1184, 661, 13, 50818], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0895469118567074, "compression_ratio": 1.444954128440367, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171680964835455e-12}, {"id": 18, "seek": 12800, "start": 137.2, "end": 146.1, "text": " And then at the very end, we are going to drop a massive 2026 plot twist about whether the universe actually needed those imaginary numbers to begin with.", "tokens": [50825, 400, 550, 412, 264, 588, 917, 11, 321, 366, 516, 281, 3270, 257, 5994, 945, 10880, 7542, 8203, 466, 1968, 264, 6445, 767, 2978, 729, 26164, 3547, 281, 1841, 365, 13, 51270], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0895469118567074, "compression_ratio": 1.444954128440367, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171680964835455e-12}, {"id": 19, "seek": 14610, "start": 146.1, "end": 149.88, "text": " But to appreciate that twist, we have to start at the absolute foundation.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 281, 4449, 300, 8203, 11, 321, 362, 281, 722, 412, 264, 8236, 7030, 13, 50554], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11022058788098787, "compression_ratio": 1.6653543307086613, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279183726209846e-12}, {"id": 20, "seek": 14610, "start": 149.88, "end": 155.7, "text": " I mean, we need to define the bare admissible rules of probabilistic reality.", "tokens": [50554, 286, 914, 11, 321, 643, 281, 6964, 264, 6949, 5910, 41073, 4474, 295, 31959, 3142, 4103, 13, 50845], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11022058788098787, "compression_ratio": 1.6653543307086613, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279183726209846e-12}, {"id": 21, "seek": 14610, "start": 156.01999999999998, "end": 156.76, "text": " Before any physics.", "tokens": [50861, 4546, 604, 10649, 13, 50898], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11022058788098787, "compression_ratio": 1.6653543307086613, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279183726209846e-12}, {"id": 22, "seek": 14610, "start": 156.92, "end": 162.84, "text": " Right, before physics. Before we talk about electrons or photons or spin or gravity, we need to look at what information actually is.", "tokens": [50906, 1779, 11, 949, 10649, 13, 4546, 321, 751, 466, 14265, 420, 40209, 420, 6060, 420, 12110, 11, 321, 643, 281, 574, 412, 437, 1589, 767, 307, 13, 51202], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11022058788098787, "compression_ratio": 1.6653543307086613, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279183726209846e-12}, {"id": 23, "seek": 14610, "start": 163.06, "end": 167.68, "text": " Let's unpack this bare foundation then, because the manuscript starts with something called the probability simplex.", "tokens": [51213, 961, 311, 26699, 341, 6949, 7030, 550, 11, 570, 264, 23928, 3719, 365, 746, 1219, 264, 8482, 2199, 87, 13, 51444], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11022058788098787, "compression_ratio": 1.6653543307086613, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279183726209846e-12}, {"id": 24, "seek": 16768, "start": 167.68, "end": 171.0, "text": " And I really appreciate how stripped down this concept is.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 286, 534, 4449, 577, 33221, 760, 341, 3410, 307, 13, 50531], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 25, "seek": 16768, "start": 171.94, "end": 175.68, "text": " Imagine you have a finite set of perfectly distinguishable alternatives.", "tokens": [50578, 11739, 291, 362, 257, 19362, 992, 295, 6239, 20206, 712, 20478, 13, 50765], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 26, "seek": 16768, "start": 176.42000000000002, "end": 177.1, "text": " Like rolling a die.", "tokens": [50802, 1743, 9439, 257, 978, 13, 50836], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 27, "seek": 16768, "start": 177.44, "end": 179.88, "text": " Yeah, like the six faces of a regular die.", "tokens": [50853, 865, 11, 411, 264, 2309, 8475, 295, 257, 3890, 978, 13, 50975], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 28, "seek": 16768, "start": 180.42000000000002, "end": 182.32, "text": " Or it could be a simple coin flip.", "tokens": [51002, 1610, 309, 727, 312, 257, 2199, 11464, 7929, 13, 51097], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 29, "seek": 16768, "start": 182.84, "end": 187.52, "text": " Or even a detector in a lab that either clicks or stays completely silent.", "tokens": [51123, 1610, 754, 257, 25712, 294, 257, 2715, 300, 2139, 18521, 420, 10834, 2584, 12784, 13, 51357], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 30, "seek": 16768, "start": 188.02, "end": 193.70000000000002, "text": " So a state of knowledge about which of those alternatives is true is, well, it's just a probability distribution.", "tokens": [51382, 407, 257, 1785, 295, 3601, 466, 597, 295, 729, 20478, 307, 2074, 307, 11, 731, 11, 309, 311, 445, 257, 8482, 7316, 13, 51666], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1617007723041609, "compression_ratio": 1.548148148148148, "no_speech_prob": 1.6684935768077058e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 19370, "start": 193.7, "end": 198.85999999999999, "text": " It's a list of non-negative numbers that add up to exactly one or, you know, 100%.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 257, 1329, 295, 2107, 12, 28561, 1166, 3547, 300, 909, 493, 281, 2293, 472, 420, 11, 291, 458, 11, 2319, 6856, 50623], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 32, "seek": 19370, "start": 198.85999999999999, "end": 202.92, "text": " And if you picture this geometrically, it forms a shape called a simplex.", "tokens": [50623, 400, 498, 291, 3036, 341, 12956, 81, 984, 11, 309, 6422, 257, 3909, 1219, 257, 2199, 87, 13, 50826], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 33, "seek": 19370, "start": 203.14, "end": 204.7, "text": " Okay, so how do we visualize that simplex?", "tokens": [50837, 1033, 11, 370, 577, 360, 321, 23273, 300, 2199, 87, 30, 50915], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 34, "seek": 19370, "start": 205.39999999999998, "end": 207.26, "text": " Let's say you have three possible outcomes.", "tokens": [50950, 961, 311, 584, 291, 362, 1045, 1944, 10070, 13, 51043], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 35, "seek": 19370, "start": 207.45999999999998, "end": 208.66, "text": " You could just draw a triangle.", "tokens": [51053, 509, 727, 445, 2642, 257, 13369, 13, 51113], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 36, "seek": 19370, "start": 209.07999999999998, "end": 214.67999999999998, "text": " The three corners or the vertices of that triangle, they represent states of absolute certainty.", "tokens": [51134, 440, 1045, 12413, 420, 264, 32053, 295, 300, 13369, 11, 436, 2906, 4368, 295, 8236, 27022, 13, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 37, "seek": 19370, "start": 215.2, "end": 217.88, "text": " Like, you know for a fact the dial landed on a one.", "tokens": [51440, 1743, 11, 291, 458, 337, 257, 1186, 264, 5502, 15336, 322, 257, 472, 13, 51574], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 38, "seek": 19370, "start": 218.54, "end": 219.7, "text": " You are 100% certain.", "tokens": [51607, 509, 366, 2319, 4, 1629, 13, 51665], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15242553338771914, "compression_ratio": 1.5759717314487633, "no_speech_prob": 1.876091729885454e-12}, {"id": 39, "seek": 21970, "start": 219.7, "end": 226.39999999999998, "text": " But if I'm not certain, if I'm, say, just guessing, my state of knowledge exists somewhere inside the body of that triangle, right?", "tokens": [50365, 583, 498, 286, 478, 406, 1629, 11, 498, 286, 478, 11, 584, 11, 445, 17939, 11, 452, 1785, 295, 3601, 8198, 4079, 1854, 264, 1772, 295, 300, 13369, 11, 558, 30, 50700], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 40, "seek": 21970, "start": 226.5, "end": 226.94, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50705, 7587, 13, 50727], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 41, "seek": 21970, "start": 227.32, "end": 229.89999999999998, "text": " Any point inside that shape is a convex combination.", "tokens": [50746, 2639, 935, 1854, 300, 3909, 307, 257, 42432, 6562, 13, 50875], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 42, "seek": 21970, "start": 230.07999999999998, "end": 230.51999999999998, "text": " It's a mixture.", "tokens": [50884, 467, 311, 257, 9925, 13, 50906], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 43, "seek": 21970, "start": 231.04, "end": 236.79999999999998, "text": " So maybe you are 50% sure it's outcome A, 25% outcome B, and 25% outcome C.", "tokens": [50932, 407, 1310, 291, 366, 2625, 4, 988, 309, 311, 9700, 316, 11, 3552, 4, 9700, 363, 11, 293, 3552, 4, 9700, 383, 13, 51220], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 44, "seek": 21970, "start": 237.44, "end": 240.04, "text": " That point loosed geometrically inside the simplex.", "tokens": [51252, 663, 935, 450, 1744, 12956, 81, 984, 1854, 264, 2199, 87, 13, 51382], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 45, "seek": 21970, "start": 240.35999999999999, "end": 242.38, "text": " Okay, so I have my list of probabilities.", "tokens": [51398, 1033, 11, 370, 286, 362, 452, 1329, 295, 33783, 13, 51499], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 46, "seek": 21970, "start": 243.0, "end": 244.45999999999998, "text": " I have my point inside the triangle.", "tokens": [51530, 286, 362, 452, 935, 1854, 264, 13369, 13, 51603], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 47, "seek": 21970, "start": 244.98, "end": 246.54, "text": " Now I want to do something physical.", "tokens": [51629, 823, 286, 528, 281, 360, 746, 4001, 13, 51707], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1252780884735344, "compression_ratio": 1.612099644128114, "no_speech_prob": 1.345930636907633e-12}, {"id": 48, "seek": 24654, "start": 246.54, "end": 250.28, "text": " I want to roll the die or shake the box or just let time pass.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 528, 281, 3373, 264, 978, 420, 10283, 264, 2424, 420, 445, 718, 565, 1320, 13, 50552], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 49, "seek": 24654, "start": 250.35999999999999, "end": 251.73999999999998, "text": " Like, I want to change the probabilities.", "tokens": [50556, 1743, 11, 286, 528, 281, 1319, 264, 33783, 13, 50625], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 50, "seek": 24654, "start": 252.56, "end": 255.79999999999998, "text": " In the math, this is called a stochastic map, right?", "tokens": [50666, 682, 264, 5221, 11, 341, 307, 1219, 257, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 11, 558, 30, 50828], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 51, "seek": 24654, "start": 255.79999999999998, "end": 258.48, "text": " Yeah, a stochastic map is simply a mathematical transformation.", "tokens": [50828, 865, 11, 257, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 307, 2935, 257, 18894, 9887, 13, 50962], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 52, "seek": 24654, "start": 259.24, "end": 266.24, "text": " You can think of it as a matrix that grabs your current point in the probability simplex and moves it to a new valid point.", "tokens": [51000, 509, 393, 519, 295, 309, 382, 257, 8141, 300, 30028, 428, 2190, 935, 294, 264, 8482, 2199, 87, 293, 6067, 309, 281, 257, 777, 7363, 935, 13, 51350], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 53, "seek": 24654, "start": 266.38, "end": 267.98, "text": " And there are rules for that movement.", "tokens": [51357, 400, 456, 366, 4474, 337, 300, 3963, 13, 51437], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 54, "seek": 24654, "start": 268.08, "end": 268.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51442, 1779, 13, 51450], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09695950094259011, "compression_ratio": 1.670940170940171, "no_speech_prob": 1.3145469955622158e-12}, {"id": 55, "seek": 26824, "start": 268.24, "end": 273.40000000000003, "text": " The only fundamental rule of a stochastic map is that it cannot break the laws of probability.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 787, 8088, 4978, 295, 257, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 307, 300, 309, 2644, 1821, 264, 6064, 295, 8482, 13, 50623], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1365180557424372, "compression_ratio": 1.5550660792951543, "no_speech_prob": 1.4269176118464344e-12}, {"id": 56, "seek": 26824, "start": 273.94, "end": 278.48, "text": " It'd have to preserve the fact that your total certainty sums to 1.", "tokens": [50650, 467, 1116, 362, 281, 15665, 264, 1186, 300, 428, 3217, 27022, 34499, 281, 502, 13, 50877], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1365180557424372, "compression_ratio": 1.5550660792951543, "no_speech_prob": 1.4269176118464344e-12}, {"id": 57, "seek": 26824, "start": 279.0, "end": 284.0, "text": " Like, no matter how you transform the system, the columns of this matrix have to add up to 1.", "tokens": [50903, 1743, 11, 572, 1871, 577, 291, 4088, 264, 1185, 11, 264, 13766, 295, 341, 8141, 362, 281, 909, 493, 281, 502, 13, 51153], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1365180557424372, "compression_ratio": 1.5550660792951543, "no_speech_prob": 1.4269176118464344e-12}, {"id": 58, "seek": 26824, "start": 284.16, "end": 286.86, "text": " And obviously, you can't suddenly have a negative probability.", "tokens": [51161, 400, 2745, 11, 291, 393, 380, 5800, 362, 257, 3671, 8482, 13, 51296], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1365180557424372, "compression_ratio": 1.5550660792951543, "no_speech_prob": 1.4269176118464344e-12}, {"id": 59, "seek": 26824, "start": 287.06, "end": 288.2, "text": " Yeah, that makes intuitive sense.", "tokens": [51306, 865, 11, 300, 1669, 21769, 2020, 13, 51363], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1365180557424372, "compression_ratio": 1.5550660792951543, "no_speech_prob": 1.4269176118464344e-12}, {"id": 60, "seek": 28820, "start": 288.2, "end": 292.12, "text": " You can't have, like, a negative 10% chance of a coin landing on heads.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 393, 380, 362, 11, 411, 11, 257, 3671, 1266, 4, 2931, 295, 257, 11464, 11202, 322, 8050, 13, 50561], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 61, "seek": 28820, "start": 292.21999999999997, "end": 292.82, "text": " That's absurd.", "tokens": [50566, 663, 311, 19774, 13, 50596], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 62, "seek": 28820, "start": 293.15999999999997, "end": 295.42, "text": " But fliction almost immediately narrows our focus here.", "tokens": [50613, 583, 932, 4105, 1920, 4258, 6397, 1509, 527, 1879, 510, 13, 50726], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 63, "seek": 28820, "start": 295.58, "end": 297.42, "text": " We don't just look at any stochastic math.", "tokens": [50734, 492, 500, 380, 445, 574, 412, 604, 342, 8997, 2750, 5221, 13, 50826], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 64, "seek": 28820, "start": 297.68, "end": 303.52, "text": " We look at a very specific restriction called a bistochastic matrix or a doubly stochastic matrix.", "tokens": [50839, 492, 574, 412, 257, 588, 2685, 29529, 1219, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 420, 257, 10831, 356, 342, 8997, 2750, 8141, 13, 51131], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 65, "seek": 28820, "start": 303.58, "end": 304.2, "text": " Yes, exactly.", "tokens": [51134, 1079, 11, 2293, 13, 51165], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 66, "seek": 28820, "start": 304.28, "end": 308.98, "text": " Walk me through the difference there because this seems to be the engine for the entire first act of the manuscript.", "tokens": [51169, 10818, 385, 807, 264, 2649, 456, 570, 341, 2544, 281, 312, 264, 2848, 337, 264, 2302, 700, 605, 295, 264, 23928, 13, 51404], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10813667990944602, "compression_ratio": 1.6023166023166022, "no_speech_prob": 1.2891262493250344e-12}, {"id": 67, "seek": 30898, "start": 308.98, "end": 312.88, "text": " The distinction is subtle, but it's mathematically profound.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 16844, 307, 13743, 11, 457, 309, 311, 44003, 14382, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 68, "seek": 30898, "start": 314.14000000000004, "end": 318.5, "text": " A regular stochastic matrix just requires its columns to sum to 1.", "tokens": [50623, 316, 3890, 342, 8997, 2750, 8141, 445, 7029, 1080, 13766, 281, 2408, 281, 502, 13, 50841], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 69, "seek": 30898, "start": 319.40000000000003, "end": 323.84000000000003, "text": " A bistochastic matrix demands that both the columns and the rows sum to 1.", "tokens": [50886, 316, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 15107, 300, 1293, 264, 13766, 293, 264, 13241, 2408, 281, 502, 13, 51108], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 70, "seek": 30898, "start": 324.0, "end": 325.22, "text": " Okay, but why does that matter?", "tokens": [51116, 1033, 11, 457, 983, 775, 300, 1871, 30, 51177], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 71, "seek": 30898, "start": 325.36, "end": 330.42, "text": " Like, what does forcing the rows to equal 1 actually do to our system physically?", "tokens": [51184, 1743, 11, 437, 775, 19030, 264, 13241, 281, 2681, 502, 767, 360, 281, 527, 1185, 9762, 30, 51437], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 72, "seek": 30898, "start": 330.72, "end": 333.24, "text": " It creates a perfectly balanced transformation.", "tokens": [51452, 467, 7829, 257, 6239, 13902, 9887, 13, 51578], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15482468551464296, "compression_ratio": 1.5895196506550218, "no_speech_prob": 2.262750533818525e-12}, {"id": 73, "seek": 33324, "start": 333.24, "end": 344.1, "text": " So think about what happens if you feed the ultimate state of uncertainty, you know, the completely uniform distribution where every single outcome is equally likely, into a bistochastic matrix.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 519, 466, 437, 2314, 498, 291, 3154, 264, 9705, 1785, 295, 15697, 11, 291, 458, 11, 264, 2584, 9452, 7316, 689, 633, 2167, 9700, 307, 12309, 3700, 11, 666, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 13, 50908], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 74, "seek": 33324, "start": 344.28000000000003, "end": 344.38, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50917, 1779, 13, 50922], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 75, "seek": 33324, "start": 344.8, "end": 349.98, "text": " Because the rows also sum to 1, the matrix spits that exact same uniform distribution right back out.", "tokens": [50943, 1436, 264, 13241, 611, 2408, 281, 502, 11, 264, 8141, 637, 1208, 300, 1900, 912, 9452, 7316, 558, 646, 484, 13, 51202], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 76, "seek": 33324, "start": 350.42, "end": 351.40000000000003, "text": " It doesn't skew the board.", "tokens": [51224, 467, 1177, 380, 8756, 86, 264, 3150, 13, 51273], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 77, "seek": 33324, "start": 351.66, "end": 353.26, "text": " It doesn't favor one outcome over another.", "tokens": [51286, 467, 1177, 380, 2294, 472, 9700, 670, 1071, 13, 51366], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 78, "seek": 33324, "start": 353.32, "end": 353.78000000000003, "text": " Oh, I see.", "tokens": [51369, 876, 11, 286, 536, 13, 51392], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 79, "seek": 33324, "start": 353.86, "end": 356.02, "text": " It's like a casino game that is actually fair.", "tokens": [51396, 467, 311, 411, 257, 36278, 1216, 300, 307, 767, 3143, 13, 51504], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 80, "seek": 33324, "start": 356.14, "end": 361.26, "text": " If you walk in with absolutely no information, the game doesn't secretly push you toward losing.", "tokens": [51510, 759, 291, 1792, 294, 365, 3122, 572, 1589, 11, 264, 1216, 1177, 380, 22611, 2944, 291, 7361, 7027, 13, 51766], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11363010997920073, "compression_ratio": 1.7032258064516128, "no_speech_prob": 1.455068270733717e-12}, {"id": 81, "seek": 36126, "start": 361.26, "end": 368.84, "text": " Let's push that idea even further, using the Burkhoff von Neumann theorem, which the manuscript relies on really heavily here.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 2944, 300, 1558, 754, 3052, 11, 1228, 264, 7031, 74, 1289, 602, 2957, 1734, 449, 969, 20904, 11, 597, 264, 23928, 30910, 322, 534, 10950, 510, 13, 50744], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 82, "seek": 36126, "start": 369.5, "end": 373.64, "text": " This theorem provides a beautiful geometric way to understand these balanced matrices.", "tokens": [50777, 639, 20904, 6417, 257, 2238, 33246, 636, 281, 1223, 613, 13902, 32284, 13, 50984], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 83, "seek": 36126, "start": 374.06, "end": 374.7, "text": " What does it say?", "tokens": [51005, 708, 775, 309, 584, 30, 51037], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 84, "seek": 36126, "start": 374.88, "end": 384.14, "text": " It basically proves that any bistochastic matrix, no matter how messy or fuzzy it looks on paper, is actually just a mixture of simple, definitive permutations.", "tokens": [51046, 467, 1936, 25019, 300, 604, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 11, 572, 1871, 577, 16191, 420, 34710, 309, 1542, 322, 3035, 11, 307, 767, 445, 257, 9925, 295, 2199, 11, 28152, 4784, 325, 763, 13, 51509], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 85, "seek": 36126, "start": 384.71999999999997, "end": 387.38, "text": " Define permutation for me in this specific context.", "tokens": [51538, 9548, 533, 4784, 11380, 337, 385, 294, 341, 2685, 4319, 13, 51671], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 86, "seek": 36126, "start": 387.38, "end": 391.06, "text": " A permutation is a strict, definitive relabeling of reality.", "tokens": [51671, 316, 4784, 11380, 307, 257, 10910, 11, 28152, 1039, 455, 11031, 295, 4103, 13, 51855], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507527263224625, "compression_ratio": 1.6343042071197411, "no_speech_prob": 1.3091444161367205e-12}, {"id": 87, "seek": 39126, "start": 391.84, "end": 393.4, "text": " Outcome 1 becomes outcome 2.", "tokens": [50394, 5925, 1102, 502, 3643, 9700, 568, 13, 50472], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 88, "seek": 39126, "start": 393.8, "end": 395.32, "text": " Outcome 2 becomes outcome 3.", "tokens": [50492, 5925, 1102, 568, 3643, 9700, 805, 13, 50568], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 89, "seek": 39126, "start": 395.78, "end": 398.36, "text": " There was no fuzziness, no rolling the dice.", "tokens": [50591, 821, 390, 572, 283, 16740, 1324, 11, 572, 9439, 264, 10313, 13, 50720], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 90, "seek": 39126, "start": 398.56, "end": 399.94, "text": " It's absolute certainty.", "tokens": [50730, 467, 311, 8236, 27022, 13, 50799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 91, "seek": 39126, "start": 400.03999999999996, "end": 401.38, "text": " So it's totally deterministic.", "tokens": [50804, 407, 309, 311, 3879, 15957, 3142, 13, 50871], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 92, "seek": 39126, "start": 401.64, "end": 401.86, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50884, 1779, 13, 50895], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 93, "seek": 39126, "start": 402.21999999999997, "end": 406.36, "text": " If you know exactly where you started, a permutation tells you exactly where you end up.", "tokens": [50913, 759, 291, 458, 2293, 689, 291, 1409, 11, 257, 4784, 11380, 5112, 291, 2293, 689, 291, 917, 493, 13, 51120], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 94, "seek": 39126, "start": 406.76, "end": 413.08, "text": " The Burkhoff von Neumann theorem says that the space of all possible bistochastic matrices forms a shape, like a polytope.", "tokens": [51140, 440, 7031, 74, 1289, 602, 2957, 1734, 449, 969, 20904, 1619, 300, 264, 1901, 295, 439, 1944, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 6422, 257, 3909, 11, 411, 257, 6754, 83, 1114, 13, 51456], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1684559009693287, "compression_ratio": 1.584033613445378, "no_speech_prob": 1.5794585691425445e-12}, {"id": 95, "seek": 41308, "start": 413.08, "end": 416.21999999999997, "text": " And the sharp corners of that shape are the pure permutations.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 264, 8199, 12413, 295, 300, 3909, 366, 264, 6075, 4784, 325, 763, 13, 50522], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 96, "seek": 41308, "start": 416.96, "end": 422.21999999999997, "text": " Every fuzzy probabilistic transition inside the shape is literally just a weighted average of those sharp corners.", "tokens": [50559, 2048, 34710, 31959, 3142, 6034, 1854, 264, 3909, 307, 3736, 445, 257, 32807, 4274, 295, 729, 8199, 12413, 13, 50822], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 97, "seek": 41308, "start": 422.64, "end": 425.84, "text": " Man, this reminds me so much of shuffling a deck of cards.", "tokens": [50843, 2458, 11, 341, 12025, 385, 370, 709, 295, 402, 1245, 1688, 257, 9341, 295, 5632, 13, 51003], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 98, "seek": 41308, "start": 426.5, "end": 428.62, "text": " Like, let's say you buy a brand new deck.", "tokens": [51036, 1743, 11, 718, 311, 584, 291, 2256, 257, 3360, 777, 9341, 13, 51142], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 99, "seek": 41308, "start": 428.78, "end": 429.97999999999996, "text": " It is perfectly ordered.", "tokens": [51150, 467, 307, 6239, 8866, 13, 51210], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 100, "seek": 41308, "start": 430.18, "end": 433.0, "text": " You know the Ace of Spades is, say, the 40th card down.", "tokens": [51220, 509, 458, 264, 24900, 295, 1738, 2977, 307, 11, 584, 11, 264, 3356, 392, 2920, 760, 13, 51361], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 101, "seek": 41308, "start": 433.18, "end": 435.36, "text": " That is a state of absolute certainty.", "tokens": [51370, 663, 307, 257, 1785, 295, 8236, 27022, 13, 51479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 102, "seek": 41308, "start": 435.59999999999997, "end": 438.0, "text": " You are at a corner of our probability simplex.", "tokens": [51491, 509, 366, 412, 257, 4538, 295, 527, 8482, 2199, 87, 13, 51611], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.145804500579834, "compression_ratio": 1.5928571428571427, "no_speech_prob": 1.561296339609719e-12}, {"id": 103, "seek": 43800, "start": 438.0, "end": 438.48, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 50389], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 104, "seek": 43800, "start": 439.0, "end": 446.68, "text": " Now, if you do a perfect deterministic cut of the deck, just taking half the cards and putting them on the bottom, that is a permutation.", "tokens": [50415, 823, 11, 498, 291, 360, 257, 2176, 15957, 3142, 1723, 295, 264, 9341, 11, 445, 1940, 1922, 264, 5632, 293, 3372, 552, 322, 264, 2767, 11, 300, 307, 257, 4784, 11380, 13, 50799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 105, "seek": 43800, "start": 447.02, "end": 449.06, "text": " And you still know exactly where the Ace of Spades is.", "tokens": [50816, 400, 291, 920, 458, 2293, 689, 264, 24900, 295, 1738, 2977, 307, 13, 50918], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 106, "seek": 43800, "start": 449.36, "end": 450.98, "text": " Your certainty hasn't degraded at all.", "tokens": [50933, 2260, 27022, 6132, 380, 24740, 292, 412, 439, 13, 51014], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 107, "seek": 43800, "start": 451.06, "end": 452.18, "text": " It just shifted its label.", "tokens": [51018, 467, 445, 18892, 1080, 7645, 13, 51074], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 108, "seek": 43800, "start": 452.34, "end": 452.58, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51082, 1779, 13, 51094], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 109, "seek": 43800, "start": 452.92, "end": 455.02, "text": " But when I do a messy Rufal Shuffle...", "tokens": [51111, 583, 562, 286, 360, 257, 16191, 497, 2947, 304, 1160, 21665, 485, 51216], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 110, "seek": 43800, "start": 455.02, "end": 455.98, "text": " Ah, yes.", "tokens": [51216, 2438, 11, 2086, 13, 51264], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 111, "seek": 43800, "start": 456.1, "end": 457.76, "text": " That is a bistochastic map.", "tokens": [51270, 663, 307, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 4471, 13, 51353], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 112, "seek": 43800, "start": 458.08, "end": 467.16, "text": " I am essentially mixing together a bunch of different deterministic permutations based on the random physical probabilities of how my thumbs let the cards in or leave.", "tokens": [51369, 286, 669, 4476, 11983, 1214, 257, 3840, 295, 819, 15957, 3142, 4784, 325, 763, 2361, 322, 264, 4974, 4001, 33783, 295, 577, 452, 8838, 718, 264, 5632, 294, 420, 1856, 13, 51823], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15378554323886304, "compression_ratio": 1.6340694006309149, "no_speech_prob": 1.8042205099724762e-12}, {"id": 113, "seek": 46716, "start": 467.16, "end": 471.44, "text": " And notice what happens to your knowledge as you keep doing that messy Rufal Shuffle.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 3449, 437, 2314, 281, 428, 3601, 382, 291, 1066, 884, 300, 16191, 497, 2947, 304, 1160, 21665, 13, 50579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 114, "seek": 46716, "start": 471.64000000000004, "end": 475.94, "text": " You started with the Ace of Spades at 100% probability in one specific slot.", "tokens": [50589, 509, 1409, 365, 264, 24900, 295, 1738, 2977, 412, 2319, 4, 8482, 294, 472, 2685, 14747, 13, 50804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 115, "seek": 46716, "start": 476.16, "end": 480.40000000000003, "text": " After one shuffle, maybe it's spread across five possible slots.", "tokens": [50815, 2381, 472, 39426, 11, 1310, 309, 311, 3974, 2108, 1732, 1944, 24266, 13, 51027], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 116, "seek": 46716, "start": 480.48, "end": 481.68, "text": " So my certainty is dropping.", "tokens": [51031, 407, 452, 27022, 307, 13601, 13, 51091], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 117, "seek": 46716, "start": 482.12, "end": 485.08000000000004, "text": " After 10 shuffles, you have absolutely no idea where it is.", "tokens": [51113, 2381, 1266, 402, 1245, 904, 11, 291, 362, 3122, 572, 1558, 689, 309, 307, 13, 51261], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 118, "seek": 46716, "start": 485.56, "end": 491.02000000000004, "text": " Your probability distribution has flattened out to be completely uniform across all 52 cards.", "tokens": [51285, 2260, 8482, 7316, 575, 24183, 292, 484, 281, 312, 2584, 9452, 2108, 439, 18079, 5632, 13, 51558], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 119, "seek": 46716, "start": 491.24, "end": 493.58000000000004, "text": " Which introduces the concept of majorization.", "tokens": [51569, 3013, 31472, 264, 3410, 295, 2563, 2144, 13, 51686], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1525898673317649, "compression_ratio": 1.5616438356164384, "no_speech_prob": 1.259448925358675e-12}, {"id": 120, "seek": 49358, "start": 493.58, "end": 497.2, "text": " I found this part so fascinating when reading the source.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 1352, 341, 644, 370, 10343, 562, 3760, 264, 4009, 13, 50546], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790151684783225, "compression_ratio": 1.6382113821138211, "no_speech_prob": 1.8112807260994845e-12}, {"id": 121, "seek": 49358, "start": 497.78, "end": 503.0, "text": " Majorization is essentially the rigorous mathematical language for that flattening out we just described.", "tokens": [50575, 15581, 2144, 307, 4476, 264, 29882, 18894, 2856, 337, 300, 24183, 278, 484, 321, 445, 7619, 13, 50836], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790151684783225, "compression_ratio": 1.6382113821138211, "no_speech_prob": 1.8112807260994845e-12}, {"id": 122, "seek": 49358, "start": 503.2, "end": 511.38, "text": " When you apply a bistochastic matrix to a probability distribution, the resulting distribution is always majorized by the original one.", "tokens": [50846, 1133, 291, 3079, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 281, 257, 8482, 7316, 11, 264, 16505, 7316, 307, 1009, 2563, 1602, 538, 264, 3380, 472, 13, 51255], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790151684783225, "compression_ratio": 1.6382113821138211, "no_speech_prob": 1.8112807260994845e-12}, {"id": 123, "seek": 49358, "start": 511.59999999999997, "end": 513.0799999999999, "text": " What does that mean operationally?", "tokens": [51266, 708, 775, 300, 914, 6916, 379, 30, 51340], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790151684783225, "compression_ratio": 1.6382113821138211, "no_speech_prob": 1.8112807260994845e-12}, {"id": 124, "seek": 49358, "start": 513.92, "end": 517.1, "text": " Operationally, it means the system is forced to become more uniform.", "tokens": [51382, 27946, 379, 11, 309, 1355, 264, 1185, 307, 7579, 281, 1813, 544, 9452, 13, 51541], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790151684783225, "compression_ratio": 1.6382113821138211, "no_speech_prob": 1.8112807260994845e-12}, {"id": 125, "seek": 51710, "start": 517.1, "end": 519.52, "text": " It's kind of like a Robin Hood effect for data.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 733, 295, 411, 257, 16533, 33213, 1802, 337, 1412, 13, 50486], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12080060958862304, "compression_ratio": 1.6583629893238434, "no_speech_prob": 1.7007785154191102e-12}, {"id": 126, "seek": 51710, "start": 520.2, "end": 527.14, "text": " The math intrinsically takes probability away from the most likely outcomes and distributes it to the least likely ones.", "tokens": [50520, 440, 5221, 28621, 984, 2516, 8482, 1314, 490, 264, 881, 3700, 10070, 293, 4400, 1819, 309, 281, 264, 1935, 3700, 2306, 13, 50867], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12080060958862304, "compression_ratio": 1.6583629893238434, "no_speech_prob": 1.7007785154191102e-12}, {"id": 127, "seek": 51710, "start": 527.3000000000001, "end": 532.82, "text": " And because it is mathematically guaranteed that this bistochastic shuffling always flattens the distribution,", "tokens": [50875, 400, 570, 309, 307, 44003, 18031, 300, 341, 18209, 8997, 2750, 402, 1245, 1688, 1009, 932, 1591, 694, 264, 7316, 11, 51151], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12080060958862304, "compression_ratio": 1.6583629893238434, "no_speech_prob": 1.7007785154191102e-12}, {"id": 128, "seek": 51710, "start": 533.26, "end": 539.28, "text": " it mathematically proves that Shannon entropy, which is the measure of disorder or missing information, can never decrease.", "tokens": [51173, 309, 44003, 25019, 300, 28974, 30867, 11, 597, 307, 264, 3481, 295, 13399, 420, 5361, 1589, 11, 393, 1128, 11514, 13, 51474], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12080060958862304, "compression_ratio": 1.6583629893238434, "no_speech_prob": 1.7007785154191102e-12}, {"id": 129, "seek": 51710, "start": 539.52, "end": 542.6600000000001, "text": " This is a really vital methodological point in the manuscript.", "tokens": [51486, 639, 307, 257, 534, 11707, 3170, 4383, 935, 294, 264, 23928, 13, 51643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12080060958862304, "compression_ratio": 1.6583629893238434, "no_speech_prob": 1.7007785154191102e-12}, {"id": 130, "seek": 54266, "start": 542.66, "end": 544.04, "text": " Look at what we just did there.", "tokens": [50365, 2053, 412, 437, 321, 445, 630, 456, 13, 50434], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 131, "seek": 54266, "start": 544.36, "end": 546.28, "text": " We proved that entropy must increase.", "tokens": [50450, 492, 14617, 300, 30867, 1633, 3488, 13, 50546], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 132, "seek": 54266, "start": 546.5799999999999, "end": 548.28, "text": " But we didn't even mention thermodynamics.", "tokens": [50561, 583, 321, 994, 380, 754, 2152, 8810, 35483, 13, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 133, "seek": 54266, "start": 548.62, "end": 553.56, "text": " We didn't talk about heat or gas particles in a box or the arrow of time.", "tokens": [50663, 492, 994, 380, 751, 466, 3738, 420, 4211, 10007, 294, 257, 2424, 420, 264, 11610, 295, 565, 13, 50910], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 134, "seek": 54266, "start": 553.8199999999999, "end": 554.06, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50923, 7587, 13, 50935], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 135, "seek": 54266, "start": 554.28, "end": 561.6, "text": " The increase of entropy here is a purely structural geometric consequence of how bistochastic matrices work.", "tokens": [50946, 440, 3488, 295, 30867, 510, 307, 257, 17491, 15067, 33246, 18326, 295, 577, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 589, 13, 51312], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 136, "seek": 54266, "start": 562.06, "end": 566.76, "text": " It is a fundamental feature of the admissible rules of mixing information.", "tokens": [51335, 467, 307, 257, 8088, 4111, 295, 264, 5910, 41073, 4474, 295, 11983, 1589, 13, 51570], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 137, "seek": 54266, "start": 567.1, "end": 569.3199999999999, "text": " Okay, so I am following the logic perfectly here.", "tokens": [51587, 1033, 11, 370, 286, 669, 3480, 264, 9952, 6239, 510, 13, 51698], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16343375871766289, "compression_ratio": 1.5772058823529411, "no_speech_prob": 2.1583455508955574e-12}, {"id": 138, "seek": 56932, "start": 569.32, "end": 577.5400000000001, "text": " We have classical probabilities, we have bistochastic matrices acting like messy card shuffles, entropy goes up, it all makes total sense.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 362, 13735, 33783, 11, 321, 362, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 6577, 411, 16191, 2920, 402, 1245, 904, 11, 30867, 1709, 493, 11, 309, 439, 1669, 3217, 2020, 13, 50776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10490007201830547, "compression_ratio": 1.594306049822064, "no_speech_prob": 1.3830275900211797e-12}, {"id": 139, "seek": 56932, "start": 578.08, "end": 584.6600000000001, "text": " So if standard probability works so perfectly, why did physicists ever need to invent imaginary numbers?", "tokens": [50803, 407, 498, 3832, 8482, 1985, 370, 6239, 11, 983, 630, 48716, 1562, 643, 281, 7962, 26164, 3547, 30, 51132], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10490007201830547, "compression_ratio": 1.594306049822064, "no_speech_prob": 1.3830275900211797e-12}, {"id": 140, "seek": 56932, "start": 584.84, "end": 588.6800000000001, "text": " Like where does this beautiful intuitive classical math actually break?", "tokens": [51141, 1743, 689, 775, 341, 2238, 21769, 13735, 5221, 767, 1821, 30, 51333], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10490007201830547, "compression_ratio": 1.594306049822064, "no_speech_prob": 1.3830275900211797e-12}, {"id": 141, "seek": 56932, "start": 589.22, "end": 591.84, "text": " Because right now I'm very comfortable in my classical casino.", "tokens": [51360, 1436, 558, 586, 286, 478, 588, 4619, 294, 452, 13735, 36278, 13, 51491], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10490007201830547, "compression_ratio": 1.594306049822064, "no_speech_prob": 1.3830275900211797e-12}, {"id": 142, "seek": 56932, "start": 592.0, "end": 594.8000000000001, "text": " Well that comfort is exactly what the manuscript is about to shatter.", "tokens": [51499, 1042, 300, 3400, 307, 2293, 437, 264, 23928, 307, 466, 281, 402, 1161, 13, 51639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10490007201830547, "compression_ratio": 1.594306049822064, "no_speech_prob": 1.3830275900211797e-12}, {"id": 143, "seek": 59480, "start": 594.8, "end": 600.18, "text": " If we want to find the fracture line where classical reality breaks, we need to introduce the concept of lifts.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 321, 528, 281, 915, 264, 36877, 1622, 689, 13735, 4103, 9857, 11, 321, 643, 281, 5366, 264, 3410, 295, 30501, 13, 50634], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 144, "seek": 59480, "start": 600.52, "end": 602.9799999999999, "text": " Okay, let's transition into the first major break in the math.", "tokens": [50651, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 6034, 666, 264, 700, 2563, 1821, 294, 264, 5221, 13, 50774], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 145, "seek": 59480, "start": 603.1999999999999, "end": 610.88, "text": " Because what blew my mind in the source material is that classical probability doesn't fail in some, you know, multi-billion dollar particle accelerator.", "tokens": [50785, 1436, 437, 19075, 452, 1575, 294, 264, 4009, 2527, 307, 300, 13735, 8482, 1177, 380, 3061, 294, 512, 11, 291, 458, 11, 4825, 12, 65, 11836, 7241, 12359, 39889, 13, 51169], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 146, "seek": 59480, "start": 611.14, "end": 614.7199999999999, "text": " It fails simply by increasing our number of choices from two to three.", "tokens": [51182, 467, 18199, 2935, 538, 5662, 527, 1230, 295, 7994, 490, 732, 281, 1045, 13, 51361], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 147, "seek": 59480, "start": 615.04, "end": 617.66, "text": " It is stunningly elegant when you lay it all out.", "tokens": [51377, 467, 307, 18550, 356, 21117, 562, 291, 2360, 309, 439, 484, 13, 51508], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 148, "seek": 59480, "start": 618.2199999999999, "end": 619.9799999999999, "text": " So let's define what we mean by a lift.", "tokens": [51536, 407, 718, 311, 6964, 437, 321, 914, 538, 257, 5533, 13, 51624], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10838187454093216, "compression_ratio": 1.652027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.3513185796037996e-12}, {"id": 149, "seek": 61998, "start": 619.98, "end": 624.5, "text": " In quantum mechanics, we don't calculate probabilities directly.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 13018, 12939, 11, 321, 500, 380, 8873, 33783, 3838, 13, 50591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 150, "seek": 61998, "start": 624.98, "end": 625.98, "text": " We calculate amplitudes.", "tokens": [50615, 492, 8873, 9731, 16451, 13, 50665], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 151, "seek": 61998, "start": 626.78, "end": 626.9200000000001, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50705, 1779, 13, 50712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 152, "seek": 61998, "start": 627.22, "end": 629.32, "text": " Amplitudes are deeper mathematical objects.", "tokens": [50727, 2012, 564, 16451, 366, 7731, 18894, 6565, 13, 50832], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 153, "seek": 61998, "start": 629.82, "end": 633.52, "text": " They can be negative or they can be complex numbers with an imaginary part.", "tokens": [50857, 814, 393, 312, 3671, 420, 436, 393, 312, 3997, 3547, 365, 364, 26164, 644, 13, 51042], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 154, "seek": 61998, "start": 633.9200000000001, "end": 642.12, "text": " To get from the hidden quantum amplitude to the physical probability that you actually measure on a screen, you have to square the amplitude.", "tokens": [51062, 1407, 483, 490, 264, 7633, 13018, 27433, 281, 264, 4001, 8482, 300, 291, 767, 3481, 322, 257, 2568, 11, 291, 362, 281, 3732, 264, 27433, 13, 51472], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17383225758870444, "compression_ratio": 1.6422018348623852, "no_speech_prob": 1.2157647935259752e-12}, {"id": 155, "seek": 64212, "start": 642.12, "end": 648.04, "text": " So if I have a 50% chance of something happening, the underlying amplitude would be the square root of 0.5.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 498, 286, 362, 257, 2625, 4, 2931, 295, 746, 2737, 11, 264, 14217, 27433, 576, 312, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 1958, 13, 20, 13, 50661], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09554196271029386, "compression_ratio": 1.7453183520599251, "no_speech_prob": 1.1646287233213481e-12}, {"id": 156, "seek": 64212, "start": 648.12, "end": 648.54, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50665, 7587, 13, 50686], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09554196271029386, "compression_ratio": 1.7453183520599251, "no_speech_prob": 1.1646287233213481e-12}, {"id": 157, "seek": 64212, "start": 648.9, "end": 651.02, "text": " So a lift is basically a reverse engineering question.", "tokens": [50704, 407, 257, 5533, 307, 1936, 257, 9943, 7043, 1168, 13, 50810], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09554196271029386, "compression_ratio": 1.7453183520599251, "no_speech_prob": 1.1646287233213481e-12}, {"id": 158, "seek": 64212, "start": 651.54, "end": 663.36, "text": " If I hand you a valid bistochastic matrix of probabilities, you can ask, hey, can I find a matrix of underlying amplitudes that, when I square every individual entry, perfectly recreates my matrix of probabilities?", "tokens": [50836, 759, 286, 1011, 291, 257, 7363, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 295, 33783, 11, 291, 393, 1029, 11, 4177, 11, 393, 286, 915, 257, 8141, 295, 14217, 9731, 16451, 300, 11, 562, 286, 3732, 633, 2609, 8729, 11, 6239, 850, 620, 279, 452, 8141, 295, 33783, 30, 51427], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09554196271029386, "compression_ratio": 1.7453183520599251, "no_speech_prob": 1.1646287233213481e-12}, {"id": 159, "seek": 64212, "start": 663.7, "end": 667.72, "text": " You are trying to lift the probability matrix back up into an amplitude matrix.", "tokens": [51444, 509, 366, 1382, 281, 5533, 264, 8482, 8141, 646, 493, 666, 364, 27433, 8141, 13, 51645], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09554196271029386, "compression_ratio": 1.7453183520599251, "no_speech_prob": 1.1646287233213481e-12}, {"id": 160, "seek": 66772, "start": 667.72, "end": 671.62, "text": " Yes, and the manuscript outlines two different tools we can use to build these lifts.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 11, 293, 264, 23928, 40125, 732, 819, 3873, 321, 393, 764, 281, 1322, 613, 30501, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 161, "seek": 66772, "start": 671.82, "end": 674.6800000000001, "text": " We have orthostochastic lifts and unistochastic lifts.", "tokens": [50570, 492, 362, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 30501, 293, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 30501, 13, 50713], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 162, "seek": 66772, "start": 674.88, "end": 675.88, "text": " Break down the difference for me.", "tokens": [50723, 16925, 760, 264, 2649, 337, 385, 13, 50773], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 163, "seek": 66772, "start": 676.22, "end": 681.34, "text": " An orthostochastic lift restricts you to using only real numbers, just regular numbers.", "tokens": [50790, 1107, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 5533, 7694, 82, 291, 281, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 11, 445, 3890, 3547, 13, 51046], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 164, "seek": 66772, "start": 682.36, "end": 689.02, "text": " Geometrically, this requires finding a real orthogonal matrix, which is basically just a rotation in standard multidimensional space.", "tokens": [51097, 2876, 649, 81, 984, 11, 341, 7029, 5006, 257, 957, 41488, 8141, 11, 597, 307, 1936, 445, 257, 12447, 294, 3832, 2120, 327, 332, 11075, 1901, 13, 51430], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 165, "seek": 66772, "start": 689.12, "end": 690.5400000000001, "text": " Okay, and the unistochastic one.", "tokens": [51435, 1033, 11, 293, 264, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 472, 13, 51506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 166, "seek": 66772, "start": 690.9, "end": 695.24, "text": " A unistochastic lift allows you to use complex numbers, you know, numbers with that imaginary eye.", "tokens": [51524, 316, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 5533, 4045, 291, 281, 764, 3997, 3547, 11, 291, 458, 11, 3547, 365, 300, 26164, 3313, 13, 51741], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10996298356489702, "compression_ratio": 1.8526315789473684, "no_speech_prob": 1.4724441284308387e-12}, {"id": 167, "seek": 69524, "start": 695.24, "end": 700.8, "text": " This requires finding a unitary matrix, which is the standard workhorse of textbook quantum mechanics.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 7029, 5006, 257, 517, 4109, 8141, 11, 597, 307, 264, 3832, 589, 45079, 295, 25591, 13018, 12939, 13, 50643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 168, "seek": 69524, "start": 701.08, "end": 703.46, "text": " So it's basically a battle of the mathematical fields.", "tokens": [50657, 407, 309, 311, 1936, 257, 4635, 295, 264, 18894, 7909, 13, 50776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 169, "seek": 69524, "start": 703.7, "end": 706.04, "text": " Real number math versus complex number math.", "tokens": [50788, 8467, 1230, 5221, 5717, 3997, 1230, 5221, 13, 50905], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 170, "seek": 69524, "start": 706.22, "end": 710.14, "text": " Infliction sets the stage with the n equals 2 degeneracy theorem.", "tokens": [50914, 682, 3423, 4105, 6352, 264, 3233, 365, 264, 297, 6915, 568, 40520, 2551, 20904, 13, 51110], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 171, "seek": 69524, "start": 711.36, "end": 716.22, "text": " Tell me what happens when our universe only has two possible outcomes, like a simple coin flip.", "tokens": [51171, 5115, 385, 437, 2314, 562, 527, 6445, 787, 575, 732, 1944, 10070, 11, 411, 257, 2199, 11464, 7929, 13, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 172, "seek": 69524, "start": 716.84, "end": 717.42, "text": " Heads or tails?", "tokens": [51445, 634, 5834, 420, 28537, 30, 51474], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414756397624592, "compression_ratio": 1.52, "no_speech_prob": 1.8825644168551925e-12}, {"id": 173, "seek": 71742, "start": 717.42, "end": 724.9599999999999, "text": " When you constrain a system to only two states, n equals 2, an incredible mathematical coincidence occurs.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 291, 1817, 7146, 257, 1185, 281, 787, 732, 4368, 11, 297, 6915, 568, 11, 364, 4651, 18894, 22137, 11843, 13, 50742], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11261249913109674, "compression_ratio": 1.6174863387978142, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552075686292577e-12}, {"id": 174, "seek": 71742, "start": 725.68, "end": 734.9399999999999, "text": " The set of all possible real-numbered orthostochastic matrices and the set of all possible complex-numbered unistochastic matrices are perfectly identical.", "tokens": [50778, 440, 992, 295, 439, 1944, 957, 12, 41261, 292, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 32284, 293, 264, 992, 295, 439, 1944, 3997, 12, 41261, 292, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 32284, 366, 6239, 14800, 13, 51241], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11261249913109674, "compression_ratio": 1.6174863387978142, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552075686292577e-12}, {"id": 175, "seek": 71742, "start": 735.04, "end": 736.4, "text": " Wait, they overlap exactly.", "tokens": [51246, 3802, 11, 436, 19959, 2293, 13, 51314], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11261249913109674, "compression_ratio": 1.6174863387978142, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552075686292577e-12}, {"id": 176, "seek": 71742, "start": 736.52, "end": 737.02, "text": " 100%.", "tokens": [51320, 2319, 6856, 51345], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11261249913109674, "compression_ratio": 1.6174863387978142, "no_speech_prob": 1.5552075686292577e-12}, {"id": 177, "seek": 73702, "start": 737.02, "end": 738.3, "text": " Meaning what, practically speaking?", "tokens": [50365, 19948, 437, 11, 15667, 4124, 30, 50429], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10763794336563502, "compression_ratio": 1.6428571428571428, "no_speech_prob": 1.6228971291831762e-12}, {"id": 178, "seek": 73702, "start": 738.3, "end": 751.02, "text": " It means if you have a 2x2 probability transition that can be recreated by squaring the entries of a complex matrix, it can also be perfectly recreated by squaring the entries of a purely real matrix.", "tokens": [50429, 467, 1355, 498, 291, 362, 257, 568, 87, 17, 8482, 6034, 300, 393, 312, 850, 26559, 538, 2339, 1921, 264, 23041, 295, 257, 3997, 8141, 11, 309, 393, 611, 312, 6239, 850, 26559, 538, 2339, 1921, 264, 23041, 295, 257, 17491, 957, 8141, 13, 51065], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10763794336563502, "compression_ratio": 1.6428571428571428, "no_speech_prob": 1.6228971291831762e-12}, {"id": 179, "seek": 73702, "start": 751.36, "end": 756.0799999999999, "text": " At the level of a single two-state system, they are mathematically indistinguishable.", "tokens": [51082, 1711, 264, 1496, 295, 257, 2167, 732, 12, 15406, 1185, 11, 436, 366, 44003, 1016, 468, 7050, 742, 712, 13, 51318], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10763794336563502, "compression_ratio": 1.6428571428571428, "no_speech_prob": 1.6228971291831762e-12}, {"id": 180, "seek": 75608, "start": 756.08, "end": 764.0400000000001, "text": " So if the entire universe only ever consisted of two state systems, we might never have stumbled into imaginary numbers at all.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 498, 264, 2302, 6445, 787, 1562, 38227, 295, 732, 1785, 3652, 11, 321, 1062, 1128, 362, 36668, 666, 26164, 3547, 412, 439, 13, 50763], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 181, "seek": 75608, "start": 764.1600000000001, "end": 767.5200000000001, "text": " Like, real numbers were doing the job perfectly well.", "tokens": [50769, 1743, 11, 957, 3547, 645, 884, 264, 1691, 6239, 731, 13, 50937], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 182, "seek": 75608, "start": 767.62, "end": 770.5400000000001, "text": " You would have absolutely no reason to invent complex numbers.", "tokens": [50942, 509, 576, 362, 3122, 572, 1778, 281, 7962, 3997, 3547, 13, 51088], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 183, "seek": 75608, "start": 770.74, "end": 778.58, "text": " A single 2x2 transition matrix simply cannot tell you whether the underlying scalar field of reality is real or complex.", "tokens": [51098, 316, 2167, 568, 87, 17, 6034, 8141, 2935, 2644, 980, 291, 1968, 264, 14217, 39684, 2519, 295, 4103, 307, 957, 420, 3997, 13, 51490], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 184, "seek": 75608, "start": 779.26, "end": 780.4000000000001, "text": " The math is degenerate.", "tokens": [51524, 440, 5221, 307, 40520, 473, 13, 51581], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 185, "seek": 75608, "start": 780.6400000000001, "end": 785.48, "text": " But then we get to the n is greater than or equal to 3 incompleteness theorem.", "tokens": [51593, 583, 550, 321, 483, 281, 264, 297, 307, 5044, 813, 420, 2681, 281, 805, 14036, 14657, 15264, 20904, 13, 51835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07761281507986563, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.2805088980420996e-12}, {"id": 186, "seek": 78548, "start": 785.48, "end": 791.62, "text": " The moment we add a third option, a three-sided die, a traffic light with three colors, the math just completely separates.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 1623, 321, 909, 257, 2636, 3614, 11, 257, 1045, 12, 30941, 978, 11, 257, 6419, 1442, 365, 1045, 4577, 11, 264, 5221, 445, 2584, 34149, 13, 50672], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 187, "seek": 78548, "start": 791.74, "end": 792.62, "text": " It doesn't just separate.", "tokens": [50678, 467, 1177, 380, 445, 4994, 13, 50722], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 188, "seek": 78548, "start": 792.76, "end": 794.24, "text": " The real numbers structurally fail.", "tokens": [50729, 440, 957, 3547, 6594, 6512, 3061, 13, 50803], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 189, "seek": 78548, "start": 794.76, "end": 800.0, "text": " The manuscript proves this using a very specific famous mathematical object as a counterexample.", "tokens": [50829, 440, 23928, 25019, 341, 1228, 257, 588, 2685, 4618, 18894, 2657, 382, 257, 1207, 323, 87, 335, 781, 13, 51091], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 190, "seek": 78548, "start": 800.28, "end": 803.66, "text": " It's called the 3x3 discrete Fourier transform matrix.", "tokens": [51105, 467, 311, 1219, 264, 805, 87, 18, 27706, 36810, 4088, 8141, 13, 51274], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 191, "seek": 78548, "start": 804.12, "end": 806.46, "text": " Now, we don't need to get bogged down in the name.", "tokens": [51297, 823, 11, 321, 500, 380, 643, 281, 483, 26132, 3004, 760, 294, 264, 1315, 13, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 192, "seek": 78548, "start": 806.76, "end": 812.24, "text": " All you really need to know is that if you take this specific 3x3 complex unitary matrix", "tokens": [51429, 1057, 291, 534, 643, 281, 458, 307, 300, 498, 291, 747, 341, 2685, 805, 87, 18, 3997, 517, 4109, 8141, 51703], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10150065187548028, "compression_ratio": 1.6335616438356164, "no_speech_prob": 2.133679517790643e-12}, {"id": 193, "seek": 81224, "start": 812.24, "end": 816.46, "text": " and you take the absolute value squared of every single entry,", "tokens": [50365, 293, 291, 747, 264, 8236, 2158, 8889, 295, 633, 2167, 8729, 11, 50576], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 194, "seek": 81224, "start": 816.88, "end": 821.38, "text": " you generate a 3x3 matrix where every single entry is exactly one-third.", "tokens": [50597, 291, 8460, 257, 805, 87, 18, 8141, 689, 633, 2167, 8729, 307, 2293, 472, 12, 25095, 13, 50822], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 195, "seek": 81224, "start": 821.38, "end": 826.44, "text": " Okay, so it creates a perfectly uniform 3x3 bistochastic matrix.", "tokens": [50822, 1033, 11, 370, 309, 7829, 257, 6239, 9452, 805, 87, 18, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 13, 51075], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 196, "seek": 81224, "start": 826.64, "end": 826.88, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51085, 1079, 13, 51097], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 197, "seek": 81224, "start": 827.46, "end": 830.74, "text": " And because we literally just built it from a complex unitary matrix,", "tokens": [51126, 400, 570, 321, 3736, 445, 3094, 309, 490, 257, 3997, 517, 4109, 8141, 11, 51290], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 198, "seek": 81224, "start": 830.98, "end": 836.32, "text": " we know for an absolute fact that this uniform probability matrix has a complex lift.", "tokens": [51302, 321, 458, 337, 364, 8236, 1186, 300, 341, 9452, 8482, 8141, 575, 257, 3997, 5533, 13, 51569], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1654880649440891, "compression_ratio": 1.6261261261261262, "no_speech_prob": 1.399327593902544e-12}, {"id": 199, "seek": 83632, "start": 836.32, "end": 838.1600000000001, "text": " It is un-stochastic.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 517, 12, 372, 8997, 2750, 13, 50457], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 200, "seek": 83632, "start": 838.7600000000001, "end": 840.5200000000001, "text": " Complex numbers can handle it just fine.", "tokens": [50487, 41184, 3547, 393, 4813, 309, 445, 2489, 13, 50575], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 201, "seek": 83632, "start": 840.7800000000001, "end": 846.46, "text": " But then Fliction asks, can we build that exact same uniform one-third matrix using only real numbers?", "tokens": [50588, 583, 550, 3235, 4105, 8962, 11, 393, 321, 1322, 300, 1900, 912, 9452, 472, 12, 25095, 8141, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 30, 50872], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 202, "seek": 83632, "start": 846.7, "end": 846.88, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50884, 1779, 13, 50893], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 203, "seek": 83632, "start": 847.0, "end": 848.7600000000001, "text": " Let's walk through what that would actually demand.", "tokens": [50899, 961, 311, 1792, 807, 437, 300, 576, 767, 4733, 13, 50987], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 204, "seek": 83632, "start": 848.94, "end": 851.2, "text": " To build it using a real orthogonal matrix,", "tokens": [50996, 1407, 1322, 309, 1228, 257, 957, 41488, 8141, 11, 51109], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 205, "seek": 83632, "start": 851.46, "end": 857.32, "text": " we need to find a 3x3 grid of real numbers where every single number, when squared, equals one-third.", "tokens": [51122, 321, 643, 281, 915, 257, 805, 87, 18, 10748, 295, 957, 3547, 689, 633, 2167, 1230, 11, 562, 8889, 11, 6915, 472, 12, 25095, 13, 51415], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 206, "seek": 83632, "start": 857.5600000000001, "end": 862.2600000000001, "text": " Which means every single entry has to be either positive or negative 1 over the square root of 3.", "tokens": [51427, 3013, 1355, 633, 2167, 8729, 575, 281, 312, 2139, 3353, 420, 3671, 502, 670, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 805, 13, 51662], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14367103576660156, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.6295939207419674e-12}, {"id": 207, "seek": 86226, "start": 862.26, "end": 862.66, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 50385], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 208, "seek": 86226, "start": 863.24, "end": 865.14, "text": " But it also has to be an orthogonal matrix.", "tokens": [50414, 583, 309, 611, 575, 281, 312, 364, 41488, 8141, 13, 50509], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 209, "seek": 86226, "start": 865.3, "end": 865.4, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50517, 865, 13, 50522], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 210, "seek": 86226, "start": 865.62, "end": 870.24, "text": " In linear algebra, orthogonal means that every row has to be perfectly perpendicular to every other row.", "tokens": [50533, 682, 8213, 21989, 11, 41488, 1355, 300, 633, 5386, 575, 281, 312, 6239, 26734, 281, 633, 661, 5386, 13, 50764], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 211, "seek": 86226, "start": 870.4399999999999, "end": 871.8199999999999, "text": " So they don't interfere with each other.", "tokens": [50774, 407, 436, 500, 380, 23946, 365, 1184, 661, 13, 50843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 212, "seek": 86226, "start": 871.88, "end": 872.04, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50846, 1779, 13, 50854], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 213, "seek": 86226, "start": 872.14, "end": 877.26, "text": " If you multiply the matching entries of any two rows together and add them up, they have to equal zero.", "tokens": [50859, 759, 291, 12972, 264, 14324, 23041, 295, 604, 732, 13241, 1214, 293, 909, 552, 493, 11, 436, 362, 281, 2681, 4018, 13, 51115], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 214, "seek": 86226, "start": 877.68, "end": 880.9399999999999, "text": " When you run the math on those specific constraints,", "tokens": [51136, 1133, 291, 1190, 264, 5221, 322, 729, 2685, 18491, 11, 51299], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 215, "seek": 86226, "start": 881.62, "end": 885.38, "text": " demanding both the one-third squaring and the perpendicular rows,", "tokens": [51333, 19960, 1293, 264, 472, 12, 25095, 2339, 1921, 293, 264, 26734, 13241, 11, 51521], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15143158238962157, "compression_ratio": 1.6564885496183206, "no_speech_prob": 1.443436299305989e-12}, {"id": 216, "seek": 88538, "start": 885.38, "end": 893.84, "text": " it turns out you are mathematically requiring the existence of what is called a 3x3 Hadamard matrix.", "tokens": [50365, 309, 4523, 484, 291, 366, 44003, 24165, 264, 9123, 295, 437, 307, 1219, 257, 805, 87, 18, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 13, 50788], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 217, "seek": 88538, "start": 893.98, "end": 895.3, "text": " I've actually seen that term before.", "tokens": [50795, 286, 600, 767, 1612, 300, 1433, 949, 13, 50861], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 218, "seek": 88538, "start": 895.6, "end": 897.4, "text": " Unpack a Hadamard matrix for me.", "tokens": [50876, 1156, 9539, 257, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 337, 385, 13, 50966], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 219, "seek": 88538, "start": 897.48, "end": 902.32, "text": " A Hadamard matrix is a square grid filled only with positive ones and negative ones,", "tokens": [50970, 316, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 307, 257, 3732, 10748, 6412, 787, 365, 3353, 2306, 293, 3671, 2306, 11, 51212], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 220, "seek": 88538, "start": 902.5, "end": 905.4, "text": " arranged in such a way that all the rows are mutually orthogonal.", "tokens": [51221, 18721, 294, 1270, 257, 636, 300, 439, 264, 13241, 366, 39144, 41488, 13, 51366], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 221, "seek": 88538, "start": 906.14, "end": 907.72, "text": " They're mathematically beautiful structures.", "tokens": [51403, 814, 434, 44003, 2238, 9227, 13, 51482], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 222, "seek": 88538, "start": 907.9399999999999, "end": 911.58, "text": " They're used heavily in error-correcting codes and classical signal processing.", "tokens": [51493, 814, 434, 1143, 10950, 294, 6713, 12, 19558, 2554, 278, 14211, 293, 13735, 6358, 9007, 13, 51675], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13167330540648295, "compression_ratio": 1.722007722007722, "no_speech_prob": 1.5308607246439232e-12}, {"id": 223, "seek": 91158, "start": 911.58, "end": 914.82, "text": " You can easily build a 2x2 Hadamard matrix.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 393, 3612, 1322, 257, 568, 87, 17, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 13, 50527], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 224, "seek": 91158, "start": 915.1800000000001, "end": 917.2, "text": " You can build a 4x4 and 8x8.", "tokens": [50545, 509, 393, 1322, 257, 1017, 87, 19, 293, 1649, 87, 23, 13, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 225, "seek": 91158, "start": 917.2800000000001, "end": 918.58, "text": " But we need a 3x3.", "tokens": [50650, 583, 321, 643, 257, 805, 87, 18, 13, 50715], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 226, "seek": 91158, "start": 918.84, "end": 921.14, "text": " And here is the brutal reality of mathematics.", "tokens": [50728, 400, 510, 307, 264, 17878, 4103, 295, 18666, 13, 50843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 227, "seek": 91158, "start": 921.88, "end": 924.8000000000001, "text": " A 3x3 Hadamard matrix does not exist.", "tokens": [50880, 316, 805, 87, 18, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 775, 406, 2514, 13, 51026], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 228, "seek": 91158, "start": 925.44, "end": 927.5400000000001, "text": " It is a mathematical impossibility.", "tokens": [51058, 467, 307, 257, 18894, 38802, 2841, 13, 51163], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 229, "seek": 91158, "start": 927.62, "end": 928.2, "text": " Wait, really?", "tokens": [51167, 3802, 11, 534, 30, 51196], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 230, "seek": 91158, "start": 928.5200000000001, "end": 928.74, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51212, 865, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 231, "seek": 91158, "start": 928.98, "end": 929.48, "text": " Think about it.", "tokens": [51235, 6557, 466, 309, 13, 51260], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 232, "seek": 91158, "start": 929.8000000000001, "end": 932.5600000000001, "text": " A 3x3 grid has nine squares.", "tokens": [51276, 316, 805, 87, 18, 10748, 575, 4949, 19368, 13, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 233, "seek": 91158, "start": 933.22, "end": 935.6800000000001, "text": " Each square can be a plus one or a minus one.", "tokens": [51447, 6947, 3732, 393, 312, 257, 1804, 472, 420, 257, 3175, 472, 13, 51570], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 234, "seek": 91158, "start": 935.96, "end": 939.94, "text": " That means there are 512 possible ways to fill that grid.", "tokens": [51584, 663, 1355, 456, 366, 1025, 4762, 1944, 2098, 281, 2836, 300, 10748, 13, 51783], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16301629743503251, "compression_ratio": 1.594142259414226, "no_speech_prob": 2.4177274765557755e-12}, {"id": 235, "seek": 93994, "start": 939.94, "end": 943.6600000000001, "text": " You can write all 512 of them down on a piece of paper.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 393, 2464, 439, 1025, 4762, 295, 552, 760, 322, 257, 2522, 295, 3035, 13, 50551], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 236, "seek": 93994, "start": 944.2, "end": 945.5600000000001, "text": " You can test every single one.", "tokens": [50578, 509, 393, 1500, 633, 2167, 472, 13, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 237, "seek": 93994, "start": 946.2, "end": 950.1800000000001, "text": " Not a single one of them will have rows that are all orthogonal to each other.", "tokens": [50678, 1726, 257, 2167, 472, 295, 552, 486, 362, 13241, 300, 366, 439, 41488, 281, 1184, 661, 13, 50877], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 238, "seek": 93994, "start": 950.4000000000001, "end": 952.0600000000001, "text": " They simply do not exist.", "tokens": [50888, 814, 2935, 360, 406, 2514, 13, 50971], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 239, "seek": 93994, "start": 952.1800000000001, "end": 952.6600000000001, "text": " Wait, wait.", "tokens": [50977, 3802, 11, 1699, 13, 51001], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 240, "seek": 93994, "start": 952.7600000000001, "end": 954.7, "text": " Let me just make sure I'm really getting the weight of this.", "tokens": [51006, 961, 385, 445, 652, 988, 286, 478, 534, 1242, 264, 3364, 295, 341, 13, 51103], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 241, "seek": 93994, "start": 955.12, "end": 957.86, "text": " A completely uniform probability transition,", "tokens": [51124, 316, 2584, 9452, 8482, 6034, 11, 51261], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 242, "seek": 93994, "start": 958.32, "end": 961.7, "text": " just a 33% chance of moving to any of the three states,", "tokens": [51284, 445, 257, 11816, 4, 2931, 295, 2684, 281, 604, 295, 264, 1045, 4368, 11, 51453], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 243, "seek": 93994, "start": 961.94, "end": 966.74, "text": " is one of the most basic, admissible, classical probabilities imaginable.", "tokens": [51465, 307, 472, 295, 264, 881, 3875, 11, 5910, 41073, 11, 13735, 33783, 23427, 712, 13, 51705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1310574402243404, "compression_ratio": 1.5963636363636364, "no_speech_prob": 1.714886804608795e-12}, {"id": 244, "seek": 96674, "start": 966.74, "end": 970.32, "text": " And you are saying that if the universe uses underlying amplitudes,", "tokens": [50365, 400, 291, 366, 1566, 300, 498, 264, 6445, 4960, 14217, 9731, 16451, 11, 50544], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21596211653489333, "compression_ratio": 1.556701030927835, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214281215760519e-12}, {"id": 245, "seek": 96674, "start": 970.5600000000001, "end": 976.0600000000001, "text": " it is literally impossible to use real number to describe that simple 33% transition.", "tokens": [50556, 309, 307, 3736, 6243, 281, 764, 957, 1230, 281, 6786, 300, 2199, 11816, 4, 6034, 13, 50831], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21596211653489333, "compression_ratio": 1.556701030927835, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214281215760519e-12}, {"id": 246, "seek": 96674, "start": 976.76, "end": 979.16, "text": " The real numbers simply lack the structural flexibility.", "tokens": [50866, 440, 957, 3547, 2935, 5011, 264, 15067, 12635, 13, 50986], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21596211653489333, "compression_ratio": 1.556701030927835, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214281215760519e-12}, {"id": 247, "seek": 96674, "start": 979.74, "end": 984.52, "text": " They are not expressive enough to capture the required geometry of a 3c uniform transition.", "tokens": [51015, 814, 366, 406, 40189, 1547, 281, 7983, 264, 4739, 18426, 295, 257, 805, 66, 9452, 6034, 13, 51254], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21596211653489333, "compression_ratio": 1.556701030927835, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214281215760519e-12}, {"id": 248, "seek": 98452, "start": 984.52, "end": 989.4, "text": " Man, it's like saying a two-way intersection works perfectly fine with regular traffic laws.", "tokens": [50365, 2458, 11, 309, 311, 411, 1566, 257, 732, 12, 676, 15236, 1985, 6239, 2489, 365, 3890, 6419, 6064, 13, 50609], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13272480781261736, "compression_ratio": 1.5932835820895523, "no_speech_prob": 2.0127976976119788e-12}, {"id": 249, "seek": 98452, "start": 989.9399999999999, "end": 993.12, "text": " You have stop signs, you have right-of-way, everything flows beautifully.", "tokens": [50636, 509, 362, 1590, 7880, 11, 291, 362, 558, 12, 2670, 12, 676, 11, 1203, 12867, 16525, 13, 50795], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13272480781261736, "compression_ratio": 1.5932835820895523, "no_speech_prob": 2.0127976976119788e-12}, {"id": 250, "seek": 98452, "start": 993.66, "end": 998.54, "text": " But the moment you try to build a three-way roundabout, the laws of physics break down.", "tokens": [50822, 583, 264, 1623, 291, 853, 281, 1322, 257, 1045, 12, 676, 3098, 21970, 11, 264, 6064, 295, 10649, 1821, 760, 13, 51066], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13272480781261736, "compression_ratio": 1.5932835820895523, "no_speech_prob": 2.0127976976119788e-12}, {"id": 251, "seek": 98452, "start": 998.88, "end": 1002.0799999999999, "text": " The cars suddenly run out of physical space to yield to each other,", "tokens": [51083, 440, 5163, 5800, 1190, 484, 295, 4001, 1901, 281, 11257, 281, 1184, 661, 11, 51243], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13272480781261736, "compression_ratio": 1.5932835820895523, "no_speech_prob": 2.0127976976119788e-12}, {"id": 252, "seek": 98452, "start": 1002.22, "end": 1007.56, "text": " and the only way to prevent a crash is to force the cars to travel through a higher invisible dimension.", "tokens": [51250, 293, 264, 787, 636, 281, 4871, 257, 8252, 307, 281, 3464, 264, 5163, 281, 3147, 807, 257, 2946, 14603, 10139, 13, 51517], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13272480781261736, "compression_ratio": 1.5932835820895523, "no_speech_prob": 2.0127976976119788e-12}, {"id": 253, "seek": 100756, "start": 1007.56, "end": 1011.56, "text": " It sounds completely absurd, but you are saying the math is ironclad.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 3263, 2584, 19774, 11, 457, 291, 366, 1566, 264, 5221, 307, 6497, 3474, 345, 13, 50565], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 254, "seek": 100756, "start": 1012.5799999999999, "end": 1014.9399999999999, "text": " The roundabout is a fantastic analogy, actually.", "tokens": [50616, 440, 3098, 21970, 307, 257, 5456, 21663, 11, 767, 13, 50734], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 255, "seek": 100756, "start": 1015.18, "end": 1017.6199999999999, "text": " The real numbers run out of topological room to maneuver.", "tokens": [50746, 440, 957, 3547, 1190, 484, 295, 1192, 4383, 1808, 281, 25976, 13, 50868], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 256, "seek": 100756, "start": 1018.26, "end": 1022.4799999999999, "text": " They cannot bend enough to accommodate three perpendicular directions simultaneously", "tokens": [50900, 814, 2644, 11229, 1547, 281, 21410, 1045, 26734, 11095, 16561, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 257, "seek": 100756, "start": 1022.4799999999999, "end": 1024.9199999999998, "text": " while maintaining that uniform distribution.", "tokens": [51111, 1339, 14916, 300, 9452, 7316, 13, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 258, "seek": 100756, "start": 1025.62, "end": 1028.4199999999998, "text": " So this rigorously establishes that at n equals 3,", "tokens": [51268, 407, 341, 42191, 5098, 8327, 279, 300, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 11, 51408], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 259, "seek": 100756, "start": 1028.8999999999999, "end": 1033.8799999999999, "text": " complex numbers are structurally necessary to realize every admissible classical probability matrix.", "tokens": [51432, 3997, 3547, 366, 6594, 6512, 4818, 281, 4325, 633, 5910, 41073, 13735, 8482, 8141, 13, 51681], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 260, "seek": 100756, "start": 1034.2, "end": 1036.06, "text": " But the manuscript doesn't stop there.", "tokens": [51697, 583, 264, 23928, 1177, 380, 1590, 456, 13, 51790], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15655435579959479, "compression_ratio": 1.6084142394822007, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798488819246392e-12}, {"id": 261, "seek": 103606, "start": 1036.06, "end": 1039.1, "text": " Because a stubborn physicist might look at that and say,", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 257, 24137, 42466, 1062, 574, 412, 300, 293, 584, 11, 50517], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 262, "seek": 103606, "start": 1039.24, "end": 1042.24, "text": " well, fine, the 3x3 roundabout is broken.", "tokens": [50524, 731, 11, 2489, 11, 264, 805, 87, 18, 3098, 21970, 307, 5463, 13, 50674], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 263, "seek": 103606, "start": 1042.6, "end": 1045.36, "text": " I will just refuse to study three-state systems.", "tokens": [50692, 286, 486, 445, 16791, 281, 2979, 1045, 12, 15406, 3652, 13, 50830], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 264, "seek": 103606, "start": 1045.74, "end": 1049.94, "text": " I will strictly stay in the n equals 2 world, the two-way intersection,", "tokens": [50849, 286, 486, 20792, 1754, 294, 264, 297, 6915, 568, 1002, 11, 264, 732, 12, 676, 15236, 11, 51059], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 265, "seek": 103606, "start": 1050.26, "end": 1054.4199999999998, "text": " where you already prove that real numbers and complex numbers agree perfectly.", "tokens": [51075, 689, 291, 1217, 7081, 300, 957, 3547, 293, 3997, 3547, 3986, 6239, 13, 51283], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 266, "seek": 103606, "start": 1054.72, "end": 1058.36, "text": " And that takes us directly into what the manuscript calls the gluing problem.", "tokens": [51298, 400, 300, 2516, 505, 3838, 666, 437, 264, 23928, 5498, 264, 1563, 9635, 1154, 13, 51480], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 267, "seek": 103606, "start": 1059.08, "end": 1063.12, "text": " Because Flixian mathematically corners the stubborn physicist here.", "tokens": [51516, 1436, 3235, 970, 952, 44003, 12413, 264, 24137, 42466, 510, 13, 51718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17095594316999488, "compression_ratio": 1.6323529411764706, "no_speech_prob": 2.0204272282997593e-12}, {"id": 268, "seek": 106312, "start": 1063.12, "end": 1068.4199999999998, "text": " The book demonstrates that even if you stick to the safe, happy world of two outcome systems,", "tokens": [50365, 440, 1446, 31034, 300, 754, 498, 291, 2897, 281, 264, 3273, 11, 2055, 1002, 295, 732, 9700, 3652, 11, 50630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15422142876519096, "compression_ratio": 1.5778894472361809, "no_speech_prob": 1.525272746646933e-12}, {"id": 269, "seek": 106312, "start": 1068.78, "end": 1073.1399999999999, "text": " if you chain enough of those safe transitions together, real numbers still break down.", "tokens": [50648, 498, 291, 5021, 1547, 295, 729, 3273, 23767, 1214, 11, 957, 3547, 920, 1821, 760, 13, 50866], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15422142876519096, "compression_ratio": 1.5778894472361809, "no_speech_prob": 1.525272746646933e-12}, {"id": 270, "seek": 106312, "start": 1073.3, "end": 1075.6999999999998, "text": " This is the concept of the composition obstruction.", "tokens": [50874, 639, 307, 264, 3410, 295, 264, 12686, 49711, 13, 50994], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15422142876519096, "compression_ratio": 1.5778894472361809, "no_speech_prob": 1.525272746646933e-12}, {"id": 271, "seek": 106312, "start": 1076.1399999999999, "end": 1076.9399999999998, "text": " Walk me through this.", "tokens": [51016, 10818, 385, 807, 341, 13, 51056], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15422142876519096, "compression_ratio": 1.5778894472361809, "no_speech_prob": 1.525272746646933e-12}, {"id": 272, "seek": 106312, "start": 1077.36, "end": 1080.1, "text": " How does chaining things together destroy the real numbers?", "tokens": [51077, 1012, 775, 417, 3686, 721, 1214, 5293, 264, 957, 3547, 30, 51214], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15422142876519096, "compression_ratio": 1.5778894472361809, "no_speech_prob": 1.525272746646933e-12}, {"id": 273, "seek": 108010, "start": 1080.1, "end": 1085.04, "text": " Let's imagine you have three different measurement contexts for a single two-state system.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 3811, 291, 362, 1045, 819, 13160, 30628, 337, 257, 2167, 732, 12, 15406, 1185, 13, 50612], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11411467222409828, "compression_ratio": 1.8739495798319328, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954035188960435e-12}, {"id": 274, "seek": 108010, "start": 1085.6, "end": 1089.9399999999998, "text": " Think of them as, say, three different angles you can orient a polarizing filter to measure a single photon.", "tokens": [50640, 6557, 295, 552, 382, 11, 584, 11, 1045, 819, 14708, 291, 393, 8579, 257, 12367, 3319, 6608, 281, 3481, 257, 2167, 37443, 13, 50857], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11411467222409828, "compression_ratio": 1.8739495798319328, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954035188960435e-12}, {"id": 275, "seek": 108010, "start": 1090.54, "end": 1093.4399999999998, "text": " We'll call them context Z, context X, and context Y.", "tokens": [50887, 492, 603, 818, 552, 4319, 1176, 11, 4319, 1783, 11, 293, 4319, 398, 13, 51032], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11411467222409828, "compression_ratio": 1.8739495798319328, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954035188960435e-12}, {"id": 276, "seek": 108010, "start": 1093.62, "end": 1100.2199999999998, "text": " Okay, so I can set up my lab to measure the transition probability of a photon going from context Z to context X.", "tokens": [51041, 1033, 11, 370, 286, 393, 992, 493, 452, 2715, 281, 3481, 264, 6034, 8482, 295, 257, 37443, 516, 490, 4319, 1176, 281, 4319, 1783, 13, 51371], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11411467222409828, "compression_ratio": 1.8739495798319328, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954035188960435e-12}, {"id": 277, "seek": 108010, "start": 1100.36, "end": 1103.9199999999998, "text": " And then I can measure a separate transition going from context X to context Y.", "tokens": [51378, 400, 550, 286, 393, 3481, 257, 4994, 6034, 516, 490, 4319, 1783, 281, 4319, 398, 13, 51556], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11411467222409828, "compression_ratio": 1.8739495798319328, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954035188960435e-12}, {"id": 278, "seek": 110392, "start": 1103.92, "end": 1109.24, "text": " Right, and you can find a perfectly valid real-numbered amplitude matrix for the Z to X transition", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 11, 293, 291, 393, 915, 257, 6239, 7363, 957, 12, 41261, 292, 27433, 8141, 337, 264, 1176, 281, 1783, 6034, 50631], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 279, "seek": 110392, "start": 1109.24, "end": 1113.5600000000002, "text": " and a perfectly valid real lift for the X to Y transition.", "tokens": [50631, 293, 257, 6239, 7363, 957, 5533, 337, 264, 1783, 281, 398, 6034, 13, 50847], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 280, "seek": 110392, "start": 1114.2, "end": 1116.5800000000002, "text": " Because we are strictly looking at N equals 2,", "tokens": [50879, 1436, 321, 366, 20792, 1237, 412, 426, 6915, 568, 11, 50998], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 281, "seek": 110392, "start": 1116.8400000000001, "end": 1121.26, "text": " we know from the degeneracy theorem that those real lifts absolutely exist.", "tokens": [51011, 321, 458, 490, 264, 40520, 2551, 20904, 300, 729, 957, 30501, 3122, 2514, 13, 51232], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 282, "seek": 110392, "start": 1121.46, "end": 1123.88, "text": " So where does the obstruction actually happen?", "tokens": [51242, 407, 689, 775, 264, 49711, 767, 1051, 30, 51363], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 283, "seek": 110392, "start": 1123.94, "end": 1127.8400000000001, "text": " The obstruction happens when you try to calculate the direct transition from Z to Y", "tokens": [51366, 440, 49711, 2314, 562, 291, 853, 281, 8873, 264, 2047, 6034, 490, 1176, 281, 398, 51561], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 284, "seek": 110392, "start": 1127.8400000000001, "end": 1130.0, "text": " by chaining the intermediate steps together.", "tokens": [51561, 538, 417, 3686, 264, 19376, 4439, 1214, 13, 51669], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13596527462913877, "compression_ratio": 1.7272727272727273, "no_speech_prob": 1.5732966145154403e-12}, {"id": 285, "seek": 113000, "start": 1130.0, "end": 1133.02, "text": " If you compose the physical probabilities directly,", "tokens": [50365, 759, 291, 35925, 264, 4001, 33783, 3838, 11, 50516], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 286, "seek": 113000, "start": 1133.44, "end": 1136.2, "text": " just multiplying the statistics like you would in a classical casino,", "tokens": [50537, 445, 30955, 264, 12523, 411, 291, 576, 294, 257, 13735, 36278, 11, 50675], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 287, "seek": 113000, "start": 1136.5, "end": 1137.5, "text": " you get the wrong answer.", "tokens": [50690, 291, 483, 264, 2085, 1867, 13, 50740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 288, "seek": 113000, "start": 1137.78, "end": 1139.96, "text": " You lose crucial physical information.", "tokens": [50754, 509, 3624, 11462, 4001, 1589, 13, 50863], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 289, "seek": 113000, "start": 1140.36, "end": 1141.18, "text": " What kind of information?", "tokens": [50883, 708, 733, 295, 1589, 30, 50924], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 290, "seek": 113000, "start": 1141.38, "end": 1144.34, "text": " Specifically, you lose the quantum interference across terms.", "tokens": [50934, 26058, 11, 291, 3624, 264, 13018, 24497, 2108, 2115, 13, 51082], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 291, "seek": 113000, "start": 1144.92, "end": 1150.24, "text": " The act of projecting down into pure probability destroys the exact composition law.", "tokens": [51111, 440, 605, 295, 43001, 760, 666, 6075, 8482, 36714, 264, 1900, 12686, 2101, 13, 51377], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 292, "seek": 113000, "start": 1150.38, "end": 1154.68, "text": " So, to get the right physical answer, we can't multiply the probabilities.", "tokens": [51384, 407, 11, 281, 483, 264, 558, 4001, 1867, 11, 321, 393, 380, 12972, 264, 33783, 13, 51599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 293, "seek": 113000, "start": 1155.28, "end": 1158.38, "text": " We have to multiply the underlying amplitude matrices first,", "tokens": [51629, 492, 362, 281, 12972, 264, 14217, 27433, 32284, 700, 11, 51784], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16877969403133214, "compression_ratio": 1.7615658362989324, "no_speech_prob": 1.8468414731548366e-12}, {"id": 294, "seek": 115838, "start": 1158.38, "end": 1161.5200000000002, "text": " let the positive and negative numbers interfere with each other,", "tokens": [50365, 718, 264, 3353, 293, 3671, 3547, 23946, 365, 1184, 661, 11, 50522], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 295, "seek": 115838, "start": 1161.7, "end": 1165.0, "text": " and then square the final result to get the new probability.", "tokens": [50531, 293, 550, 3732, 264, 2572, 1874, 281, 483, 264, 777, 8482, 13, 50696], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 296, "seek": 115838, "start": 1165.3000000000002, "end": 1167.18, "text": " You've hit on the core tension there perfectly.", "tokens": [50711, 509, 600, 2045, 322, 264, 4965, 8980, 456, 6239, 13, 50805], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 297, "seek": 115838, "start": 1167.46, "end": 1168.74, "text": " We have to compose the lifts.", "tokens": [50819, 492, 362, 281, 35925, 264, 30501, 13, 50883], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 298, "seek": 115838, "start": 1169.3200000000002, "end": 1171.5800000000002, "text": " And this leads to the concept of context graphs.", "tokens": [50912, 400, 341, 6689, 281, 264, 3410, 295, 4319, 24877, 13, 51025], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 299, "seek": 115838, "start": 1172.18, "end": 1174.2800000000002, "text": " Imagine drawing this out on a whiteboard.", "tokens": [51055, 11739, 6316, 341, 484, 322, 257, 2418, 3787, 13, 51160], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 300, "seek": 115838, "start": 1174.68, "end": 1175.48, "text": " You draw a triangle.", "tokens": [51180, 509, 2642, 257, 13369, 13, 51220], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 301, "seek": 115838, "start": 1176.0, "end": 1181.0400000000002, "text": " The three corners of the triangle are our three measurement contexts, Z, X, and Y.", "tokens": [51246, 440, 1045, 12413, 295, 264, 13369, 366, 527, 1045, 13160, 30628, 11, 1176, 11, 1783, 11, 293, 398, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 302, "seek": 115838, "start": 1181.1000000000001, "end": 1181.4, "text": " Got it.", "tokens": [51501, 5803, 309, 13, 51516], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 303, "seek": 115838, "start": 1181.5, "end": 1183.9, "text": " The three lines connecting the corners, the edges,", "tokens": [51521, 440, 1045, 3876, 11015, 264, 12413, 11, 264, 8819, 11, 51641], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 304, "seek": 115838, "start": 1184.16, "end": 1187.5800000000002, "text": " are the transition probabilities between those contexts.", "tokens": [51654, 366, 264, 6034, 33783, 1296, 729, 30628, 13, 51825], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17471648752689362, "compression_ratio": 1.7909407665505226, "no_speech_prob": 1.567405927271892e-12}, {"id": 305, "seek": 118758, "start": 1187.58, "end": 1190.1999999999998, "text": " And this is where the source draws a massive distinction", "tokens": [50365, 400, 341, 307, 689, 264, 4009, 20045, 257, 5994, 16844, 50496], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 306, "seek": 118758, "start": 1190.1999999999998, "end": 1192.9199999999998, "text": " between an edgewise truth and a global truth.", "tokens": [50496, 1296, 364, 4691, 3711, 3494, 293, 257, 4338, 3494, 13, 50632], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 307, "seek": 118758, "start": 1193.4199999999998, "end": 1194.5, "text": " Let's really dig into this,", "tokens": [50657, 961, 311, 534, 2528, 666, 341, 11, 50711], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 308, "seek": 118758, "start": 1194.54, "end": 1197.8799999999999, "text": " because it feels like the philosophical heart of the whole first half of the book.", "tokens": [50713, 570, 309, 3417, 411, 264, 25066, 1917, 295, 264, 1379, 700, 1922, 295, 264, 1446, 13, 50880], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 309, "seek": 118758, "start": 1198.4399999999998, "end": 1199.34, "text": " It absolutely is.", "tokens": [50908, 467, 3122, 307, 13, 50953], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 310, "seek": 118758, "start": 1199.52, "end": 1201.84, "text": " Let's say you have a graph of measurement contexts,", "tokens": [50962, 961, 311, 584, 291, 362, 257, 4295, 295, 13160, 30628, 11, 51078], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 311, "seek": 118758, "start": 1202.1799999999998, "end": 1204.3799999999999, "text": " but instead of triangle, it's shaped like a tree.", "tokens": [51095, 457, 2602, 295, 13369, 11, 309, 311, 13475, 411, 257, 4230, 13, 51205], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 312, "seek": 118758, "start": 1204.98, "end": 1208.3, "text": " It has branches, but no closed loops, no cycles.", "tokens": [51235, 467, 575, 14770, 11, 457, 572, 5395, 16121, 11, 572, 17796, 13, 51401], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 313, "seek": 118758, "start": 1208.3799999999999, "end": 1209.22, "text": " Okay, a tree structure.", "tokens": [51405, 1033, 11, 257, 4230, 3877, 13, 51447], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 314, "seek": 118758, "start": 1209.5, "end": 1211.6, "text": " The math proves that on a tree graph,", "tokens": [51461, 440, 5221, 25019, 300, 322, 257, 4230, 4295, 11, 51566], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 315, "seek": 118758, "start": 1211.9399999999998, "end": 1216.72, "text": " you can always find a consistent set of real numbered amplitudes for all your measurements.", "tokens": [51583, 291, 393, 1009, 915, 257, 8398, 992, 295, 957, 40936, 9731, 16451, 337, 439, 428, 15383, 13, 51822], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14125589011372, "compression_ratio": 1.6802507836990597, "no_speech_prob": 1.6557123510771432e-12}, {"id": 316, "seek": 121672, "start": 1216.72, "end": 1220.26, "text": " You just start at the trunk, assign it a real frame,", "tokens": [50365, 509, 445, 722, 412, 264, 19849, 11, 6269, 309, 257, 957, 3920, 11, 50542], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 317, "seek": 121672, "start": 1220.68, "end": 1223.64, "text": " and propagate your real numbered lists outwards down the branches.", "tokens": [50563, 293, 48256, 428, 957, 40936, 14511, 484, 2015, 760, 264, 14770, 13, 50711], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 318, "seek": 121672, "start": 1224.28, "end": 1226.7, "text": " Because the branches never loop back and cross each other,", "tokens": [50743, 1436, 264, 14770, 1128, 6367, 646, 293, 3278, 1184, 661, 11, 50864], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 319, "seek": 121672, "start": 1226.94, "end": 1228.32, "text": " you never run into a contradiction.", "tokens": [50876, 291, 1128, 1190, 666, 257, 34937, 13, 50945], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 320, "seek": 121672, "start": 1229.52, "end": 1233.72, "text": " Edgewise, real admissibility guarantees global real admissibility.", "tokens": [51005, 19328, 3711, 11, 957, 5910, 891, 2841, 32567, 4338, 957, 5910, 891, 2841, 13, 51215], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 321, "seek": 121672, "start": 1234.06, "end": 1235.88, "text": " But a whiteboard drawing isn't a tree.", "tokens": [51232, 583, 257, 2418, 3787, 6316, 1943, 380, 257, 4230, 13, 51323], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 322, "seek": 121672, "start": 1236.28, "end": 1237.1000000000001, "text": " It's a triangle.", "tokens": [51343, 467, 311, 257, 13369, 13, 51384], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 323, "seek": 121672, "start": 1237.3600000000001, "end": 1238.4, "text": " It's a closed loop.", "tokens": [51397, 467, 311, 257, 5395, 6367, 13, 51449], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 324, "seek": 121672, "start": 1238.68, "end": 1241.72, "text": " It has what the source calls a cycle rank of one.", "tokens": [51463, 467, 575, 437, 264, 4009, 5498, 257, 6586, 6181, 295, 472, 13, 51615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 325, "seek": 121672, "start": 1241.8, "end": 1243.82, "text": " And that one single loop ruins everything.", "tokens": [51619, 400, 300, 472, 2167, 6367, 24747, 1203, 13, 51720], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1620295206705729, "compression_ratio": 1.6853932584269662, "no_speech_prob": 1.707782678293801e-12}, {"id": 326, "seek": 124382, "start": 1243.82, "end": 1247.36, "text": " The triangle gluing theorem in the manuscript proves that when you have a cycle,", "tokens": [50365, 440, 13369, 1563, 9635, 20904, 294, 264, 23928, 25019, 300, 562, 291, 362, 257, 6586, 11, 50542], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 327, "seek": 124382, "start": 1247.78, "end": 1251.46, "text": " edgewise real admissibility does not imply global real admissibility.", "tokens": [50563, 4691, 3711, 957, 5910, 891, 2841, 775, 406, 33616, 4338, 957, 5910, 891, 2841, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 328, "seek": 124382, "start": 1251.7, "end": 1254.1799999999998, "text": " Meaning, I can look at any single line on my triangle,", "tokens": [50759, 19948, 11, 286, 393, 574, 412, 604, 2167, 1622, 322, 452, 13369, 11, 50883], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 329, "seek": 124382, "start": 1254.28, "end": 1256.02, "text": " and it works perfectly with real numbers.", "tokens": [50888, 293, 309, 1985, 6239, 365, 957, 3547, 13, 50975], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 330, "seek": 124382, "start": 1256.26, "end": 1258.3799999999999, "text": " But I can't make the whole triangle work at once.", "tokens": [50987, 583, 286, 393, 380, 652, 264, 1379, 13369, 589, 412, 1564, 13, 51093], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 331, "seek": 124382, "start": 1258.6399999999999, "end": 1259.0, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51106, 7587, 13, 51124], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 332, "seek": 124382, "start": 1259.34, "end": 1261.7, "text": " You can have transition probabilities on all three edges", "tokens": [51141, 509, 393, 362, 6034, 33783, 322, 439, 1045, 8819, 51259], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 333, "seek": 124382, "start": 1261.7, "end": 1265.76, "text": " that look perfectly fine and have valid real numbered lifts individually.", "tokens": [51259, 300, 574, 6239, 2489, 293, 362, 7363, 957, 40936, 30501, 16652, 13, 51462], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 334, "seek": 124382, "start": 1266.24, "end": 1269.8, "text": " But when you try to assign a single consistent global reality,", "tokens": [51486, 583, 562, 291, 853, 281, 6269, 257, 2167, 8398, 4338, 4103, 11, 51664], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13790823030872507, "compression_ratio": 1.7482517482517483, "no_speech_prob": 1.6686333304677392e-12}, {"id": 335, "seek": 126980, "start": 1269.8, "end": 1274.04, "text": " like a single real numbered framework to Z, X, and Y simultaneously,", "tokens": [50365, 411, 257, 2167, 957, 40936, 8388, 281, 1176, 11, 1783, 11, 293, 398, 16561, 11, 50577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 336, "seek": 126980, "start": 1274.3799999999999, "end": 1278.9199999999998, "text": " that successfully reproduces all three transition edges without contradiction,", "tokens": [50594, 300, 10727, 11408, 887, 439, 1045, 6034, 8819, 1553, 34937, 11, 50821], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 337, "seek": 126980, "start": 1279.72, "end": 1281.26, "text": " it is mathematically impossible.", "tokens": [50861, 309, 307, 44003, 6243, 13, 50938], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 338, "seek": 126980, "start": 1281.5, "end": 1284.22, "text": " The specific example Slick-Sugent uses to prove this", "tokens": [50950, 440, 2685, 1365, 6187, 618, 12, 50, 697, 317, 4960, 281, 7081, 341, 51086], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 339, "seek": 126980, "start": 1284.22, "end": 1287.02, "text": " is the real mutually unbiased basis obstruction,", "tokens": [51086, 307, 264, 957, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 5143, 49711, 11, 51226], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 340, "seek": 126980, "start": 1287.54, "end": 1288.8999999999999, "text": " the MUB obstruction.", "tokens": [51252, 264, 17935, 33, 49711, 13, 51320], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 341, "seek": 126980, "start": 1289.84, "end": 1290.84, "text": " Let's unpack that jargon.", "tokens": [51367, 961, 311, 26699, 300, 15181, 10660, 13, 51417], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 342, "seek": 126980, "start": 1291.22, "end": 1294.72, "text": " What does mutually unbiased actually mean operationally in a lab?", "tokens": [51436, 708, 775, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 767, 914, 6916, 379, 294, 257, 2715, 30, 51611], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 343, "seek": 126980, "start": 1294.86, "end": 1297.3, "text": " Mutually unbiased basically means the measurement contexts", "tokens": [51618, 18517, 671, 517, 5614, 1937, 1936, 1355, 264, 13160, 30628, 51740], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19318320534446023, "compression_ratio": 1.6691176470588236, "no_speech_prob": 1.4897994947069004e-12}, {"id": 344, "seek": 129730, "start": 1297.3, "end": 1299.76, "text": " are as fundamentally different as they can possibly be.", "tokens": [50365, 366, 382, 17879, 819, 382, 436, 393, 6264, 312, 13, 50488], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 345, "seek": 129730, "start": 1300.08, "end": 1301.98, "text": " It is maximum incompatibility.", "tokens": [50504, 467, 307, 6674, 40393, 267, 2841, 13, 50599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 346, "seek": 129730, "start": 1302.3799999999999, "end": 1305.76, "text": " If you are 100% certain of a particle's state in context Z,", "tokens": [50619, 759, 291, 366, 2319, 4, 1629, 295, 257, 12359, 311, 1785, 294, 4319, 1176, 11, 50788], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 347, "seek": 129730, "start": 1306.0, "end": 1309.1, "text": " you are maximally uncertain about its state in context X.", "tokens": [50800, 291, 366, 5138, 379, 11308, 466, 1080, 1785, 294, 4319, 1783, 13, 50955], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 348, "seek": 129730, "start": 1309.28, "end": 1312.22, "text": " Oh, like it acts exactly like a 50-50 coin flip.", "tokens": [50964, 876, 11, 411, 309, 10672, 2293, 411, 257, 2625, 12, 2803, 11464, 7929, 13, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 349, "seek": 129730, "start": 1312.3999999999999, "end": 1312.6, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51120, 1779, 13, 51130], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 350, "seek": 129730, "start": 1312.68, "end": 1315.06, "text": " This is the essence of Heisenberg's uncertainty principle.", "tokens": [51134, 639, 307, 264, 12801, 295, 634, 11106, 6873, 311, 15697, 8665, 13, 51253], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 351, "seek": 129730, "start": 1315.26, "end": 1316.6, "text": " If you know the position perfectly,", "tokens": [51263, 759, 291, 458, 264, 2535, 6239, 11, 51330], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 352, "seek": 129730, "start": 1316.9199999999998, "end": 1319.04, "text": " the momentum is completely mutually unbiased.", "tokens": [51346, 264, 11244, 307, 2584, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 13, 51452], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 353, "seek": 129730, "start": 1319.12, "end": 1319.9199999999998, "text": " It's totally fuzzy.", "tokens": [51456, 467, 311, 3879, 34710, 13, 51496], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13823424629543138, "compression_ratio": 1.6068702290076335, "no_speech_prob": 1.5009390215078855e-12}, {"id": 354, "seek": 131992, "start": 1319.92, "end": 1323.22, "text": " So, we set up our triangle so that all three edges,", "tokens": [50365, 407, 11, 321, 992, 493, 527, 13369, 370, 300, 439, 1045, 8819, 11, 50530], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 355, "seek": 131992, "start": 1323.44, "end": 1327.1200000000001, "text": " Z to X, X to Y, and Y to Z, are mutually unbiased.", "tokens": [50541, 1176, 281, 1783, 11, 1783, 281, 398, 11, 293, 398, 281, 1176, 11, 366, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 13, 50725], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 356, "seek": 131992, "start": 1327.5800000000002, "end": 1330.0800000000002, "text": " Every transition is a 50-50 coin flip.", "tokens": [50748, 2048, 6034, 307, 257, 2625, 12, 2803, 11464, 7929, 13, 50873], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 357, "seek": 131992, "start": 1330.5800000000002, "end": 1334.22, "text": " And if you force that specific, highly symmetric scenario,", "tokens": [50898, 400, 498, 291, 3464, 300, 2685, 11, 5405, 32330, 9005, 11, 51080], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 358, "seek": 131992, "start": 1334.92, "end": 1337.5600000000002, "text": " the real number math creates a legal contradiction.", "tokens": [51115, 264, 957, 1230, 5221, 7829, 257, 5089, 34937, 13, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 359, "seek": 131992, "start": 1338.3200000000002, "end": 1341.1000000000001, "text": " To make the real amplitudes balance around the loop,", "tokens": [51285, 1407, 652, 264, 957, 9731, 16451, 4772, 926, 264, 6367, 11, 51424], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 360, "seek": 131992, "start": 1341.48, "end": 1345.2, "text": " the geometry demands that a certain angle equation must hold true.", "tokens": [51443, 264, 18426, 15107, 300, 257, 1629, 5802, 5367, 1633, 1797, 2074, 13, 51629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15302204849696396, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 361, "seek": 134520, "start": 1345.2, "end": 1350.74, "text": " It basically demands that an angle of 45 degrees must be geometrically equivalent", "tokens": [50365, 467, 1936, 15107, 300, 364, 5802, 295, 6905, 5310, 1633, 312, 12956, 81, 984, 10344, 50642], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 362, "seek": 134520, "start": 1350.74, "end": 1352.9, "text": " to either 0 degrees or 90 degrees.", "tokens": [50642, 281, 2139, 1958, 5310, 420, 4289, 5310, 13, 50750], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 363, "seek": 134520, "start": 1353.02, "end": 1354.98, "text": " Which is obviously a catastrophic contradiction.", "tokens": [50756, 3013, 307, 2745, 257, 34915, 34937, 13, 50854], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 364, "seek": 134520, "start": 1355.14, "end": 1356.64, "text": " 45 does not equal 90.", "tokens": [50862, 6905, 775, 406, 2681, 4289, 13, 50937], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 365, "seek": 134520, "start": 1356.82, "end": 1358.48, "text": " It is a total structural collapse.", "tokens": [50946, 467, 307, 257, 3217, 15067, 15584, 13, 51029], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 366, "seek": 134520, "start": 1358.64, "end": 1362.32, "text": " I actually really liked an analogy you hinted at earlier when we were reviewing the notes.", "tokens": [51037, 286, 767, 534, 4501, 364, 21663, 291, 12075, 292, 412, 3071, 562, 321, 645, 19576, 264, 5570, 13, 51221], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 367, "seek": 134520, "start": 1362.78, "end": 1363.9, "text": " The Escher staircase.", "tokens": [51244, 440, 2313, 6759, 35359, 13, 51300], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 368, "seek": 134520, "start": 1364.16, "end": 1364.46, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51313, 1079, 13, 51328], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 369, "seek": 134520, "start": 1365.24, "end": 1368.64, "text": " Visualizing this as an MC Escher drawing really makes it click for me.", "tokens": [51367, 23187, 3319, 341, 382, 364, 8797, 2313, 6759, 6316, 534, 1669, 309, 2052, 337, 385, 13, 51537], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 370, "seek": 134520, "start": 1368.76, "end": 1371.4, "text": " If you look at any single corner of an Escher staircase,", "tokens": [51543, 759, 291, 574, 412, 604, 2167, 4538, 295, 364, 2313, 6759, 35359, 11, 51675], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14951207457470292, "compression_ratio": 1.6537102473498233, "no_speech_prob": 2.0764392809347054e-12}, {"id": 371, "seek": 137140, "start": 1371.4, "end": 1374.7800000000002, "text": " which represents one edge of our context graph, one transition,", "tokens": [50365, 597, 8855, 472, 4691, 295, 527, 4319, 4295, 11, 472, 6034, 11, 50534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 372, "seek": 137140, "start": 1374.96, "end": 1376.0600000000002, "text": " it looks completely normal.", "tokens": [50543, 309, 1542, 2584, 2710, 13, 50598], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 373, "seek": 137140, "start": 1376.26, "end": 1376.98, "text": " It obeys gravity.", "tokens": [50608, 467, 36346, 749, 12110, 13, 50644], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 374, "seek": 137140, "start": 1377.1200000000001, "end": 1378.2, "text": " It obeys the laws of perspective.", "tokens": [50651, 467, 36346, 749, 264, 6064, 295, 4585, 13, 50705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 375, "seek": 137140, "start": 1378.6000000000001, "end": 1382.44, "text": " You could build that single corner out of real wood in a real carpenter shop.", "tokens": [50725, 509, 727, 1322, 300, 2167, 4538, 484, 295, 957, 4576, 294, 257, 957, 26103, 14278, 3945, 13, 50917], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 376, "seek": 137140, "start": 1382.6000000000001, "end": 1383.9, "text": " It is edgewise admissible.", "tokens": [50925, 467, 307, 4691, 3711, 5910, 41073, 13, 50990], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 377, "seek": 137140, "start": 1384.1000000000001, "end": 1385.68, "text": " The local geometry is flawless.", "tokens": [51000, 440, 2654, 18426, 307, 45693, 13, 51079], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 378, "seek": 137140, "start": 1386.18, "end": 1390.7, "text": " But when you zoom out and your eye follows the stairs around the entire loop,", "tokens": [51104, 583, 562, 291, 8863, 484, 293, 428, 3313, 10002, 264, 13471, 926, 264, 2302, 6367, 11, 51330], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 379, "seek": 137140, "start": 1391.1200000000001, "end": 1392.8200000000002, "text": " the geometry contradicts itself.", "tokens": [51351, 264, 18426, 28900, 82, 2564, 13, 51436], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 380, "seek": 137140, "start": 1393.16, "end": 1396.8200000000002, "text": " You are perpetually walking upwards, taking positive steps,", "tokens": [51453, 509, 366, 16211, 671, 4494, 22167, 11, 1940, 3353, 4439, 11, 51636], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 381, "seek": 137140, "start": 1397.0, "end": 1400.44, "text": " but you somehow end up on the exact same floor where you started.", "tokens": [51645, 457, 291, 6063, 917, 493, 322, 264, 1900, 912, 4123, 689, 291, 1409, 13, 51817], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 382, "seek": 137140, "start": 1400.44, "end": 1400.96, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51817, 1079, 13, 51843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17486645733868633, "compression_ratio": 1.6624203821656052, "no_speech_prob": 1.98879974988575e-12}, {"id": 383, "seek": 140096, "start": 1400.96, "end": 1404.14, "text": " The real numbers fail to glue together to close the loop.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 957, 3547, 3061, 281, 8998, 1214, 281, 1998, 264, 6367, 13, 50524], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 384, "seek": 140096, "start": 1404.26, "end": 1406.24, "text": " You absolutely need the extra phase information,", "tokens": [50530, 509, 3122, 643, 264, 2857, 5574, 1589, 11, 50629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 385, "seek": 140096, "start": 1406.42, "end": 1410.92, "text": " the extra dimension of complex numbers to twist the staircase through a higher dimension", "tokens": [50638, 264, 2857, 10139, 295, 3997, 3547, 281, 8203, 264, 35359, 807, 257, 2946, 10139, 50863], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 386, "seek": 140096, "start": 1410.92, "end": 1413.32, "text": " and close the cycle without breaking the map.", "tokens": [50863, 293, 1998, 264, 6586, 1553, 7697, 264, 4471, 13, 50983], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 387, "seek": 140096, "start": 1413.6200000000001, "end": 1416.16, "text": " The Escher staircase captures the essence perfectly.", "tokens": [50998, 440, 2313, 6759, 35359, 27986, 264, 12801, 6239, 13, 51125], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 388, "seek": 140096, "start": 1416.9, "end": 1418.1200000000001, "text": " The contradiction isn't local.", "tokens": [51162, 440, 34937, 1943, 380, 2654, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 389, "seek": 140096, "start": 1418.54, "end": 1420.38, "text": " The real numbers aren't failing at the corners.", "tokens": [51244, 440, 957, 3547, 3212, 380, 18223, 412, 264, 12413, 13, 51336], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 390, "seek": 140096, "start": 1420.58, "end": 1421.98, "text": " They are failing topologically.", "tokens": [51346, 814, 366, 18223, 1192, 17157, 13, 51416], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 391, "seek": 140096, "start": 1422.3, "end": 1423.56, "text": " They fail globally.", "tokens": [51432, 814, 3061, 18958, 13, 51495], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 392, "seek": 140096, "start": 1424.14, "end": 1428.24, "text": " And what's fascinating is how meticulously Flixian audits this failure.", "tokens": [51524, 400, 437, 311, 10343, 307, 577, 41566, 25038, 3235, 970, 952, 2379, 1208, 341, 7763, 13, 51729], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 393, "seek": 140096, "start": 1428.76, "end": 1430.48, "text": " The triangle is the minimal witness.", "tokens": [51755, 440, 13369, 307, 264, 13206, 7286, 13, 51841], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16103665161132813, "compression_ratio": 1.7919463087248322, "no_speech_prob": 1.5984594290557874e-12}, {"id": 394, "seek": 143048, "start": 1430.48, "end": 1430.88, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50365, 865, 13, 50385], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2101011405120025, "compression_ratio": 1.296, "no_speech_prob": 1.567165125969383e-12}, {"id": 395, "seek": 143048, "start": 1430.88, "end": 1435.34, "text": " It rigorously proves that real numbers are fundamentally incapable of supporting cycle", "tokens": [50385, 467, 42191, 5098, 25019, 300, 957, 3547, 366, 17879, 44174, 295, 7231, 6586, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2101011405120025, "compression_ratio": 1.296, "no_speech_prob": 1.567165125969383e-12}, {"id": 396, "seek": 143048, "start": 1435.34, "end": 1439.08, "text": " consistency, even for the simplest two-state systems in the universe.", "tokens": [50608, 14416, 11, 754, 337, 264, 22811, 732, 12, 15406, 3652, 294, 264, 6445, 13, 50795], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2101011405120025, "compression_ratio": 1.296, "no_speech_prob": 1.567165125969383e-12}, {"id": 397, "seek": 143908, "start": 1439.08, "end": 1443.6799999999998, "text": " So after the roundabout failure at n equals 3 and the Escher staircase failure at n equals 2,", "tokens": [50365, 407, 934, 264, 3098, 21970, 7763, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 293, 264, 2313, 6759, 35359, 7763, 412, 297, 6915, 568, 11, 50595], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12450726305852171, "compression_ratio": 1.5128205128205128, "no_speech_prob": 1.817938377959849e-12}, {"id": 398, "seek": 143908, "start": 1443.76, "end": 1446.3999999999999, "text": " I have to ask, is this the final nail in the coffin?", "tokens": [50599, 286, 362, 281, 1029, 11, 307, 341, 264, 2572, 10173, 294, 264, 38361, 30, 50731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12450726305852171, "compression_ratio": 1.5128205128205128, "no_speech_prob": 1.817938377959849e-12}, {"id": 399, "seek": 143908, "start": 1446.54, "end": 1450.3, "text": " Like, is this absolute proof that real numbers are dead and the universe must be complex?", "tokens": [50738, 1743, 11, 307, 341, 8236, 8177, 300, 957, 3547, 366, 3116, 293, 264, 6445, 1633, 312, 3997, 30, 50926], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12450726305852171, "compression_ratio": 1.5128205128205128, "no_speech_prob": 1.817938377959849e-12}, {"id": 400, "seek": 145030, "start": 1450.3, "end": 1452.62, "text": " It is a devastating blow.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 257, 21280, 6327, 13, 50481], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20969193121966193, "compression_ratio": 1.4948979591836735, "no_speech_prob": 2.092740260598025e-12}, {"id": 401, "seek": 145030, "start": 1452.62, "end": 1459.12, "text": " But it only rules out real numbers for a single isolated system experiencing transitions around a loop.", "tokens": [50481, 583, 309, 787, 4474, 484, 957, 3547, 337, 257, 2167, 14621, 1185, 11139, 23767, 926, 257, 6367, 13, 50806], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20969193121966193, "compression_ratio": 1.4948979591836735, "no_speech_prob": 2.092740260598025e-12}, {"id": 402, "seek": 145030, "start": 1459.6399999999999, "end": 1466.5, "text": " If we want to truly test the scalar field of reality to see if the universe is fundamentally real or complex,", "tokens": [50832, 759, 321, 528, 281, 4908, 1500, 264, 39684, 2519, 295, 4103, 281, 536, 498, 264, 6445, 307, 17879, 957, 420, 3997, 11, 51175], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20969193121966193, "compression_ratio": 1.4948979591836735, "no_speech_prob": 2.092740260598025e-12}, {"id": 403, "seek": 145030, "start": 1466.98, "end": 1470.02, "text": " we can't just look at one particle talking to itself.", "tokens": [51199, 321, 393, 380, 445, 574, 412, 472, 12359, 1417, 281, 2564, 13, 51351], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20969193121966193, "compression_ratio": 1.4948979591836735, "no_speech_prob": 2.092740260598025e-12}, {"id": 404, "seek": 147002, "start": 1470.02, "end": 1475.6399999999999, "text": " We have to look at what happens when two completely separate, independent systems interact with each other.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 362, 281, 574, 412, 437, 2314, 562, 732, 2584, 4994, 11, 6695, 3652, 4648, 365, 1184, 661, 13, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19512658960678997, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.7902504565597832e-12}, {"id": 405, "seek": 147002, "start": 1475.8799999999999, "end": 1479.3, "text": " And that introduces section 4, bringing systems together.", "tokens": [50658, 400, 300, 31472, 3541, 1017, 11, 5062, 3652, 1214, 13, 50829], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19512658960678997, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.7902504565597832e-12}, {"id": 406, "seek": 147002, "start": 1479.98, "end": 1483.54, "text": " This is where we test the third axis of the manuscript's architecture,", "tokens": [50863, 639, 307, 689, 321, 1500, 264, 2636, 10298, 295, 264, 23928, 311, 9482, 11, 51041], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19512658960678997, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.7902504565597832e-12}, {"id": 407, "seek": 147002, "start": 1483.9, "end": 1488.22, "text": " and it introduces a concept that I found deeply profound, local tomography.", "tokens": [51059, 293, 309, 31472, 257, 3410, 300, 286, 1352, 8760, 14382, 11, 2654, 2916, 5820, 13, 51275], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19512658960678997, "compression_ratio": 1.5, "no_speech_prob": 1.7902504565597832e-12}, {"id": 408, "seek": 148822, "start": 1488.6200000000001, "end": 1492.34, "text": " Define local tomography for me, because this seems to be the linchpin of the whole debate.", "tokens": [50385, 9548, 533, 2654, 2916, 5820, 337, 385, 11, 570, 341, 2544, 281, 312, 264, 287, 12415, 17836, 295, 264, 1379, 7958, 13, 50571], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 409, "seek": 148822, "start": 1492.48, "end": 1495.28, "text": " Local tomography is a foundational operational principle.", "tokens": [50578, 22755, 2916, 5820, 307, 257, 32195, 16607, 8665, 13, 50718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 410, "seek": 148822, "start": 1495.9, "end": 1499.1200000000001, "text": " It is essentially the mathematical formalization of reductionism.", "tokens": [50749, 467, 307, 4476, 264, 18894, 9860, 2144, 295, 11004, 1434, 13, 50910], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 411, "seek": 148822, "start": 1499.56, "end": 1502.94, "text": " It's the idea that if you have a joint system made of two independent parts,", "tokens": [50932, 467, 311, 264, 1558, 300, 498, 291, 362, 257, 7225, 1185, 1027, 295, 732, 6695, 3166, 11, 51101], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 412, "seek": 148822, "start": 1502.94, "end": 1507.46, "text": " let's say Alice has a particle in her lab on Earth and Bob has a particle in his lab on Mars.", "tokens": [51101, 718, 311, 584, 16004, 575, 257, 12359, 294, 720, 2715, 322, 4755, 293, 6085, 575, 257, 12359, 294, 702, 2715, 322, 9692, 13, 51327], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 413, "seek": 148822, "start": 1508.2, "end": 1514.68, "text": " The state of the total combined system should be perfectly 100% describable by Alice locally testing her system,", "tokens": [51364, 440, 1785, 295, 264, 3217, 9354, 1185, 820, 312, 6239, 2319, 4, 2189, 65, 712, 538, 16004, 16143, 4997, 720, 1185, 11, 51688], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15243278115482653, "compression_ratio": 1.6996587030716723, "no_speech_prob": 2.254228704742789e-12}, {"id": 414, "seek": 151468, "start": 1514.68, "end": 1519.16, "text": " Bob locally testing his system, and then just multiplying their lists of data together.", "tokens": [50365, 6085, 16143, 4997, 702, 1185, 11, 293, 550, 445, 30955, 641, 14511, 295, 1412, 1214, 13, 50589], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 415, "seek": 151468, "start": 1519.5, "end": 1522.96, "text": " In other words, if I want to know everything there is to know about the joint system,", "tokens": [50606, 682, 661, 2283, 11, 498, 286, 528, 281, 458, 1203, 456, 307, 281, 458, 466, 264, 7225, 1185, 11, 50779], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 416, "seek": 151468, "start": 1523.18, "end": 1526.8600000000001, "text": " I just need to ask Alice everything she can possibly know about her side,", "tokens": [50790, 286, 445, 643, 281, 1029, 16004, 1203, 750, 393, 6264, 458, 466, 720, 1252, 11, 50974], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 417, "seek": 151468, "start": 1526.98, "end": 1529.94, "text": " and ask Bob everything he can possibly know about his side.", "tokens": [50980, 293, 1029, 6085, 1203, 415, 393, 6264, 458, 466, 702, 1252, 13, 51128], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 418, "seek": 151468, "start": 1530.28, "end": 1534.74, "text": " There shouldn't be any spooky, holistic parameters that only reveal themselves", "tokens": [51145, 821, 4659, 380, 312, 604, 30510, 11, 30334, 9834, 300, 787, 10658, 2969, 51368], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 419, "seek": 151468, "start": 1534.74, "end": 1539.68, "text": " when you put the two particles in the exact same room and measure them together.", "tokens": [51368, 562, 291, 829, 264, 732, 10007, 294, 264, 1900, 912, 1808, 293, 3481, 552, 1214, 13, 51615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 420, "seek": 151468, "start": 1540.0600000000002, "end": 1540.54, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51634, 7587, 13, 51658], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1371953596762561, "compression_ratio": 1.7962264150943397, "no_speech_prob": 1.5798820585111173e-12}, {"id": 421, "seek": 154054, "start": 1540.54, "end": 1546.1399999999999, "text": " Local tomography demands that the whole is exactly equal to the sum of its local parts.", "tokens": [50365, 22755, 2916, 5820, 15107, 300, 264, 1379, 307, 2293, 2681, 281, 264, 2408, 295, 1080, 2654, 3166, 13, 50645], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 422, "seek": 154054, "start": 1546.68, "end": 1548.18, "text": " Now, we have to be very careful here.", "tokens": [50672, 823, 11, 321, 362, 281, 312, 588, 5026, 510, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 423, "seek": 154054, "start": 1548.62, "end": 1553.98, "text": " The manuscript explicitly flags that local tomography is an assumption.", "tokens": [50769, 440, 23928, 20803, 23265, 300, 2654, 2916, 5820, 307, 364, 15302, 13, 51037], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 424, "seek": 154054, "start": 1554.52, "end": 1556.26, "text": " It is not a guaranteed law of nature.", "tokens": [51064, 467, 307, 406, 257, 18031, 2101, 295, 3687, 13, 51151], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 425, "seek": 154054, "start": 1556.62, "end": 1560.86, "text": " But it is an incredibly intuitive, physically reasonable assumption", "tokens": [51169, 583, 309, 307, 364, 6252, 21769, 11, 9762, 10585, 15302, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 426, "seek": 154054, "start": 1560.86, "end": 1563.72, "text": " that matches almost all of our classical experience.", "tokens": [51381, 300, 10676, 1920, 439, 295, 527, 13735, 1752, 13, 51524], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 427, "seek": 154054, "start": 1563.8, "end": 1563.92, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51528, 1779, 13, 51534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 428, "seek": 154054, "start": 1563.98, "end": 1567.62, "text": " If I have two cars, I don't need to crash them together to know their individual weights and speeds.", "tokens": [51537, 759, 286, 362, 732, 5163, 11, 286, 500, 380, 643, 281, 8252, 552, 1214, 281, 458, 641, 2609, 17443, 293, 16411, 13, 51719], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 429, "seek": 154054, "start": 1567.86, "end": 1568.3799999999999, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [51731, 48746, 736, 13, 51757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14537157805069634, "compression_ratio": 1.6267123287671232, "no_speech_prob": 1.5075413790574532e-12}, {"id": 430, "seek": 156838, "start": 1568.38, "end": 1570.66, "text": " So, the manuscript runs a stress test.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 11, 264, 23928, 6676, 257, 4244, 1500, 13, 50479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 431, "seek": 156838, "start": 1570.98, "end": 1576.0800000000002, "text": " It asks, how do our different number systems fare when we demand that local tomography must be true?", "tokens": [50495, 467, 8962, 11, 577, 360, 527, 819, 1230, 3652, 11994, 562, 321, 4733, 300, 2654, 2916, 5820, 1633, 312, 2074, 30, 50750], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 432, "seek": 156838, "start": 1576.44, "end": 1579.7600000000002, "text": " And the way flexion does this is brilliantly simple.", "tokens": [50768, 400, 264, 636, 5896, 313, 775, 341, 307, 8695, 42580, 2199, 13, 50934], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 433, "seek": 156838, "start": 1580.5400000000002, "end": 1582.0400000000002, "text": " It doesn't require deep calculus.", "tokens": [50973, 467, 1177, 380, 3651, 2452, 33400, 13, 51048], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 434, "seek": 156838, "start": 1582.3000000000002, "end": 1584.5200000000002, "text": " It literally just requires counting dimensions.", "tokens": [51061, 467, 3736, 445, 7029, 13251, 12819, 13, 51172], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 435, "seek": 156838, "start": 1585.16, "end": 1590.1200000000001, "text": " Counting how many parameters, how many dials it takes to completely describe a state space.", "tokens": [51204, 5247, 278, 577, 867, 9834, 11, 577, 867, 5502, 82, 309, 2516, 281, 2584, 6786, 257, 1785, 1901, 13, 51452], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 436, "seek": 156838, "start": 1590.44, "end": 1593.68, "text": " Walk me through this dimension counting because the results are staggering.", "tokens": [51468, 10818, 385, 807, 341, 10139, 13251, 570, 264, 3542, 366, 42974, 13, 51630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 437, "seek": 156838, "start": 1594.24, "end": 1597.0400000000002, "text": " Let's start with the reigning champion, complex numbers.", "tokens": [51658, 961, 311, 722, 365, 264, 319, 9676, 10971, 11, 3997, 3547, 13, 51798], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 438, "seek": 156838, "start": 1597.16, "end": 1597.66, "text": " Let's start there.", "tokens": [51804, 961, 311, 722, 456, 13, 51829], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13897617401615267, "compression_ratio": 1.6872964169381108, "no_speech_prob": 1.5610573814509032e-12}, {"id": 439, "seek": 159766, "start": 1597.66, "end": 1606.44, "text": " If Alice has a generic quantum system of dimension D, her complex state space requires a specific number of parameters to fully describe.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 16004, 575, 257, 19577, 13018, 1185, 295, 10139, 413, 11, 720, 3997, 1785, 1901, 7029, 257, 2685, 1230, 295, 9834, 281, 4498, 6786, 13, 50804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11994140625, "compression_ratio": 1.731958762886598, "no_speech_prob": 1.2350964435220435e-12}, {"id": 440, "seek": 159766, "start": 1607.22, "end": 1609.5, "text": " Mathematically, it requires the dimension squared.", "tokens": [50843, 15776, 40197, 11, 309, 7029, 264, 10139, 8889, 13, 50957], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11994140625, "compression_ratio": 1.731958762886598, "no_speech_prob": 1.2350964435220435e-12}, {"id": 441, "seek": 159766, "start": 1610.1200000000001, "end": 1618.78, "text": " So, if Alice has a basic two-level system, a quivit, it takes two squared or four independent parameters to fully describe her complex state space.", "tokens": [50988, 407, 11, 498, 16004, 575, 257, 3875, 732, 12, 12418, 1185, 11, 257, 421, 592, 270, 11, 309, 2516, 732, 8889, 420, 1451, 6695, 9834, 281, 4498, 6786, 720, 3997, 1785, 1901, 13, 51421], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11994140625, "compression_ratio": 1.731958762886598, "no_speech_prob": 1.2350964435220435e-12}, {"id": 442, "seek": 161878, "start": 1618.78, "end": 1623.28, "text": " And Bob also has a two-level quivit, so his state space also takes four parameters.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 6085, 611, 575, 257, 732, 12, 12418, 421, 592, 270, 11, 370, 702, 1785, 1901, 611, 2516, 1451, 9834, 13, 50590], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 443, "seek": 161878, "start": 1623.5, "end": 1623.6399999999999, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50601, 1779, 13, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 444, "seek": 161878, "start": 1623.8799999999999, "end": 1626.16, "text": " Okay, Alice has four dials, Bob has four dials.", "tokens": [50620, 1033, 11, 16004, 575, 1451, 5502, 82, 11, 6085, 575, 1451, 5502, 82, 13, 50734], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 445, "seek": 161878, "start": 1626.24, "end": 1630.3, "text": " Now, what happens when we put them together into a joint four-level composite system?", "tokens": [50738, 823, 11, 437, 2314, 562, 321, 829, 552, 1214, 666, 257, 7225, 1451, 12, 12418, 25557, 1185, 30, 50941], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 446, "seek": 161878, "start": 1630.44, "end": 1631.34, "text": " We run the same formula.", "tokens": [50948, 492, 1190, 264, 912, 8513, 13, 50993], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 447, "seek": 161878, "start": 1631.78, "end": 1636.6399999999999, "text": " A joint four-level complex system requires four squared, which is exactly 16 parameters.", "tokens": [51015, 316, 7225, 1451, 12, 12418, 3997, 1185, 7029, 1451, 8889, 11, 597, 307, 2293, 3165, 9834, 13, 51258], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 448, "seek": 161878, "start": 1636.84, "end": 1641.6, "text": " And if you look at our local dials, Alice's four times Bob's four perfectly equals 16.", "tokens": [51268, 400, 498, 291, 574, 412, 527, 2654, 5502, 82, 11, 16004, 311, 1451, 1413, 6085, 311, 1451, 6239, 6915, 3165, 13, 51506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 449, "seek": 161878, "start": 1641.76, "end": 1642.16, "text": " Oh, wow.", "tokens": [51514, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 51534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11491243504295665, "compression_ratio": 1.6953125, "no_speech_prob": 1.2594556474121443e-12}, {"id": 450, "seek": 164216, "start": 1642.16, "end": 1644.5800000000002, "text": " The dimensions match flawlessly.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 12819, 2995, 13717, 12048, 13, 50486], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16960275173187256, "compression_ratio": 1.1826923076923077, "no_speech_prob": 2.514016338855951e-12}, {"id": 451, "seek": 164216, "start": 1645.7, "end": 1650.0, "text": " Every single parameter of the joint system can be accounted for by the local measurements.", "tokens": [50542, 2048, 2167, 13075, 295, 264, 7225, 1185, 393, 312, 43138, 337, 538, 264, 2654, 15383, 13, 50757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16960275173187256, "compression_ratio": 1.1826923076923077, "no_speech_prob": 2.514016338855951e-12}, {"id": 452, "seek": 165000, "start": 1650.0, "end": 1656.4, "text": " The math of combining two complex systems perfectly matches our physical intuition of local independence.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 5221, 295, 21928, 732, 3997, 3652, 6239, 10676, 527, 4001, 24002, 295, 2654, 14640, 13, 50685], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 453, "seek": 165000, "start": 1656.78, "end": 1658.78, "text": " It's the Goldilocks mathematical field.", "tokens": [50704, 467, 311, 264, 6731, 388, 35507, 18894, 2519, 13, 50804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 454, "seek": 165000, "start": 1659.06, "end": 1660.32, "text": " Everything is just right.", "tokens": [50818, 5471, 307, 445, 558, 13, 50881], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 455, "seek": 165000, "start": 1661.02, "end": 1664.4, "text": " But what happens when we force the universe to use real numbers?", "tokens": [50916, 583, 437, 2314, 562, 321, 3464, 264, 6445, 281, 764, 957, 3547, 30, 51085], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 456, "seek": 165000, "start": 1664.86, "end": 1666.6, "text": " We strip away the imaginary eye.", "tokens": [51108, 492, 12828, 1314, 264, 26164, 3313, 13, 51195], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 457, "seek": 165000, "start": 1666.9, "end": 1668.38, "text": " What happens to the dials?", "tokens": [51210, 708, 2314, 281, 264, 5502, 82, 30, 51284], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17758695844193578, "compression_ratio": 1.48, "no_speech_prob": 2.794060989519398e-12}, {"id": 458, "seek": 166838, "start": 1668.38, "end": 1669.38, "text": " The math changes.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 5221, 2962, 13, 50415], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 459, "seek": 166838, "start": 1670.2800000000002, "end": 1675.7, "text": " If we use strictly real numbers, a two-level symmetric system requires only three parameters.", "tokens": [50460, 759, 321, 764, 20792, 957, 3547, 11, 257, 732, 12, 12418, 32330, 1185, 7029, 787, 1045, 9834, 13, 50731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 460, "seek": 166838, "start": 1676.2, "end": 1678.22, "text": " So Alice has three local dials to play with.", "tokens": [50756, 407, 16004, 575, 1045, 2654, 5502, 82, 281, 862, 365, 13, 50857], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 461, "seek": 166838, "start": 1678.5600000000002, "end": 1680.7800000000002, "text": " Bob has a two-level system, so he has three dials.", "tokens": [50874, 6085, 575, 257, 732, 12, 12418, 1185, 11, 370, 415, 575, 1045, 5502, 82, 13, 50985], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 462, "seek": 166838, "start": 1681.1000000000001, "end": 1684.5800000000002, "text": " Locally, they could discover three times three, which is nine total independent parameters.", "tokens": [51001, 12859, 379, 11, 436, 727, 4411, 1045, 1413, 1045, 11, 597, 307, 4949, 3217, 6695, 9834, 13, 51175], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 463, "seek": 166838, "start": 1684.7600000000002, "end": 1686.14, "text": " Okay, nine local parameters.", "tokens": [51184, 1033, 11, 4949, 2654, 9834, 13, 51253], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 464, "seek": 166838, "start": 1686.38, "end": 1689.3400000000001, "text": " But what does the math say a joint four-level real system requires?", "tokens": [51265, 583, 437, 775, 264, 5221, 584, 257, 7225, 1451, 12, 12418, 957, 1185, 7029, 30, 51413], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 465, "seek": 166838, "start": 1689.5400000000002, "end": 1692.92, "text": " A joint four-level real symmetric system requires ten parameters.", "tokens": [51423, 316, 7225, 1451, 12, 12418, 957, 32330, 1185, 7029, 2064, 9834, 13, 51592], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 466, "seek": 166838, "start": 1693.16, "end": 1693.42, "text": " Ten?", "tokens": [51604, 9380, 30, 51617], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 467, "seek": 166838, "start": 1693.5800000000002, "end": 1695.0400000000002, "text": " Wait, so there is a missing dimension.", "tokens": [51625, 3802, 11, 370, 456, 307, 257, 5361, 10139, 13, 51698], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 468, "seek": 166838, "start": 1695.42, "end": 1697.14, "text": " The joint system has too many parameters.", "tokens": [51717, 440, 7225, 1185, 575, 886, 867, 9834, 13, 51803], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15001291586152204, "compression_ratio": 1.957142857142857, "no_speech_prob": 1.5549679599491384e-12}, {"id": 469, "seek": 169714, "start": 1697.14, "end": 1698.8200000000002, "text": " Ten does not equal nine.", "tokens": [50365, 9380, 775, 406, 2681, 4949, 13, 50449], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 470, "seek": 169714, "start": 1699.18, "end": 1700.5600000000002, "text": " This is a monumental fracture.", "tokens": [50467, 639, 307, 257, 43105, 36877, 13, 50536], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 471, "seek": 169714, "start": 1701.44, "end": 1706.8400000000001, "text": " It means that a real numbered composite system carries a genuinely non-local degree of freedom.", "tokens": [50580, 467, 1355, 300, 257, 957, 40936, 25557, 1185, 16402, 257, 17839, 2107, 12, 5842, 304, 4314, 295, 5645, 13, 50850], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 472, "seek": 169714, "start": 1707.5600000000002, "end": 1715.5400000000002, "text": " It has a hidden tenth dial, a parameter that is completely, fundamentally invisible to any local measurement scheme Alice and Bob could ever devise,", "tokens": [50886, 467, 575, 257, 7633, 27269, 5502, 11, 257, 13075, 300, 307, 2584, 11, 17879, 14603, 281, 604, 2654, 13160, 12232, 16004, 293, 6085, 727, 1562, 1905, 908, 11, 51285], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 473, "seek": 169714, "start": 1715.72, "end": 1717.5, "text": " no matter how clever their experiments are.", "tokens": [51294, 572, 1871, 577, 13494, 641, 12050, 366, 13, 51383], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 474, "seek": 169714, "start": 1718.0600000000002, "end": 1723.18, "text": " They could measure their local particles for a thousand years, and they would never see the tenth parameter.", "tokens": [51411, 814, 727, 3481, 641, 2654, 10007, 337, 257, 4714, 924, 11, 293, 436, 576, 1128, 536, 264, 27269, 13075, 13, 51667], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 475, "seek": 169714, "start": 1723.38, "end": 1726.44, "text": " The source identifies this missing direction explicitly.", "tokens": [51677, 440, 4009, 34597, 341, 5361, 3513, 20803, 13, 51830], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1550652787492082, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 1.5856244268974695e-12}, {"id": 476, "seek": 172644, "start": 1727.14, "end": 1731.92, "text": " Let's get into the mechanics of this, because I want to understand how a dimension can just be missing.", "tokens": [50400, 961, 311, 483, 666, 264, 12939, 295, 341, 11, 570, 286, 528, 281, 1223, 577, 257, 10139, 393, 445, 312, 5361, 13, 50639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1857808794294085, "compression_ratio": 1.4262295081967213, "no_speech_prob": 2.2890990318547022e-12}, {"id": 477, "seek": 172644, "start": 1732.78, "end": 1738.28, "text": " The book says this hidden dimension is spanned by the tensor product of a matrix called JJ tensor.", "tokens": [50682, 440, 1446, 1619, 341, 7633, 10139, 307, 637, 5943, 538, 264, 40863, 1674, 295, 257, 8141, 1219, 21386, 40863, 13, 50957], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1857808794294085, "compression_ratio": 1.4262295081967213, "no_speech_prob": 2.2890990318547022e-12}, {"id": 478, "seek": 172644, "start": 1739.26, "end": 1740.5800000000002, "text": " Jay, unpack that for me.", "tokens": [51006, 11146, 11, 26699, 300, 337, 385, 13, 51072], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1857808794294085, "compression_ratio": 1.4262295081967213, "no_speech_prob": 2.2890990318547022e-12}, {"id": 479, "seek": 172644, "start": 1740.64, "end": 1741.22, "text": " What is Jay?", "tokens": [51075, 708, 307, 11146, 30, 51104], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1857808794294085, "compression_ratio": 1.4262295081967213, "no_speech_prob": 2.2890990318547022e-12}, {"id": 480, "seek": 172644, "start": 1741.3200000000002, "end": 1742.3400000000001, "text": " Let's visualize Jay.", "tokens": [51109, 961, 311, 23273, 11146, 13, 51160], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1857808794294085, "compression_ratio": 1.4262295081967213, "no_speech_prob": 2.2890990318547022e-12}, {"id": 481, "seek": 174234, "start": 1742.34, "end": 1748.84, "text": " In linear algebra, Jay is the standard matrix that represents a 90-degree quarter-turn rotation in a 2D plane.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 8213, 21989, 11, 11146, 307, 264, 3832, 8141, 300, 8855, 257, 4289, 12, 34368, 6555, 12, 33886, 12447, 294, 257, 568, 35, 5720, 13, 50690], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1515132056342231, "compression_ratio": 1.5416666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.817939679002456e-12}, {"id": 482, "seek": 174234, "start": 1749.5, "end": 1754.06, "text": " It takes a vector pointing along the x-axis and rotates it to point along the y-axis.", "tokens": [50723, 467, 2516, 257, 8062, 12166, 2051, 264, 2031, 12, 24633, 293, 42133, 309, 281, 935, 2051, 264, 288, 12, 24633, 13, 50951], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1515132056342231, "compression_ratio": 1.5416666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.817939679002456e-12}, {"id": 483, "seek": 174234, "start": 1754.62, "end": 1758.26, "text": " Now, the crucial property of Jay is that it is an antisymmetric matrix.", "tokens": [50979, 823, 11, 264, 11462, 4707, 295, 11146, 307, 300, 309, 307, 364, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 8141, 13, 51161], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1515132056342231, "compression_ratio": 1.5416666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.817939679002456e-12}, {"id": 484, "seek": 174234, "start": 1758.3999999999999, "end": 1759.5, "text": " Antisymmetric meaning what?", "tokens": [51168, 5130, 14169, 2174, 17475, 3620, 437, 30, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1515132056342231, "compression_ratio": 1.5416666666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.817939679002456e-12}, {"id": 485, "seek": 175950, "start": 1759.5, "end": 1765.68, "text": " Meaning if you reflect it across its diagonal, all the positive numbers become negative, and all the negative numbers become positive.", "tokens": [50365, 19948, 498, 291, 5031, 309, 2108, 1080, 21539, 11, 439, 264, 3353, 3547, 1813, 3671, 11, 293, 439, 264, 3671, 3547, 1813, 3353, 13, 50674], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1143348217010498, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832561378412382e-12}, {"id": 486, "seek": 175950, "start": 1765.88, "end": 1767.44, "text": " It mirrors itself inversely.", "tokens": [50684, 467, 24238, 2564, 21378, 736, 13, 50762], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1143348217010498, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832561378412382e-12}, {"id": 487, "seek": 175950, "start": 1767.76, "end": 1776.86, "text": " In quantum mechanics, observable physical properties, like things you can actually measure on a screen, like momentum or energy, must be symmetric, not antisymmetric.", "tokens": [50778, 682, 13018, 12939, 11, 9951, 712, 4001, 7221, 11, 411, 721, 291, 393, 767, 3481, 322, 257, 2568, 11, 411, 11244, 420, 2281, 11, 1633, 312, 32330, 11, 406, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 13, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1143348217010498, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832561378412382e-12}, {"id": 488, "seek": 177686, "start": 1776.86, "end": 1786.08, "text": " Because Jay is antisymmetric, it does not correspond to a valid, measurable, physical observable for Alice in her lab, or Bob in his lab.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 11146, 307, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 11, 309, 775, 406, 6805, 281, 257, 7363, 11, 43615, 11, 4001, 9951, 712, 337, 16004, 294, 720, 2715, 11, 420, 6085, 294, 702, 2715, 13, 50826], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16989791684034394, "compression_ratio": 1.555, "no_speech_prob": 1.769169229517975e-12}, {"id": 489, "seek": 177686, "start": 1786.3, "end": 1788.6599999999999, "text": " They physically cannot see a pure quarter-turn.", "tokens": [50837, 814, 9762, 2644, 536, 257, 6075, 6555, 12, 33886, 13, 50955], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16989791684034394, "compression_ratio": 1.555, "no_speech_prob": 1.769169229517975e-12}, {"id": 490, "seek": 177686, "start": 1789.12, "end": 1792.1799999999998, "text": " Okay, so Jay is invisible to Alice, and Jay is invisible to Bob.", "tokens": [50978, 1033, 11, 370, 11146, 307, 14603, 281, 16004, 11, 293, 11146, 307, 14603, 281, 6085, 13, 51131], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16989791684034394, "compression_ratio": 1.555, "no_speech_prob": 1.769169229517975e-12}, {"id": 491, "seek": 177686, "start": 1792.4799999999998, "end": 1795.34, "text": " But what happens when they combine them, the Jay tensor Jay?", "tokens": [51146, 583, 437, 2314, 562, 436, 10432, 552, 11, 264, 11146, 40863, 11146, 30, 51289], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16989791684034394, "compression_ratio": 1.555, "no_speech_prob": 1.769169229517975e-12}, {"id": 492, "seek": 179534, "start": 1795.34, "end": 1797.1399999999999, "text": " This is where the math gets almost magical.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 689, 264, 5221, 2170, 1920, 12066, 13, 50455], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 493, "seek": 179534, "start": 1798.08, "end": 1804.56, "text": " When you take the tensor product of two antisymmetric matrices, when you multiply Alice's Jay by Bob's Jay to look at the joint system,", "tokens": [50502, 1133, 291, 747, 264, 40863, 1674, 295, 732, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 32284, 11, 562, 291, 12972, 16004, 311, 11146, 538, 6085, 311, 11146, 281, 574, 412, 264, 7225, 1185, 11, 50826], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 494, "seek": 179534, "start": 1805.06, "end": 1811.08, "text": " the negative signs from Alice's side multiply with the negative signs from Bob's side, and a negative times a negative equals a positive.", "tokens": [50851, 264, 3671, 7880, 490, 16004, 311, 1252, 12972, 365, 264, 3671, 7880, 490, 6085, 311, 1252, 11, 293, 257, 3671, 1413, 257, 3671, 6915, 257, 3353, 13, 51152], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 495, "seek": 179534, "start": 1811.3, "end": 1811.74, "text": " Ooh!", "tokens": [51163, 7951, 0, 51185], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 496, "seek": 179534, "start": 1812.1, "end": 1813.84, "text": " The antisymmetry cancels itself out.", "tokens": [51203, 440, 2511, 14169, 2174, 9889, 393, 66, 1625, 2564, 484, 13, 51290], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 497, "seek": 179534, "start": 1814.1999999999998, "end": 1817.74, "text": " The resulting joint matrix, Jay tensor Jay, is perfectly symmetric.", "tokens": [51308, 440, 16505, 7225, 8141, 11, 11146, 40863, 11146, 11, 307, 6239, 32330, 13, 51485], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 498, "seek": 179534, "start": 1818.0, "end": 1818.6799999999998, "text": " Oh, wow.", "tokens": [51498, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 51532], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15936569618967783, "compression_ratio": 1.7580645161290323, "no_speech_prob": 1.6108508841053415e-12}, {"id": 499, "seek": 181868, "start": 1818.68, "end": 1827.26, "text": " So, locally, the parameter is unmeasurable because it's negative, but globally, the negatives cancel, and it becomes a perfectly valid, measurable parameter for the joint system.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 11, 16143, 11, 264, 13075, 307, 517, 1398, 296, 25863, 570, 309, 311, 3671, 11, 457, 18958, 11, 264, 40019, 10373, 11, 293, 309, 3643, 257, 6239, 7363, 11, 43615, 13075, 337, 264, 7225, 1185, 13, 50794], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 500, "seek": 181868, "start": 1827.6200000000001, "end": 1828.02, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50812, 7587, 13, 50832], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 501, "seek": 181868, "start": 1829.04, "end": 1831.48, "text": " Jay tensor Jay becomes the hidden tenth dial.", "tokens": [50883, 11146, 40863, 11146, 3643, 264, 7633, 27269, 5502, 13, 51005], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 502, "seek": 181868, "start": 1832.24, "end": 1838.74, "text": " It is an observable that only exists in the relationship between the two systems, never in the systems themselves.", "tokens": [51043, 467, 307, 364, 9951, 712, 300, 787, 8198, 294, 264, 2480, 1296, 264, 732, 3652, 11, 1128, 294, 264, 3652, 2969, 13, 51368], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 503, "seek": 181868, "start": 1839.14, "end": 1843.68, "text": " I tried to think of an analogy for this, and originally I was thinking about a joint bank account where money is hidden,", "tokens": [51388, 286, 3031, 281, 519, 295, 364, 21663, 337, 341, 11, 293, 7993, 286, 390, 1953, 466, 257, 7225, 3765, 2696, 689, 1460, 307, 7633, 11, 51615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 504, "seek": 181868, "start": 1844.2, "end": 1848.3200000000002, "text": " but that doesn't really explain the mechanism of the antisymmetry cancels out.", "tokens": [51641, 457, 300, 1177, 380, 534, 2903, 264, 7513, 295, 264, 2511, 14169, 2174, 9889, 393, 66, 1625, 484, 13, 51847], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10990025103092194, "compression_ratio": 1.7792207792207793, "no_speech_prob": 1.3149106369708674e-12}, {"id": 505, "seek": 184832, "start": 1848.32, "end": 1848.8799999999999, "text": " Let's try this.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 853, 341, 13, 50393], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 506, "seek": 184832, "start": 1849.26, "end": 1853.24, "text": " It's like Alice and Bob both have heavily encrypted computer files.", "tokens": [50412, 467, 311, 411, 16004, 293, 6085, 1293, 362, 10950, 36663, 3820, 7098, 13, 50611], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 507, "seek": 184832, "start": 1853.36, "end": 1854.4199999999998, "text": " Okay, like where this is going.", "tokens": [50617, 1033, 11, 411, 689, 341, 307, 516, 13, 50670], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 508, "seek": 184832, "start": 1854.84, "end": 1859.12, "text": " If you open Alice's file on her local computer, it is pure random gibberish.", "tokens": [50691, 759, 291, 1269, 16004, 311, 3991, 322, 720, 2654, 3820, 11, 309, 307, 6075, 4974, 4553, 43189, 13, 50905], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 509, "seek": 184832, "start": 1859.3999999999999, "end": 1862.4199999999998, "text": " The data is antisymmetric, it's noise, it's totally unreadable.", "tokens": [50919, 440, 1412, 307, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 11, 309, 311, 5658, 11, 309, 311, 3879, 517, 2538, 712, 13, 51070], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 510, "seek": 184832, "start": 1862.76, "end": 1867.12, "text": " If you open Bob's file on his local computer, it is also pure random gibberish.", "tokens": [51087, 759, 291, 1269, 6085, 311, 3991, 322, 702, 2654, 3820, 11, 309, 307, 611, 6075, 4974, 4553, 43189, 13, 51305], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 511, "seek": 184832, "start": 1867.48, "end": 1875.98, "text": " But if you network the two computers together and overlay the files, Alice's gibberish acts as the perfect cryptographic key to Bob's gibberish.", "tokens": [51323, 583, 498, 291, 3209, 264, 732, 10807, 1214, 293, 31741, 264, 7098, 11, 16004, 311, 4553, 43189, 10672, 382, 264, 2176, 9844, 12295, 2141, 281, 6085, 311, 4553, 43189, 13, 51748], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 512, "seek": 184832, "start": 1876.3999999999999, "end": 1877.6599999999999, "text": " The noise cancels the noise.", "tokens": [51769, 440, 5658, 393, 66, 1625, 264, 5658, 13, 51832], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109072312064793, "compression_ratio": 1.8888888888888888, "no_speech_prob": 1.642621585626336e-12}, {"id": 513, "seek": 187766, "start": 1877.66, "end": 1881.94, "text": " Right, and suddenly a secret, perfectly readable message appears on the screen.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 11, 293, 5800, 257, 4054, 11, 6239, 49857, 3636, 7038, 322, 264, 2568, 13, 50579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10276570320129394, "compression_ratio": 1.574468085106383, "no_speech_prob": 1.636211890802819e-12}, {"id": 514, "seek": 187766, "start": 1882.74, "end": 1888.4, "text": " The information, the tenth dial, didn't exist on Alice's hard drive, and it didn't exist on Bob's hard drive.", "tokens": [50619, 440, 1589, 11, 264, 27269, 5502, 11, 994, 380, 2514, 322, 16004, 311, 1152, 3332, 11, 293, 309, 994, 380, 2514, 322, 6085, 311, 1152, 3332, 13, 50902], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10276570320129394, "compression_ratio": 1.574468085106383, "no_speech_prob": 1.636211890802819e-12}, {"id": 515, "seek": 187766, "start": 1888.52, "end": 1892.4, "text": " It existed entirely in the mathematical relationship between the two drives.", "tokens": [50908, 467, 13135, 7696, 294, 264, 18894, 2480, 1296, 264, 732, 11754, 13, 51102], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10276570320129394, "compression_ratio": 1.574468085106383, "no_speech_prob": 1.636211890802819e-12}, {"id": 516, "seek": 187766, "start": 1892.6000000000001, "end": 1894.1000000000001, "text": " That is a phenomenal analogy.", "tokens": [51112, 663, 307, 257, 17778, 21663, 13, 51187], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10276570320129394, "compression_ratio": 1.574468085106383, "no_speech_prob": 1.636211890802819e-12}, {"id": 517, "seek": 189410, "start": 1894.1, "end": 1898.7199999999998, "text": " The cryptographic key perfectly captures the essence of J tensor J.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 9844, 12295, 2141, 6239, 27986, 264, 12801, 295, 508, 40863, 508, 13, 50596], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2609997076146743, "compression_ratio": 0.9710144927536232, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122540786426946e-12}, {"id": 518, "seek": 189872, "start": 1898.72, "end": 1903.76, "text": " The information is locked in the correlation, invisible to the local observer.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 1589, 307, 9376, 294, 264, 20009, 11, 14603, 281, 264, 2654, 27878, 13, 50617], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13082664542728, "compression_ratio": 1.0833333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442618951788116e-12}, {"id": 519, "seek": 190376, "start": 1903.76, "end": 1909.06, "text": " And just to close the loop on the dimension counting, the manuscript also looks at quaternions, right?", "tokens": [50365, 400, 445, 281, 1998, 264, 6367, 322, 264, 10139, 13251, 11, 264, 23928, 611, 1542, 412, 421, 771, 77, 626, 11, 558, 30, 50630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11485906000490542, "compression_ratio": 1.186046511627907, "no_speech_prob": 1.5671696796185075e-12}, {"id": 520, "seek": 190906, "start": 1909.06, "end": 1915.3, "text": " This is an even more complicated number system with three different imaginary axes, i, j, and k.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 364, 754, 544, 6179, 1230, 1185, 365, 1045, 819, 26164, 35387, 11, 741, 11, 361, 11, 293, 350, 13, 50677], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18008750363400108, "compression_ratio": 1.2118644067796611, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281873631799782e-12}, {"id": 521, "seek": 190906, "start": 1916.08, "end": 1918.22, "text": " What happens if the universe uses quaternions?", "tokens": [50716, 708, 2314, 498, 264, 6445, 4960, 421, 771, 77, 626, 30, 50823], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18008750363400108, "compression_ratio": 1.2118644067796611, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281873631799782e-12}, {"id": 522, "seek": 191822, "start": 1918.22, "end": 1923.3, "text": " If real numbers have too many global parameters, quaternions have the exact opposite problem.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 957, 3547, 362, 886, 867, 4338, 9834, 11, 421, 771, 77, 626, 362, 264, 1900, 6182, 1154, 13, 50619], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10210495409758194, "compression_ratio": 1.7316017316017316, "no_speech_prob": 1.5796981778226638e-12}, {"id": 523, "seek": 191822, "start": 1923.46, "end": 1924.18, "text": " They have too few.", "tokens": [50627, 814, 362, 886, 1326, 13, 50663], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10210495409758194, "compression_ratio": 1.7316017316017316, "no_speech_prob": 1.5796981778226638e-12}, {"id": 524, "seek": 191822, "start": 1924.8, "end": 1932.1200000000001, "text": " The naive product of local quaternionic bases produces way more parameters than the joint quaternionic state space actually has room for.", "tokens": [50694, 440, 29052, 1674, 295, 2654, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 17949, 14725, 636, 544, 9834, 813, 264, 7225, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 1785, 1901, 767, 575, 1808, 337, 13, 51060], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10210495409758194, "compression_ratio": 1.7316017316017316, "no_speech_prob": 1.5796981778226638e-12}, {"id": 525, "seek": 191822, "start": 1932.6200000000001, "end": 1934.32, "text": " The system is overdetermined.", "tokens": [51085, 440, 1185, 307, 670, 49136, 2001, 13, 51170], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10210495409758194, "compression_ratio": 1.7316017316017316, "no_speech_prob": 1.5796981778226638e-12}, {"id": 526, "seek": 191822, "start": 1934.96, "end": 1941.2, "text": " Complex numbers are the unique Goldilocks fixed point where local dimensions perfectly multiply into global dimensions.", "tokens": [51202, 41184, 3547, 366, 264, 3845, 6731, 388, 35507, 6806, 935, 689, 2654, 12819, 6239, 12972, 666, 4338, 12819, 13, 51514], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10210495409758194, "compression_ratio": 1.7316017316017316, "no_speech_prob": 1.5796981778226638e-12}, {"id": 527, "seek": 194120, "start": 1941.2, "end": 1948.6000000000001, "text": " So real systems have a hidden cryptographic vault, which transitions us perfectly into section 5, the illusion of entanglement.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 957, 3652, 362, 257, 7633, 9844, 12295, 27134, 11, 597, 23767, 505, 6239, 666, 3541, 1025, 11, 264, 18854, 295, 948, 656, 3054, 13, 50735], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1571676964853324, "compression_ratio": 1.6101694915254237, "no_speech_prob": 1.935630041666192e-12}, {"id": 528, "seek": 194120, "start": 1948.94, "end": 1953.8600000000001, "text": " Because this hidden vault in the real numbers isn't just some abstract mathematical glitch on a chalkboard.", "tokens": [50752, 1436, 341, 7633, 27134, 294, 264, 957, 3547, 1943, 380, 445, 512, 12649, 18894, 23552, 322, 257, 28660, 3787, 13, 50998], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1571676964853324, "compression_ratio": 1.6101694915254237, "no_speech_prob": 1.935630041666192e-12}, {"id": 529, "seek": 194120, "start": 1954.1000000000001, "end": 1957.38, "text": " It has massive, mind-bending physical consequences.", "tokens": [51010, 467, 575, 5994, 11, 1575, 12, 65, 2029, 4001, 10098, 13, 51174], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1571676964853324, "compression_ratio": 1.6101694915254237, "no_speech_prob": 1.935630041666192e-12}, {"id": 530, "seek": 194120, "start": 1957.8600000000001, "end": 1963.66, "text": " This is where the manuscript transitions from structural math into profound physical interpretation.", "tokens": [51198, 639, 307, 689, 264, 23928, 23767, 490, 15067, 5221, 666, 14382, 4001, 14174, 13, 51488], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1571676964853324, "compression_ratio": 1.6101694915254237, "no_speech_prob": 1.935630041666192e-12}, {"id": 531, "seek": 194120, "start": 1964.46, "end": 1969.7, "text": " Let's talk about that hidden 10th dimension, the cryptographic message of J tensor, J.", "tokens": [51528, 961, 311, 751, 466, 300, 7633, 1266, 392, 10139, 11, 264, 9844, 12295, 3636, 295, 508, 40863, 11, 508, 13, 51790], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1571676964853324, "compression_ratio": 1.6101694915254237, "no_speech_prob": 1.935630041666192e-12}, {"id": 532, "seek": 196970, "start": 1969.7, "end": 1973.98, "text": " Chapter 10 of the book proves that this isn't just an artifact.", "tokens": [50365, 18874, 1266, 295, 264, 1446, 25019, 300, 341, 1943, 380, 445, 364, 34806, 13, 50579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 533, "seek": 196970, "start": 1974.64, "end": 1981.28, "text": " You can build a whole continuous family of valid physical joint states that rely heavily on this hidden dimension.", "tokens": [50612, 509, 393, 1322, 257, 1379, 10957, 1605, 295, 7363, 4001, 7225, 4368, 300, 10687, 10950, 322, 341, 7633, 10139, 13, 50944], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 534, "seek": 196970, "start": 1981.44, "end": 1982.0, "text": " Oh, interesting.", "tokens": [50952, 876, 11, 1880, 13, 50980], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 535, "seek": 196970, "start": 1982.38, "end": 1988.48, "text": " The book parametrizes this family with a coefficient called c, which can slide anywhere between negative 1 and 1.", "tokens": [50999, 440, 1446, 6220, 302, 470, 12214, 341, 1605, 365, 257, 17619, 1219, 269, 11, 597, 393, 4137, 4992, 1296, 3671, 502, 293, 502, 13, 51304], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 536, "seek": 196970, "start": 1988.72, "end": 1994.92, "text": " Okay, so we have this physical family of states sliding from negative 1 to 1 based on our hidden cryptographic key.", "tokens": [51316, 1033, 11, 370, 321, 362, 341, 4001, 1605, 295, 4368, 21169, 490, 3671, 502, 281, 502, 2361, 322, 527, 7633, 9844, 12295, 2141, 13, 51626], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 537, "seek": 196970, "start": 1995.4, "end": 1998.14, "text": " And then the manuscript applies an operation called the partial trace.", "tokens": [51650, 400, 550, 264, 23928, 13165, 364, 6916, 1219, 264, 14641, 13508, 13, 51787], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12109105927603585, "compression_ratio": 1.6588628762541806, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248191987987557e-12}, {"id": 538, "seek": 199814, "start": 1998.14, "end": 2000.0600000000002, "text": " We need to explain this for the listener.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 643, 281, 2903, 341, 337, 264, 31569, 13, 50461], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15760968436657544, "compression_ratio": 1.5792079207920793, "no_speech_prob": 2.361754455199039e-12}, {"id": 539, "seek": 199814, "start": 2000.6000000000001, "end": 2003.0400000000002, "text": " What exactly is a partial trace operationally?", "tokens": [50488, 708, 2293, 307, 257, 14641, 13508, 6916, 379, 30, 50610], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15760968436657544, "compression_ratio": 1.5792079207920793, "no_speech_prob": 2.361754455199039e-12}, {"id": 540, "seek": 199814, "start": 2003.38, "end": 2008.16, "text": " The partial trace is the rigorous mathematical operation for ignoring part of the universe.", "tokens": [50627, 440, 14641, 13508, 307, 264, 29882, 18894, 6916, 337, 26258, 644, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 50866], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15760968436657544, "compression_ratio": 1.5792079207920793, "no_speech_prob": 2.361754455199039e-12}, {"id": 541, "seek": 199814, "start": 2008.5, "end": 2016.64, "text": " It is how we mathematically formalize Alice, focusing solely on her particle and completely ignoring whatever Bob is doing on Mars.", "tokens": [50883, 467, 307, 577, 321, 44003, 9860, 1125, 16004, 11, 8416, 23309, 322, 720, 12359, 293, 2584, 26258, 2035, 6085, 307, 884, 322, 9692, 13, 51290], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15760968436657544, "compression_ratio": 1.5792079207920793, "no_speech_prob": 2.361754455199039e-12}, {"id": 542, "seek": 199814, "start": 2016.8200000000002, "end": 2017.0200000000002, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51299, 1779, 13, 51309], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15760968436657544, "compression_ratio": 1.5792079207920793, "no_speech_prob": 2.361754455199039e-12}, {"id": 543, "seek": 201702, "start": 2017.02, "end": 2025.44, "text": " When you do a partial trace over Bob's system, you're essentially averaging out all the possible things Bob's particle could be doing, so you can see what Alice is left with.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 291, 360, 257, 14641, 13508, 670, 6085, 311, 1185, 11, 291, 434, 4476, 47308, 484, 439, 264, 1944, 721, 6085, 311, 12359, 727, 312, 884, 11, 370, 291, 393, 536, 437, 16004, 307, 1411, 365, 13, 50786], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11466611756218804, "compression_ratio": 1.5025641025641026, "no_speech_prob": 1.875642870186045e-12}, {"id": 544, "seek": 201702, "start": 2025.62, "end": 2030.22, "text": " And what happens to our hidden J tensor J states when we average out Bob?", "tokens": [50795, 400, 437, 2314, 281, 527, 7633, 508, 40863, 508, 4368, 562, 321, 4274, 484, 6085, 30, 51025], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11466611756218804, "compression_ratio": 1.5025641025641026, "no_speech_prob": 1.875642870186045e-12}, {"id": 545, "seek": 201702, "start": 2030.66, "end": 2032.72, "text": " Think back to the antisymmetry we discussed.", "tokens": [51047, 6557, 646, 281, 264, 2511, 14169, 2174, 9889, 321, 7152, 13, 51150], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11466611756218804, "compression_ratio": 1.5025641025641026, "no_speech_prob": 1.875642870186045e-12}, {"id": 546, "seek": 203272, "start": 2032.72, "end": 2038.64, "text": " Because the J matrix is antisymmetric, its positive parts perfectly mirror its negative parts.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 264, 508, 8141, 307, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 11, 1080, 3353, 3166, 6239, 8013, 1080, 3671, 3166, 13, 50661], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15082298625599255, "compression_ratio": 1.5357142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.415523622795567e-12}, {"id": 547, "seek": 203272, "start": 2039.1000000000001, "end": 2045.08, "text": " When you average it out across all possibilities, the pluses and minuses perfectly cancel each other into zero.", "tokens": [50684, 1133, 291, 4274, 309, 484, 2108, 439, 12178, 11, 264, 1804, 279, 293, 3175, 279, 6239, 10373, 1184, 661, 666, 4018, 13, 50983], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15082298625599255, "compression_ratio": 1.5357142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.415523622795567e-12}, {"id": 548, "seek": 203272, "start": 2045.54, "end": 2048.14, "text": " The hidden parameter mathematically vanishes.", "tokens": [51006, 440, 7633, 13075, 44003, 3161, 16423, 13, 51136], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15082298625599255, "compression_ratio": 1.5357142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.415523622795567e-12}, {"id": 549, "seek": 203272, "start": 2048.52, "end": 2051.08, "text": " The manuscript calls this marginal invisibility.", "tokens": [51155, 440, 23928, 5498, 341, 16885, 13308, 2841, 13, 51283], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15082298625599255, "compression_ratio": 1.5357142857142858, "no_speech_prob": 1.415523622795567e-12}, {"id": 550, "seek": 205108, "start": 2051.08, "end": 2051.68, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 50395], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 551, "seek": 205108, "start": 2052.16, "end": 2055.2999999999997, "text": " Under the partial trace, the entire family of states collapses.", "tokens": [50419, 6974, 264, 14641, 13508, 11, 264, 2302, 1605, 295, 4368, 48765, 13, 50576], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 552, "seek": 205108, "start": 2056.0, "end": 2060.4, "text": " If you trace out Bob, Alice's particle looks like the maximally mixed state.", "tokens": [50611, 759, 291, 13508, 484, 6085, 11, 16004, 311, 12359, 1542, 411, 264, 5138, 379, 7467, 1785, 13, 50831], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 553, "seek": 205108, "start": 2060.7, "end": 2062.92, "text": " It looks like pure, structureless, random noise.", "tokens": [50846, 467, 1542, 411, 6075, 11, 3877, 1832, 11, 4974, 5658, 13, 50957], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 554, "seek": 205108, "start": 2063.2, "end": 2066.54, "text": " If you trace out Alice, Bob sees the exact same thing.", "tokens": [50971, 759, 291, 13508, 484, 16004, 11, 6085, 8194, 264, 1900, 912, 551, 13, 51138], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 555, "seek": 205108, "start": 2067.06, "end": 2068.42, "text": " Pure, random noise.", "tokens": [51164, 29474, 11, 4974, 5658, 13, 51232], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 556, "seek": 205108, "start": 2069.18, "end": 2075.3199999999997, "text": " The global state could be changing wildly, traversing this whole interval from C equals negative one to one.", "tokens": [51270, 440, 4338, 1785, 727, 312, 4473, 34731, 11, 23149, 278, 341, 1379, 15035, 490, 383, 6915, 3671, 472, 281, 472, 13, 51577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 557, "seek": 205108, "start": 2075.3199999999997, "end": 2080.04, "text": " And Alice and Bob, looking only at their local dials, would notice absolutely nothing changing.", "tokens": [51577, 400, 16004, 293, 6085, 11, 1237, 787, 412, 641, 2654, 5502, 82, 11, 576, 3449, 3122, 1825, 4473, 13, 51813], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17187963905981032, "compression_ratio": 1.7, "no_speech_prob": 2.0280233655606272e-12}, {"id": 558, "seek": 208004, "start": 2080.04, "end": 2081.94, "text": " Wait a minute. I'm going to make sure I'm fully grasping this.", "tokens": [50365, 3802, 257, 3456, 13, 286, 478, 516, 281, 652, 988, 286, 478, 4498, 29444, 3381, 341, 13, 50460], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 559, "seek": 208004, "start": 2082.02, "end": 2087.56, "text": " You are describing a physical state where the global system holds specific structured information, the C coefficient.", "tokens": [50464, 509, 366, 16141, 257, 4001, 1785, 689, 264, 4338, 1185, 9190, 2685, 18519, 1589, 11, 264, 383, 17619, 13, 50741], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 560, "seek": 208004, "start": 2087.82, "end": 2092.08, "text": " But when you look at the local parts, they look like pure, correlated, random noise.", "tokens": [50754, 583, 562, 291, 574, 412, 264, 2654, 3166, 11, 436, 574, 411, 6075, 11, 38574, 11, 4974, 5658, 13, 50967], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 561, "seek": 208004, "start": 2092.18, "end": 2093.82, "text": " Yeah. I mean, that is exactly what I'm describing.", "tokens": [50972, 865, 13, 286, 914, 11, 300, 307, 2293, 437, 286, 478, 16141, 13, 51054], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 562, "seek": 208004, "start": 2094.08, "end": 2098.1, "text": " But that is the literal textbook definition of quantum entanglement.", "tokens": [51067, 583, 300, 307, 264, 20411, 25591, 7123, 295, 13018, 948, 656, 3054, 13, 51268], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 563, "seek": 208004, "start": 2098.8, "end": 2100.1, "text": " Spooky action at a distance.", "tokens": [51303, 1738, 24091, 3069, 412, 257, 4560, 13, 51368], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 564, "seek": 208004, "start": 2100.1, "end": 2102.9, "text": " You've arrived at the grand realization of Chapter 12.", "tokens": [51368, 509, 600, 6678, 412, 264, 2697, 25138, 295, 18874, 2272, 13, 51508], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 565, "seek": 208004, "start": 2104.06, "end": 2107.84, "text": " That hidden, real direction, that J tensor J, is entanglement.", "tokens": [51566, 663, 7633, 11, 957, 3513, 11, 300, 508, 40863, 508, 11, 307, 948, 656, 3054, 13, 51755], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1453387611790707, "compression_ratio": 1.6729559748427674, "no_speech_prob": 2.0047149704160994e-12}, {"id": 566, "seek": 210784, "start": 2107.84, "end": 2110.76, "text": " It's the mathematical mechanism of non-locality.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 264, 18894, 7513, 295, 2107, 12, 5842, 1860, 13, 50511], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12993248118910677, "compression_ratio": 1.6206896551724137, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012438991587884e-12}, {"id": 567, "seek": 210784, "start": 2111.6200000000003, "end": 2115.26, "text": " But here is the massive philosophical reveal that fliction drops.", "tokens": [50554, 583, 510, 307, 264, 5994, 25066, 10658, 300, 932, 4105, 11438, 13, 50736], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12993248118910677, "compression_ratio": 1.6206896551724137, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012438991587884e-12}, {"id": 568, "seek": 210784, "start": 2116.2000000000003, "end": 2122.76, "text": " Separability, which is the opposite of entanglement, meaning the state is just a boring classical mixture, is field relative.", "tokens": [50783, 43480, 2310, 11, 597, 307, 264, 6182, 295, 948, 656, 3054, 11, 3620, 264, 1785, 307, 445, 257, 9989, 13735, 9925, 11, 307, 2519, 4972, 13, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12993248118910677, "compression_ratio": 1.6206896551724137, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012438991587884e-12}, {"id": 569, "seek": 210784, "start": 2123.2200000000003, "end": 2130.2400000000002, "text": " Field relative, meaning whether two particles are entangled or not, depends entirely on what number system you're using to do the math.", "tokens": [51134, 17952, 4972, 11, 3620, 1968, 732, 10007, 366, 948, 39101, 420, 406, 11, 5946, 7696, 322, 437, 1230, 1185, 291, 434, 1228, 281, 360, 264, 5221, 13, 51485], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12993248118910677, "compression_ratio": 1.6206896551724137, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012438991587884e-12}, {"id": 570, "seek": 213024, "start": 2130.24, "end": 2140.18, "text": " Yes. The manuscript proves that this exact same family of physical states, this row of C, is mathematically entangled if you are forcing the universe to use real numbers.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 440, 23928, 25019, 300, 341, 1900, 912, 1605, 295, 4001, 4368, 11, 341, 5386, 295, 383, 11, 307, 44003, 948, 39101, 498, 291, 366, 19030, 264, 6445, 281, 764, 957, 3547, 13, 50862], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06719921747843424, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 2.707132261414724e-12}, {"id": 571, "seek": 213024, "start": 2140.7, "end": 2150.3199999999997, "text": " Because that J tensor J direction cannot be built out of valid local real states, you are forced to invoke spooky non-local correlations to explain the hidden tenth dial.", "tokens": [50888, 1436, 300, 508, 40863, 508, 3513, 2644, 312, 3094, 484, 295, 7363, 2654, 957, 4368, 11, 291, 366, 7579, 281, 41117, 30510, 2107, 12, 5842, 304, 13983, 763, 281, 2903, 264, 7633, 27269, 5502, 13, 51369], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06719921747843424, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 2.707132261414724e-12}, {"id": 572, "seek": 215032, "start": 2150.32, "end": 2162.5, "text": " But if you take that exact same physical state matrix with the exact same data and you analyze it using complex numbers, which have more internal degrees of freedom because of the imaginary I am, the state becomes completely separable.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 498, 291, 747, 300, 1900, 912, 4001, 1785, 8141, 365, 264, 1900, 912, 1412, 293, 291, 12477, 309, 1228, 3997, 3547, 11, 597, 362, 544, 6920, 5310, 295, 5645, 570, 295, 264, 26164, 286, 669, 11, 264, 1785, 3643, 2584, 3128, 712, 13, 50974], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10128418403335765, "compression_ratio": 1.5353982300884956, "no_speech_prob": 1.8317962164476898e-12}, {"id": 573, "seek": 215032, "start": 2162.6000000000004, "end": 2162.98, "text": " Wait, what?", "tokens": [50979, 3802, 11, 437, 30, 50998], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10128418403335765, "compression_ratio": 1.5353982300884956, "no_speech_prob": 1.8317962164476898e-12}, {"id": 574, "seek": 215032, "start": 2163.32, "end": 2164.6000000000004, "text": " It is no longer entangled.", "tokens": [51015, 467, 307, 572, 2854, 948, 39101, 13, 51079], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10128418403335765, "compression_ratio": 1.5353982300884956, "no_speech_prob": 1.8317962164476898e-12}, {"id": 575, "seek": 215032, "start": 2165.2000000000003, "end": 2169.38, "text": " It could be perfectly described as a classical, local mixture of states.", "tokens": [51109, 467, 727, 312, 6239, 7619, 382, 257, 13735, 11, 2654, 9925, 295, 4368, 13, 51318], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10128418403335765, "compression_ratio": 1.5353982300884956, "no_speech_prob": 1.8317962164476898e-12}, {"id": 576, "seek": 216938, "start": 2169.38, "end": 2171.28, "text": " Consider my mind officially blown.", "tokens": [50365, 17416, 452, 1575, 12053, 16479, 13, 50460], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342136768379597, "compression_ratio": 1.6962962962962962, "no_speech_prob": 1.489800578909073e-12}, {"id": 577, "seek": 216938, "start": 2171.84, "end": 2179.7000000000003, "text": " Are you saying that entanglement, the thing that Einstein hated, the thing that quantum computers rely on, might just be an artifact of using the wrong math?", "tokens": [50488, 2014, 291, 1566, 300, 948, 656, 3054, 11, 264, 551, 300, 23486, 17398, 11, 264, 551, 300, 13018, 10807, 10687, 322, 11, 1062, 445, 312, 364, 34806, 295, 1228, 264, 2085, 5221, 30, 50881], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342136768379597, "compression_ratio": 1.6962962962962962, "no_speech_prob": 1.489800578909073e-12}, {"id": 578, "seek": 216938, "start": 2180.1800000000003, "end": 2187.2000000000003, "text": " Like if I put on real number goggles, two particles look deeply, fundamentally entangled across space.", "tokens": [50905, 1743, 498, 286, 829, 322, 957, 1230, 39808, 11, 732, 10007, 574, 8760, 11, 17879, 948, 39101, 2108, 1901, 13, 51256], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342136768379597, "compression_ratio": 1.6962962962962962, "no_speech_prob": 1.489800578909073e-12}, {"id": 579, "seek": 216938, "start": 2187.54, "end": 2196.62, "text": " But if I take those off and swap them for complex number goggles, those exact same particles just look like two completely normal, classically correlated objects.", "tokens": [51273, 583, 498, 286, 747, 729, 766, 293, 18135, 552, 337, 3997, 1230, 39808, 11, 729, 1900, 912, 10007, 445, 574, 411, 732, 2584, 2710, 11, 1508, 984, 38574, 6565, 13, 51727], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09342136768379597, "compression_ratio": 1.6962962962962962, "no_speech_prob": 1.489800578909073e-12}, {"id": 580, "seek": 219662, "start": 2196.62, "end": 2200.66, "text": " That is exactly what the math demonstrates for this specific family of states.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 2293, 437, 264, 5221, 31034, 337, 341, 2685, 1605, 295, 4368, 13, 50567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 581, "seek": 219662, "start": 2200.98, "end": 2203.0, "text": " Now, we ought to be very careful not to overstate this.", "tokens": [50583, 823, 11, 321, 13416, 281, 312, 588, 5026, 406, 281, 670, 15406, 341, 13, 50684], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 582, "seek": 219662, "start": 2203.1, "end": 2207.2999999999997, "text": " The manuscript is not claiming that all entanglement in our universe is an illusion.", "tokens": [50689, 440, 23928, 307, 406, 19232, 300, 439, 948, 656, 3054, 294, 527, 6445, 307, 364, 18854, 13, 50899], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 583, "seek": 219662, "start": 2208.18, "end": 2215.72, "text": " Standard complex quantum mechanics has plenty of states, like the famous bell states, that are genuinely entangled even when you use complex numbers.", "tokens": [50943, 21298, 3997, 13018, 12939, 575, 7140, 295, 4368, 11, 411, 264, 4618, 4549, 4368, 11, 300, 366, 17839, 948, 39101, 754, 562, 291, 764, 3997, 3547, 13, 51320], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 584, "seek": 219662, "start": 2215.92, "end": 2217.54, "text": " Right. I mean, quantum computers still work.", "tokens": [51330, 1779, 13, 286, 914, 11, 13018, 10807, 920, 589, 13, 51411], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 585, "seek": 219662, "start": 2217.88, "end": 2218.4, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51428, 7587, 13, 51454], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 586, "seek": 219662, "start": 2218.4, "end": 2226.0, "text": " What this proves is how deeply the concept of entanglement is tethered to the mathematical field you are using to describe the local systems.", "tokens": [51454, 708, 341, 25019, 307, 577, 8760, 264, 3410, 295, 948, 656, 3054, 307, 256, 1666, 292, 281, 264, 18894, 2519, 291, 366, 1228, 281, 6786, 264, 2654, 3652, 13, 51834], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09785047918558121, "compression_ratio": 1.7173252279635258, "no_speech_prob": 1.427269001770537e-12}, {"id": 587, "seek": 222662, "start": 2226.62, "end": 2233.58, "text": " Entanglement is not always an intrinsic, objective property of a physical state matrix floating out in the ether.", "tokens": [50365, 3951, 656, 3054, 307, 406, 1009, 364, 35698, 11, 10024, 4707, 295, 257, 4001, 1785, 8141, 12607, 484, 294, 264, 37096, 13, 50713], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1301870346069336, "compression_ratio": 1.6525821596244132, "no_speech_prob": 1.4160363420029354e-12}, {"id": 588, "seek": 222662, "start": 2234.12, "end": 2237.68, "text": " It is a property of the state relative to the rules of your theory.", "tokens": [50740, 467, 307, 257, 4707, 295, 264, 1785, 4972, 281, 264, 4474, 295, 428, 5261, 13, 50918], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1301870346069336, "compression_ratio": 1.6525821596244132, "no_speech_prob": 1.4160363420029354e-12}, {"id": 589, "seek": 222662, "start": 2237.8599999999997, "end": 2238.58, "text": " That makes sense.", "tokens": [50927, 663, 1669, 2020, 13, 50963], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1301870346069336, "compression_ratio": 1.6525821596244132, "no_speech_prob": 1.4160363420029354e-12}, {"id": 590, "seek": 222662, "start": 2238.94, "end": 2246.8399999999997, "text": " If your theory doesn't have enough local parameters, like the real numbers, the math will essentially invent entanglement to hide the extra information.", "tokens": [50981, 759, 428, 5261, 1177, 380, 362, 1547, 2654, 9834, 11, 411, 264, 957, 3547, 11, 264, 5221, 486, 4476, 7962, 948, 656, 3054, 281, 6479, 264, 2857, 1589, 13, 51376], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1301870346069336, "compression_ratio": 1.6525821596244132, "no_speech_prob": 1.4160363420029354e-12}, {"id": 591, "seek": 224684, "start": 2246.84, "end": 2250.1800000000003, "text": " This is an incredible structural insight.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 307, 364, 4651, 15067, 11269, 13, 50532], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439540676954316, "compression_ratio": 1.4978902953586497, "no_speech_prob": 1.898040969186554e-12}, {"id": 592, "seek": 224684, "start": 2250.4, "end": 2259.32, "text": " We have built this towering mathematical architecture, found these wild, hidden cryptographic variables, and watched entanglement shift based on our goggles.", "tokens": [50543, 492, 362, 3094, 341, 10567, 278, 18894, 9482, 11, 1352, 613, 4868, 11, 7633, 9844, 12295, 9102, 11, 293, 6337, 948, 656, 3054, 5513, 2361, 322, 527, 39808, 13, 50989], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439540676954316, "compression_ratio": 1.4978902953586497, "no_speech_prob": 1.898040969186554e-12}, {"id": 593, "seek": 224684, "start": 2259.7400000000002, "end": 2262.2000000000003, "text": " And we haven't even assumed standard quantum mechanics yet.", "tokens": [51010, 400, 321, 2378, 380, 754, 15895, 3832, 13018, 12939, 1939, 13, 51133], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439540676954316, "compression_ratio": 1.4978902953586497, "no_speech_prob": 1.898040969186554e-12}, {"id": 594, "seek": 224684, "start": 2262.26, "end": 2263.7400000000002, "text": " We just follow the rules of probability.", "tokens": [51136, 492, 445, 1524, 264, 4474, 295, 8482, 13, 51210], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439540676954316, "compression_ratio": 1.4978902953586497, "no_speech_prob": 1.898040969186554e-12}, {"id": 595, "seek": 224684, "start": 2264.2000000000003, "end": 2267.6400000000003, "text": " But now, in section six, we have to cash in our chips.", "tokens": [51233, 583, 586, 11, 294, 3541, 2309, 11, 321, 362, 281, 6388, 294, 527, 11583, 13, 51405], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1439540676954316, "compression_ratio": 1.4978902953586497, "no_speech_prob": 1.898040969186554e-12}, {"id": 596, "seek": 226764, "start": 2267.64, "end": 2271.54, "text": " Yes. This brings us to part three of the book, The Recovered Structure.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 639, 5607, 505, 281, 644, 1045, 295, 264, 1446, 11, 440, 1300, 12516, 292, 745, 2885, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11547186544963292, "compression_ratio": 1.688135593220339, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171630007333349e-12}, {"id": 597, "seek": 226764, "start": 2271.7799999999997, "end": 2280.12, "text": " It's time to see what it actually costs to derive the textbook formulas that physicists use every day, because fliction is incredibly rigorous about auditing assumptions.", "tokens": [50572, 467, 311, 565, 281, 536, 437, 309, 767, 5497, 281, 28446, 264, 25591, 30546, 300, 48716, 764, 633, 786, 11, 570, 932, 4105, 307, 6252, 29882, 466, 2379, 1748, 17695, 13, 50989], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11547186544963292, "compression_ratio": 1.688135593220339, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171630007333349e-12}, {"id": 598, "seek": 226764, "start": 2280.7, "end": 2285.68, "text": " It is so easy in theoretical physics to accidentally smuggle in the answer you want without realizing it.", "tokens": [51018, 467, 307, 370, 1858, 294, 20864, 10649, 281, 15715, 899, 31726, 294, 264, 1867, 291, 528, 1553, 16734, 309, 13, 51267], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11547186544963292, "compression_ratio": 1.688135593220339, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171630007333349e-12}, {"id": 599, "seek": 226764, "start": 2285.8799999999997, "end": 2288.96, "text": " It's the most common trap in foundational physics, actually.", "tokens": [51277, 467, 311, 264, 881, 2689, 11487, 294, 32195, 10649, 11, 767, 13, 51431], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11547186544963292, "compression_ratio": 1.688135593220339, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171630007333349e-12}, {"id": 600, "seek": 226764, "start": 2289.46, "end": 2294.02, "text": " You try to derive quantum mechanics, but you accidentally assume half of it on page one.", "tokens": [51456, 509, 853, 281, 28446, 13018, 12939, 11, 457, 291, 15715, 6552, 1922, 295, 309, 322, 3028, 472, 13, 51684], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11547186544963292, "compression_ratio": 1.688135593220339, "no_speech_prob": 1.6171630007333349e-12}, {"id": 601, "seek": 229402, "start": 2294.02, "end": 2298.4, "text": " Chapter 18 of this manuscript is dedicated to the Assumption Audit.", "tokens": [50365, 18874, 2443, 295, 341, 23928, 307, 8374, 281, 264, 6281, 449, 1695, 8821, 270, 13, 50584], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 602, "seek": 229402, "start": 2298.94, "end": 2310.28, "text": " It literally collects every single explicitly flagged assumption required to get from our bare classical probability simplex to a modern graduate-level physics textbook.", "tokens": [50611, 467, 3736, 39897, 633, 2167, 20803, 7166, 3004, 15302, 4739, 281, 483, 490, 527, 6949, 13735, 8482, 2199, 87, 281, 257, 4363, 8080, 12, 12418, 10649, 25591, 13, 51178], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 603, "seek": 229402, "start": 2310.7599999999998, "end": 2312.78, "text": " And the shocking part is that there are only four of them.", "tokens": [51202, 400, 264, 18776, 644, 307, 300, 456, 366, 787, 1451, 295, 552, 13, 51303], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 604, "seek": 229402, "start": 2313.2, "end": 2315.7, "text": " Four basic costs to earn the entire textbook.", "tokens": [51324, 7451, 3875, 5497, 281, 6012, 264, 2302, 25591, 13, 51449], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 605, "seek": 229402, "start": 2316.2599999999998, "end": 2320.66, "text": " Let's walk the listener through these, one by one, because this is where the magic trick is revealed.", "tokens": [51477, 961, 311, 1792, 264, 31569, 807, 613, 11, 472, 538, 472, 11, 570, 341, 307, 689, 264, 5585, 4282, 307, 9599, 13, 51697], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 606, "seek": 229402, "start": 2321.08, "end": 2323.58, "text": " Assumption one, we have already spent a lot of time on.", "tokens": [51718, 6281, 449, 1695, 472, 11, 321, 362, 1217, 4418, 257, 688, 295, 565, 322, 13, 51843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1597764554231063, "compression_ratio": 1.6233766233766234, "no_speech_prob": 1.4668408631832164e-12}, {"id": 607, "seek": 232402, "start": 2324.02, "end": 2325.04, "text": " Local tomography.", "tokens": [50365, 22755, 2916, 5820, 13, 50416], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 608, "seek": 232402, "start": 2325.82, "end": 2329.7, "text": " The assumption that a joint state is perfectly described by local tests.", "tokens": [50455, 440, 15302, 300, 257, 7225, 1785, 307, 6239, 7619, 538, 2654, 6921, 13, 50649], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 609, "seek": 232402, "start": 2330.32, "end": 2334.96, "text": " We saw that if you demand this principle, it brutally filters out the real numbers and the quaternions,", "tokens": [50680, 492, 1866, 300, 498, 291, 4733, 341, 8665, 11, 309, 48476, 15995, 484, 264, 957, 3547, 293, 264, 421, 771, 77, 626, 11, 50912], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 610, "seek": 232402, "start": 2335.22, "end": 2339.7, "text": " and selects complex numbers as the unique Goldilocks fit for our state spaces.", "tokens": [50925, 293, 3048, 82, 3997, 3547, 382, 264, 3845, 6731, 388, 35507, 3318, 337, 527, 1785, 7673, 13, 51149], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 611, "seek": 232402, "start": 2340.06, "end": 2343.38, "text": " Okay, one assumption down, and we have complex vector spaces.", "tokens": [51167, 1033, 11, 472, 15302, 760, 11, 293, 321, 362, 3997, 8062, 7673, 13, 51333], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 612, "seek": 232402, "start": 2343.56, "end": 2344.48, "text": " What is Assumption two?", "tokens": [51342, 708, 307, 6281, 449, 1695, 732, 30, 51388], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 613, "seek": 232402, "start": 2344.68, "end": 2349.5, "text": " Assumption two is that pure states are operationally representable as frame-completable vectors.", "tokens": [51398, 6281, 449, 1695, 732, 307, 300, 6075, 4368, 366, 6916, 379, 2906, 712, 382, 3920, 12, 1112, 14657, 712, 18875, 13, 51639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17818716952675268, "compression_ratio": 1.7338403041825095, "no_speech_prob": 1.721180381379639e-12}, {"id": 614, "seek": 234950, "start": 2349.5, "end": 2351.62, "text": " Uh, that sounds horribly dense.", "tokens": [50365, 4019, 11, 300, 3263, 45028, 18011, 13, 50471], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 615, "seek": 234950, "start": 2351.7, "end": 2352.7, "text": " Can you break that down for me?", "tokens": [50475, 1664, 291, 1821, 300, 760, 337, 385, 30, 50525], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 616, "seek": 234950, "start": 2352.92, "end": 2354.34, "text": " It's operationally very simple.", "tokens": [50536, 467, 311, 6916, 379, 588, 2199, 13, 50607], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 617, "seek": 234950, "start": 2354.98, "end": 2360.76, "text": " In part one, we talked about contexts or frames, which are just entire sets of mutually exclusive outcomes,", "tokens": [50639, 682, 644, 472, 11, 321, 2825, 466, 30628, 420, 12083, 11, 597, 366, 445, 2302, 6352, 295, 39144, 13005, 10070, 11, 50928], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 618, "seek": 234950, "start": 2361.02, "end": 2364.96, "text": " like the six faces of a die or a full orthogonal basis in linear algebra.", "tokens": [50941, 411, 264, 2309, 8475, 295, 257, 978, 420, 257, 1577, 41488, 5143, 294, 8213, 21989, 13, 51138], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 619, "seek": 234950, "start": 2365.18, "end": 2365.3, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51149, 1779, 13, 51155], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18147253672281902, "compression_ratio": 1.4343434343434343, "no_speech_prob": 1.2992366514238962e-12}, {"id": 620, "seek": 236530, "start": 2365.3, "end": 2371.34, "text": " Assumption two just says that if you prepare a single, absolutely pure state of certainty in your lab,", "tokens": [50365, 6281, 449, 1695, 732, 445, 1619, 300, 498, 291, 5940, 257, 2167, 11, 3122, 6075, 1785, 295, 27022, 294, 428, 2715, 11, 50667], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.093100677217756, "compression_ratio": 1.5027027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.215950517358122e-12}, {"id": 621, "seek": 236530, "start": 2371.48, "end": 2376.76, "text": " we can mathematically treat it as one specific arrow, one vector belonging to one of those full frames.", "tokens": [50674, 321, 393, 44003, 2387, 309, 382, 472, 2685, 11610, 11, 472, 8062, 22957, 281, 472, 295, 729, 1577, 12083, 13, 50938], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.093100677217756, "compression_ratio": 1.5027027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.215950517358122e-12}, {"id": 622, "seek": 236530, "start": 2376.92, "end": 2379.26, "text": " Okay, so a state is just an arrow in a framework.", "tokens": [50946, 1033, 11, 370, 257, 1785, 307, 445, 364, 11610, 294, 257, 8388, 13, 51063], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.093100677217756, "compression_ratio": 1.5027027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.215950517358122e-12}, {"id": 623, "seek": 236530, "start": 2379.5, "end": 2380.48, "text": " Why is that powerful?", "tokens": [51075, 1545, 307, 300, 4005, 30, 51124], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.093100677217756, "compression_ratio": 1.5027027027027027, "no_speech_prob": 1.215950517358122e-12}, {"id": 624, "seek": 238048, "start": 2380.48, "end": 2387.84, "text": " Because once you allow that simple geometric mapping, the famous Born rule literally falls out of the math for free.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 1564, 291, 2089, 300, 2199, 33246, 18350, 11, 264, 4618, 29808, 4978, 3736, 8804, 484, 295, 264, 5221, 337, 1737, 13, 50733], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 625, "seek": 238048, "start": 2388.2, "end": 2394.44, "text": " The Born rule being the rule that says probability is the amplitude squared, the thing Max Born won a Nobel Prize for.", "tokens": [50751, 440, 29808, 4978, 885, 264, 4978, 300, 1619, 8482, 307, 264, 27433, 8889, 11, 264, 551, 7402, 29808, 1582, 257, 24611, 22604, 337, 13, 51063], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 626, "seek": 238048, "start": 2394.6, "end": 2395.76, "text": " That exact rule.", "tokens": [51071, 663, 1900, 4978, 13, 51129], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 627, "seek": 238048, "start": 2396.34, "end": 2398.76, "text": " In most textbooks, the Born rule is a postulate.", "tokens": [51158, 682, 881, 33587, 11, 264, 29808, 4978, 307, 257, 2183, 5256, 13, 51279], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 628, "seek": 238048, "start": 2399.08, "end": 2400.6, "text": " It's just handed down from on high.", "tokens": [51295, 467, 311, 445, 16013, 760, 490, 322, 1090, 13, 51371], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 629, "seek": 238048, "start": 2400.84, "end": 2402.34, "text": " That should square the amplitude.", "tokens": [51383, 663, 820, 3732, 264, 27433, 13, 51458], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 630, "seek": 238048, "start": 2402.62, "end": 2402.82, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51472, 865, 13, 51482], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 631, "seek": 238048, "start": 2402.94, "end": 2408.56, "text": " But fliction shows that if states are frame-completable vectors, the Born rule is mathematically inevitable.", "tokens": [51488, 583, 932, 4105, 3110, 300, 498, 4368, 366, 3920, 12, 1112, 14657, 712, 18875, 11, 264, 29808, 4978, 307, 44003, 21451, 13, 51769], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1498109673251625, "compression_ratio": 1.6993006993006994, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396326129099805e-12}, {"id": 632, "seek": 240856, "start": 2408.56, "end": 2417.96, "text": " It is mathematically identical to extracting a single column from the un-stochastic transition matrices we proved must exist back in section two.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 44003, 14800, 281, 49844, 257, 2167, 7738, 490, 264, 517, 12, 372, 8997, 2750, 6034, 32284, 321, 14617, 1633, 2514, 646, 294, 3541, 732, 13, 50835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 633, "seek": 240856, "start": 2418.58, "end": 2420.48, "text": " You don't have to postulate the Born rule.", "tokens": [50866, 509, 500, 380, 362, 281, 2183, 5256, 264, 29808, 4978, 13, 50961], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 634, "seek": 240856, "start": 2420.92, "end": 2424.4, "text": " It is a structural consequence of classical probability limits.", "tokens": [50983, 467, 307, 257, 15067, 18326, 295, 13735, 8482, 10406, 13, 51157], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 635, "seek": 240856, "start": 2424.54, "end": 2425.46, "text": " That is incredible.", "tokens": [51164, 663, 307, 4651, 13, 51210], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 636, "seek": 240856, "start": 2426.0, "end": 2427.02, "text": " Okay, assumption three.", "tokens": [51237, 1033, 11, 15302, 1045, 13, 51288], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 637, "seek": 240856, "start": 2428.18, "end": 2430.62, "text": " Measurement outcomes are labeled by real numbers.", "tokens": [51346, 41436, 518, 10070, 366, 21335, 538, 957, 3547, 13, 51468], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 638, "seek": 240856, "start": 2431.34, "end": 2434.68, "text": " This is largely a modeling choice about how human beings do science.", "tokens": [51504, 639, 307, 11611, 257, 15983, 3922, 466, 577, 1952, 8958, 360, 3497, 13, 51671], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1790589182000411, "compression_ratio": 1.5427509293680297, "no_speech_prob": 1.335251353928868e-12}, {"id": 639, "seek": 243468, "start": 2434.68, "end": 2442.46, "text": " When we measure something in a lab, the position of a particle, its momentum, its spin, we don't read imaginary numbers off the digital screen.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 321, 3481, 746, 294, 257, 2715, 11, 264, 2535, 295, 257, 12359, 11, 1080, 11244, 11, 1080, 6060, 11, 321, 500, 380, 1401, 26164, 3547, 766, 264, 4562, 2568, 13, 50754], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 640, "seek": 243468, "start": 2442.6, "end": 2446.3399999999997, "text": " The screen prints out a real number, five meters per second or plus one half spin.", "tokens": [50761, 440, 2568, 22305, 484, 257, 957, 1230, 11, 1732, 8146, 680, 1150, 420, 1804, 472, 1922, 6060, 13, 50948], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 641, "seek": 243468, "start": 2446.48, "end": 2446.64, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50955, 1779, 13, 50963], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 642, "seek": 243468, "start": 2446.7599999999998, "end": 2448.94, "text": " Our rulers are made of real numbers.", "tokens": [50969, 2621, 35009, 366, 1027, 295, 957, 3547, 13, 51078], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 643, "seek": 243468, "start": 2449.08, "end": 2456.3799999999997, "text": " If you formally assume that outcomes must be real numbers, and you combine that with the sharp projective geometry of the probability frames we just built,", "tokens": [51085, 759, 291, 25983, 6552, 300, 10070, 1633, 312, 957, 3547, 11, 293, 291, 10432, 300, 365, 264, 8199, 1716, 488, 18426, 295, 264, 8482, 12083, 321, 445, 3094, 11, 51450], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 644, "seek": 243468, "start": 2456.74, "end": 2460.46, "text": " you mathematically force all quantum observables to be Hermitian operators.", "tokens": [51468, 291, 44003, 3464, 439, 13018, 9951, 2965, 281, 312, 21842, 270, 952, 19077, 13, 51654], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 645, "seek": 243468, "start": 2460.6, "end": 2461.8199999999997, "text": " Another piece of heavy jargon.", "tokens": [51661, 3996, 2522, 295, 4676, 15181, 10660, 13, 51722], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08888742828369141, "compression_ratio": 1.65527950310559, "no_speech_prob": 1.7011255685345228e-12}, {"id": 646, "seek": 246182, "start": 2461.82, "end": 2466.5, "text": " A Hermitian operator is just a matrix that has entirely real Eakin values.", "tokens": [50365, 316, 21842, 270, 952, 12973, 307, 445, 257, 8141, 300, 575, 7696, 957, 462, 12709, 4190, 13, 50599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 647, "seek": 246182, "start": 2466.86, "end": 2469.6200000000003, "text": " It's the standard tool for observables in quantum math.", "tokens": [50617, 467, 311, 264, 3832, 2290, 337, 9951, 2965, 294, 13018, 5221, 13, 50755], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 648, "seek": 246182, "start": 2470.36, "end": 2473.6400000000003, "text": " Again, textbooks postulate that observables are Hermitian.", "tokens": [50792, 3764, 11, 33587, 2183, 5256, 300, 9951, 2965, 366, 21842, 270, 952, 13, 50956], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 649, "seek": 246182, "start": 2474.1600000000003, "end": 2479.46, "text": " This manuscript proves that if your ruler reads real numbers, the operator must be Hermitian.", "tokens": [50982, 639, 23928, 25019, 300, 498, 428, 19661, 15700, 957, 3547, 11, 264, 12973, 1633, 312, 21842, 270, 952, 13, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 650, "seek": 246182, "start": 2480.2000000000003, "end": 2481.88, "text": " It's an inevitable equivalence.", "tokens": [51284, 467, 311, 364, 21451, 9052, 655, 13, 51368], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 651, "seek": 246182, "start": 2482.2400000000002, "end": 2484.4, "text": " Which brings us to the final piece of the puzzle.", "tokens": [51386, 3013, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 2572, 2522, 295, 264, 12805, 13, 51494], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 652, "seek": 246182, "start": 2485.02, "end": 2485.78, "text": " Assumption four.", "tokens": [51525, 6281, 449, 1695, 1451, 13, 51563], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16588123321533202, "compression_ratio": 1.5916666666666666, "no_speech_prob": 1.5132597865757935e-12}, {"id": 653, "seek": 248578, "start": 2485.78, "end": 2490.0600000000004, "text": " Up until now, everything we've talked about has been a static snapshot.", "tokens": [50365, 5858, 1826, 586, 11, 1203, 321, 600, 2825, 466, 575, 668, 257, 13437, 30163, 13, 50579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1574545456812932, "compression_ratio": 1.3448275862068966, "no_speech_prob": 1.675021124407372e-12}, {"id": 654, "seek": 248578, "start": 2490.6400000000003, "end": 2491.7400000000002, "text": " An arrow in a frame.", "tokens": [50608, 1107, 11610, 294, 257, 3920, 13, 50663], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1574545456812932, "compression_ratio": 1.3448275862068966, "no_speech_prob": 1.675021124407372e-12}, {"id": 655, "seek": 248578, "start": 2492.1600000000003, "end": 2493.2400000000002, "text": " But the universe moves.", "tokens": [50684, 583, 264, 6445, 6067, 13, 50738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1574545456812932, "compression_ratio": 1.3448275862068966, "no_speech_prob": 1.675021124407372e-12}, {"id": 656, "seek": 248578, "start": 2494.1200000000003, "end": 2498.28, "text": " Assumption four is that time evolution is a strongly continuous unitary group.", "tokens": [50782, 6281, 449, 1695, 1451, 307, 300, 565, 9303, 307, 257, 10613, 10957, 517, 4109, 1594, 13, 50990], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1574545456812932, "compression_ratio": 1.3448275862068966, "no_speech_prob": 1.675021124407372e-12}, {"id": 657, "seek": 249828, "start": 2498.28, "end": 2505.2200000000003, "text": " Let's spend some deep time unpacking that phrase, because I know what it leads to, and I want the listener to really feel the weight of it.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 3496, 512, 2452, 565, 26699, 278, 300, 9535, 11, 570, 286, 458, 437, 309, 6689, 281, 11, 293, 286, 528, 264, 31569, 281, 534, 841, 264, 3364, 295, 309, 13, 50712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 658, "seek": 249828, "start": 2505.26, "end": 2506.6000000000004, "text": " Let's break it down word by word.", "tokens": [50714, 961, 311, 1821, 309, 760, 1349, 538, 1349, 13, 50781], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 659, "seek": 249828, "start": 2506.86, "end": 2507.84, "text": " Unitary, we already know.", "tokens": [50794, 1156, 4109, 11, 321, 1217, 458, 13, 50843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 660, "seek": 249828, "start": 2508.32, "end": 2511.5800000000004, "text": " It means the transformation is perfectly balanced like our bistochastic matrices.", "tokens": [50867, 467, 1355, 264, 9887, 307, 6239, 13902, 411, 527, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 13, 51030], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 661, "seek": 249828, "start": 2512.2200000000003, "end": 2513.42, "text": " Information is never lost.", "tokens": [51062, 15357, 307, 1128, 2731, 13, 51122], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 662, "seek": 249828, "start": 2513.5400000000004, "end": 2515.92, "text": " The probabilities always balance out to 100%.", "tokens": [51128, 440, 33783, 1009, 4772, 484, 281, 2319, 6856, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 663, "seek": 249828, "start": 2515.92, "end": 2516.2200000000003, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [51247, 1033, 13, 51262], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13871164219353788, "compression_ratio": 1.5126050420168067, "no_speech_prob": 1.4892542494343575e-12}, {"id": 664, "seek": 251622, "start": 2516.22, "end": 2518.72, "text": " Continuous means time flows smoothly.", "tokens": [50365, 14674, 12549, 1355, 565, 12867, 19565, 13, 50490], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 665, "seek": 251622, "start": 2519.2799999999997, "end": 2522.62, "text": " It doesn't jump in glitchy, discrete frames like a lagging video game.", "tokens": [50518, 467, 1177, 380, 3012, 294, 23552, 88, 11, 27706, 12083, 411, 257, 8953, 3249, 960, 1216, 13, 50685], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 666, "seek": 251622, "start": 2522.7999999999997, "end": 2525.5, "text": " It evolves smoothly from one millisecond to the next.", "tokens": [50694, 467, 43737, 19565, 490, 472, 27940, 18882, 281, 264, 958, 13, 50829], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 667, "seek": 251622, "start": 2525.8199999999997, "end": 2529.04, "text": " Okay, so time is smooth and information is preserved.", "tokens": [50845, 1033, 11, 370, 565, 307, 5508, 293, 1589, 307, 22242, 13, 51006], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 668, "seek": 251622, "start": 2529.3799999999997, "end": 2532.62, "text": " That seems like the most basic definition of reality possible.", "tokens": [51023, 663, 2544, 411, 264, 881, 3875, 7123, 295, 4103, 1944, 13, 51185], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 669, "seek": 251622, "start": 2532.9399999999996, "end": 2533.3799999999997, "text": " It is.", "tokens": [51201, 467, 307, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 670, "seek": 251622, "start": 2533.7799999999997, "end": 2538.2999999999997, "text": " But if you formally assume those two properties, a continuous unitary group,", "tokens": [51243, 583, 498, 291, 25983, 6552, 729, 732, 7221, 11, 257, 10957, 517, 4109, 1594, 11, 51469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15544702718546102, "compression_ratio": 1.5714285714285714, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248835180670106e-12}, {"id": 671, "seek": 253830, "start": 2538.3, "end": 2542.32, "text": " you can apply a piece of pure mathematics called Stone's Theorem,", "tokens": [50365, 291, 393, 3079, 257, 2522, 295, 6075, 18666, 1219, 15012, 311, 440, 37956, 11, 50566], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 672, "seek": 253830, "start": 2542.82, "end": 2546.7000000000003, "text": " which deals with the generators of linear, ordinary differential equations.", "tokens": [50591, 597, 11215, 365, 264, 38662, 295, 8213, 11, 10547, 15756, 11787, 13, 50785], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 673, "seek": 253830, "start": 2547.36, "end": 2553.1800000000003, "text": " If you run that theorem on our continuous unitary evolution, a specific differential equation drops out.", "tokens": [50818, 759, 291, 1190, 300, 20904, 322, 527, 10957, 517, 4109, 9303, 11, 257, 2685, 15756, 5367, 11438, 484, 13, 51109], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 674, "seek": 253830, "start": 2553.36, "end": 2556.1200000000003, "text": " And that equation is Schrodinger's equation.", "tokens": [51118, 400, 300, 5367, 307, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 13, 51256], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 675, "seek": 253830, "start": 2556.4, "end": 2556.7400000000002, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51270, 1079, 13, 51287], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 676, "seek": 253830, "start": 2557.1400000000003, "end": 2559.54, "text": " The most famous equation in quantum mechanics.", "tokens": [51307, 440, 881, 4618, 5367, 294, 13018, 12939, 13, 51427], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 677, "seek": 253830, "start": 2559.84, "end": 2562.5800000000004, "text": " The equation that governs the wave function of the universe.", "tokens": [51442, 440, 5367, 300, 1980, 82, 264, 5772, 2445, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 51579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 678, "seek": 253830, "start": 2562.7200000000003, "end": 2563.5800000000004, "text": " Let's just pause on that.", "tokens": [51586, 961, 311, 445, 10465, 322, 300, 13, 51629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 679, "seek": 253830, "start": 2563.6400000000003, "end": 2565.1600000000003, "text": " You derive Schrodinger's equation.", "tokens": [51632, 509, 28446, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 13, 51708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414680280183492, "compression_ratio": 1.7415730337078652, "no_speech_prob": 1.1069640213148824e-12}, {"id": 680, "seek": 256516, "start": 2565.16, "end": 2569.7999999999997, "text": " Like, you don't just write it on a chalkboard as Axiom 6 and tell the grad students to memorize it.", "tokens": [50365, 1743, 11, 291, 500, 380, 445, 2464, 309, 322, 257, 28660, 3787, 382, 20118, 72, 298, 1386, 293, 980, 264, 2771, 1731, 281, 27478, 309, 13, 50597], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 681, "seek": 256516, "start": 2569.94, "end": 2570.2799999999997, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50604, 7587, 13, 50621], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 682, "seek": 256516, "start": 2570.7, "end": 2575.22, "text": " In this framework, Schrodinger's equation is just the infinitesimal mathematical consequence", "tokens": [50642, 682, 341, 8388, 11, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 307, 445, 264, 7193, 3324, 10650, 18894, 18326, 50868], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 683, "seek": 256516, "start": 2575.22, "end": 2578.96, "text": " of assuming that probabilities evolve smoothly and reversibly.", "tokens": [50868, 295, 11926, 300, 33783, 16693, 19565, 293, 14582, 3545, 13, 51055], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 684, "seek": 256516, "start": 2579.3999999999996, "end": 2582.08, "text": " It is a mathematical theorem, not a physical postulate.", "tokens": [51077, 467, 307, 257, 18894, 20904, 11, 406, 257, 4001, 2183, 5256, 13, 51211], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 685, "seek": 256516, "start": 2582.42, "end": 2585.48, "text": " It really is like reverse engineering a massive video game.", "tokens": [51228, 467, 534, 307, 411, 9943, 7043, 257, 5994, 960, 1216, 13, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 686, "seek": 256516, "start": 2585.8999999999996, "end": 2588.54, "text": " Imagine we are trying to understand how a game engine works,", "tokens": [51402, 11739, 321, 366, 1382, 281, 1223, 577, 257, 1216, 2848, 1985, 11, 51534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 687, "seek": 256516, "start": 2588.8999999999996, "end": 2592.62, "text": " but we don't have access to the source code, which is the textbook physics.", "tokens": [51552, 457, 321, 500, 380, 362, 2105, 281, 264, 4009, 3089, 11, 597, 307, 264, 25591, 10649, 13, 51738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 688, "seek": 256516, "start": 2592.68, "end": 2593.58, "text": " We just have the game.", "tokens": [51741, 492, 445, 362, 264, 1216, 13, 51786], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14019156214016587, "compression_ratio": 1.641337386018237, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014809057536938e-12}, {"id": 689, "seek": 259358, "start": 2593.58, "end": 2598.02, "text": " So we poke around, we map out the hitboxes, we test how gravity behaves,", "tokens": [50365, 407, 321, 19712, 926, 11, 321, 4471, 484, 264, 2045, 4995, 279, 11, 321, 1500, 577, 12110, 36896, 11, 50587], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 690, "seek": 259358, "start": 2598.3199999999997, "end": 2600.7999999999997, "text": " we find the invisible walls at the edge of the map.", "tokens": [50602, 321, 915, 264, 14603, 7920, 412, 264, 4691, 295, 264, 4471, 13, 50726], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 691, "seek": 259358, "start": 2601.04, "end": 2602.8199999999997, "text": " Defining the admissibility constraints.", "tokens": [50738, 9548, 1760, 264, 5910, 891, 2841, 18491, 13, 50827], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 692, "seek": 259358, "start": 2603.0, "end": 2603.2, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50836, 1779, 13, 50846], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 693, "seek": 259358, "start": 2603.5, "end": 2610.12, "text": " And from just four simple rules about those boundaries, local tomography, vectors, real rulers, smooth time,", "tokens": [50861, 400, 490, 445, 1451, 2199, 4474, 466, 729, 13180, 11, 2654, 2916, 5820, 11, 18875, 11, 957, 35009, 11, 5508, 565, 11, 51192], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 694, "seek": 259358, "start": 2610.52, "end": 2614.88, "text": " we perfectly reconstruct the entire million-line source code from scratch.", "tokens": [51212, 321, 6239, 31499, 264, 2302, 2459, 12, 1889, 4009, 3089, 490, 8459, 13, 51430], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15852567884657118, "compression_ratio": 1.5707964601769913, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674049514899369e-12}, {"id": 695, "seek": 261488, "start": 2614.88, "end": 2617.7000000000003, "text": " It is a tour de force of foundational physics.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 257, 3512, 368, 3464, 295, 32195, 10649, 13, 50506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 696, "seek": 261488, "start": 2618.1, "end": 2623.6400000000003, "text": " It strips away a century of historical baggage and shows exactly what the quantum architecture actually rests on.", "tokens": [50526, 467, 19842, 1314, 257, 4901, 295, 8584, 41567, 293, 3110, 2293, 437, 264, 13018, 9482, 767, 39755, 322, 13, 50803], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 697, "seek": 261488, "start": 2624.1600000000003, "end": 2628.28, "text": " And as a final flex, the book even derives Neymar's Dilation Theorem from scratch.", "tokens": [50829, 400, 382, 257, 2572, 5896, 11, 264, 1446, 754, 1163, 1539, 1734, 4199, 289, 311, 413, 16067, 440, 37956, 490, 8459, 13, 51035], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 698, "seek": 261488, "start": 2628.38, "end": 2629.42, "text": " What does that theorem do?", "tokens": [51040, 708, 775, 300, 20904, 360, 30, 51092], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 699, "seek": 261488, "start": 2629.76, "end": 2631.58, "text": " It deals with messy reality.", "tokens": [51109, 467, 11215, 365, 16191, 4103, 13, 51200], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 700, "seek": 261488, "start": 2632.12, "end": 2635.2000000000003, "text": " In a real lab, you rarely make perfect, sharp measurements.", "tokens": [51227, 682, 257, 957, 2715, 11, 291, 13752, 652, 2176, 11, 8199, 15383, 13, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 701, "seek": 261488, "start": 2635.54, "end": 2637.34, "text": " Your detectors have noise, they are fuzzy.", "tokens": [51398, 2260, 46866, 362, 5658, 11, 436, 366, 34710, 13, 51488], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 702, "seek": 261488, "start": 2637.7200000000003, "end": 2640.1400000000003, "text": " These are called POVMs, generalized measurements.", "tokens": [51507, 1981, 366, 1219, 22299, 53, 26386, 11, 44498, 15383, 13, 51628], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15313420253517354, "compression_ratio": 1.5322033898305085, "no_speech_prob": 1.7149477367708887e-12}, {"id": 703, "seek": 264014, "start": 2640.14, "end": 2644.7999999999997, "text": " Neymar's Theorem proves that every messy, fuzzy measurement you can possibly make", "tokens": [50365, 1734, 4199, 289, 311, 440, 37956, 25019, 300, 633, 16191, 11, 34710, 13160, 291, 393, 6264, 652, 50598], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13102880744046944, "compression_ratio": 1.5875, "no_speech_prob": 1.309145066658024e-12}, {"id": 704, "seek": 264014, "start": 2644.7999999999997, "end": 2651.06, "text": " is mathematically identical to a sharp, perfect measurement happening on a larger system that includes the environment.", "tokens": [50598, 307, 44003, 14800, 281, 257, 8199, 11, 2176, 13160, 2737, 322, 257, 4833, 1185, 300, 5974, 264, 2823, 13, 50911], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13102880744046944, "compression_ratio": 1.5875, "no_speech_prob": 1.309145066658024e-12}, {"id": 705, "seek": 264014, "start": 2651.58, "end": 2655.02, "text": " We just trace out the environment, we ignore it, and the measurement looks fuzzy to us.", "tokens": [50937, 492, 445, 13508, 484, 264, 2823, 11, 321, 11200, 309, 11, 293, 264, 13160, 1542, 34710, 281, 505, 13, 51109], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13102880744046944, "compression_ratio": 1.5875, "no_speech_prob": 1.309145066658024e-12}, {"id": 706, "seek": 264014, "start": 2655.14, "end": 2656.2999999999997, "text": " So let's take stock.", "tokens": [51115, 407, 718, 311, 747, 4127, 13, 51173], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13102880744046944, "compression_ratio": 1.5875, "no_speech_prob": 1.309145066658024e-12}, {"id": 707, "seek": 264014, "start": 2656.74, "end": 2660.44, "text": " Four assumptions give us standard, complex-numbered quantum mechanics.", "tokens": [51195, 7451, 17695, 976, 505, 3832, 11, 3997, 12, 41261, 292, 13018, 12939, 13, 51380], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13102880744046944, "compression_ratio": 1.5875, "no_speech_prob": 1.309145066658024e-12}, {"id": 708, "seek": 266044, "start": 2660.44, "end": 2664.0, "text": " We tried to use real numbers early on, and they broke on the three-way roundabout.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 3031, 281, 764, 957, 3547, 2440, 322, 11, 293, 436, 6902, 322, 264, 1045, 12, 676, 3098, 21970, 13, 50543], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 709, "seek": 266044, "start": 2664.48, "end": 2666.26, "text": " They broke on the Escher staircase cycle.", "tokens": [50567, 814, 6902, 322, 264, 2313, 6759, 35359, 6586, 13, 50656], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 710, "seek": 266044, "start": 2666.52, "end": 2670.6, "text": " They failed the local tomography dimension counting by hiding cryptographic keys.", "tokens": [50669, 814, 7612, 264, 2654, 2916, 5820, 10139, 13251, 538, 10596, 9844, 12295, 9317, 13, 50873], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 711, "seek": 266044, "start": 2671.1, "end": 2675.62, "text": " Complex numbers swooped in, saved the day, and effortlessly generated the textbook formulas.", "tokens": [50898, 41184, 3547, 13291, 27277, 294, 11, 6624, 264, 786, 11, 293, 4630, 12048, 10833, 264, 25591, 30546, 13, 51124], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 712, "seek": 266044, "start": 2675.96, "end": 2678.56, "text": " So the book proved that the universe must be complex.", "tokens": [51141, 407, 264, 1446, 14617, 300, 264, 6445, 1633, 312, 3997, 13, 51271], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 713, "seek": 266044, "start": 2679.06, "end": 2679.78, "text": " Case closed, right?", "tokens": [51296, 17791, 5395, 11, 558, 30, 51332], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 714, "seek": 266044, "start": 2680.16, "end": 2683.12, "text": " And here is where the 2026 plot twist drops.", "tokens": [51351, 400, 510, 307, 689, 264, 945, 10880, 7542, 8203, 11438, 13, 51499], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 715, "seek": 266044, "start": 2683.7400000000002, "end": 2685.88, "text": " Because if you read the preface of the manuscript,", "tokens": [51530, 1436, 498, 291, 1401, 264, 659, 2868, 295, 264, 23928, 11, 51637], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 716, "seek": 266044, "start": 2686.5, "end": 2689.98, "text": " Fliction explicitly warns the reader against taking that exact conclusion.", "tokens": [51668, 3235, 4105, 20803, 12286, 82, 264, 15149, 1970, 1940, 300, 1900, 10063, 13, 51842], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1788111889001095, "compression_ratio": 1.6484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 2.0201351442344917e-12}, {"id": 717, "seek": 268998, "start": 2690.54, "end": 2693.44, "text": " To say, this book proves the universe requires complex numbers,", "tokens": [50393, 1407, 584, 11, 341, 1446, 25019, 264, 6445, 7029, 3997, 3547, 11, 50538], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 718, "seek": 268998, "start": 2693.86, "end": 2696.84, "text": " is to claim something the author explicitly argues against.", "tokens": [50559, 307, 281, 3932, 746, 264, 3793, 20803, 38218, 1970, 13, 50708], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 719, "seek": 268998, "start": 2697.1, "end": 2698.12, "text": " Which blew my mind.", "tokens": [50721, 3013, 19075, 452, 1575, 13, 50772], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 720, "seek": 268998, "start": 2698.66, "end": 2700.18, "text": " Let's unpack this in section 7.", "tokens": [50799, 961, 311, 26699, 341, 294, 3541, 1614, 13, 50875], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 721, "seek": 268998, "start": 2700.56, "end": 2701.4, "text": " The plot twist.", "tokens": [50894, 440, 7542, 8203, 13, 50936], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 722, "seek": 268998, "start": 2702.08, "end": 2704.18, "text": " Do we actually need complex numbers?", "tokens": [50970, 1144, 321, 767, 643, 3997, 3547, 30, 51075], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 723, "seek": 268998, "start": 2704.84, "end": 2708.7, "text": " If everything we just talked about for the last hour is mathematically true,", "tokens": [51108, 759, 1203, 321, 445, 2825, 466, 337, 264, 1036, 1773, 307, 44003, 2074, 11, 51301], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 724, "seek": 268998, "start": 2709.06, "end": 2712.36, "text": " how is there possibly a loophole left for real numbers?", "tokens": [51319, 577, 307, 456, 6264, 257, 6367, 14094, 1411, 337, 957, 3547, 30, 51484], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 725, "seek": 268998, "start": 2712.36, "end": 2716.14, "text": " The loophole lies in the deepest, most invisible assumption of all.", "tokens": [51484, 440, 6367, 14094, 9134, 294, 264, 28288, 11, 881, 14603, 15302, 295, 439, 13, 51673], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 726, "seek": 268998, "start": 2717.26, "end": 2717.7400000000002, "text": " Representation.", "tokens": [51729, 19945, 399, 13, 51753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20975265846596108, "compression_ratio": 1.6007194244604317, "no_speech_prob": 2.36238134089517e-12}, {"id": 727, "seek": 271774, "start": 2717.74, "end": 2723.22, "text": " To understand it, the book introduces a concept coined by mathematician Tristan Needham", "tokens": [50365, 1407, 1223, 309, 11, 264, 1446, 31472, 257, 3410, 45222, 538, 48281, 1765, 7559, 16984, 4822, 50639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 728, "seek": 271774, "start": 2723.22, "end": 2725.02, "text": " called the amplitwist.", "tokens": [50639, 1219, 264, 9731, 270, 86, 468, 13, 50729], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 729, "seek": 271774, "start": 2725.4199999999996, "end": 2725.9399999999996, "text": " Amplitwist.", "tokens": [50749, 2012, 564, 270, 86, 468, 13, 50775], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 730, "seek": 271774, "start": 2726.0, "end": 2726.9199999999996, "text": " I love that word.", "tokens": [50778, 286, 959, 300, 1349, 13, 50824], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 731, "seek": 271774, "start": 2727.02, "end": 2729.68, "text": " It perfectly describes what is actually happening.", "tokens": [50829, 467, 6239, 15626, 437, 307, 767, 2737, 13, 50962], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 732, "seek": 271774, "start": 2730.54, "end": 2734.9399999999996, "text": " We have this mystical imaginary number e, the square root of negative 1.", "tokens": [51005, 492, 362, 341, 40565, 26164, 1230, 308, 11, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 3671, 502, 13, 51225], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 733, "seek": 271774, "start": 2735.3399999999997, "end": 2737.6, "text": " For a century, people have treated it like magic.", "tokens": [51245, 1171, 257, 4901, 11, 561, 362, 8668, 309, 411, 5585, 13, 51358], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 734, "seek": 271774, "start": 2738.06, "end": 2742.2599999999998, "text": " But what does multiplying a number by e actually do geometrically?", "tokens": [51381, 583, 437, 775, 30955, 257, 1230, 538, 308, 767, 360, 12956, 81, 984, 30, 51591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18666744232177734, "compression_ratio": 1.548780487804878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971946630543356e-12}, {"id": 735, "seek": 274226, "start": 2742.26, "end": 2746.2000000000003, "text": " It rotates a vector by 90 degrees in the complex plane, a quarter turn.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 42133, 257, 8062, 538, 4289, 5310, 294, 264, 3997, 5720, 11, 257, 6555, 1261, 13, 50562], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 736, "seek": 274226, "start": 2746.28, "end": 2746.7200000000003, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50566, 7587, 13, 50588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 737, "seek": 274226, "start": 2747.1600000000003, "end": 2748.98, "text": " Complex multiplication isn't mystical at all.", "tokens": [50610, 41184, 27290, 1943, 380, 40565, 412, 439, 13, 50701], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 738, "seek": 274226, "start": 2749.1400000000003, "end": 2752.44, "text": " It is literally just a combination of scaling the length of an arrow, the amplitude,", "tokens": [50709, 467, 307, 3736, 445, 257, 6562, 295, 21589, 264, 4641, 295, 364, 11610, 11, 264, 27433, 11, 50874], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 739, "seek": 274226, "start": 2752.82, "end": 2755.5200000000004, "text": " and rotating the arrow, the twist, amplitwist.", "tokens": [50893, 293, 19627, 264, 11610, 11, 264, 8203, 11, 9731, 270, 86, 468, 13, 51028], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 740, "seek": 274226, "start": 2755.9, "end": 2757.6000000000004, "text": " But here's the crucial realization.", "tokens": [51047, 583, 510, 311, 264, 11462, 25138, 13, 51132], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 741, "seek": 274226, "start": 2758.1000000000004, "end": 2760.44, "text": " You don't need imaginary numbers to do a quarter turn.", "tokens": [51157, 509, 500, 380, 643, 26164, 3547, 281, 360, 257, 6555, 1261, 13, 51274], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 742, "seek": 274226, "start": 2760.48, "end": 2761.94, "text": " You can just do it with real geometry.", "tokens": [51276, 509, 393, 445, 360, 309, 365, 957, 18426, 13, 51349], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1417233242708094, "compression_ratio": 1.6302521008403361, "no_speech_prob": 1.6883007578766263e-12}, {"id": 743, "seek": 276194, "start": 2761.94, "end": 2762.62, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 50399], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 744, "seek": 276194, "start": 2763.36, "end": 2769.2000000000003, "text": " You can write down a perfectly normal 2x2 matrix of completely real classical numbers", "tokens": [50436, 509, 393, 2464, 760, 257, 6239, 2710, 568, 87, 17, 8141, 295, 2584, 957, 13735, 3547, 50728], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 745, "seek": 276194, "start": 2769.2000000000003, "end": 2773.2400000000002, "text": " that takes any vector in a 2D plane and rotates it by 90 degrees.", "tokens": [50728, 300, 2516, 604, 8062, 294, 257, 568, 35, 5720, 293, 42133, 309, 538, 4289, 5310, 13, 50930], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 746, "seek": 276194, "start": 2773.78, "end": 2778.0, "text": " Specifically, it's a matrix with a 0 and a negative 1 on the top row and a 1 and a 0", "tokens": [50957, 26058, 11, 309, 311, 257, 8141, 365, 257, 1958, 293, 257, 3671, 502, 322, 264, 1192, 5386, 293, 257, 502, 293, 257, 1958, 51168], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 747, "seek": 276194, "start": 2778.0, "end": 2778.94, "text": " on the bottom row.", "tokens": [51168, 322, 264, 2767, 5386, 13, 51215], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 748, "seek": 276194, "start": 2779.02, "end": 2779.56, "text": " Wait a second.", "tokens": [51219, 3802, 257, 1150, 13, 51246], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 749, "seek": 276194, "start": 2779.8, "end": 2781.5, "text": " 0, negative 1, 1, 0.", "tokens": [51258, 1958, 11, 3671, 502, 11, 502, 11, 1958, 13, 51343], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 750, "seek": 276194, "start": 2781.6, "end": 2784.62, "text": " Is that the anti-symmetric J matrix we talked about earlier?", "tokens": [51348, 1119, 300, 264, 6061, 12, 3187, 2174, 17475, 508, 8141, 321, 2825, 466, 3071, 30, 51499], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1472676948264793, "compression_ratio": 1.5321888412017168, "no_speech_prob": 2.0285359763477784e-12}, {"id": 751, "seek": 278462, "start": 2784.62, "end": 2789.3399999999997, "text": " It is the exact same J matrix, the same matrix that caused all the trouble in our local tomography", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 264, 1900, 912, 508, 8141, 11, 264, 912, 8141, 300, 7008, 439, 264, 5253, 294, 527, 2654, 2916, 5820, 50601], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 752, "seek": 278462, "start": 2789.3399999999997, "end": 2791.94, "text": " dimension counting, the quarter turn generator.", "tokens": [50601, 10139, 13251, 11, 264, 6555, 1261, 19265, 13, 50731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 753, "seek": 278462, "start": 2792.56, "end": 2796.04, "text": " It is literally the imaginary unit eiding in a real number disguise.", "tokens": [50762, 467, 307, 3736, 264, 26164, 4985, 308, 2819, 294, 257, 957, 1230, 32751, 13, 50936], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 754, "seek": 278462, "start": 2796.64, "end": 2800.56, "text": " Everything that e does in quantum mechanics can be written entirely using real matrices", "tokens": [50966, 5471, 300, 308, 775, 294, 13018, 12939, 393, 312, 3720, 7696, 1228, 957, 32284, 51162], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 755, "seek": 278462, "start": 2800.56, "end": 2801.2999999999997, "text": " containing J.", "tokens": [51162, 19273, 508, 13, 51199], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 756, "seek": 278462, "start": 2801.2999999999997, "end": 2806.24, "text": " So the mystical imaginary E was basically just a physical quarter turn rotation hiding", "tokens": [51199, 407, 264, 40565, 26164, 462, 390, 1936, 445, 257, 4001, 6555, 1261, 12447, 10596, 51446], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 757, "seek": 278462, "start": 2806.24, "end": 2808.22, "text": " in a mathematical trench coat this whole time.", "tokens": [51446, 294, 257, 18894, 39052, 10690, 341, 1379, 565, 13, 51545], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 758, "seek": 278462, "start": 2808.58, "end": 2808.8199999999997, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51563, 1079, 13, 51575], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 759, "seek": 278462, "start": 2809.48, "end": 2811.54, "text": " And this brings us to the cutting edge of the field.", "tokens": [51608, 400, 341, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 6492, 4691, 295, 264, 2519, 13, 51711], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16731873536721253, "compression_ratio": 1.7138047138047139, "no_speech_prob": 1.4783412124672046e-12}, {"id": 760, "seek": 281154, "start": 2811.54, "end": 2816.02, "text": " The 2025 and 2026 literature discussed in Chapter 18.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 39209, 293, 945, 10880, 10394, 7152, 294, 18874, 2443, 13, 50589], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 761, "seek": 281154, "start": 2816.7599999999998, "end": 2820.94, "text": " Papers by researchers like Hoffermann and Woods and Barrios-Hita and colleagues.", "tokens": [50626, 430, 14441, 538, 10309, 411, 29135, 966, 969, 293, 31559, 293, 4156, 37994, 12, 39, 2786, 293, 7734, 13, 50835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 762, "seek": 281154, "start": 2821.84, "end": 2825.06, "text": " These physicists asked a brilliant disruptive question.", "tokens": [50880, 1981, 48716, 2351, 257, 10248, 37865, 1168, 13, 51041], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 763, "seek": 281154, "start": 2826.02, "end": 2831.84, "text": " If I is just a real rotation matrix in disguise, can we do all of quantum mechanics using only", "tokens": [51089, 759, 286, 307, 445, 257, 957, 12447, 8141, 294, 32751, 11, 393, 321, 360, 439, 295, 13018, 12939, 1228, 787, 51380], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 764, "seek": 281154, "start": 2831.84, "end": 2832.58, "text": " real numbers?", "tokens": [51380, 957, 3547, 30, 51417], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 765, "seek": 281154, "start": 2832.88, "end": 2834.96, "text": " But we literally just proved you can't.", "tokens": [51432, 583, 321, 3736, 445, 14617, 291, 393, 380, 13, 51536], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 766, "seek": 281154, "start": 2835.06, "end": 2840.38, "text": " We proved real numbers fail local tomography because of that hidden J tensor J dimension,", "tokens": [51541, 492, 14617, 957, 3547, 3061, 2654, 2916, 5820, 570, 295, 300, 7633, 508, 40863, 508, 10139, 11, 51807], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17174491141606302, "compression_ratio": 1.5105633802816902, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539520163483706e-12}, {"id": 767, "seek": 284038, "start": 2840.38, "end": 2842.04, "text": " the math didn't balance.", "tokens": [50365, 264, 5221, 994, 380, 4772, 13, 50448], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 768, "seek": 284038, "start": 2842.3, "end": 2845.86, "text": " Ah, but how do we combine Alice and Bob's systems when we counted the dimensions?", "tokens": [50461, 2438, 11, 457, 577, 360, 321, 10432, 16004, 293, 6085, 311, 3652, 562, 321, 20150, 264, 12819, 30, 50639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 769, "seek": 284038, "start": 2846.34, "end": 2848.42, "text": " We used the standard Kronecker tensor product.", "tokens": [50663, 492, 1143, 264, 3832, 6332, 546, 9178, 40863, 1674, 13, 50767], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 770, "seek": 284038, "start": 2848.56, "end": 2850.6400000000003, "text": " The standard way you multiply matrices together.", "tokens": [50774, 440, 3832, 636, 291, 12972, 32284, 1214, 13, 50878], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 771, "seek": 284038, "start": 2850.7400000000002, "end": 2850.96, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50883, 1079, 13, 50894], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 772, "seek": 284038, "start": 2851.1800000000003, "end": 2853.48, "text": " That is the assumption we didn't even realize we were making.", "tokens": [50905, 663, 307, 264, 15302, 321, 994, 380, 754, 4325, 321, 645, 1455, 13, 51020], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 773, "seek": 284038, "start": 2853.88, "end": 2857.94, "text": " We assumed that the mathematical rule for how systems compose was fixed and unquestionable.", "tokens": [51040, 492, 15895, 300, 264, 18894, 4978, 337, 577, 3652, 35925, 390, 6806, 293, 517, 20343, 313, 712, 13, 51243], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 774, "seek": 284038, "start": 2858.06, "end": 2858.9, "text": " Oh my god.", "tokens": [51249, 876, 452, 3044, 13, 51291], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1727063877066386, "compression_ratio": 1.537190082644628, "no_speech_prob": 2.1934194911754634e-12}, {"id": 775, "seek": 285890, "start": 2858.9, "end": 2863.9, "text": " These new 2025 and 2026 papers show that you can do quantum mechanics using entirely real", "tokens": [50365, 1981, 777, 39209, 293, 945, 10880, 10577, 855, 300, 291, 393, 360, 13018, 12939, 1228, 7696, 957, 50615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 776, "seek": 285890, "start": 2863.9, "end": 2868.48, "text": " numbers, reproducing every single experimental prediction of standard quantum mechanics, from", "tokens": [50615, 3547, 11, 11408, 2175, 633, 2167, 17069, 17630, 295, 3832, 13018, 12939, 11, 490, 50844], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 777, "seek": 285890, "start": 2868.48, "end": 2873.36, "text": " entanglement to Schrodinger's equation, if you change the mathematical rule for how systems", "tokens": [50844, 948, 656, 3054, 281, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 11, 498, 291, 1319, 264, 18894, 4978, 337, 577, 3652, 51088], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 778, "seek": 285890, "start": 2873.36, "end": 2873.8, "text": " compose.", "tokens": [51088, 35925, 13, 51110], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 779, "seek": 285890, "start": 2873.96, "end": 2874.84, "text": " You change the multiplication.", "tokens": [51118, 509, 1319, 264, 27290, 13, 51162], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 780, "seek": 285890, "start": 2875.38, "end": 2875.78, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51189, 7587, 13, 51209], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 781, "seek": 285890, "start": 2876.36, "end": 2880.86, "text": " If you abandon the standard Kronecker product and use a different mathematical representation", "tokens": [51238, 759, 291, 9072, 264, 3832, 6332, 546, 9178, 1674, 293, 764, 257, 819, 18894, 10290, 51463], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 782, "seek": 285890, "start": 2880.86, "end": 2886.08, "text": " of the tensor product that naturally accommodates the J matrix structure, real numbers work", "tokens": [51463, 295, 264, 40863, 1674, 300, 8195, 11713, 1024, 264, 508, 8141, 3877, 11, 957, 3547, 589, 51724], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1111058313912208, "compression_ratio": 1.7406143344709897, "no_speech_prob": 1.592090174973304e-12}, {"id": 783, "seek": 288608, "start": 2886.08, "end": 2886.58, "text": " perfectly.", "tokens": [50365, 6239, 13, 50390], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 784, "seek": 288608, "start": 2887.14, "end": 2888.6, "text": " The missing dimensions vanish.", "tokens": [50418, 440, 5361, 12819, 43584, 13, 50491], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 785, "seek": 288608, "start": 2888.96, "end": 2890.16, "text": " The contradictions resolve.", "tokens": [50509, 440, 15858, 15607, 14151, 13, 50569], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 786, "seek": 288608, "start": 2890.74, "end": 2891.6, "text": " That is just.", "tokens": [50598, 663, 307, 445, 13, 50641], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 787, "seek": 288608, "start": 2891.72, "end": 2893.1, "text": " The irony is unbelievable.", "tokens": [50647, 440, 35365, 307, 16605, 13, 50716], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 788, "seek": 288608, "start": 2893.34, "end": 2897.06, "text": " After ruling out real numbers in section two with the roundabout, and section three with", "tokens": [50728, 2381, 21437, 484, 957, 3547, 294, 3541, 732, 365, 264, 3098, 21970, 11, 293, 3541, 1045, 365, 50914], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 789, "seek": 288608, "start": 2897.06, "end": 2901.62, "text": " the Escher stairs, and section four with the cryptographic key, we find out at the very", "tokens": [50914, 264, 2313, 6759, 13471, 11, 293, 3541, 1451, 365, 264, 9844, 12295, 2141, 11, 321, 915, 484, 412, 264, 588, 51142], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 790, "seek": 288608, "start": 2901.62, "end": 2908.02, "text": " end that real numbers can actually work if we just change our definition of the word and when we say Alice and Bob.", "tokens": [51142, 917, 300, 957, 3547, 393, 767, 589, 498, 321, 445, 1319, 527, 7123, 295, 264, 1349, 293, 562, 321, 584, 16004, 293, 6085, 13, 51462], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 791, "seek": 288608, "start": 2908.16, "end": 2910.2999999999997, "text": " It is the ultimate lesson in theoretical physics.", "tokens": [51469, 467, 307, 264, 9705, 6898, 294, 20864, 10649, 13, 51576], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 792, "seek": 288608, "start": 2910.7999999999997, "end": 2913.84, "text": " It reveals a profound truth about reality versus representation.", "tokens": [51601, 467, 20893, 257, 14382, 3494, 466, 4103, 5717, 10290, 13, 51753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21525410908024487, "compression_ratio": 1.7559322033898306, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283471083293314e-12}, {"id": 793, "seek": 291384, "start": 2913.84, "end": 2917.7200000000003, "text": " The apparent necessity of complex numbers wasn't a physical law.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 18335, 24217, 295, 3997, 3547, 2067, 380, 257, 4001, 2101, 13, 50559], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 794, "seek": 291384, "start": 2918.06, "end": 2919.42, "text": " It was a representation hazard.", "tokens": [50576, 467, 390, 257, 10290, 20790, 13, 50644], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 795, "seek": 291384, "start": 2920.2000000000003, "end": 2923.54, "text": " The universe has a specific, hard-coded geometric structure.", "tokens": [50683, 440, 6445, 575, 257, 2685, 11, 1152, 12, 66, 12340, 33246, 3877, 13, 50850], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 796, "seek": 291384, "start": 2924.1600000000003, "end": 2927.2400000000002, "text": " It fundamentally requires a quarter-turn rotation to function.", "tokens": [50881, 467, 17879, 7029, 257, 6555, 12, 33886, 12447, 281, 2445, 13, 51035], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 797, "seek": 291384, "start": 2927.96, "end": 2929.5, "text": " That is the physical truth.", "tokens": [51071, 663, 307, 264, 4001, 3494, 13, 51148], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 798, "seek": 291384, "start": 2929.54, "end": 2931.48, "text": " And how we spell that truth is up to us.", "tokens": [51150, 400, 577, 321, 9827, 300, 3494, 307, 493, 281, 505, 13, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 799, "seek": 291384, "start": 2931.86, "end": 2932.38, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [51266, 48746, 736, 13, 51292], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 800, "seek": 291384, "start": 2933.04, "end": 2936.88, "text": " We humans chose to write that rotation using the symbol I.", "tokens": [51325, 492, 6255, 5111, 281, 2464, 300, 12447, 1228, 264, 5986, 286, 13, 51517], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 801, "seek": 291384, "start": 2937.2400000000002, "end": 2939.4, "text": " We chose to use the Kronecker tensor product.", "tokens": [51535, 492, 5111, 281, 764, 264, 6332, 546, 9178, 40863, 1674, 13, 51643], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1755730946858724, "compression_ratio": 1.588235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 1.868320168713078e-12}, {"id": 802, "seek": 293940, "start": 2939.4, "end": 2944.6800000000003, "text": " And because we made those specific choices, we forced ourselves into a complex numbered framework.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 570, 321, 1027, 729, 2685, 7994, 11, 321, 7579, 4175, 666, 257, 3997, 40936, 8388, 13, 50629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 803, "seek": 293940, "start": 2945.3, "end": 2950.8, "text": " But if we change our mathematical conventions, real numbers can describe the exact same physical reality.", "tokens": [50660, 583, 498, 321, 1319, 527, 18894, 33520, 11, 957, 3547, 393, 6786, 264, 1900, 912, 4001, 4103, 13, 50935], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 804, "seek": 293940, "start": 2951.36, "end": 2954.64, "text": " The underlying physical truth is the geometry, the structure.", "tokens": [50963, 440, 14217, 4001, 3494, 307, 264, 18426, 11, 264, 3877, 13, 51127], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 805, "seek": 293940, "start": 2955.06, "end": 2957.04, "text": " It is not the alphabet we use to spell it out.", "tokens": [51148, 467, 307, 406, 264, 23339, 321, 764, 281, 9827, 309, 484, 13, 51247], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 806, "seek": 293940, "start": 2957.28, "end": 2960.4, "text": " It really makes you question everything you learned in physics class.", "tokens": [51259, 467, 534, 1669, 291, 1168, 1203, 291, 3264, 294, 10649, 1508, 13, 51415], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 807, "seek": 293940, "start": 2960.62, "end": 2963.4, "text": " And just to throw one more wrench into the gears before we finish,", "tokens": [51426, 400, 445, 281, 3507, 472, 544, 25406, 666, 264, 20915, 949, 321, 2413, 11, 51565], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13055391700900332, "compression_ratio": 1.6129032258064515, "no_speech_prob": 1.847004320321144e-12}, {"id": 808, "seek": 296340, "start": 2963.4, "end": 2969.0, "text": " the manuscript briefly mentions the quaternionic frontier in Chapter 24, right, the 4D numbers.", "tokens": [50365, 264, 23928, 10515, 23844, 264, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 35853, 294, 18874, 4022, 11, 558, 11, 264, 1017, 35, 3547, 13, 50645], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 809, "seek": 296340, "start": 2969.1800000000003, "end": 2972.3, "text": " Yes, just as a hint of how much deeper this rabbit hole goes.", "tokens": [50654, 1079, 11, 445, 382, 257, 12075, 295, 577, 709, 7731, 341, 19509, 5458, 1709, 13, 50810], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 810, "seek": 296340, "start": 2972.86, "end": 2976.94, "text": " If you try to build quantum mechanics with quaternions, which have three different imaginary axes,", "tokens": [50838, 759, 291, 853, 281, 1322, 13018, 12939, 365, 421, 771, 77, 626, 11, 597, 362, 1045, 819, 26164, 35387, 11, 51042], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 811, "seek": 296340, "start": 2977.58, "end": 2979.42, "text": " the book proves a mathematical bound,", "tokens": [51074, 264, 1446, 25019, 257, 18894, 5472, 11, 51166], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 812, "seek": 296340, "start": 2979.82, "end": 2985.34, "text": " showing that quaternionic, mutually unbiased bases can outnumber complex ones by a factor of roughly two.", "tokens": [51186, 4099, 300, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 11, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 393, 484, 41261, 3997, 2306, 538, 257, 5952, 295, 9810, 732, 13, 51462], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 813, "seek": 296340, "start": 2986.02, "end": 2990.54, "text": " Specifically, a formula of 2D plus 1 instead of the standard D plus 1.", "tokens": [51496, 26058, 11, 257, 8513, 295, 568, 35, 1804, 502, 2602, 295, 264, 3832, 413, 1804, 502, 13, 51722], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 814, "seek": 296340, "start": 2990.56, "end": 2991.36, "text": " Which implies what?", "tokens": [51723, 3013, 18779, 437, 30, 51763], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14628236255948507, "compression_ratio": 1.5686900958466454, "no_speech_prob": 1.7758195088035666e-12}, {"id": 815, "seek": 299136, "start": 2991.36, "end": 2995.82, "text": " It suggests that there are even more exotic, highly symmetric structures", "tokens": [50365, 467, 13409, 300, 456, 366, 754, 544, 27063, 11, 5405, 32330, 9227, 50588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 816, "seek": 299136, "start": 2995.82, "end": 3000.86, "text": " that are mathematically admissible under the bare rules of probability,", "tokens": [50588, 300, 366, 44003, 5910, 41073, 833, 264, 6949, 4474, 295, 8482, 11, 50840], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 817, "seek": 299136, "start": 3001.2400000000002, "end": 3004.1600000000003, "text": " even though we haven't found a reason for nature to use them yet.", "tokens": [50859, 754, 1673, 321, 2378, 380, 1352, 257, 1778, 337, 3687, 281, 764, 552, 1939, 13, 51005], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 818, "seek": 299136, "start": 3004.6600000000003, "end": 3010.34, "text": " It means the space of possible physics is much larger than the physics we actually observe.", "tokens": [51030, 467, 1355, 264, 1901, 295, 1944, 10649, 307, 709, 4833, 813, 264, 10649, 321, 767, 11441, 13, 51314], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 819, "seek": 299136, "start": 3010.84, "end": 3012.06, "text": " Okay, I need to take a breath.", "tokens": [51339, 1033, 11, 286, 643, 281, 747, 257, 6045, 13, 51400], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 820, "seek": 299136, "start": 3012.56, "end": 3014.48, "text": " Let's bring this all back home for the listener.", "tokens": [51425, 961, 311, 1565, 341, 439, 646, 1280, 337, 264, 31569, 13, 51521], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 821, "seek": 299136, "start": 3014.98, "end": 3016.6600000000003, "text": " We have been on quite a journey today.", "tokens": [51546, 492, 362, 668, 322, 1596, 257, 4671, 965, 13, 51630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1308810073550385, "compression_ratio": 1.6130268199233717, "no_speech_prob": 1.6747531096303336e-12}, {"id": 822, "seek": 301666, "start": 3016.66, "end": 3021.08, "text": " We started with the absolute bare bones of classical probability,", "tokens": [50365, 492, 1409, 365, 264, 8236, 6949, 10491, 295, 13735, 8482, 11, 50586], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14086974341914338, "compression_ratio": 1.485207100591716, "no_speech_prob": 1.5428702152681106e-12}, {"id": 823, "seek": 301666, "start": 3021.3799999999997, "end": 3024.7599999999998, "text": " just shuffling decks of cards and looking at points inside triangles.", "tokens": [50601, 445, 402, 1245, 1688, 32607, 295, 5632, 293, 1237, 412, 2793, 1854, 29896, 13, 50770], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14086974341914338, "compression_ratio": 1.485207100591716, "no_speech_prob": 1.5428702152681106e-12}, {"id": 824, "seek": 301666, "start": 3025.3799999999997, "end": 3031.12, "text": " We watched classical real numbers mathematically fail to glue together in the closed loops of our Escher staircase.", "tokens": [50801, 492, 6337, 13735, 957, 3547, 44003, 3061, 281, 8998, 1214, 294, 264, 5395, 16121, 295, 527, 2313, 6759, 35359, 13, 51088], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14086974341914338, "compression_ratio": 1.485207100591716, "no_speech_prob": 1.5428702152681106e-12}, {"id": 825, "seek": 303112, "start": 3031.12, "end": 3036.3399999999997, "text": " We saw complex numbers perfectly balance the ledgers of composite systems,", "tokens": [50365, 492, 1866, 3997, 3547, 6239, 4772, 264, 4684, 9458, 295, 25557, 3652, 11, 50626], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15674810111522675, "compression_ratio": 1.3017241379310345, "no_speech_prob": 2.379768257454451e-12}, {"id": 826, "seek": 303112, "start": 3036.58, "end": 3040.74, "text": " while real numbers tried to hide extra dimensions behind cryptographic keys.", "tokens": [50638, 1339, 957, 3547, 3031, 281, 6479, 2857, 12819, 2261, 9844, 12295, 9317, 13, 50846], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15674810111522675, "compression_ratio": 1.3017241379310345, "no_speech_prob": 2.379768257454451e-12}, {"id": 827, "seek": 304074, "start": 3040.74, "end": 3045.2, "text": " We realized that whether a particle is deeply entangled or just classically correlated", "tokens": [50365, 492, 5334, 300, 1968, 257, 12359, 307, 8760, 948, 39101, 420, 445, 1508, 984, 38574, 50588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507423871829186, "compression_ratio": 1.5573770491803278, "no_speech_prob": 1.762012302557181e-12}, {"id": 828, "seek": 304074, "start": 3045.2, "end": 3049.1, "text": " might depend entirely on the mathematical goggles you were wearing when you look at it.", "tokens": [50588, 1062, 5672, 7696, 322, 264, 18894, 39808, 291, 645, 4769, 562, 291, 574, 412, 309, 13, 50783], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507423871829186, "compression_ratio": 1.5573770491803278, "no_speech_prob": 1.762012302557181e-12}, {"id": 829, "seek": 304074, "start": 3049.54, "end": 3052.4599999999996, "text": " We watched the entire standard textbook of quantum mechanics,", "tokens": [50805, 492, 6337, 264, 2302, 3832, 25591, 295, 13018, 12939, 11, 50951], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507423871829186, "compression_ratio": 1.5573770491803278, "no_speech_prob": 1.762012302557181e-12}, {"id": 830, "seek": 304074, "start": 3052.56, "end": 3054.12, "text": " the Born rule, Schrodinger's equation,", "tokens": [50956, 264, 29808, 4978, 11, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 11, 51034], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507423871829186, "compression_ratio": 1.5573770491803278, "no_speech_prob": 1.762012302557181e-12}, {"id": 831, "seek": 304074, "start": 3054.5, "end": 3060.08, "text": " fall out as the inevitable result of just four basic operational assumptions about time and measurement.", "tokens": [51053, 2100, 484, 382, 264, 21451, 1874, 295, 445, 1451, 3875, 16607, 17695, 466, 565, 293, 13160, 13, 51332], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13507423871829186, "compression_ratio": 1.5573770491803278, "no_speech_prob": 1.762012302557181e-12}, {"id": 832, "seek": 306008, "start": 3060.08, "end": 3063.84, "text": " And then, just when we thought the complex numbers had won,", "tokens": [50365, 400, 550, 11, 445, 562, 321, 1194, 264, 3997, 3547, 632, 1582, 11, 50553], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 833, "seek": 306008, "start": 3063.94, "end": 3069.0, "text": " the Amplit Wist and the 2026 literature pulled the rug out from under us entirely,", "tokens": [50558, 264, 2012, 564, 270, 343, 468, 293, 264, 945, 10880, 10394, 7373, 264, 18329, 484, 490, 833, 505, 7696, 11, 50811], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 834, "seek": 306008, "start": 3069.72, "end": 3072.52, "text": " showing that standard quantum mechanics is just one dialect,", "tokens": [50847, 4099, 300, 3832, 13018, 12939, 307, 445, 472, 24652, 11, 50987], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 835, "seek": 306008, "start": 3072.84, "end": 3078.2999999999997, "text": " one way to write down a geometric truth that can equally be expressed entirely with real numbers", "tokens": [51003, 472, 636, 281, 2464, 760, 257, 33246, 3494, 300, 393, 12309, 312, 12675, 7696, 365, 957, 3547, 51276], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 836, "seek": 306008, "start": 3078.2999999999997, "end": 3080.92, "text": " if you were just willing to change how systems compose.", "tokens": [51276, 498, 291, 645, 445, 4950, 281, 1319, 577, 3652, 35925, 13, 51407], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 837, "seek": 306008, "start": 3081.14, "end": 3084.66, "text": " It is a stunning display of what theoretical physics should be.", "tokens": [51418, 467, 307, 257, 18550, 4674, 295, 437, 20864, 10649, 820, 312, 13, 51594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 838, "seek": 306008, "start": 3085.2599999999998, "end": 3088.42, "text": " Stripping away the dogma, showing exactly what our assumptions cost,", "tokens": [51624, 20390, 3759, 1314, 264, 3000, 1696, 11, 4099, 2293, 437, 527, 17695, 2063, 11, 51782], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13810024430266524, "compression_ratio": 1.6409395973154361, "no_speech_prob": 1.7763141218346545e-12}, {"id": 839, "seek": 308842, "start": 3088.42, "end": 3091.62, "text": " and carefully separating the structural reality of the universe", "tokens": [50365, 293, 7500, 29279, 264, 15067, 4103, 295, 264, 6445, 50525], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 840, "seek": 308842, "start": 3091.62, "end": 3094.9, "text": " from the human conventions we use to write it down on a whiteboard.", "tokens": [50525, 490, 264, 1952, 33520, 321, 764, 281, 2464, 309, 760, 322, 257, 2418, 3787, 13, 50689], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 841, "seek": 308842, "start": 3095.32, "end": 3098.0, "text": " It forces us to distinguish the territory from the map.", "tokens": [50710, 467, 5874, 505, 281, 20206, 264, 11360, 490, 264, 4471, 13, 50844], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 842, "seek": 308842, "start": 3098.1, "end": 3101.5, "text": " Which leaves me with a final thought for you, the listener, to mull over.", "tokens": [50849, 3013, 5510, 385, 365, 257, 2572, 1194, 337, 291, 11, 264, 31569, 11, 281, 275, 858, 670, 13, 51019], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 843, "seek": 308842, "start": 3102.16, "end": 3106.02, "text": " If the absolute necessity of imaginary numbers and quantum mechanics,", "tokens": [51052, 759, 264, 8236, 24217, 295, 26164, 3547, 293, 13018, 12939, 11, 51245], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 844, "seek": 308842, "start": 3106.26, "end": 3109.4, "text": " something that Nobel laureates have sworn by for a hundred years,", "tokens": [51257, 746, 300, 24611, 49469, 1024, 362, 40068, 538, 337, 257, 3262, 924, 11, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13987373170398532, "compression_ratio": 1.694189602446483, "no_speech_prob": 1.9279306883585035e-12}, {"id": 845, "seek": 30884