mathematical vocabulary you were using. And finally, we watched the text rebuild the towering achievements of 20th century physics, the Born rule, Hermitian observables, and Schrodinger's equation, using nothing more than probability geometry and four simple logical operational assumptions. It is a monumental achievement in changing how we conceptualize the subject. It transforms quantum mechanics from a dictatorial list of demands the universe makes of us into a beautiful, inevitable puzzle that solves itself through logic. It truly embodies the philosophy of putting constraint before content. But as always on the Deep Dive, we want to leave you with a final thought, something to chew on that goes just beyond the text we've discussed today. What is the question we're leaving them with? If complex numbers are the unique Goldilocks zone, the only mathematical field that perfectly satisfies local tomography, where the parts exactly and cleanly explain the whole without any missing or hidden dimensions, what does that mean for the future of physics? If we connect this to the bigger picture of modern research, that is a profound question. Because right now, the greatest minds in physics are hunting for a theory of quantum gravity. We are mathematically probing the extreme edges of black holes, looking at holographic principles and the extreme limits of entanglement entropy. What if one day, we discover a physical system in the crushing gravity of a black hole, where local tomography actually fails? A system where the hole truly is greater than the sum of its parts. Where measuring the local boundaries simply isn't enough to understand the interior. Exactly. If local tomography breaks under extreme gravity, would we be forced to abandon our beloved complex numbers? Would the universe suddenly require the messy, overdetermined quaternions to balance the cosmic data ledger? Or would we discover an entirely new, unimaginable mathematical field hiding inside the singularity? This raises an important question about the limits of our current mathematical constraints. It deeply suggests that the fundamental math of the universe might actually evolve as the physical constraints of the environment change. And that is exactly why we do this. Keep questioning the constraints that shape your reality. Thank you for joining us on this incredibly deep dive. Keep thinking and keep exploring. Keep thinking and keep exploring.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.8000000000000003, "text": " I want you to imagine, just for a moment, learning quantum mechanics.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 528, 291, 281, 3811, 11, 445, 337, 257, 1623, 11, 2539, 13018, 12939, 13, 50555], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 4.82, "end": 9.48, "text": " And I don't mean the way it is usually taught to, like, a terrified college sophomore.", "tokens": [50606, 400, 286, 500, 380, 914, 264, 636, 309, 307, 2673, 5928, 281, 11, 411, 11, 257, 23051, 3859, 35798, 13, 50839], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 2, "seek": 0, "start": 9.82, "end": 14.76, "text": " Right. Yeah, the traditional pedagogical approach is very much an exercise in blunt force.", "tokens": [50856, 1779, 13, 865, 11, 264, 5164, 5670, 31599, 804, 3109, 307, 588, 709, 364, 5380, 294, 32246, 3464, 13, 51103], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 3, "seek": 0, "start": 15.02, "end": 19.1, "text": " It's about handing you the strange counterintuitive rules of the quantum world.", "tokens": [51116, 467, 311, 466, 34774, 291, 264, 5861, 5682, 686, 48314, 4474, 295, 264, 13018, 1002, 13, 51320], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 4, "seek": 0, "start": 19.2, "end": 19.38, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51325, 865, 13, 51334], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 5, "seek": 0, "start": 19.52, "end": 22.1, "text": " And just saying, you know, here is how reality operates.", "tokens": [51341, 400, 445, 1566, 11, 291, 458, 11, 510, 307, 577, 4103, 22577, 13, 51470], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 6, "seek": 0, "start": 22.22, "end": 22.6, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51476, 7587, 13, 51495], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 7, "seek": 0, "start": 22.6, "end": 29.6, "text": " You walk into a physics classroom and the professor immediately starts throwing these bizarre magical concepts at the whiteboard.", "tokens": [51495, 509, 1792, 666, 257, 10649, 7419, 293, 264, 8304, 4258, 3719, 10238, 613, 18265, 12066, 10392, 412, 264, 2418, 3787, 13, 51845], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15837773132324218, "compression_ratio": 1.5933734939759037, "no_speech_prob": 1.555332035038659e-12}, {"id": 8, "seek": 3000, "start": 30.0, "end": 36.62, "text": " Like, you get cats in boxes that are somehow miraculously alive and dead at the exact same time.", "tokens": [50365, 1743, 11, 291, 483, 11111, 294, 9002, 300, 366, 6063, 30686, 25038, 5465, 293, 3116, 412, 264, 1900, 912, 565, 13, 50696], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 9, "seek": 3000, "start": 36.68, "end": 37.980000000000004, "text": " Which is always a fun one for the students.", "tokens": [50699, 3013, 307, 1009, 257, 1019, 472, 337, 264, 1731, 13, 50764], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 10, "seek": 3000, "start": 38.019999999999996, "end": 38.5, "text": " Oh, for sure.", "tokens": [50766, 876, 11, 337, 988, 13, 50790], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 11, "seek": 3000, "start": 38.72, "end": 46.68, "text": " And you get particles communicating instantly across the vastness of the universe in what Einstein famously dismissed as spooky action at a distance.", "tokens": [50801, 400, 291, 483, 10007, 17559, 13518, 2108, 264, 8369, 1287, 295, 264, 6445, 294, 437, 23486, 34360, 29970, 382, 30510, 3069, 412, 257, 4560, 13, 51199], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 12, "seek": 3000, "start": 46.94, "end": 48.32, "text": " Yeah, he really hated that.", "tokens": [51212, 865, 11, 415, 534, 17398, 300, 13, 51281], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 13, "seek": 3000, "start": 48.44, "end": 54.42, "text": " But you get these massive, heavy, mathematically dense postulates just dropping from the sky, right?", "tokens": [51287, 583, 291, 483, 613, 5994, 11, 4676, 11, 44003, 18011, 2183, 26192, 445, 13601, 490, 264, 5443, 11, 558, 30, 51586], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 14, "seek": 3000, "start": 54.72, "end": 57.46, "text": " Telling you that the universe is just fundamentally weird at the bottom.", "tokens": [51601, 5115, 278, 291, 300, 264, 6445, 307, 445, 17879, 3657, 412, 264, 2767, 13, 51738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 15, "seek": 3000, "start": 57.519999999999996, "end": 58.96, "text": " And you just have to accept it and calculate.", "tokens": [51741, 400, 291, 445, 362, 281, 3241, 309, 293, 8873, 13, 51813], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10493954878586989, "compression_ratio": 1.7037037037037037, "no_speech_prob": 7.462798825089156e-13}, {"id": 16, "seek": 5896, "start": 58.96, "end": 60.78, "text": " It absolutely feels like magic.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 3122, 3417, 411, 5585, 13, 50456], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 17, "seek": 5896, "start": 61.56, "end": 63.54, "text": " But what if we completely flip the script on that?", "tokens": [50495, 583, 437, 498, 321, 2584, 7929, 264, 5755, 322, 300, 30, 50594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 18, "seek": 5896, "start": 63.54, "end": 66.88, "text": " I mean, that is a vastly more demanding way to look at the universe.", "tokens": [50594, 286, 914, 11, 300, 307, 257, 41426, 544, 19960, 636, 281, 574, 412, 264, 6445, 13, 50761], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 19, "seek": 5896, "start": 67.24, "end": 68.48, "text": " You aren't just asking what happens.", "tokens": [50779, 509, 3212, 380, 445, 3365, 437, 2314, 13, 50841], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 20, "seek": 5896, "start": 68.66, "end": 69.38, "text": " Well, you're asking why.", "tokens": [50850, 1042, 11, 291, 434, 3365, 983, 13, 50886], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 21, "seek": 5896, "start": 69.54, "end": 69.72, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50894, 7587, 13, 50903], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 22, "seek": 5896, "start": 69.82, "end": 72.96000000000001, "text": " You're asking why must it happen this way and no other way.", "tokens": [50908, 509, 434, 3365, 983, 1633, 309, 1051, 341, 636, 293, 572, 661, 636, 13, 51065], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 23, "seek": 5896, "start": 73.24000000000001, "end": 77.66, "text": " But ultimately, it is a much more satisfying lens through which to view reality.", "tokens": [51079, 583, 6284, 11, 309, 307, 257, 709, 544, 18348, 6765, 807, 597, 281, 1910, 4103, 13, 51300], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19366114480154856, "compression_ratio": 1.5782608695652174, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554013798104506e-12}, {"id": 24, "seek": 7766, "start": 77.66, "end": 81.42, "text": " And that is the entire mission for today's Deep Dive.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 300, 307, 264, 2302, 4447, 337, 965, 311, 14895, 413, 488, 13, 50553], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20584914867694562, "compression_ratio": 1.3229166666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279278051798852e-12}, {"id": 25, "seek": 7766, "start": 81.94, "end": 87.75999999999999, "text": " You, the listener, are going to explore a truly fascinating text published in 2026.", "tokens": [50579, 509, 11, 264, 31569, 11, 366, 516, 281, 6839, 257, 4908, 10343, 2487, 6572, 294, 945, 10880, 13, 50870], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20584914867694562, "compression_ratio": 1.3229166666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279278051798852e-12}, {"id": 26, "seek": 7766, "start": 88.12, "end": 95.6, "text": " It's titled, Quantum Mechanics from Admissible Probabilistic Composition by an Independent Researcher Named Flixion.", "tokens": [50888, 467, 311, 19841, 11, 44964, 30175, 1167, 490, 46292, 41073, 8736, 5177, 3142, 6620, 5830, 538, 364, 40310, 10303, 260, 426, 3475, 3235, 970, 313, 13, 51262], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20584914867694562, "compression_ratio": 1.3229166666666667, "no_speech_prob": 1.7279278051798852e-12}, {"id": 27, "seek": 9560, "start": 95.6, "end": 97.05999999999999, "text": " It's quite a title.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 1596, 257, 4876, 13, 50438], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 28, "seek": 9560, "start": 97.19999999999999, "end": 97.67999999999999, "text": " It is.", "tokens": [50445, 467, 307, 13, 50469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 29, "seek": 9560, "start": 97.97999999999999, "end": 103.25999999999999, "text": " But the guiding light we are going to follow today is a brilliantly simple phrase coined by the author.", "tokens": [50484, 583, 264, 25061, 1442, 321, 366, 516, 281, 1524, 965, 307, 257, 8695, 42580, 2199, 9535, 45222, 538, 264, 3793, 13, 50748], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 30, "seek": 9560, "start": 104.22, "end": 105.86, "text": " Constraint before content.", "tokens": [50796, 8574, 424, 686, 949, 2701, 13, 50878], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 31, "seek": 9560, "start": 106.38, "end": 110.46, "text": " Constraint before content is a beautiful philosophy for unpacking physics.", "tokens": [50904, 8574, 424, 686, 949, 2701, 307, 257, 2238, 10675, 337, 26699, 278, 10649, 13, 51108], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 32, "seek": 9560, "start": 111.08, "end": 114.63999999999999, "text": " It means we are deliberately not going to talk about physical objects right away.", "tokens": [51139, 467, 1355, 321, 366, 23506, 406, 516, 281, 751, 466, 4001, 6565, 558, 1314, 13, 51317], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2019808201850215, "compression_ratio": 1.6269430051813472, "no_speech_prob": 1.4888491915027169e-12}, {"id": 33, "seek": 11464, "start": 114.64, "end": 116.28, "text": " So no electrons or photons.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 572, 14265, 420, 40209, 13, 50447], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 34, "seek": 11464, "start": 116.48, "end": 116.72, "text": " That's right.", "tokens": [50457, 663, 311, 558, 13, 50469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 35, "seek": 11464, "start": 116.8, "end": 121.38, "text": " We are banning the words electrons, photons, and energy levels from our vocabulary for the time being.", "tokens": [50473, 492, 366, 5643, 773, 264, 2283, 14265, 11, 40209, 11, 293, 2281, 4358, 490, 527, 19864, 337, 264, 565, 885, 13, 50702], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 36, "seek": 11464, "start": 121.52, "end": 121.7, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50709, 865, 13, 50718], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 37, "seek": 11464, "start": 121.8, "end": 124.76, "text": " We are going to strip everything back to the absolute bare minimum.", "tokens": [50723, 492, 366, 516, 281, 12828, 1203, 646, 281, 264, 8236, 6949, 7285, 13, 50871], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 38, "seek": 11464, "start": 124.82, "end": 128.54, "text": " Looking purely at the logical rules of moving information around, right?", "tokens": [50874, 11053, 17491, 412, 264, 14978, 4474, 295, 2684, 1589, 926, 11, 558, 30, 51060], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 39, "seek": 11464, "start": 129.06, "end": 129.54, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [51086, 48746, 736, 13, 51110], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 40, "seek": 11464, "start": 130.02, "end": 135.02, "text": " Just the foundational constraints on how probabilities have to work when you compose systems.", "tokens": [51134, 1449, 264, 32195, 18491, 322, 577, 33783, 362, 281, 589, 562, 291, 35925, 3652, 13, 51384], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 41, "seek": 11464, "start": 135.58, "end": 143.9, "text": " And from those seemingly innocent constraints, we are going to watch the entirety of textbook quantum mechanics magically reconstruct itself.", "tokens": [51412, 400, 490, 729, 18709, 13171, 18491, 11, 321, 366, 516, 281, 1159, 264, 31557, 295, 25591, 13018, 12939, 39763, 31499, 2564, 13, 51828], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16167417894892333, "compression_ratio": 1.67601246105919, "no_speech_prob": 1.7967537180307858e-12}, {"id": 42, "seek": 14390, "start": 143.9, "end": 145.84, "text": " Here's where it gets really interesting.", "tokens": [50365, 1692, 311, 689, 309, 2170, 534, 1880, 13, 50462], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 43, "seek": 14390, "start": 146.4, "end": 150.94, "text": " This text challenges the very foundation of how we think about the fabric of reality.", "tokens": [50490, 639, 2487, 4759, 264, 588, 7030, 295, 577, 321, 519, 466, 264, 7253, 295, 4103, 13, 50717], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 44, "seek": 14390, "start": 151.1, "end": 151.82, "text": " It really does.", "tokens": [50725, 467, 534, 775, 13, 50761], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 45, "seek": 14390, "start": 151.96, "end": 160.14000000000001, "text": " Because it treats quantum math not as some weird curiosity that we stumbled upon in a laboratory experiment, but as the thing to be explained.", "tokens": [50768, 1436, 309, 19566, 13018, 5221, 406, 382, 512, 3657, 18769, 300, 321, 36668, 3564, 294, 257, 16523, 5120, 11, 457, 382, 264, 551, 281, 312, 8825, 13, 51177], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 46, "seek": 14390, "start": 160.34, "end": 161.84, "text": " It adds a daring question.", "tokens": [51187, 467, 10860, 257, 43128, 1168, 13, 51262], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 47, "seek": 14390, "start": 162.56, "end": 167.48000000000002, "text": " Under what logical structural constraints does the universe have to use quantum mechanics to function?", "tokens": [51298, 6974, 437, 14978, 15067, 18491, 775, 264, 6445, 362, 281, 764, 13018, 12939, 281, 2445, 30, 51544], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 48, "seek": 14390, "start": 167.48000000000002, "end": 172.86, "text": " And to answer a question that profound the author, takes us on a journey starting from absolute zero.", "tokens": [51544, 400, 281, 1867, 257, 1168, 300, 14382, 264, 3793, 11, 2516, 505, 322, 257, 4671, 2891, 490, 8236, 4018, 13, 51813], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13634562703360498, "compression_ratio": 1.6309148264984228, "no_speech_prob": 2.3995900752527e-12}, {"id": 49, "seek": 17286, "start": 172.86, "end": 176.38000000000002, "text": " We have to begin with the most basic concept imaginable.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 362, 281, 1841, 365, 264, 881, 3875, 3410, 23427, 712, 13, 50541], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 50, "seek": 17286, "start": 176.88000000000002, "end": 179.8, "text": " Let's look at a finite set of distinguishable alternatives.", "tokens": [50566, 961, 311, 574, 412, 257, 19362, 992, 295, 20206, 712, 20478, 13, 50712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 51, "seek": 17286, "start": 180.04000000000002, "end": 181.9, "text": " Okay, so no quantum weirdness yet.", "tokens": [50724, 1033, 11, 370, 572, 13018, 3657, 1287, 1939, 13, 50817], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 52, "seek": 17286, "start": 182.04000000000002, "end": 184.24, "text": " Just regular, everyday states of knowledge.", "tokens": [50824, 1449, 3890, 11, 7429, 4368, 295, 3601, 13, 50934], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 53, "seek": 17286, "start": 184.34, "end": 184.54000000000002, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50939, 1779, 13, 50949], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 54, "seek": 17286, "start": 184.72000000000003, "end": 186.48000000000002, "text": " Think of flipping a standard coin.", "tokens": [50958, 6557, 295, 26886, 257, 3832, 11464, 13, 51046], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 55, "seek": 17286, "start": 186.72000000000003, "end": 188.72000000000003, "text": " It lands on heads or it lands on tails.", "tokens": [51058, 467, 5949, 322, 8050, 420, 309, 5949, 322, 28537, 13, 51158], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 56, "seek": 17286, "start": 189.22000000000003, "end": 194.78000000000003, "text": " Or imagine a basic laboratory detector that either clicks when something hits it or just remains silent.", "tokens": [51183, 1610, 3811, 257, 3875, 16523, 25712, 300, 2139, 18521, 562, 746, 8664, 309, 420, 445, 7023, 12784, 13, 51461], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16848947604497275, "compression_ratio": 1.528, "no_speech_prob": 1.5014884951689011e-12}, {"id": 57, "seek": 19478, "start": 194.78, "end": 198.44, "text": " So we have a set of distinct mutually exclusive outcomes.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 321, 362, 257, 992, 295, 10644, 39144, 13005, 10070, 13, 50548], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2498697830458819, "compression_ratio": 1.4152046783625731, "no_speech_prob": 1.6682421503239064e-12}, {"id": 58, "seek": 19478, "start": 198.96, "end": 199.3, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50574, 7587, 13, 50591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2498697830458819, "compression_ratio": 1.4152046783625731, "no_speech_prob": 1.6682421503239064e-12}, {"id": 59, "seek": 19478, "start": 199.72, "end": 203.52, "text": " And we have a state of knowledge about which outcome is going to happen.", "tokens": [50612, 400, 321, 362, 257, 1785, 295, 3601, 466, 597, 9700, 307, 516, 281, 1051, 13, 50802], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2498697830458819, "compression_ratio": 1.4152046783625731, "no_speech_prob": 1.6682421503239064e-12}, {"id": 60, "seek": 19478, "start": 203.9, "end": 206.08, "text": " This is just a standard probability distribution.", "tokens": [50821, 639, 307, 445, 257, 3832, 8482, 7316, 13, 50930], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2498697830458819, "compression_ratio": 1.4152046783625731, "no_speech_prob": 1.6682421503239064e-12}, {"id": 61, "seek": 19478, "start": 206.64, "end": 210.18, "text": " A list of positive numbers that sum to exactly 100%.", "tokens": [50958, 316, 1329, 295, 3353, 3547, 300, 2408, 281, 2293, 2319, 6856, 51135], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2498697830458819, "compression_ratio": 1.4152046783625731, "no_speech_prob": 1.6682421503239064e-12}, {"id": 62, "seek": 21018, "start": 210.18, "end": 218.88, "text": " And in mathematics, this space of all possible probability distributions for a given set of outcomes is called the probability simplex.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 294, 18666, 11, 341, 1901, 295, 439, 1944, 8482, 37870, 337, 257, 2212, 992, 295, 10070, 307, 1219, 264, 8482, 2199, 87, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 63, "seek": 21018, "start": 219.0, "end": 219.20000000000002, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50806, 1779, 13, 50816], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 64, "seek": 21018, "start": 219.26000000000002, "end": 223.76000000000002, "text": " So if we visualize that for the coin flip, your simplex is just a simple straight line segment.", "tokens": [50819, 407, 498, 321, 23273, 300, 337, 264, 11464, 7929, 11, 428, 2199, 87, 307, 445, 257, 2199, 2997, 1622, 9469, 13, 51044], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 65, "seek": 21018, "start": 224.32, "end": 228.08, "text": " One end of the line is 100% heads, 0% tails.", "tokens": [51072, 1485, 917, 295, 264, 1622, 307, 2319, 4, 8050, 11, 1958, 4, 28537, 13, 51260], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 66, "seek": 21018, "start": 228.48000000000002, "end": 231.12, "text": " And the other end is 0% heads, 100% tails.", "tokens": [51280, 400, 264, 661, 917, 307, 1958, 4, 8050, 11, 2319, 4, 28537, 13, 51412], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 67, "seek": 21018, "start": 231.26000000000002, "end": 231.8, "text": " You got it.", "tokens": [51419, 509, 658, 309, 13, 51446], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 68, "seek": 21018, "start": 231.86, "end": 234.84, "text": " Any point on that line is a valid state of your knowledge.", "tokens": [51449, 2639, 935, 322, 300, 1622, 307, 257, 7363, 1785, 295, 428, 3601, 13, 51598], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12517111769346434, "compression_ratio": 1.620408163265306, "no_speech_prob": 1.555349924374505e-12}, {"id": 69, "seek": 23484, "start": 234.84, "end": 239.08, "text": " You could be sitting right in the middle at a 50-50 state of maximum ignorance.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 727, 312, 3798, 558, 294, 264, 2808, 412, 257, 2625, 12, 2803, 1785, 295, 6674, 25390, 13, 50577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 70, "seek": 23484, "start": 239.48, "end": 243.16, "text": " Or sitting closer to one end at like 70-30 if you think the coin is weighted.", "tokens": [50597, 1610, 3798, 4966, 281, 472, 917, 412, 411, 5285, 12, 3446, 498, 291, 519, 264, 11464, 307, 32807, 13, 50781], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 71, "seek": 23484, "start": 243.54, "end": 243.74, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50800, 865, 13, 50810], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 72, "seek": 23484, "start": 243.82, "end": 250.14000000000001, "text": " And those points at the very ends, the 100% certainties where you know exactly what will happen, those are called the extreme points of the simplex.", "tokens": [50814, 400, 729, 2793, 412, 264, 588, 5314, 11, 264, 2319, 4, 1629, 6097, 689, 291, 458, 2293, 437, 486, 1051, 11, 729, 366, 1219, 264, 8084, 2793, 295, 264, 2199, 87, 13, 51130], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 73, "seek": 23484, "start": 250.54, "end": 253.5, "text": " So far, we are dealing with pure classical logic.", "tokens": [51150, 407, 1400, 11, 321, 366, 6260, 365, 6075, 13735, 9952, 13, 51298], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 74, "seek": 23484, "start": 253.74, "end": 253.94, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [51310, 1033, 13, 51320], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 75, "seek": 23484, "start": 254.06, "end": 254.92000000000002, "text": " So what's the next step?", "tokens": [51326, 407, 437, 311, 264, 958, 1823, 30, 51369], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 76, "seek": 23484, "start": 255.3, "end": 260.82, "text": " The next evolutionary step is to ask how do we mathematically transform one state of knowledge into another?", "tokens": [51388, 440, 958, 27567, 1823, 307, 281, 1029, 577, 360, 321, 44003, 4088, 472, 1785, 295, 3601, 666, 1071, 30, 51664], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 77, "seek": 23484, "start": 261.38, "end": 263.0, "text": " Like how does the system evolve?", "tokens": [51692, 1743, 577, 775, 264, 1185, 16693, 30, 51773], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10397618704468664, "compression_ratio": 1.621212121212121, "no_speech_prob": 1.4839956520573683e-12}, {"id": 78, "seek": 26300, "start": 263.0, "end": 265.2, "text": " Because things change over time.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 721, 1319, 670, 565, 13, 50475], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 79, "seek": 26300, "start": 265.94, "end": 266.9, "text": " Information updates.", "tokens": [50512, 15357, 9205, 13, 50560], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 80, "seek": 26300, "start": 267.16, "end": 267.6, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50573, 7587, 13, 50595], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 81, "seek": 26300, "start": 268.06, "end": 273.44, "text": " In this bare bones framework, a transformation is represented by something called a stochastic map.", "tokens": [50618, 682, 341, 6949, 10491, 8388, 11, 257, 9887, 307, 10379, 538, 746, 1219, 257, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 13, 50887], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 82, "seek": 26300, "start": 273.62, "end": 274.58, "text": " A stochastic map.", "tokens": [50896, 316, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 13, 50944], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 83, "seek": 26300, "start": 274.8, "end": 275.08, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50955, 1033, 13, 50969], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 84, "seek": 26300, "start": 275.42, "end": 282.38, "text": " If you imagine your probabilities as a vertical column of numbers, a stochastic map is a grid of numbers.", "tokens": [50986, 759, 291, 3811, 428, 33783, 382, 257, 9429, 7738, 295, 3547, 11, 257, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 307, 257, 10748, 295, 3547, 13, 51334], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 85, "seek": 26300, "start": 282.8, "end": 288.14, "text": " A matrix that multiplies your current probability distribution to update it into a new one.", "tokens": [51355, 316, 8141, 300, 12788, 530, 428, 2190, 8482, 7316, 281, 5623, 309, 666, 257, 777, 472, 13, 51622], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18890019257863364, "compression_ratio": 1.6768558951965065, "no_speech_prob": 1.755387176761991e-12}, {"id": 86, "seek": 28814, "start": 288.14, "end": 294.08, "text": " The fundamental constraint here is that the columns of this matrix must sum to one.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 8088, 25534, 510, 307, 300, 264, 13766, 295, 341, 8141, 1633, 2408, 281, 472, 13, 50662], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1069462409386268, "compression_ratio": 1.489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.50148578466347e-12}, {"id": 87, "seek": 28814, "start": 294.08, "end": 298.08, "text": " That makes intuitive sense because probability has to be conserved right.", "tokens": [50662, 663, 1669, 21769, 2020, 570, 8482, 575, 281, 312, 1014, 6913, 558, 13, 50862], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1069462409386268, "compression_ratio": 1.489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.50148578466347e-12}, {"id": 88, "seek": 28814, "start": 298.24, "end": 305.44, "text": " You can't put a 100% chance of something happening into a machine and have the machine spit out 120% total chance across all outcomes.", "tokens": [50870, 509, 393, 380, 829, 257, 2319, 4, 2931, 295, 746, 2737, 666, 257, 3479, 293, 362, 264, 3479, 22127, 484, 10411, 4, 3217, 2931, 2108, 439, 10070, 13, 51230], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1069462409386268, "compression_ratio": 1.489795918367347, "no_speech_prob": 1.50148578466347e-12}, {"id": 89, "seek": 30544, "start": 305.44, "end": 306.8, "text": " No, you definitely can't.", "tokens": [50365, 883, 11, 291, 2138, 393, 380, 13, 50433], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 90, "seek": 30544, "start": 307.2, "end": 311.2, "text": " The universe ultimately has to land on one of the alternatives when all is said and done.", "tokens": [50453, 440, 6445, 6284, 575, 281, 2117, 322, 472, 295, 264, 20478, 562, 439, 307, 848, 293, 1096, 13, 50653], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 91, "seek": 30544, "start": 311.38, "end": 314.02, "text": " So the total probability must always anchor at one.", "tokens": [50662, 407, 264, 3217, 8482, 1633, 1009, 18487, 412, 472, 13, 50794], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 92, "seek": 30544, "start": 314.4, "end": 316.9, "text": " So the column normalization ensures exactly that.", "tokens": [50813, 407, 264, 7738, 2710, 2144, 28111, 2293, 300, 13, 50938], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 93, "seek": 30544, "start": 316.9, "end": 323.62, "text": " If you feed the matrix a valid probability distribution, it guarantees you will get a valid probability distribution out the other side.", "tokens": [50938, 759, 291, 3154, 264, 8141, 257, 7363, 8482, 7316, 11, 309, 32567, 291, 486, 483, 257, 7363, 8482, 7316, 484, 264, 661, 1252, 13, 51274], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 94, "seek": 30544, "start": 323.96, "end": 324.02, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51291, 1779, 13, 51294], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 95, "seek": 30544, "start": 324.12, "end": 325.86, "text": " And the text highlights a key concept here.", "tokens": [51299, 400, 264, 2487, 14254, 257, 2141, 3410, 510, 13, 51386], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19132971512643915, "compression_ratio": 1.6804979253112033, "no_speech_prob": 1.1285613285907936e-12}, {"id": 96, "seek": 32586, "start": 325.86, "end": 333.92, "text": " If a transformation perfectly maps distinct inputs to distinct outputs without blurring them together, it's a deterministic map.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 257, 9887, 6239, 11317, 10644, 15743, 281, 10644, 23930, 1553, 14257, 2937, 552, 1214, 11, 309, 311, 257, 15957, 3142, 4471, 13, 50768], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 97, "seek": 32586, "start": 334.16, "end": 335.22, "text": " Like a clean transition.", "tokens": [50780, 1743, 257, 2541, 6034, 13, 50833], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 98, "seek": 32586, "start": 335.52000000000004, "end": 335.92, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50848, 7587, 13, 50868], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 99, "seek": 32586, "start": 336.34000000000003, "end": 344.06, "text": " These deterministic maps, these clean transitions, form the extreme corners of the set of all possible stochastic maps.", "tokens": [50889, 1981, 15957, 3142, 11317, 11, 613, 2541, 23767, 11, 1254, 264, 8084, 12413, 295, 264, 992, 295, 439, 1944, 342, 8997, 2750, 11317, 13, 51275], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 100, "seek": 32586, "start": 344.24, "end": 345.5, "text": " That feels very foundational.", "tokens": [51284, 663, 3417, 588, 32195, 13, 51347], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 101, "seek": 32586, "start": 346.18, "end": 350.96000000000004, "text": " We have probabilities and we have matrices that shuffle those probabilities from one state to another.", "tokens": [51381, 492, 362, 33783, 293, 321, 362, 32284, 300, 39426, 729, 33783, 490, 472, 1785, 281, 1071, 13, 51620], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13969008127848306, "compression_ratio": 1.7364016736401673, "no_speech_prob": 1.4105156929608564e-12}, {"id": 102, "seek": 35096, "start": 350.96, "end": 360.26, "text": " But the author introduces a critical structural constraint, next right, moving from a standard stochastic map to something called a bistochastic map.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 264, 3793, 31472, 257, 4924, 15067, 25534, 11, 958, 558, 11, 2684, 490, 257, 3832, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 281, 746, 1219, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 4471, 13, 50830], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 103, "seek": 35096, "start": 360.34, "end": 361.91999999999996, "text": " This is a major pivot in the logic.", "tokens": [50834, 639, 307, 257, 2563, 14538, 294, 264, 9952, 13, 50913], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 104, "seek": 35096, "start": 362.32, "end": 367.02, "text": " A standard stochastic map only demands that the columns sum to one to conserve probability.", "tokens": [50933, 316, 3832, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 787, 15107, 300, 264, 13766, 2408, 281, 472, 281, 45240, 8482, 13, 51168], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 105, "seek": 35096, "start": 367.58, "end": 374.32, "text": " A bistochastic map, which is sometimes called a doubly stochastic map, demands that the columns in the rows sum to one.", "tokens": [51196, 316, 18209, 8997, 2750, 4471, 11, 597, 307, 2171, 1219, 257, 10831, 356, 342, 8997, 2750, 4471, 11, 15107, 300, 264, 13766, 294, 264, 13241, 2408, 281, 472, 13, 51533], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 106, "seek": 35096, "start": 374.44, "end": 376.03999999999996, "text": " Wait, why do we care about the rows?", "tokens": [51539, 3802, 11, 983, 360, 321, 1127, 466, 264, 13241, 30, 51619], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 107, "seek": 35096, "start": 376.17999999999995, "end": 378.74, "text": " Conserving probability down the columns makes physical sense.", "tokens": [51626, 6923, 20186, 8482, 760, 264, 13766, 1669, 4001, 2020, 13, 51754], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11638600485665458, "compression_ratio": 1.9450980392156862, "no_speech_prob": 1.5071282980297362e-12}, {"id": 108, "seek": 37874, "start": 378.74, "end": 382.86, "text": " But what physical reality does making the rows sum to one actually represent?", "tokens": [50365, 583, 437, 4001, 4103, 775, 1455, 264, 13241, 2408, 281, 472, 767, 2906, 30, 50571], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 109, "seek": 37874, "start": 383.2, "end": 387.90000000000003, "text": " Requiring the rows to sum to one imposes a profound physical constraint on the system.", "tokens": [50588, 42029, 5057, 264, 13241, 281, 2408, 281, 472, 704, 4201, 257, 14382, 4001, 25534, 322, 264, 1185, 13, 50823], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 110, "seek": 37874, "start": 388.76, "end": 391.44, "text": " Reversibility and the preservation of maximum uncertainty.", "tokens": [50866, 1300, 840, 2841, 293, 264, 27257, 295, 6674, 15697, 13, 51000], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 111, "seek": 37874, "start": 391.68, "end": 392.76, "text": " Preservation of uncertainty.", "tokens": [51012, 2718, 6864, 295, 15697, 13, 51066], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 112, "seek": 37874, "start": 393.18, "end": 393.36, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51087, 865, 13, 51096], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 113, "seek": 37874, "start": 393.44, "end": 403.76, "text": " If you take the uniform distribution, the state where everything is equally likely, like our perfectly fair 50-50 coin, and you apply a bistochastic map to it, you get the uniform distribution right back.", "tokens": [51100, 759, 291, 747, 264, 9452, 7316, 11, 264, 1785, 689, 1203, 307, 12309, 3700, 11, 411, 527, 6239, 3143, 2625, 12, 2803, 11464, 11, 293, 291, 3079, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 4471, 281, 309, 11, 291, 483, 264, 9452, 7316, 558, 646, 13, 51616], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 114, "seek": 37874, "start": 403.88, "end": 404.28000000000003, "text": " Oh, I see.", "tokens": [51622, 876, 11, 286, 536, 13, 51642], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 115, "seek": 37874, "start": 404.34000000000003, "end": 406.56, "text": " So the map preserves your complete ignorance.", "tokens": [51645, 407, 264, 4471, 1183, 9054, 428, 3566, 25390, 13, 51756], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 116, "seek": 37874, "start": 406.82, "end": 407.12, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51769, 7587, 13, 51784], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11195841431617737, "compression_ratio": 1.7516556291390728, "no_speech_prob": 1.4954081809653852e-12}, {"id": 117, "seek": 40712, "start": 407.12, "end": 414.96, "text": " If you start out knowing absolutely nothing about the outcome a bistochastic process guarantees, you will still know absolutely nothing after it happens.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 291, 722, 484, 5276, 3122, 1825, 466, 264, 9700, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 1399, 32567, 11, 291, 486, 920, 458, 3122, 1825, 934, 309, 2314, 13, 50757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12347597099212279, "compression_ratio": 1.5779816513761469, "no_speech_prob": 1.350988548462495e-12}, {"id": 118, "seek": 40712, "start": 415.16, "end": 416.4, "text": " Okay, let's unpack this.", "tokens": [50767, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 26699, 341, 13, 50829], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12347597099212279, "compression_ratio": 1.5779816513761469, "no_speech_prob": 1.350988548462495e-12}, {"id": 119, "seek": 40712, "start": 416.92, "end": 423.34000000000003, "text": " The author brings in a heavy-hitter mathematical theorem to explain what these bistochastic maps actually look like under the hood.", "tokens": [50855, 440, 3793, 5607, 294, 257, 4676, 12, 71, 3904, 18894, 20904, 281, 2903, 437, 613, 18209, 8997, 2750, 11317, 767, 574, 411, 833, 264, 13376, 13, 51176], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12347597099212279, "compression_ratio": 1.5779816513761469, "no_speech_prob": 1.350988548462495e-12}, {"id": 120, "seek": 40712, "start": 423.8, "end": 425.5, "text": " The Berkhoff-Von Neumann theorem.", "tokens": [51199, 440, 5637, 74, 1289, 602, 12, 53, 266, 1734, 449, 969, 20904, 13, 51284], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12347597099212279, "compression_ratio": 1.5779816513761469, "no_speech_prob": 1.350988548462495e-12}, {"id": 121, "seek": 42550, "start": 425.5, "end": 427.98, "text": " It is a stunning piece of geometry.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 257, 18550, 2522, 295, 18426, 13, 50489], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10569608572757605, "compression_ratio": 1.4230769230769231, "no_speech_prob": 1.838460373521089e-12}, {"id": 122, "seek": 42550, "start": 428.86, "end": 442.32, "text": " The Berkhoff-Von Neumann theorem states that if you take every single possible bistochastic matrix and group them together, they form a highly specific geometric shape in multidimensional space called the Berkhoff polytope.", "tokens": [50533, 440, 5637, 74, 1289, 602, 12, 53, 266, 1734, 449, 969, 20904, 4368, 300, 498, 291, 747, 633, 2167, 1944, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 293, 1594, 552, 1214, 11, 436, 1254, 257, 5405, 2685, 33246, 3909, 294, 2120, 327, 332, 11075, 1901, 1219, 264, 5637, 74, 1289, 602, 6754, 83, 1114, 13, 51206], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10569608572757605, "compression_ratio": 1.4230769230769231, "no_speech_prob": 1.838460373521089e-12}, {"id": 123, "seek": 44232, "start": 442.32, "end": 449.92, "text": " Just to make sure we are visualizing this correctly, a polytope is essentially a geometric shape with flat sides and sharp corners, right?", "tokens": [50365, 1449, 281, 652, 988, 321, 366, 5056, 3319, 341, 8944, 11, 257, 6754, 83, 1114, 307, 4476, 257, 33246, 3909, 365, 4962, 4881, 293, 8199, 12413, 11, 558, 30, 50745], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0563347881490534, "compression_ratio": 1.2321428571428572, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947621691437709e-12}, {"id": 124, "seek": 44992, "start": 449.92, "end": 456.18, "text": " Like a 2D polygon or a 3D polyhedron, like a cube, but generalized into any number of higher dimensions.", "tokens": [50365, 1743, 257, 568, 35, 48242, 420, 257, 805, 35, 6754, 27096, 2044, 11, 411, 257, 13728, 11, 457, 44498, 666, 604, 1230, 295, 2946, 12819, 13, 50678], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16285652809954704, "compression_ratio": 1.5524193548387097, "no_speech_prob": 1.5977305199352254e-12}, {"id": 125, "seek": 44992, "start": 456.64000000000004, "end": 458.08000000000004, "text": " That is a perfect visualization.", "tokens": [50701, 663, 307, 257, 2176, 25801, 13, 50773], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16285652809954704, "compression_ratio": 1.5524193548387097, "no_speech_prob": 1.5977305199352254e-12}, {"id": 126, "seek": 44992, "start": 459.04, "end": 461.72, "text": " It's a closed shape defined by flat boundaries.", "tokens": [50821, 467, 311, 257, 5395, 3909, 7642, 538, 4962, 13180, 13, 50955], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16285652809954704, "compression_ratio": 1.5524193548387097, "no_speech_prob": 1.5977305199352254e-12}, {"id": 127, "seek": 44992, "start": 462.82, "end": 465.96000000000004, "text": " The theorem proves something extraordinary about this shape, though.", "tokens": [51010, 440, 20904, 25019, 746, 10581, 466, 341, 3909, 11, 1673, 13, 51167], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16285652809954704, "compression_ratio": 1.5524193548387097, "no_speech_prob": 1.5977305199352254e-12}, {"id": 128, "seek": 44992, "start": 466.36, "end": 473.52000000000004, "text": " The extreme corners of the Berkhoff polytope, the sharpest points of the geometry, are exactly the classical permutation matrices.", "tokens": [51187, 440, 8084, 12413, 295, 264, 5637, 74, 1289, 602, 6754, 83, 1114, 11, 264, 8199, 377, 2793, 295, 264, 18426, 11, 366, 2293, 264, 13735, 4784, 11380, 32284, 13, 51545], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16285652809954704, "compression_ratio": 1.5524193548387097, "no_speech_prob": 1.5977305199352254e-12}, {"id": 129, "seek": 47352, "start": 474.08, "end": 479.62, "text": " Permutation matrices, by that we mean maps that do nothing but cleanly swap labels around, right?", "tokens": [50393, 41006, 11380, 32284, 11, 538, 300, 321, 914, 11317, 300, 360, 1825, 457, 2541, 356, 18135, 16949, 926, 11, 558, 30, 50670], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 130, "seek": 47352, "start": 479.84, "end": 480.32, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50681, 7587, 13, 50705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 131, "seek": 47352, "start": 480.94, "end": 483.2, "text": " Taking outcome A and calling it outcome B.", "tokens": [50736, 17837, 9700, 316, 293, 5141, 309, 9700, 363, 13, 50849], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 132, "seek": 47352, "start": 483.7, "end": 485.82, "text": " Taking outcome B and calling it outcome C.", "tokens": [50874, 17837, 9700, 363, 293, 5141, 309, 9700, 383, 13, 50980], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 133, "seek": 47352, "start": 486.46, "end": 488.35999999999996, "text": " Pure reversible label swapping.", "tokens": [51012, 29474, 44788, 7645, 1693, 10534, 13, 51107], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 134, "seek": 47352, "start": 488.52, "end": 488.74, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51115, 1779, 13, 51126], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 135, "seek": 47352, "start": 488.79999999999995, "end": 500.12, "text": " The theorem proves that any bistochastic matrix, no matter how complicated or fuzzy it looks, can be written mathematically as a convex combination, a weighted average of these pure classical label swaps.", "tokens": [51129, 440, 20904, 25019, 300, 604, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 11, 572, 1871, 577, 6179, 420, 34710, 309, 1542, 11, 393, 312, 3720, 44003, 382, 257, 42432, 6562, 11, 257, 32807, 4274, 295, 613, 6075, 13735, 7645, 1693, 2382, 13, 51695], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14321407904991737, "compression_ratio": 1.6704980842911878, "no_speech_prob": 1.7281809663871606e-12}, {"id": 136, "seek": 50012, "start": 500.12, "end": 504.12, "text": " The text actually walks through the proof of this, and the mechanism is fascinating.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2487, 767, 12896, 807, 264, 8177, 295, 341, 11, 293, 264, 7513, 307, 10343, 13, 50565], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08271576994556491, "compression_ratio": 1.7137931034482758, "no_speech_prob": 2.2014065917397296e-12}, {"id": 137, "seek": 50012, "start": 504.42, "end": 506.88, "text": " They use something called a bipartite support graph.", "tokens": [50580, 814, 764, 746, 1219, 257, 28741, 642, 1406, 4295, 13, 50703], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08271576994556491, "compression_ratio": 1.7137931034482758, "no_speech_prob": 2.2014065917397296e-12}, {"id": 138, "seek": 50012, "start": 507.12, "end": 510.34000000000003, "text": " Yeah, they imagine drawing a set of dots for the rows and a set of dots for the columns.", "tokens": [50715, 865, 11, 436, 3811, 6316, 257, 992, 295, 15026, 337, 264, 13241, 293, 257, 992, 295, 15026, 337, 264, 13766, 13, 50876], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08271576994556491, "compression_ratio": 1.7137931034482758, "no_speech_prob": 2.2014065917397296e-12}, {"id": 139, "seek": 50012, "start": 511.08, "end": 516.22, "text": " And they draw lines connecting a row to a column if there's a non-zero probability sitting in that spot in matrix.", "tokens": [50913, 400, 436, 2642, 3876, 11015, 257, 5386, 281, 257, 7738, 498, 456, 311, 257, 2107, 12, 32226, 8482, 3798, 294, 300, 4008, 294, 8141, 13, 51170], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08271576994556491, "compression_ratio": 1.7137931034482758, "no_speech_prob": 2.2014065917397296e-12}, {"id": 140, "seek": 50012, "start": 516.62, "end": 528.36, "text": " And the brilliant part is they prove that if the matrix isn't just a simple, clean label swap, this web of connections must contain a cycle, a closed loop.", "tokens": [51190, 400, 264, 10248, 644, 307, 436, 7081, 300, 498, 264, 8141, 1943, 380, 445, 257, 2199, 11, 2541, 7645, 18135, 11, 341, 3670, 295, 9271, 1633, 5304, 257, 6586, 11, 257, 5395, 6367, 13, 51777], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08271576994556491, "compression_ratio": 1.7137931034482758, "no_speech_prob": 2.2014065917397296e-12}, {"id": 141, "seek": 52836, "start": 528.36, "end": 531.78, "text": " That loop is the vulnerability that proof exploits.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 6367, 307, 264, 24210, 300, 8177, 12382, 1208, 13, 50536], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08039331436157227, "compression_ratio": 1.6391304347826088, "no_speech_prob": 1.4381194802723374e-12}, {"id": 142, "seek": 52836, "start": 532.4, "end": 538.76, "text": " Because there's a closed cycle of probabilities, you can slightly tweak the numbers running around that loop.", "tokens": [50567, 1436, 456, 311, 257, 5395, 6586, 295, 33783, 11, 291, 393, 4748, 29879, 264, 3547, 2614, 926, 300, 6367, 13, 50885], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08039331436157227, "compression_ratio": 1.6391304347826088, "no_speech_prob": 1.4381194802723374e-12}, {"id": 143, "seek": 52836, "start": 538.86, "end": 540.1, "text": " Like shifting the values.", "tokens": [50890, 1743, 17573, 264, 4190, 13, 50952], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08039331436157227, "compression_ratio": 1.6391304347826088, "no_speech_prob": 1.4381194802723374e-12}, {"id": 144, "seek": 52836, "start": 540.62, "end": 540.86, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50978, 1779, 13, 50990], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08039331436157227, "compression_ratio": 1.6391304347826088, "no_speech_prob": 1.4381194802723374e-12}, {"id": 145, "seek": 52836, "start": 540.96, "end": 549.16, "text": " You can add a microscopic amount of probability to one connection and subtract that exact same amount from the next connection in the circle, rippling this change all the way around.", "tokens": [50995, 509, 393, 909, 257, 47897, 2372, 295, 8482, 281, 472, 4984, 293, 16390, 300, 1900, 912, 2372, 490, 264, 958, 4984, 294, 264, 6329, 11, 367, 2488, 1688, 341, 1319, 439, 264, 636, 926, 13, 51405], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08039331436157227, "compression_ratio": 1.6391304347826088, "no_speech_prob": 1.4381194802723374e-12}, {"id": 146, "seek": 54916, "start": 549.16, "end": 553.8399999999999, "text": " Oh, wow. So you just ripple a little plus epsilon, minus epsilon around the circle.", "tokens": [50365, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 407, 291, 445, 40688, 257, 707, 1804, 17889, 11, 3175, 17889, 926, 264, 6329, 13, 50599], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11696736530591083, "compression_ratio": 1.6219512195121952, "no_speech_prob": 1.6102624875863336e-12}, {"id": 147, "seek": 54916, "start": 553.9399999999999, "end": 558.26, "text": " And because it's a closed loop, the row totals and the column totals never actually change.", "tokens": [50604, 400, 570, 309, 311, 257, 5395, 6367, 11, 264, 5386, 1993, 1124, 293, 264, 7738, 1993, 1124, 1128, 767, 1319, 13, 50820], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11696736530591083, "compression_ratio": 1.6219512195121952, "no_speech_prob": 1.6102624875863336e-12}, {"id": 148, "seek": 54916, "start": 558.3199999999999, "end": 560.04, "text": " They remain perfectly balanced at one.", "tokens": [50823, 814, 6222, 6239, 13902, 412, 472, 13, 50909], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11696736530591083, "compression_ratio": 1.6219512195121952, "no_speech_prob": 1.6102624875863336e-12}, {"id": 149, "seek": 54916, "start": 560.1, "end": 561.4, "text": " You're hitting on the core mechanism.", "tokens": [50912, 509, 434, 8850, 322, 264, 4965, 7513, 13, 50977], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11696736530591083, "compression_ratio": 1.6219512195121952, "no_speech_prob": 1.6102624875863336e-12}, {"id": 150, "seek": 54916, "start": 561.7199999999999, "end": 570.0, "text": " If you can slightly tweak a matrix in two different opposing directions around this loop and still maintain a perfectly valid bistochastic matrix,", "tokens": [50993, 759, 291, 393, 4748, 29879, 257, 8141, 294, 732, 819, 27890, 11095, 926, 341, 6367, 293, 920, 6909, 257, 6239, 7363, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 11, 51407], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11696736530591083, "compression_ratio": 1.6219512195121952, "no_speech_prob": 1.6102624875863336e-12}, {"id": 151, "seek": 57000, "start": 570.0, "end": 574.8, "text": " then geometrically that matrix cannot be an extreme corner of the shape.", "tokens": [50365, 550, 12956, 81, 984, 300, 8141, 2644, 312, 364, 8084, 4538, 295, 264, 3909, 13, 50605], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16568277098915793, "compression_ratio": 1.6682464454976302, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938708043684245e-12}, {"id": 152, "seek": 57000, "start": 574.86, "end": 575.82, "text": " Because it has wiggle room.", "tokens": [50608, 1436, 309, 575, 33377, 1808, 13, 50656], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16568277098915793, "compression_ratio": 1.6682464454976302, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938708043684245e-12}, {"id": 153, "seek": 57000, "start": 576.14, "end": 583.26, "text": " Exactly. It has wiggle room, meaning it must be somewhere flat on a face or somewhere deep inside the volume of the polycope.", "tokens": [50672, 7587, 13, 467, 575, 33377, 1808, 11, 3620, 309, 1633, 312, 4079, 4962, 322, 257, 1851, 420, 4079, 2452, 1854, 264, 5523, 295, 264, 6754, 13960, 13, 51028], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16568277098915793, "compression_ratio": 1.6682464454976302, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938708043684245e-12}, {"id": 154, "seek": 57000, "start": 584.08, "end": 590.28, "text": " Therefore, the only matrices that are the extreme immovable corners are the ones with no loops at all.", "tokens": [51069, 7504, 11, 264, 787, 32284, 300, 366, 264, 8084, 566, 3280, 17915, 12413, 366, 264, 2306, 365, 572, 16121, 412, 439, 13, 51379], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16568277098915793, "compression_ratio": 1.6682464454976302, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938708043684245e-12}, {"id": 155, "seek": 57000, "start": 591.0, "end": 592.24, "text": " The pure permutations.", "tokens": [51415, 440, 6075, 4784, 325, 763, 13, 51477], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16568277098915793, "compression_ratio": 1.6682464454976302, "no_speech_prob": 1.3938708043684245e-12}, {"id": 156, "seek": 59224, "start": 592.24, "end": 599.5, "text": " So any process that preserves the uniform distribution is ultimately just a fuzzy weighted average of perfectly clean label swaps.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 604, 1399, 300, 1183, 9054, 264, 9452, 7316, 307, 6284, 445, 257, 34710, 32807, 4274, 295, 6239, 2541, 7645, 1693, 2382, 13, 50728], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16421716759003788, "compression_ratio": 1.4979757085020242, "no_speech_prob": 1.543102885054326e-12}, {"id": 157, "seek": 59224, "start": 599.7, "end": 599.82, "text": " Ah.", "tokens": [50738, 2438, 13, 50744], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16421716759003788, "compression_ratio": 1.4979757085020242, "no_speech_prob": 1.543102885054326e-12}, {"id": 158, "seek": 59224, "start": 600.2, "end": 600.88, "text": " That's wild.", "tokens": [50763, 663, 311, 4868, 13, 50797], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16421716759003788, "compression_ratio": 1.4979757085020242, "no_speech_prob": 1.543102885054326e-12}, {"id": 159, "seek": 59224, "start": 601.4, "end": 609.08, "text": " But then the text introduces another layer to this called majorization, specifically the Hardy Littlewood-Polya majorization concept.", "tokens": [50823, 583, 550, 264, 2487, 31472, 1071, 4583, 281, 341, 1219, 2563, 2144, 11, 4682, 264, 43930, 8022, 6092, 12, 47, 16453, 64, 2563, 2144, 3410, 13, 51207], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16421716759003788, "compression_ratio": 1.4979757085020242, "no_speech_prob": 1.543102885054326e-12}, {"id": 160, "seek": 59224, "start": 609.58, "end": 614.08, "text": " This theorem connects our geometric bistochastic maps to the physical concept of mixing.", "tokens": [51232, 639, 20904, 16967, 527, 33246, 18209, 8997, 2750, 11317, 281, 264, 4001, 3410, 295, 11983, 13, 51457], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16421716759003788, "compression_ratio": 1.4979757085020242, "no_speech_prob": 1.543102885054326e-12}, {"id": 161, "seek": 61408, "start": 614.08, "end": 622.26, "text": " It states that if you apply a bistochastic map to any probability distribution, the resulting distribution is always majorized by the original one.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 4368, 300, 498, 291, 3079, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 4471, 281, 604, 8482, 7316, 11, 264, 16505, 7316, 307, 1009, 2563, 1602, 538, 264, 3380, 472, 13, 50774], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 162, "seek": 61408, "start": 622.76, "end": 623.2, "text": " Majorized?", "tokens": [50799, 15581, 1602, 30, 50821], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 163, "seek": 61408, "start": 623.32, "end": 623.44, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50827, 1779, 13, 50833], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 164, "seek": 61408, "start": 623.44, "end": 624.34, "text": " Meaning what, exactly?", "tokens": [50833, 19948, 437, 11, 2293, 30, 50878], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 165, "seek": 61408, "start": 624.36, "end": 628.46, "text": " In plain terms, the distribution is forced to become more uniform, more smeared out.", "tokens": [50879, 682, 11121, 2115, 11, 264, 7316, 307, 7579, 281, 1813, 544, 9452, 11, 544, 41818, 1642, 484, 13, 51084], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 166, "seek": 61408, "start": 628.7800000000001, "end": 630.98, "text": " It can never become more concentrated or more certain.", "tokens": [51100, 467, 393, 1128, 1813, 544, 21321, 420, 544, 1629, 13, 51210], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 167, "seek": 61408, "start": 631.12, "end": 634.88, "text": " Wait, making things more uniform, forcing a system to become more smeared out?", "tokens": [51217, 3802, 11, 1455, 721, 544, 9452, 11, 19030, 257, 1185, 281, 1813, 544, 41818, 1642, 484, 30, 51405], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 168, "seek": 61408, "start": 635.0600000000001, "end": 636.5200000000001, "text": " Isn't that just entropy increasing?", "tokens": [51414, 6998, 380, 300, 445, 30867, 5662, 30, 51487], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 169, "seek": 61408, "start": 636.6800000000001, "end": 638.58, "text": " Are we secretly doing thermodynamics here?", "tokens": [51495, 2014, 321, 22611, 884, 8810, 35483, 510, 30, 51590], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 170, "seek": 61408, "start": 638.62, "end": 639.9000000000001, "text": " It sounds a lot like it, doesn't it?", "tokens": [51592, 467, 3263, 257, 688, 411, 309, 11, 1177, 380, 309, 30, 51656], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11469520685326962, "compression_ratio": 1.7728813559322034, "no_speech_prob": 2.2013851245367144e-12}, {"id": 171, "seek": 63990, "start": 639.9, "end": 649.76, "text": " Because if I take a perfectly ordered brand new deck of plain cards, Ace of Spades, on top two of spades, next, all the way down, that represents highly concentrated certain probability.", "tokens": [50365, 1436, 498, 286, 747, 257, 6239, 8866, 3360, 777, 9341, 295, 11121, 5632, 11, 24900, 295, 1738, 2977, 11, 322, 1192, 732, 295, 637, 2977, 11, 958, 11, 439, 264, 636, 760, 11, 300, 8855, 5405, 21321, 1629, 8482, 13, 50858], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 172, "seek": 63990, "start": 650.0, "end": 650.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50870, 1779, 13, 50882], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 173, "seek": 63990, "start": 650.38, "end": 651.62, "text": " You know exactly where everything is.", "tokens": [50889, 509, 458, 2293, 689, 1203, 307, 13, 50951], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 174, "seek": 63990, "start": 651.72, "end": 657.0, "text": " But if I shuffle it, which is physically applying random permutations, it gets more mixed up.", "tokens": [50956, 583, 498, 286, 39426, 309, 11, 597, 307, 9762, 9275, 4974, 4784, 325, 763, 11, 309, 2170, 544, 7467, 493, 13, 51220], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 175, "seek": 63990, "start": 657.0799999999999, "end": 659.48, "text": " I am literally applying bistochastic maps.", "tokens": [51224, 286, 669, 3736, 9275, 18209, 8997, 2750, 11317, 13, 51344], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 176, "seek": 63990, "start": 660.0799999999999, "end": 665.5799999999999, "text": " And the deck gets closer and closer to a totally uniform, random state where any card could be anywhere.", "tokens": [51374, 400, 264, 9341, 2170, 4966, 293, 4966, 281, 257, 3879, 9452, 11, 4974, 1785, 689, 604, 2920, 727, 312, 4992, 13, 51649], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 177, "seek": 63990, "start": 665.98, "end": 667.96, "text": " That is the definition of entropy increasing.", "tokens": [51669, 663, 307, 264, 7123, 295, 30867, 5662, 13, 51768], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11635876470996488, "compression_ratio": 1.6476190476190475, "no_speech_prob": 1.5733018186858683e-12}, {"id": 178, "seek": 66796, "start": 667.96, "end": 671.14, "text": " The card shuffling analogy captures the mathematics perfectly.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2920, 402, 1245, 1688, 21663, 27986, 264, 18666, 6239, 13, 50524], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 179, "seek": 66796, "start": 671.6800000000001, "end": 675.84, "text": " But what's fascinating here is the author's vital clarification on why this is happening.", "tokens": [50551, 583, 437, 311, 10343, 510, 307, 264, 3793, 311, 11707, 34449, 322, 983, 341, 307, 2737, 13, 50759], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 180, "seek": 66796, "start": 676.4200000000001, "end": 679.82, "text": " Yes, entropy cannot decrease under bistochastic evolution.", "tokens": [50788, 1079, 11, 30867, 2644, 11514, 833, 18209, 8997, 2750, 9303, 13, 50958], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 181, "seek": 66796, "start": 680.48, "end": 687.86, "text": " But in this framework, this is not a physical thermodynamic postulate that we arbitrarily stapled onto our math.", "tokens": [50991, 583, 294, 341, 8388, 11, 341, 307, 406, 257, 4001, 8810, 34988, 2183, 5256, 300, 321, 19071, 3289, 11135, 15551, 3911, 527, 5221, 13, 51360], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 182, "seek": 66796, "start": 687.98, "end": 688.12, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51366, 1779, 13, 51373], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 183, "seek": 66796, "start": 688.1600000000001, "end": 693.98, "text": " We didn't just walk into the room, write the second law of thermodynamics on the chalkboard, and demand that the universe obey it.", "tokens": [51375, 492, 994, 380, 445, 1792, 666, 264, 1808, 11, 2464, 264, 1150, 2101, 295, 8810, 35483, 322, 264, 28660, 3787, 11, 293, 4733, 300, 264, 6445, 19297, 309, 13, 51666], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 184, "seek": 66796, "start": 694.34, "end": 695.26, "text": " We didn't have to.", "tokens": [51684, 492, 994, 380, 362, 281, 13, 51730], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12018025332483752, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.9506458080742473e-12}, {"id": 185, "seek": 69526, "start": 695.26, "end": 703.1, "text": " The non-decrease of entropy is a structural, inescapable consequence of the geometry of the Berkhoff polytope itself.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2107, 12, 1479, 14066, 651, 295, 30867, 307, 257, 15067, 11, 294, 279, 9485, 712, 18326, 295, 264, 18426, 295, 264, 5637, 74, 1289, 602, 6754, 83, 1114, 2564, 13, 50757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 186, "seek": 69526, "start": 703.4, "end": 709.9399999999999, "text": " Because bistochastic maps are, by geometric definition, just weighted averages of permutations.", "tokens": [50772, 1436, 18209, 8997, 2750, 11317, 366, 11, 538, 33246, 7123, 11, 445, 32807, 42257, 295, 4784, 325, 763, 13, 51099], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 187, "seek": 69526, "start": 710.3, "end": 713.88, "text": " Applying them inherently mixes and dilutes the probability.", "tokens": [51117, 3132, 7310, 552, 27993, 37121, 293, 11504, 1819, 264, 8482, 13, 51296], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 188, "seek": 69526, "start": 714.04, "end": 717.06, "text": " And mathematical structure forces the entropy to increase.", "tokens": [51304, 400, 18894, 3877, 5874, 264, 30867, 281, 3488, 13, 51455], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 189, "seek": 69526, "start": 717.4399999999999, "end": 720.54, "text": " The thermodynamics falls out of the geometry completely for free.", "tokens": [51474, 440, 8810, 35483, 8804, 484, 295, 264, 18426, 2584, 337, 1737, 13, 51629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 190, "seek": 69526, "start": 720.6, "end": 720.98, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51632, 7587, 13, 51651], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13503093135600186, "compression_ratio": 1.6150793650793651, "no_speech_prob": 1.5248942516685182e-12}, {"id": 191, "seek": 72098, "start": 720.98, "end": 724.5, "text": " That is deeply satisfying. It removes the magic from thermodynamics.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 8760, 18348, 13, 467, 30445, 264, 5585, 490, 8810, 35483, 13, 50541], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 192, "seek": 72098, "start": 725.14, "end": 729.2, "text": " But looking at the assumption ledger, the author keeps track of everything we've explored so far.", "tokens": [50573, 583, 1237, 412, 264, 15302, 4684, 1321, 11, 264, 3793, 5965, 2837, 295, 1203, 321, 600, 24016, 370, 1400, 13, 50776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 193, "seek": 72098, "start": 729.9, "end": 737.96, "text": " Probability simplices, bistochastic matrices, entropy increasing through permutations is perfectly 100% classical.", "tokens": [50811, 8736, 2310, 6883, 1473, 11, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 11, 30867, 5662, 807, 4784, 325, 763, 307, 6239, 2319, 4, 13735, 13, 51214], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 194, "seek": 72098, "start": 738.12, "end": 740.54, "text": " Yes, we are still just flipping coins and shuffling cards.", "tokens": [51222, 1079, 11, 321, 366, 920, 445, 26886, 13561, 293, 402, 1245, 1688, 5632, 13, 51343], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 195, "seek": 72098, "start": 740.7, "end": 742.4200000000001, "text": " There is zero quantum mechanics happening.", "tokens": [51351, 821, 307, 4018, 13018, 12939, 2737, 13, 51437], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 196, "seek": 72098, "start": 742.5, "end": 743.24, "text": " None whatsoever.", "tokens": [51441, 14492, 17076, 13, 51478], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 197, "seek": 72098, "start": 743.58, "end": 747.26, "text": " A classical computer can simulate everything we have discussed with absolute perfection.", "tokens": [51495, 316, 13735, 3820, 393, 27817, 1203, 321, 362, 7152, 365, 8236, 19708, 13, 51679], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 198, "seek": 72098, "start": 747.48, "end": 747.96, "text": " Entirely.", "tokens": [51690, 3951, 621, 356, 13, 51714], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1213822281151487, "compression_ratio": 1.549689440993789, "no_speech_prob": 1.455292483742987e-12}, {"id": 199, "seek": 74796, "start": 747.96, "end": 751.0600000000001, "text": " So what breaks this beautiful, intuitive classical picture?", "tokens": [50365, 407, 437, 9857, 341, 2238, 11, 21769, 13735, 3036, 30, 50520], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15753306448459625, "compression_ratio": 1.5343137254901962, "no_speech_prob": 2.4086975903420127e-12}, {"id": 200, "seek": 74796, "start": 751.5600000000001, "end": 759.0600000000001, "text": " What geometric crisis forces us to invent quantum amplitudes and leave the comfortable everyday world of classical probabilities behind?", "tokens": [50545, 708, 33246, 5869, 5874, 505, 281, 7962, 13018, 9731, 16451, 293, 1856, 264, 4619, 7429, 1002, 295, 13735, 33783, 2261, 30, 50920], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15753306448459625, "compression_ratio": 1.5343137254901962, "no_speech_prob": 2.4086975903420127e-12}, {"id": 201, "seek": 74796, "start": 759.34, "end": 763.24, "text": " That brings us to the first major fracture in the classical foundation.", "tokens": [50934, 663, 5607, 505, 281, 264, 700, 2563, 36877, 294, 264, 13735, 7030, 13, 51129], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15753306448459625, "compression_ratio": 1.5343137254901962, "no_speech_prob": 2.4086975903420127e-12}, {"id": 202, "seek": 74796, "start": 763.9000000000001, "end": 766.0, "text": " This is where real numbers begin to fail us.", "tokens": [51162, 639, 307, 689, 957, 3547, 1841, 281, 3061, 505, 13, 51267], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15753306448459625, "compression_ratio": 1.5343137254901962, "no_speech_prob": 2.4086975903420127e-12}, {"id": 203, "seek": 76600, "start": 766.0, "end": 768.0, "text": " Let's dive into chapter 3.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 9192, 666, 7187, 805, 13, 50465], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 204, "seek": 76600, "start": 768.66, "end": 774.94, "text": " The author introduces two new terms to describe specific flavors of these bistochastic matrices we've been talking about.", "tokens": [50498, 440, 3793, 31472, 732, 777, 2115, 281, 6786, 2685, 16303, 295, 613, 18209, 8997, 2750, 32284, 321, 600, 668, 1417, 466, 13, 50812], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 205, "seek": 76600, "start": 776.06, "end": 778.58, "text": " Orthostochastic and unistochastic.", "tokens": [50868, 27554, 555, 8997, 2750, 293, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 13, 50994], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 206, "seek": 76600, "start": 779.0, "end": 781.38, "text": " Let's get these defined clearly for the listener.", "tokens": [51015, 961, 311, 483, 613, 7642, 4448, 337, 264, 31569, 13, 51134], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 207, "seek": 76600, "start": 781.88, "end": 784.54, "text": " We established what a bistochastic matrix is.", "tokens": [51159, 492, 7545, 437, 257, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 307, 13, 51292], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 208, "seek": 76600, "start": 784.9, "end": 788.68, "text": " A grid of probabilities where both rows and columns sum to one.", "tokens": [51310, 316, 10748, 295, 33783, 689, 1293, 13241, 293, 13766, 2408, 281, 472, 13, 51499], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 209, "seek": 76600, "start": 789.22, "end": 791.0, "text": " But we have to ask a deeper question.", "tokens": [51526, 583, 321, 362, 281, 1029, 257, 7731, 1168, 13, 51615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 210, "seek": 76600, "start": 791.32, "end": 793.56, "text": " Where do those probabilities actually come from?", "tokens": [51631, 2305, 360, 729, 33783, 767, 808, 490, 30, 51743], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 211, "seek": 76600, "start": 793.74, "end": 793.98, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51752, 1779, 13, 51764], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.21784029780207453, "compression_ratio": 1.6305970149253732, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283590345532287e-12}, {"id": 212, "seek": 79398, "start": 793.98, "end": 798.34, "text": " In textbook quantum mechanics, we know that probabilities are not the foundational layer.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 25591, 13018, 12939, 11, 321, 458, 300, 33783, 366, 406, 264, 32195, 4583, 13, 50583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 213, "seek": 79398, "start": 799.28, "end": 802.6800000000001, "text": " Probabilities are generated by squaring underlying amplitudes.", "tokens": [50630, 8736, 6167, 366, 10833, 538, 2339, 1921, 14217, 9731, 16451, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 214, "seek": 79398, "start": 802.88, "end": 803.7, "text": " The famous Born rule.", "tokens": [50810, 440, 4618, 29808, 4978, 13, 50851], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 215, "seek": 79398, "start": 803.96, "end": 808.78, "text": " You take the wave function, you square the absolute value, and boom, you have your probability of finding the particle.", "tokens": [50864, 509, 747, 264, 5772, 2445, 11, 291, 3732, 264, 8236, 2158, 11, 293, 9351, 11, 291, 362, 428, 8482, 295, 5006, 264, 12359, 13, 51105], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 216, "seek": 79398, "start": 808.98, "end": 809.96, "text": " That's the mechanism.", "tokens": [51115, 663, 311, 264, 7513, 13, 51164], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 217, "seek": 79398, "start": 810.32, "end": 816.64, "text": " So an orthostochastic matrix is one where the probabilities in the grid come from squaring the entries of a real orthogonal matrix.", "tokens": [51182, 407, 364, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 8141, 307, 472, 689, 264, 33783, 294, 264, 10748, 808, 490, 2339, 1921, 264, 23041, 295, 257, 957, 41488, 8141, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 218, "seek": 79398, "start": 816.84, "end": 822.7, "text": " Which means we are building our underlying amplitude layer using only real numbers, standard positive and negative numbers.", "tokens": [51508, 3013, 1355, 321, 366, 2390, 527, 14217, 27433, 4583, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 11, 3832, 3353, 293, 3671, 3547, 13, 51801], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11566186145069153, "compression_ratio": 1.79874213836478, "no_speech_prob": 2.0442430300610104e-12}, {"id": 219, "seek": 82270, "start": 822.7, "end": 830.48, "text": " Exactly. And a unistochastic matrix is one where the probabilities come from the squared magnitudes of a unitary matrix.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 400, 257, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 8141, 307, 472, 689, 264, 33783, 808, 490, 264, 8889, 4944, 16451, 295, 257, 517, 4109, 8141, 13, 50754], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13816065788269044, "compression_ratio": 1.4712041884816753, "no_speech_prob": 1.5312383522606e-12}, {"id": 220, "seek": 82270, "start": 830.96, "end": 834.86, "text": " This means we are building our amplitude layer using complex numbers.", "tokens": [50778, 639, 1355, 321, 366, 2390, 527, 27433, 4583, 1228, 3997, 3547, 13, 50973], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13816065788269044, "compression_ratio": 1.4712041884816753, "no_speech_prob": 1.5312383522606e-12}, {"id": 221, "seek": 82270, "start": 834.98, "end": 840.0200000000001, "text": " Numbers that contain imaginary parts which physically correspond to rotating phase angles.", "tokens": [50979, 22592, 1616, 300, 5304, 26164, 3166, 597, 9762, 6805, 281, 19627, 5574, 14708, 13, 51231], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13816065788269044, "compression_ratio": 1.4712041884816753, "no_speech_prob": 1.5312383522606e-12}, {"id": 222, "seek": 84002, "start": 840.02, "end": 845.12, "text": " Right. So to simplify orthostochastic means a process that can be built using only real numbers.", "tokens": [50365, 1779, 13, 407, 281, 20460, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 1355, 257, 1399, 300, 393, 312, 3094, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 13, 50620], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.128629574408898, "compression_ratio": 1.4811320754716981, "no_speech_prob": 1.1786461563689787e-12}, {"id": 223, "seek": 84002, "start": 845.76, "end": 848.72, "text": " Unistochastic means a process that requires complex numbers.", "tokens": [50652, 1156, 468, 8997, 2750, 1355, 257, 1399, 300, 7029, 3997, 3547, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.128629574408898, "compression_ratio": 1.4811320754716981, "no_speech_prob": 1.1786461563689787e-12}, {"id": 224, "seek": 84872, "start": 848.72, "end": 854.34, "text": " And the million dollar question the text poses is, are these two sets of matrices actually different?", "tokens": [50365, 400, 264, 2459, 7241, 1168, 264, 2487, 26059, 307, 11, 366, 613, 732, 6352, 295, 32284, 767, 819, 30, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 225, "seek": 84872, "start": 854.5, "end": 859.5, "text": " Like, does the universe care if we use real or complex numbers to build its probability transitions?", "tokens": [50654, 1743, 11, 775, 264, 6445, 1127, 498, 321, 764, 957, 420, 3997, 3547, 281, 1322, 1080, 8482, 23767, 30, 50904], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 226, "seek": 84872, "start": 859.94, "end": 864.08, "text": " The author begins testing this by looking at the simplest possible system you could construct.", "tokens": [50926, 440, 3793, 7338, 4997, 341, 538, 1237, 412, 264, 22811, 1944, 1185, 291, 727, 7690, 13, 51133], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 227, "seek": 84872, "start": 864.6600000000001, "end": 867.28, "text": " A system with only two mutually exclusive outcomes.", "tokens": [51162, 316, 1185, 365, 787, 732, 39144, 13005, 10070, 13, 51293], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 228, "seek": 84872, "start": 867.76, "end": 869.0400000000001, "text": " An N equals 2 system.", "tokens": [51317, 1107, 426, 6915, 568, 1185, 13, 51381], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 229, "seek": 84872, "start": 869.74, "end": 872.0600000000001, "text": " Think of a single isolated quantum bit, a quibit.", "tokens": [51416, 6557, 295, 257, 2167, 14621, 13018, 857, 11, 257, 421, 897, 270, 13, 51532], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16819030046463013, "compression_ratio": 1.5767790262172285, "no_speech_prob": 1.6045767145533851e-12}, {"id": 230, "seek": 87206, "start": 872.06, "end": 874.5999999999999, "text": " And what happens when we test the math at N equals 2?", "tokens": [50365, 400, 437, 2314, 562, 321, 1500, 264, 5221, 412, 426, 6915, 568, 30, 50492], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1305829216452206, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832593904477557e-12}, {"id": 231, "seek": 87206, "start": 874.6999999999999, "end": 878.52, "text": " At N equals 2, the mathematics simply cannot tell the difference between the two fields.", "tokens": [50497, 1711, 426, 6915, 568, 11, 264, 18666, 2935, 2644, 980, 264, 2649, 1296, 264, 732, 7909, 13, 50688], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1305829216452206, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832593904477557e-12}, {"id": 232, "seek": 87206, "start": 878.9, "end": 881.3, "text": " The author proves the N equals 2 degeneracy theorem.", "tokens": [50707, 440, 3793, 25019, 264, 426, 6915, 568, 40520, 2551, 20904, 13, 50827], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1305829216452206, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832593904477557e-12}, {"id": 233, "seek": 87206, "start": 881.78, "end": 888.52, "text": " It demonstrates that every single 2 by 2 bistochastic matrix is simultaneously orthostochastic and unistochastic.", "tokens": [50851, 467, 31034, 300, 633, 2167, 568, 538, 568, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8141, 307, 16561, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 293, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 13, 51188], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1305829216452206, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832593904477557e-12}, {"id": 234, "seek": 87206, "start": 888.52, "end": 896.9799999999999, "text": " Yeah, any valid two-outcome probability transition you can dream up can be perfectly flawlessly explained using only real numbers.", "tokens": [51188, 865, 11, 604, 7363, 732, 12, 346, 1102, 8482, 6034, 291, 393, 3055, 493, 393, 312, 6239, 13717, 12048, 8825, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 13, 51611], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1305829216452206, "compression_ratio": 1.6417910447761195, "no_speech_prob": 1.8832593904477557e-12}, {"id": 235, "seek": 89698, "start": 896.98, "end": 902.46, "text": " You absolutely do not need complex numbers to describe a single isolated two-level system.", "tokens": [50365, 509, 3122, 360, 406, 643, 3997, 3547, 281, 6786, 257, 2167, 14621, 732, 12, 12418, 1185, 13, 50639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 236, "seek": 89698, "start": 902.82, "end": 909.1800000000001, "text": " So if the entire universe consisted of nothing but isolated coin flips, scientists would never have invented complex quantum mechanics.", "tokens": [50657, 407, 498, 264, 2302, 6445, 38227, 295, 1825, 457, 14621, 11464, 40249, 11, 7708, 576, 1128, 362, 14479, 3997, 13018, 12939, 13, 50975], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 237, "seek": 89698, "start": 909.58, "end": 911.8000000000001, "text": " A purely real number theory works flawlessly.", "tokens": [50995, 316, 17491, 957, 1230, 5261, 1985, 13717, 12048, 13, 51106], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 238, "seek": 89698, "start": 912.0, "end": 913.1, "text": " This is entirely sufficient.", "tokens": [51116, 639, 307, 7696, 11563, 13, 51171], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 239, "seek": 89698, "start": 913.1800000000001, "end": 916.6, "text": " But the universe is obviously vastly larger than a two-sided coin.", "tokens": [51175, 583, 264, 6445, 307, 2745, 41426, 4833, 813, 257, 732, 12, 30941, 11464, 13, 51346], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 240, "seek": 89698, "start": 917.0, "end": 920.14, "text": " What happens when we add just one single additional outcome?", "tokens": [51366, 708, 2314, 562, 321, 909, 445, 472, 2167, 4497, 9700, 30, 51523], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 241, "seek": 89698, "start": 920.32, "end": 922.24, "text": " What happens when we move to N equals 3?", "tokens": [51532, 708, 2314, 562, 321, 1286, 281, 426, 6915, 805, 30, 51628], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 242, "seek": 89698, "start": 922.5, "end": 926.32, "text": " At N equals 3, the classical real number picture violently shatters.", "tokens": [51641, 1711, 426, 6915, 805, 11, 264, 13735, 957, 1230, 3036, 46728, 402, 37690, 13, 51832], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1205914576848348, "compression_ratio": 1.71656050955414, "no_speech_prob": 1.9203965674619017e-12}, {"id": 243, "seek": 92632, "start": 926.32, "end": 931.1600000000001, "text": " The text presents the N greater than or equal to 3 incompleteness theorem.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2487, 13533, 264, 426, 5044, 813, 420, 2681, 281, 805, 14036, 14657, 15264, 20904, 13, 50607], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 244, "seek": 92632, "start": 931.58, "end": 932.22, "text": " Sounds ominous.", "tokens": [50628, 14576, 46812, 563, 13, 50660], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 245, "seek": 92632, "start": 932.32, "end": 933.7800000000001, "text": " It kind of is for classical math.", "tokens": [50665, 467, 733, 295, 307, 337, 13735, 5221, 13, 50738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 246, "seek": 92632, "start": 933.94, "end": 940.5400000000001, "text": " It proves mathematically that there are valid probability transitions that exist perfectly in the complex unistochastic world,", "tokens": [50746, 467, 25019, 44003, 300, 456, 366, 7363, 8482, 23767, 300, 2514, 6239, 294, 264, 3997, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 1002, 11, 51076], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 247, "seek": 92632, "start": 940.7, "end": 944.3000000000001, "text": " but are physically impossible to construct in the real orthostochastic world.", "tokens": [51084, 457, 366, 9762, 6243, 281, 7690, 294, 264, 957, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 1002, 13, 51264], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 248, "seek": 92632, "start": 944.4000000000001, "end": 946.6800000000001, "text": " And the author doesn't just state this abstractly.", "tokens": [51269, 400, 264, 3793, 1177, 380, 445, 1785, 341, 12649, 356, 13, 51383], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 249, "seek": 92632, "start": 946.74, "end": 950.1400000000001, "text": " They bring out a devastatingly concrete counterexample.", "tokens": [51386, 814, 1565, 484, 257, 21280, 356, 9859, 1207, 323, 87, 335, 781, 13, 51556], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 250, "seek": 92632, "start": 950.5200000000001, "end": 954.0400000000001, "text": " The 3 by 3 discrete Fourier transform matrix.", "tokens": [51575, 440, 805, 538, 805, 27706, 36810, 4088, 8141, 13, 51751], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1483248005742612, "compression_ratio": 1.6338983050847458, "no_speech_prob": 1.6359694631970512e-12}, {"id": 251, "seek": 95404, "start": 954.04, "end": 954.9399999999999, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 50410], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 252, "seek": 95404, "start": 955.56, "end": 959.8, "text": " The DFT matrix is a cornerstone of complex mathematics and signal processing.", "tokens": [50441, 440, 413, 25469, 8141, 307, 257, 4538, 11243, 295, 3997, 18666, 293, 6358, 9007, 13, 50653], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 253, "seek": 95404, "start": 960.38, "end": 966.8199999999999, "text": " It's a 3 by 3 unitary matrix filled with complex phases, imaginary numbers representing different rotations.", "tokens": [50682, 467, 311, 257, 805, 538, 805, 517, 4109, 8141, 6412, 365, 3997, 18764, 11, 26164, 3547, 13460, 819, 44796, 13, 51004], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 254, "seek": 95404, "start": 967.0999999999999, "end": 968.9599999999999, "text": " Okay, so it's full of complex numbers.", "tokens": [51018, 1033, 11, 370, 309, 311, 1577, 295, 3997, 3547, 13, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 255, "seek": 95404, "start": 969.3399999999999, "end": 969.5999999999999, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51130, 1779, 13, 51143], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 256, "seek": 95404, "start": 969.6999999999999, "end": 975.56, "text": " But if you take the absolute value squared of every single entry in this complex matrix, the phases disappear.", "tokens": [51148, 583, 498, 291, 747, 264, 8236, 2158, 8889, 295, 633, 2167, 8729, 294, 341, 3997, 8141, 11, 264, 18764, 11596, 13, 51441], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19518870399111793, "compression_ratio": 1.519650655021834, "no_speech_prob": 2.083815325154559e-12}, {"id": 257, "seek": 97556, "start": 975.56, "end": 981.3, "text": " And you get a very simple 3 by 3 grid where every single entry is exactly one third.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 291, 483, 257, 588, 2199, 805, 538, 805, 10748, 689, 633, 2167, 8729, 307, 2293, 472, 2636, 13, 50652], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13438578287760417, "compression_ratio": 1.5979899497487438, "no_speech_prob": 1.803399443667253e-12}, {"id": 258, "seek": 97556, "start": 981.66, "end": 984.9799999999999, "text": " A perfectly valid, incredibly boring by stochastic matrix.", "tokens": [50670, 316, 6239, 7363, 11, 6252, 9989, 538, 342, 8997, 2750, 8141, 13, 50836], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13438578287760417, "compression_ratio": 1.5979899497487438, "no_speech_prob": 1.803399443667253e-12}, {"id": 259, "seek": 97556, "start": 985.3, "end": 988.8199999999999, "text": " Just one third, one third, one third across every row and column.", "tokens": [50852, 1449, 472, 2636, 11, 472, 2636, 11, 472, 2636, 2108, 633, 5386, 293, 7738, 13, 51028], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13438578287760417, "compression_ratio": 1.5979899497487438, "no_speech_prob": 1.803399443667253e-12}, {"id": 260, "seek": 97556, "start": 989.0799999999999, "end": 995.26, "text": " It represents a process that takes any state and completely randomizes it equally across the three outcomes.", "tokens": [51041, 467, 8855, 257, 1399, 300, 2516, 604, 1785, 293, 2584, 4974, 5660, 309, 12309, 2108, 264, 1045, 10070, 13, 51350], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13438578287760417, "compression_ratio": 1.5979899497487438, "no_speech_prob": 1.803399443667253e-12}, {"id": 261, "seek": 99526, "start": 995.26, "end": 995.78, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50365, 7587, 13, 50391], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 262, "seek": 99526, "start": 995.9399999999999, "end": 1000.0, "text": " Because we derive this one third grid by squaring a complex unitary matrix.", "tokens": [50399, 1436, 321, 28446, 341, 472, 2636, 10748, 538, 2339, 1921, 257, 3997, 517, 4109, 8141, 13, 50602], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 263, "seek": 99526, "start": 1000.12, "end": 1000.3199999999999, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50608, 1779, 13, 50618], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 264, "seek": 99526, "start": 1000.6, "end": 1003.2, "text": " We know for a fact that it is unistochastic.", "tokens": [50632, 492, 458, 337, 257, 1186, 300, 309, 307, 517, 468, 8997, 2750, 13, 50762], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 265, "seek": 99526, "start": 1003.34, "end": 1005.68, "text": " It happily exists in the complex quantum framework.", "tokens": [50769, 467, 19909, 8198, 294, 264, 3997, 13018, 8388, 13, 50886], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 266, "seek": 99526, "start": 1005.76, "end": 1005.9399999999999, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50890, 1779, 13, 50899], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 267, "seek": 99526, "start": 1005.9399999999999, "end": 1012.88, "text": " The trap the author sets is asking, can we build this exact same one third grid from the bottom up using only real numbers?", "tokens": [50899, 440, 11487, 264, 3793, 6352, 307, 3365, 11, 393, 321, 1322, 341, 1900, 912, 472, 2636, 10748, 490, 264, 2767, 493, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 30, 51246], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 268, "seek": 99526, "start": 1013.04, "end": 1023.16, "text": " Meaning, can we find a 3 by 3 matrix of purely real numbers, positive and negative values, that are all orthogonal to each other, and when you square every number, they all equal one third?", "tokens": [51254, 19948, 11, 393, 321, 915, 257, 805, 538, 805, 8141, 295, 17491, 957, 3547, 11, 3353, 293, 3671, 4190, 11, 300, 366, 439, 41488, 281, 1184, 661, 11, 293, 562, 291, 3732, 633, 1230, 11, 436, 439, 2681, 472, 2636, 30, 51760], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15230586916901345, "compression_ratio": 1.6966666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 2.2349778278085708e-12}, {"id": 269, "seek": 102316, "start": 1023.16, "end": 1024.44, "text": " Let's follow the logic.", "tokens": [50365, 961, 311, 1524, 264, 9952, 13, 50429], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 270, "seek": 102316, "start": 1025.34, "end": 1036.6399999999999, "text": " If you square a real number, and the answer is one third, the original number must have been either positive 1 over the square root of 3 or negative 1 over the square root of 3.", "tokens": [50474, 759, 291, 3732, 257, 957, 1230, 11, 293, 264, 1867, 307, 472, 2636, 11, 264, 3380, 1230, 1633, 362, 668, 2139, 3353, 502, 670, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 805, 420, 3671, 502, 670, 264, 3732, 5593, 295, 805, 13, 51039], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 271, "seek": 102316, "start": 1036.7, "end": 1039.32, "text": " Because there are no other options in the real number field?", "tokens": [51042, 1436, 456, 366, 572, 661, 3956, 294, 264, 957, 1230, 2519, 30, 51173], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 272, "seek": 102316, "start": 1039.48, "end": 1039.76, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51181, 1779, 13, 51195], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 273, "seek": 102316, "start": 1040.02, "end": 1050.24, "text": " So we are searching for a 3 by 3 matrix filled entirely with positive or negative 1 over root 3, where every single row is orthogonal to every other row.", "tokens": [51208, 407, 321, 366, 10808, 337, 257, 805, 538, 805, 8141, 6412, 7696, 365, 3353, 420, 3671, 502, 670, 5593, 805, 11, 689, 633, 2167, 5386, 307, 41488, 281, 633, 661, 5386, 13, 51719], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 274, "seek": 102316, "start": 1050.24, "end": 1052.86, "text": " I want to make sure everyone understands orthogonal in this context.", "tokens": [51719, 286, 528, 281, 652, 988, 1518, 15146, 41488, 294, 341, 4319, 13, 51850], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10598360258957436, "compression_ratio": 1.8021978021978022, "no_speech_prob": 1.4779768121170322e-12}, {"id": 275, "seek": 105316, "start": 1053.16, "end": 1064.28, "text": " If I have two rows of numbers and they are orthogonal, it means if I multiply the matching numbers together and add up the results, taking the dot product, they perfectly cancel each other out to equal 0.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 286, 362, 732, 13241, 295, 3547, 293, 436, 366, 41488, 11, 309, 1355, 498, 286, 12972, 264, 14324, 3547, 1214, 293, 909, 493, 264, 3542, 11, 1940, 264, 5893, 1674, 11, 436, 6239, 10373, 1184, 661, 484, 281, 2681, 1958, 13, 50921], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08147255290638317, "compression_ratio": 1.463855421686747, "no_speech_prob": 1.919162311708744e-12}, {"id": 276, "seek": 105316, "start": 1064.5600000000002, "end": 1066.52, "text": " That is the exact mathematical hurdle.", "tokens": [50935, 663, 307, 264, 1900, 18894, 47423, 13, 51033], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08147255290638317, "compression_ratio": 1.463855421686747, "no_speech_prob": 1.919162311708744e-12}, {"id": 277, "seek": 106652, "start": 1066.52, "end": 1078.92, "text": " If we factor out the 1 over root 3 from our hypothetical matrix, what we are actually hunting for is a 3 by 3 matrix consisting entirely of positive ones and negative ones, whose rows dot product to 0.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 321, 5952, 484, 264, 502, 670, 5593, 805, 490, 527, 33053, 8141, 11, 437, 321, 366, 767, 12599, 337, 307, 257, 805, 538, 805, 8141, 33921, 7696, 295, 3353, 2306, 293, 3671, 2306, 11, 6104, 13241, 5893, 1674, 281, 1958, 13, 50985], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08852908724830263, "compression_ratio": 1.5458333333333334, "no_speech_prob": 1.966115421511705e-12}, {"id": 278, "seek": 106652, "start": 1079.08, "end": 1083.9, "text": " And in mathematical literature, this highly specific object is called a Hadamard matrix, right?", "tokens": [50993, 400, 294, 18894, 10394, 11, 341, 5405, 2685, 2657, 307, 1219, 257, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 11, 558, 30, 51234], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08852908724830263, "compression_ratio": 1.5458333333333334, "no_speech_prob": 1.966115421511705e-12}, {"id": 279, "seek": 106652, "start": 1084.1399999999999, "end": 1087.8799999999999, "text": " And here is the punchline that completely blew my mind when reading this.", "tokens": [51246, 400, 510, 307, 264, 8135, 1889, 300, 2584, 19075, 452, 1575, 562, 3760, 341, 13, 51433], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08852908724830263, "compression_ratio": 1.5458333333333334, "no_speech_prob": 1.966115421511705e-12}, {"id": 280, "seek": 108788, "start": 1087.88, "end": 1094.1000000000001, "text": " How many 3 by 3 hadamard matrices actually exist in the entirety of mathematics?", "tokens": [50365, 1012, 867, 805, 538, 805, 632, 335, 515, 32284, 767, 2514, 294, 264, 31557, 295, 18666, 30, 50676], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 281, "seek": 108788, "start": 1094.3600000000001, "end": 1094.68, "text": " Zero.", "tokens": [50689, 17182, 13, 50705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 282, "seek": 108788, "start": 1094.8400000000001, "end": 1095.88, "text": " Not a single one.", "tokens": [50713, 1726, 257, 2167, 472, 13, 50765], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 283, "seek": 108788, "start": 1096.0600000000002, "end": 1099.3400000000001, "text": " It is a rigorous, brute force impossibility.", "tokens": [50774, 467, 307, 257, 29882, 11, 47909, 3464, 38802, 2841, 13, 50938], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 284, "seek": 108788, "start": 1100.22, "end": 1101.48, "text": " Think about it mechanically.", "tokens": [50982, 6557, 466, 309, 4236, 984, 13, 51045], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 285, "seek": 108788, "start": 1102.0400000000002, "end": 1105.16, "text": " There are 9 entries in a 3 by 3 matrix.", "tokens": [51073, 821, 366, 1722, 23041, 294, 257, 805, 538, 805, 8141, 13, 51229], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 286, "seek": 108788, "start": 1105.66, "end": 1108.5200000000002, "text": " Each entry can only be a plus 1 or a minus 1.", "tokens": [51254, 6947, 8729, 393, 787, 312, 257, 1804, 502, 420, 257, 3175, 502, 13, 51397], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18889907598495484, "compression_ratio": 1.3968253968253967, "no_speech_prob": 2.475763300965883e-12}, {"id": 287, "seek": 110852, "start": 1108.52, "end": 1116.16, "text": " Okay, so that means there are exactly 2 to the power of 9 or 512 possible combinations of signs you could arrange.", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 370, 300, 1355, 456, 366, 2293, 568, 281, 264, 1347, 295, 1722, 420, 1025, 4762, 1944, 21267, 295, 7880, 291, 727, 9424, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 288, "seek": 110852, "start": 1116.24, "end": 1116.42, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50751, 1779, 13, 50760], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 289, "seek": 110852, "start": 1116.52, "end": 1121.58, "text": " You can easily program a basic computer script to check all 512 of those combinations.", "tokens": [50765, 509, 393, 3612, 1461, 257, 3875, 3820, 5755, 281, 1520, 439, 1025, 4762, 295, 729, 21267, 13, 51018], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 290, "seek": 110852, "start": 1121.72, "end": 1121.8799999999999, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51025, 865, 13, 51033], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 291, "seek": 110852, "start": 1122.24, "end": 1126.3, "text": " And you will find that exactly 0 of them have rows that are orthogonal to each other.", "tokens": [51051, 400, 291, 486, 915, 300, 2293, 1958, 295, 552, 362, 13241, 300, 366, 41488, 281, 1184, 661, 13, 51254], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 292, "seek": 110852, "start": 1126.3799999999999, "end": 1130.0, "text": " Wait, I can actually see why that fails just by thinking about the dot product.", "tokens": [51258, 3802, 11, 286, 393, 767, 536, 983, 300, 18199, 445, 538, 1953, 466, 264, 5893, 1674, 13, 51439], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13526077270507814, "compression_ratio": 1.5447154471544715, "no_speech_prob": 2.184804637553328e-12}, {"id": 293, "seek": 113000, "start": 1130.0, "end": 1138.38, "text": " If I take a row of 3 1s or minus 1s, and I multiply it by another row of 3 1s or minus 1s, I get 3 numbers that are each either plus 1 or minus 1.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 286, 747, 257, 5386, 295, 805, 502, 82, 420, 3175, 502, 82, 11, 293, 286, 12972, 309, 538, 1071, 5386, 295, 805, 502, 82, 420, 3175, 502, 82, 11, 286, 483, 805, 3547, 300, 366, 1184, 2139, 1804, 502, 420, 3175, 502, 13, 50784], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10646218723720974, "compression_ratio": 1.874074074074074, "no_speech_prob": 1.57675912257349e-12}, {"id": 294, "seek": 113000, "start": 1138.64, "end": 1145.34, "text": " If I add 3 of those together, I mean, I can get 3, or I can get 1, or I can get minus 1, or I can get minus 3, but I can never, ever get 0.", "tokens": [50797, 759, 286, 909, 805, 295, 729, 1214, 11, 286, 914, 11, 286, 393, 483, 805, 11, 420, 286, 393, 483, 502, 11, 420, 286, 393, 483, 3175, 502, 11, 420, 286, 393, 483, 3175, 805, 11, 457, 286, 393, 1128, 11, 1562, 483, 1958, 13, 51132], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10646218723720974, "compression_ratio": 1.874074074074074, "no_speech_prob": 1.57675912257349e-12}, {"id": 295, "seek": 113000, "start": 1145.86, "end": 1150.88, "text": " You need an even number of terms for the plus 1s and minus 1s to perfectly cancel each other out.", "tokens": [51158, 509, 643, 364, 754, 1230, 295, 2115, 337, 264, 1804, 502, 82, 293, 3175, 502, 82, 281, 6239, 10373, 1184, 661, 484, 13, 51409], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10646218723720974, "compression_ratio": 1.874074074074074, "no_speech_prob": 1.57675912257349e-12}, {"id": 296, "seek": 113000, "start": 1151.12, "end": 1152.82, "text": " That is a brilliant realization.", "tokens": [51421, 663, 307, 257, 10248, 25138, 13, 51506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10646218723720974, "compression_ratio": 1.874074074074074, "no_speech_prob": 1.57675912257349e-12}, {"id": 297, "seek": 113000, "start": 1153.04, "end": 1159.44, "text": " The classical mathematics of Hadamard matrices proves this exact intuition more broadly.", "tokens": [51517, 440, 13735, 18666, 295, 12298, 335, 515, 32284, 25019, 341, 1900, 24002, 544, 19511, 13, 51837], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10646218723720974, "compression_ratio": 1.874074074074074, "no_speech_prob": 1.57675912257349e-12}, {"id": 298, "seek": 116000, "start": 1160.0, "end": 1163.94, "text": " They only exist in sizes 1, 2, and multiples of 4.", "tokens": [50365, 814, 787, 2514, 294, 11602, 502, 11, 568, 11, 293, 46099, 295, 1017, 13, 50562], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 299, "seek": 116000, "start": 1164.7, "end": 1168.52, "text": " A 3x3 Hadamard matrix is a logical contradiction.", "tokens": [50600, 316, 805, 87, 18, 12298, 335, 515, 8141, 307, 257, 14978, 34937, 13, 50791], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 300, "seek": 116000, "start": 1168.8, "end": 1169.28, "text": " Which means?", "tokens": [50805, 3013, 1355, 30, 50829], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 301, "seek": 116000, "start": 1169.52, "end": 1177.16, "text": " Which means it is fundamentally impossible to generate that uniform 1 third probability transition using only real amplitudes.", "tokens": [50841, 3013, 1355, 309, 307, 17879, 6243, 281, 8460, 300, 9452, 502, 2636, 8482, 6034, 1228, 787, 957, 9731, 16451, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 302, "seek": 116000, "start": 1177.56, "end": 1182.92, "text": " The real numbers simply do not possess the geometric flexibility to remain strictly orthogonal to each other,", "tokens": [51243, 440, 957, 3547, 2935, 360, 406, 17490, 264, 33246, 12635, 281, 6222, 20792, 41488, 281, 1184, 661, 11, 51511], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 303, "seek": 116000, "start": 1183.1, "end": 1187.7, "text": " while simultaneously distributing probability equally across three distinct outcomes.", "tokens": [51520, 1339, 16561, 41406, 8482, 12309, 2108, 1045, 10644, 10070, 13, 51750], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 304, "seek": 116000, "start": 1187.9, "end": 1189.6, "text": " But the complex numbers can't.", "tokens": [51760, 583, 264, 3997, 3547, 393, 380, 13, 51845], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15945045546729966, "compression_ratio": 1.6215277777777777, "no_speech_prob": 1.7350963331039249e-12}, {"id": 305, "seek": 118960, "start": 1189.6, "end": 1189.78, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 50374], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 306, "seek": 118960, "start": 1189.9199999999998, "end": 1193.9599999999998, "text": " The complex numbers, however, possess a continuous spectrum of rotating phase angles.", "tokens": [50381, 440, 3997, 3547, 11, 4461, 11, 17490, 257, 10957, 11143, 295, 19627, 5574, 14708, 13, 50583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 307, "seek": 118960, "start": 1194.48, "end": 1197.36, "text": " They can twist and slide into orthogonal positions effortlessly.", "tokens": [50609, 814, 393, 8203, 293, 4137, 666, 41488, 8432, 4630, 12048, 13, 50753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 308, "seek": 118960, "start": 1197.78, "end": 1198.06, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50774, 1033, 13, 50788], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 309, "seek": 118960, "start": 1198.48, "end": 1203.3, "text": " I have a massive pushback here, and it's something I grappled with when I first read the text.", "tokens": [50809, 286, 362, 257, 5994, 2944, 3207, 510, 11, 293, 309, 311, 746, 286, 27165, 1493, 365, 562, 286, 700, 1401, 264, 2487, 13, 51050], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 310, "seek": 118960, "start": 1203.7199999999998, "end": 1207.8799999999999, "text": " When my physics brain kicked in, I thought, okay, sure, complex numbers win here.", "tokens": [51071, 1133, 452, 10649, 3567, 14609, 294, 11, 286, 1194, 11, 1392, 11, 988, 11, 3997, 3547, 1942, 510, 13, 51279], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17414043116015057, "compression_ratio": 1.4889867841409692, "no_speech_prob": 1.9280931018439418e-12}, {"id": 311, "seek": 120788, "start": 1207.88, "end": 1210.38, "text": " But isn't that just incredibly obvious?", "tokens": [50365, 583, 1943, 380, 300, 445, 6252, 6322, 30, 50490], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.3390155721593786, "compression_ratio": 1.2, "no_speech_prob": 2.020446743938864e-12}, {"id": 312, "seek": 120788, "start": 1210.38, "end": 1212.88, "text": " Isn't that simply because of interference?", "tokens": [50490, 6998, 380, 300, 2935, 570, 295, 24497, 30, 50615], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.3390155721593786, "compression_ratio": 1.2, "no_speech_prob": 2.020446743938864e-12}, {"id": 313, "seek": 120788, "start": 1213.3600000000001, "end": 1213.8400000000001, "text": " Interference?", "tokens": [50639, 5751, 5158, 30, 50663], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.3390155721593786, "compression_ratio": 1.2, "no_speech_prob": 2.020446743938864e-12}, {"id": 314, "seek": 121384, "start": 1213.84, "end": 1214.84, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50365, 865, 13, 50415], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2126535213354862, "compression_ratio": 1.5561497326203209, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248304745013495e-12}, {"id": 315, "seek": 121384, "start": 1214.84, "end": 1221.24, "text": " We are taught that when you square quantum amplitudes to get probabilities, you lose the cross terms.", "tokens": [50415, 492, 366, 5928, 300, 562, 291, 3732, 13018, 9731, 16451, 281, 483, 33783, 11, 291, 3624, 264, 3278, 2115, 13, 50735], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2126535213354862, "compression_ratio": 1.5561497326203209, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248304745013495e-12}, {"id": 316, "seek": 121384, "start": 1221.74, "end": 1225.1999999999998, "text": " You lose the interference patterns that dictate how waves interact.", "tokens": [50760, 509, 3624, 264, 24497, 8294, 300, 36071, 577, 9417, 4648, 13, 50933], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2126535213354862, "compression_ratio": 1.5561497326203209, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248304745013495e-12}, {"id": 317, "seek": 121384, "start": 1225.52, "end": 1230.84, "text": " So obviously, if we drop down to a layer where we only look at probabilities, we have permanently lost information,", "tokens": [50949, 407, 2745, 11, 498, 321, 3270, 760, 281, 257, 4583, 689, 321, 787, 574, 412, 33783, 11, 321, 362, 24042, 2731, 1589, 11, 51215], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2126535213354862, "compression_ratio": 1.5561497326203209, "no_speech_prob": 2.6248304745013495e-12}, {"id": 318, "seek": 123084, "start": 1230.84, "end": 1236.08, "text": " and therefore we must have complex amplitudes under the hood to keep track of that lost interference.", "tokens": [50365, 293, 4412, 321, 1633, 362, 3997, 9731, 16451, 833, 264, 13376, 281, 1066, 2837, 295, 300, 2731, 24497, 13, 50627], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13269820646806196, "compression_ratio": 1.4658385093167703, "no_speech_prob": 1.4409352617344995e-12}, {"id": 319, "seek": 123084, "start": 1236.24, "end": 1236.36, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50635, 1779, 13, 50641], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13269820646806196, "compression_ratio": 1.4658385093167703, "no_speech_prob": 1.4409352617344995e-12}, {"id": 320, "seek": 123084, "start": 1236.58, "end": 1239.4399999999998, "text": " Isn't the necessity of interference the reason real numbers fail?", "tokens": [50652, 6998, 380, 264, 24217, 295, 24497, 264, 1778, 957, 3547, 3061, 30, 50795], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13269820646806196, "compression_ratio": 1.4658385093167703, "no_speech_prob": 1.4409352617344995e-12}, {"id": 321, "seek": 123084, "start": 1239.52, "end": 1242.6799999999998, "text": " That is a very common and very standard intuition in physics.", "tokens": [50799, 663, 307, 257, 588, 2689, 293, 588, 3832, 24002, 294, 10649, 13, 50957], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13269820646806196, "compression_ratio": 1.4658385093167703, "no_speech_prob": 1.4409352617344995e-12}, {"id": 322, "seek": 124268, "start": 1242.68, "end": 1248.3200000000002, "text": " But the author dedicates Chapter 4 to proving exactly why that intuition is fundamentally incomplete.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 264, 3793, 37071, 1024, 18874, 1017, 281, 27221, 2293, 983, 300, 24002, 307, 17879, 31709, 13, 50647], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16958562259016366, "compression_ratio": 1.65, "no_speech_prob": 1.8760850078319846e-12}, {"id": 323, "seek": 124268, "start": 1248.64, "end": 1251.5, "text": " What you are describing is known as the composition obstruction.", "tokens": [50663, 708, 291, 366, 16141, 307, 2570, 382, 264, 12686, 49711, 13, 50806], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16958562259016366, "compression_ratio": 1.65, "no_speech_prob": 1.8760850078319846e-12}, {"id": 324, "seek": 124268, "start": 1251.92, "end": 1258.4, "text": " It is undeniably true that when you compose two processes at the amplitude level and then square them down to get probabilities,", "tokens": [50827, 467, 307, 674, 15711, 1188, 2074, 300, 562, 291, 35925, 732, 7555, 412, 264, 27433, 1496, 293, 550, 3732, 552, 760, 281, 483, 33783, 11, 51151], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16958562259016366, "compression_ratio": 1.65, "no_speech_prob": 1.8760850078319846e-12}, {"id": 325, "seek": 124268, "start": 1258.76, "end": 1266.3400000000001, "text": " you generate interference cross terms that you absolutely would not get if you had just multiplied the probabilities directly.", "tokens": [51169, 291, 8460, 24497, 3278, 2115, 300, 291, 3122, 576, 406, 483, 498, 291, 632, 445, 17207, 264, 33783, 3838, 13, 51548], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16958562259016366, "compression_ratio": 1.65, "no_speech_prob": 1.8760850078319846e-12}, {"id": 326, "seek": 124268, "start": 1266.5800000000002, "end": 1266.88, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51560, 1779, 13, 51575], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16958562259016366, "compression_ratio": 1.65, "no_speech_prob": 1.8760850078319846e-12}, {"id": 327, "seek": 126688, "start": 1266.88, "end": 1274.74, "text": " The whole probability of event A, then event B happening is not just the probability of A times the probability of B phenomenon.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 1379, 8482, 295, 2280, 316, 11, 550, 2280, 363, 2737, 307, 406, 445, 264, 8482, 295, 316, 1413, 264, 8482, 295, 363, 14029, 13, 50758], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 328, "seek": 126688, "start": 1275.0800000000002, "end": 1277.22, "text": " The paths interfere with each other.", "tokens": [50775, 440, 14518, 23946, 365, 1184, 661, 13, 50882], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 329, "seek": 126688, "start": 1277.3200000000002, "end": 1277.6200000000001, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50887, 7587, 13, 50902], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 330, "seek": 126688, "start": 1277.96, "end": 1283.3200000000002, "text": " Information is demonstrably lost when you project from the world of amplitudes down to the world of probabilities.", "tokens": [50919, 15357, 307, 5516, 5305, 356, 2731, 562, 291, 1716, 490, 264, 1002, 295, 9731, 16451, 760, 281, 264, 1002, 295, 33783, 13, 51187], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 331, "seek": 126688, "start": 1284.0800000000002, "end": 1292.7600000000002, "text": " However, Chapter 4 explicitly and rigorously proves that this loss of interference information happens in both the complex theory and the real theory.", "tokens": [51225, 2908, 11, 18874, 1017, 20803, 293, 42191, 5098, 25019, 300, 341, 4470, 295, 24497, 1589, 2314, 294, 1293, 264, 3997, 5261, 293, 264, 957, 5261, 13, 51659], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 332, "seek": 126688, "start": 1292.88, "end": 1293.0400000000002, "text": " Wait.", "tokens": [51665, 3802, 13, 51673], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 333, "seek": 126688, "start": 1293.14, "end": 1295.22, "text": " Real numbers experience interference too.", "tokens": [51678, 8467, 3547, 1752, 24497, 886, 13, 51782], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 334, "seek": 126688, "start": 1295.3600000000001, "end": 1296.3200000000002, "text": " They absolutely do.", "tokens": [51789, 814, 3122, 360, 13, 51837], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12729530675070627, "compression_ratio": 1.7700348432055748, "no_speech_prob": 1.7971884831019524e-12}, {"id": 335, "seek": 129632, "start": 1296.32, "end": 1301.8, "text": " If you compose two real orthogonal matrices together and then square the resulting entries to get probabilities,", "tokens": [50365, 759, 291, 35925, 732, 957, 41488, 32284, 1214, 293, 550, 3732, 264, 16505, 23041, 281, 483, 33783, 11, 50639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 336, "seek": 129632, "start": 1302.1, "end": 1305.12, "text": " you produce the exact same cross-term interference structure.", "tokens": [50654, 291, 5258, 264, 1900, 912, 3278, 12, 7039, 24497, 3877, 13, 50805], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 337, "seek": 129632, "start": 1305.3799999999999, "end": 1307.6599999999999, "text": " You lose information in the exact same manner.", "tokens": [50818, 509, 3624, 1589, 294, 264, 1900, 912, 9060, 13, 50932], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 338, "seek": 129632, "start": 1307.74, "end": 1308.3, "text": " Oh, wow.", "tokens": [50936, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 50964], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 339, "seek": 129632, "start": 1308.52, "end": 1308.6399999999999, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [50975, 865, 13, 50981], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 340, "seek": 129632, "start": 1308.7, "end": 1314.54, "text": " The fact that projecting down to probability destroys interference does not single out complex numbers as uniquely necessary.", "tokens": [50984, 440, 1186, 300, 43001, 760, 281, 8482, 36714, 24497, 775, 406, 2167, 484, 3997, 3547, 382, 31474, 4818, 13, 51276], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 341, "seek": 129632, "start": 1314.82, "end": 1318.9399999999998, "text": " The physical need for interference is equally well satisfied by real amplitudes.", "tokens": [51290, 440, 4001, 643, 337, 24497, 307, 12309, 731, 11239, 538, 957, 9731, 16451, 13, 51496], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 342, "seek": 129632, "start": 1319.26, "end": 1321.3999999999999, "text": " So my intuition was entirely backwards.", "tokens": [51512, 407, 452, 24002, 390, 7696, 12204, 13, 51619], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1842825459498985, "compression_ratio": 1.7067137809187278, "no_speech_prob": 1.935152775869864e-12}, {"id": 343, "seek": 132140, "start": 1321.4, "end": 1328.0800000000002, "text": " The catastrophic failure of real numbers at n equals 3 isn't because real numbers can't do interference.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 34915, 7763, 295, 957, 3547, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 1943, 380, 570, 957, 3547, 393, 380, 360, 24497, 13, 50699], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.160806959325617, "compression_ratio": 1.4285714285714286, "no_speech_prob": 2.235603846142964e-12}, {"id": 344, "seek": 132140, "start": 1328.0800000000002, "end": 1330.1000000000001, "text": " They can do interference just fine.", "tokens": [50699, 814, 393, 360, 24497, 445, 2489, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.160806959325617, "compression_ratio": 1.4285714285714286, "no_speech_prob": 2.235603846142964e-12}, {"id": 345, "seek": 133010, "start": 1330.1, "end": 1335.1399999999999, "text": " The failure is a completely separate, deeper geometric inadequacy.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 7763, 307, 257, 2584, 4994, 11, 7731, 33246, 35441, 2551, 13, 50617], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.28373745509556364, "compression_ratio": 1.013157894736842, "no_speech_prob": 1.9806493684743076e-12}, {"id": 346, "seek": 133010, "start": 1335.3, "end": 1335.8999999999999, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [50625, 48746, 736, 13, 50655], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.28373745509556364, "compression_ratio": 1.013157894736842, "no_speech_prob": 1.9806493684743076e-12}, {"id": 347, "seek": 133590, "start": 1335.9, "end": 1346.0400000000002, "text": " The real numbers literally lack the geometric space to build that specific 3x3 orthogonal structure, whereas the complex numbers have the space to accommodate it.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 957, 3547, 3736, 5011, 264, 33246, 1901, 281, 1322, 300, 2685, 805, 87, 18, 41488, 3877, 11, 9735, 264, 3997, 3547, 362, 264, 1901, 281, 21410, 309, 13, 50872], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19002265641183563, "compression_ratio": 1.361344537815126, "no_speech_prob": 1.897758642940839e-12}, {"id": 348, "seek": 134604, "start": 1346.04, "end": 1347.58, "text": " That's the reality of the math.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 311, 264, 4103, 295, 264, 5221, 13, 50442], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2016049948605624, "compression_ratio": 1.4338235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 2.0842691721839612e-12}, {"id": 349, "seek": 134604, "start": 1347.58, "end": 1356.42, "text": " If the existence of interference doesn't prove that we need complex numbers, we have to look elsewhere for the ultimate geometric trap that real numbers fall into.", "tokens": [50442, 759, 264, 9123, 295, 24497, 1177, 380, 7081, 300, 321, 643, 3997, 3547, 11, 321, 362, 281, 574, 14517, 337, 264, 9705, 33246, 11487, 300, 957, 3547, 2100, 666, 13, 50884], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.2016049948605624, "compression_ratio": 1.4338235294117647, "no_speech_prob": 2.0842691721839612e-12}, {"id": 350, "seek": 135642, "start": 1356.42, "end": 1363.52, "text": " And to find that trap, we have to stop looking at single matrices and start looking at what happens when you attempt to travel in a circle.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 281, 915, 300, 11487, 11, 321, 362, 281, 1590, 1237, 412, 2167, 32284, 293, 722, 1237, 412, 437, 2314, 562, 291, 5217, 281, 3147, 294, 257, 6329, 13, 50720], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11073243882921006, "compression_ratio": 1.6680327868852458, "no_speech_prob": 1.7901828023442201e-12}, {"id": 351, "seek": 135642, "start": 1363.72, "end": 1364.8600000000001, "text": " Okay, let's unpack this.", "tokens": [50730, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 26699, 341, 13, 50787], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11073243882921006, "compression_ratio": 1.6680327868852458, "no_speech_prob": 1.7901828023442201e-12}, {"id": 352, "seek": 135642, "start": 1365.1200000000001, "end": 1369.1200000000001, "text": " Chapters 5 and 6 of the text delve into context graphs and cycle consistency.", "tokens": [50800, 24187, 1559, 1025, 293, 1386, 295, 264, 2487, 43098, 666, 4319, 24877, 293, 6586, 14416, 13, 51000], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11073243882921006, "compression_ratio": 1.6680327868852458, "no_speech_prob": 1.7901828023442201e-12}, {"id": 353, "seek": 135642, "start": 1369.68, "end": 1377.96, "text": " This is where we stop staring at a single isolated transformation and start looking at how different measurement contexts relate to one another in a broader system.", "tokens": [51028, 639, 307, 689, 321, 1590, 18043, 412, 257, 2167, 14621, 9887, 293, 722, 1237, 412, 577, 819, 13160, 30628, 10961, 281, 472, 1071, 294, 257, 13227, 1185, 13, 51442], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11073243882921006, "compression_ratio": 1.6680327868852458, "no_speech_prob": 1.7901828023442201e-12}, {"id": 354, "seek": 137796, "start": 1377.96, "end": 1383.66, "text": " Imagine you have a physical system in front of you, and you have three completely different ways you could choose to measure it.", "tokens": [50365, 11739, 291, 362, 257, 4001, 1185, 294, 1868, 295, 291, 11, 293, 291, 362, 1045, 2584, 819, 2098, 291, 727, 2826, 281, 3481, 309, 13, 50650], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17819767731886643, "compression_ratio": 1.7386363636363635, "no_speech_prob": 1.6882925179401154e-12}, {"id": 355, "seek": 137796, "start": 1383.66, "end": 1386.66, "text": " Let's label these measurement contexts z, x, and y.", "tokens": [50650, 961, 311, 7645, 613, 13160, 30628, 710, 11, 2031, 11, 293, 288, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17819767731886643, "compression_ratio": 1.7386363636363635, "no_speech_prob": 1.6882925179401154e-12}, {"id": 356, "seek": 137796, "start": 1386.66, "end": 1390.76, "text": " Each of these contexts represents a complete set of orthogonal states.", "tokens": [50800, 6947, 295, 613, 30628, 8855, 257, 3566, 992, 295, 41488, 4368, 13, 51005], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17819767731886643, "compression_ratio": 1.7386363636363635, "no_speech_prob": 1.6882925179401154e-12}, {"id": 357, "seek": 137796, "start": 1391.44, "end": 1399.64, "text": " If we're trying to force this into a real number theory, you can visualize these contexts as three different rigid coordinate frames.", "tokens": [51039, 759, 321, 434, 1382, 281, 3464, 341, 666, 257, 957, 1230, 5261, 11, 291, 393, 23273, 613, 30628, 382, 1045, 819, 22195, 15670, 12083, 13, 51449], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17819767731886643, "compression_ratio": 1.7386363636363635, "no_speech_prob": 1.6882925179401154e-12}, {"id": 358, "seek": 137796, "start": 1400.06, "end": 1403.76, "text": " Three different ways to orient the x and y axes on a flat piece of paper.", "tokens": [51470, 6244, 819, 2098, 281, 8579, 264, 2031, 293, 288, 35387, 322, 257, 4962, 2522, 295, 3035, 13, 51655], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17819767731886643, "compression_ratio": 1.7386363636363635, "no_speech_prob": 1.6882925179401154e-12}, {"id": 359, "seek": 140376, "start": 1403.76, "end": 1408.08, "text": " Like holding a piece of graph paper and rotating it to three different arbitrary angles.", "tokens": [50365, 1743, 5061, 257, 2522, 295, 4295, 3035, 293, 19627, 309, 281, 1045, 819, 23211, 14708, 13, 50581], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 360, "seek": 140376, "start": 1408.24, "end": 1408.7, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50589, 7587, 13, 50612], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 361, "seek": 140376, "start": 1408.94, "end": 1416.2, "text": " Now, suppose you have carefully calibrated your equipment, and you know exactly how context z mathematically relates to context x.", "tokens": [50624, 823, 11, 7297, 291, 362, 7500, 21583, 5468, 428, 5927, 11, 293, 291, 458, 2293, 577, 4319, 710, 44003, 16155, 281, 4319, 2031, 13, 50987], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 362, "seek": 140376, "start": 1416.3799999999999, "end": 1420.56, "text": " You have a precise transition matrix that describes the angle of rotation between them.", "tokens": [50996, 509, 362, 257, 13600, 6034, 8141, 300, 15626, 264, 5802, 295, 12447, 1296, 552, 13, 51205], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 363, "seek": 140376, "start": 1420.64, "end": 1421.04, "text": " Got it.", "tokens": [51209, 5803, 309, 13, 51229], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 364, "seek": 140376, "start": 1421.14, "end": 1426.42, "text": " And you know exactly how context x relates to context y, and finally you know how y relates back to z.", "tokens": [51234, 400, 291, 458, 2293, 577, 4319, 2031, 16155, 281, 4319, 288, 11, 293, 2721, 291, 458, 577, 288, 16155, 646, 281, 710, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 365, "seek": 140376, "start": 1426.42, "end": 1433.56, "text": " Well, if we were looking at the pure upper level underlying amplitude math, the full information unsquared matrices, this should be a trivial exercise, right?", "tokens": [51498, 1042, 11, 498, 321, 645, 1237, 412, 264, 6075, 6597, 1496, 14217, 27433, 5221, 11, 264, 1577, 1589, 2693, 358, 1642, 32284, 11, 341, 820, 312, 257, 26703, 5380, 11, 558, 30, 51855], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11274021588839017, "compression_ratio": 1.7650602409638554, "no_speech_prob": 1.3355737956549651e-12}, {"id": 366, "seek": 143376, "start": 1433.76, "end": 1442.12, "text": " If you mathematically transform your coordinates from z to x, and then from x to y, and then from y back to z, you have just walked in a complete closed circle.", "tokens": [50365, 759, 291, 44003, 4088, 428, 21056, 490, 710, 281, 2031, 11, 293, 550, 490, 2031, 281, 288, 11, 293, 550, 490, 288, 646, 281, 710, 11, 291, 362, 445, 7628, 294, 257, 3566, 5395, 6329, 13, 50783], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09459105730056763, "compression_ratio": 1.3675213675213675, "no_speech_prob": 2.1672687516155476e-12}, {"id": 367, "seek": 144212, "start": 1442.12, "end": 1450.9199999999998, "text": " In that exact upper level amplitude math, multiplying those three transformation matrices together must exactly equal the identity matrix.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 300, 1900, 6597, 1496, 27433, 5221, 11, 30955, 729, 1045, 9887, 32284, 1214, 1633, 2293, 2681, 264, 6575, 8141, 13, 50805], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 368, "seek": 144212, "start": 1451.4199999999998, "end": 1453.84, "text": " You are right back to where you started with no changes.", "tokens": [50830, 509, 366, 558, 646, 281, 689, 291, 1409, 365, 572, 2962, 13, 50951], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 369, "seek": 144212, "start": 1454.3999999999999, "end": 1459.3, "text": " The matrix LZx multiplied by Lxy multiplied by Lyz equals I.", "tokens": [50979, 440, 8141, 441, 57, 87, 17207, 538, 441, 12876, 17207, 538, 12687, 89, 6915, 286, 13, 51224], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 370, "seek": 144212, "start": 1459.7399999999998, "end": 1461.9199999999998, "text": " The mathematical loop closes perfectly.", "tokens": [51246, 440, 18894, 6367, 24157, 6239, 13, 51355], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 371, "seek": 144212, "start": 1462.26, "end": 1465.9199999999998, "text": " But the whole point of this exercise is that we don't live in the upper level math.", "tokens": [51372, 583, 264, 1379, 935, 295, 341, 5380, 307, 300, 321, 500, 380, 1621, 294, 264, 6597, 1496, 5221, 13, 51555], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 372, "seek": 144212, "start": 1466.28, "end": 1467.7399999999998, "text": " We live in a physical laboratory.", "tokens": [51573, 492, 1621, 294, 257, 4001, 16523, 13, 51646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1975397411145662, "compression_ratio": 1.6299212598425197, "no_speech_prob": 1.5012523559357338e-12}, {"id": 373, "seek": 146774, "start": 1467.74, "end": 1472.18, "text": " We only ever see the squared probabilities clicking on our detectors.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 787, 1562, 536, 264, 8889, 33783, 9697, 322, 527, 46866, 13, 50587], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1272541854692542, "compression_ratio": 1.7165991902834008, "no_speech_prob": 2.1933568242898938e-12}, {"id": 374, "seek": 146774, "start": 1472.64, "end": 1474.94, "text": " We only see the shadows dancing on the cave wall.", "tokens": [50610, 492, 787, 536, 264, 14740, 8898, 322, 264, 11730, 2929, 13, 50725], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1272541854692542, "compression_ratio": 1.7165991902834008, "no_speech_prob": 2.1933568242898938e-12}, {"id": 375, "seek": 146774, "start": 1475.26, "end": 1477.28, "text": " And that is the crux of the entire problem.", "tokens": [50741, 400, 300, 307, 264, 5140, 87, 295, 264, 2302, 1154, 13, 50842], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1272541854692542, "compression_ratio": 1.7165991902834008, "no_speech_prob": 2.1933568242898938e-12}, {"id": 376, "seek": 146774, "start": 1478.04, "end": 1488.08, "text": " When you are forced to drop down to probability observations, when you square those delicate transition matrices to get your observable probability grids, you irreversibly lose information.", "tokens": [50880, 1133, 291, 366, 7579, 281, 3270, 760, 281, 8482, 18163, 11, 562, 291, 3732, 729, 21417, 6034, 32284, 281, 483, 428, 9951, 712, 8482, 677, 3742, 11, 291, 16014, 840, 3545, 3624, 1589, 13, 51382], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1272541854692542, "compression_ratio": 1.7165991902834008, "no_speech_prob": 2.1933568242898938e-12}, {"id": 377, "seek": 146774, "start": 1488.42, "end": 1491.84, "text": " How much information are we actually losing in this specific scenario?", "tokens": [51399, 1012, 709, 1589, 366, 321, 767, 7027, 294, 341, 2685, 9005, 30, 51570], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1272541854692542, "compression_ratio": 1.7165991902834008, "no_speech_prob": 2.1933568242898938e-12}, {"id": 378, "seek": 149184, "start": 1491.84, "end": 1496.4399999999998, "text": " The author calculates this with pinpoint precision in what they call the triangle gluing theorem.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3793, 4322, 1024, 341, 365, 40837, 18356, 294, 437, 436, 818, 264, 13369, 1563, 9635, 20904, 13, 50595], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07640500201119317, "compression_ratio": 1.5462962962962963, "no_speech_prob": 1.5671602470596069e-12}, {"id": 379, "seek": 149184, "start": 1497.12, "end": 1506.6, "text": " When you strip away the underlying signs and phases, and you are only allowed to look at the raw transition probabilities between these 2D frames, you lose exactly one bit of information per loop.", "tokens": [50629, 1133, 291, 12828, 1314, 264, 14217, 7880, 293, 18764, 11, 293, 291, 366, 787, 4350, 281, 574, 412, 264, 8936, 6034, 33783, 1296, 613, 568, 35, 12083, 11, 291, 3624, 2293, 472, 857, 295, 1589, 680, 6367, 13, 51103], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07640500201119317, "compression_ratio": 1.5462962962962963, "no_speech_prob": 1.5671602470596069e-12}, {"id": 380, "seek": 149184, "start": 1506.9399999999998, "end": 1508.86, "text": " Specifically, you lose a relative sign.", "tokens": [51120, 26058, 11, 291, 3624, 257, 4972, 1465, 13, 51216], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.07640500201119317, "compression_ratio": 1.5462962962962963, "no_speech_prob": 1.5671602470596069e-12}, {"id": 381, "seek": 150886, "start": 1508.86, "end": 1513.3799999999999, "text": " I was trying to visualize this when reading, and I kept coming back to a woodshop analogy.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 390, 1382, 281, 23273, 341, 562, 3760, 11, 293, 286, 4305, 1348, 646, 281, 257, 4576, 29431, 21663, 13, 50591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 382, "seek": 150886, "start": 1514.12, "end": 1524.62, "text": " Imagine I give you three pieces of wood, and I tell you the exact angles you need to cut on the ends of each piece so that they will fit together to form a perfect closed triangle.", "tokens": [50628, 11739, 286, 976, 291, 1045, 3755, 295, 4576, 11, 293, 286, 980, 291, 264, 1900, 14708, 291, 643, 281, 1723, 322, 264, 5314, 295, 1184, 2522, 370, 300, 436, 486, 3318, 1214, 281, 1254, 257, 2176, 5395, 13369, 13, 51153], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 383, "seek": 150886, "start": 1525.02, "end": 1526.02, "text": " Okay, I can picture that.", "tokens": [51173, 1033, 11, 286, 393, 3036, 300, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 384, "seek": 150886, "start": 1526.1, "end": 1527.62, "text": " You take piece C and piece X.", "tokens": [51227, 509, 747, 2522, 383, 293, 2522, 1783, 13, 51303], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 385, "seek": 150886, "start": 1527.9399999999998, "end": 1529.84, "text": " You cut the joint, and they fit perfectly.", "tokens": [51319, 509, 1723, 264, 7225, 11, 293, 436, 3318, 6239, 13, 51414], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 386, "seek": 150886, "start": 1530.3799999999999, "end": 1533.4399999999998, "text": " You take X and Y, cut the joint, and they fit flawlessly.", "tokens": [51441, 509, 747, 1783, 293, 398, 11, 1723, 264, 7225, 11, 293, 436, 3318, 13717, 12048, 13, 51594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 387, "seek": 150886, "start": 1533.86, "end": 1537.82, "text": " You go to connect the final joint, bringing Y back to Z.", "tokens": [51615, 509, 352, 281, 1745, 264, 2572, 7225, 11, 5062, 398, 646, 281, 1176, 13, 51813], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10840356533343976, "compression_ratio": 1.8233082706766917, "no_speech_prob": 1.573532862168825e-12}, {"id": 388, "seek": 153782, "start": 1537.82, "end": 1538.6599999999999, "text": " And it doesn't fit.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 309, 1177, 380, 3318, 13, 50407], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 389, "seek": 153782, "start": 1538.72, "end": 1539.54, "text": " It doesn't fit.", "tokens": [50410, 467, 1177, 380, 3318, 13, 50451], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 390, "seek": 153782, "start": 1539.54, "end": 1552.08, "text": " Because if you are forced to keep all three pieces of wood completely flat on the workbench, which physically represents being trapped in the flat 2D real number plane, that final corner physically cannot meet.", "tokens": [50451, 1436, 498, 291, 366, 7579, 281, 1066, 439, 1045, 3755, 295, 4576, 2584, 4962, 322, 264, 589, 47244, 11, 597, 9762, 8855, 885, 14994, 294, 264, 4962, 568, 35, 957, 1230, 5720, 11, 300, 2572, 4538, 9762, 2644, 1677, 13, 51078], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 391, "seek": 153782, "start": 1552.3799999999999, "end": 1555.98, "text": " That woodshop analogy is an exceptional way to visualize the constraint.", "tokens": [51093, 663, 4576, 29431, 21663, 307, 364, 19279, 636, 281, 23273, 264, 25534, 13, 51273], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 392, "seek": 153782, "start": 1556.28, "end": 1565.96, "text": " The angles that looked perfectly valid and mathematically sound individually are globally inconsistent when you force them to form a closed loop on a restricted flat surface.", "tokens": [51288, 440, 14708, 300, 2956, 6239, 7363, 293, 44003, 1626, 16652, 366, 18958, 36891, 562, 291, 3464, 552, 281, 1254, 257, 5395, 6367, 322, 257, 20608, 4962, 3753, 13, 51772], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 393, "seek": 153782, "start": 1566.34, "end": 1566.82, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51791, 7587, 13, 51815], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10170998396696868, "compression_ratio": 1.6384364820846906, "no_speech_prob": 1.279092825580419e-12}, {"id": 394, "seek": 156682, "start": 1566.82, "end": 1574.54, "text": " The individual transition probabilities between the contexts might be perfectly valid, perfectly orthostochastic on their own.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2609, 6034, 33783, 1296, 264, 30628, 1062, 312, 6239, 7363, 11, 6239, 19052, 555, 8997, 2750, 322, 641, 1065, 13, 50751], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11354503631591797, "compression_ratio": 1.5515463917525774, "no_speech_prob": 2.0519146277930833e-12}, {"id": 395, "seek": 156682, "start": 1574.9399999999998, "end": 1586.0, "text": " But when you attempt to assign a single globally consistent set of real number axes to all three contexts simultaneously, the math snags on that lost bit of sign information.", "tokens": [50771, 583, 562, 291, 5217, 281, 6269, 257, 2167, 18958, 8398, 992, 295, 957, 1230, 35387, 281, 439, 1045, 30628, 16561, 11, 264, 5221, 2406, 12109, 322, 300, 2731, 857, 295, 1465, 1589, 13, 51324], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11354503631591797, "compression_ratio": 1.5515463917525774, "no_speech_prob": 2.0519146277930833e-12}, {"id": 396, "seek": 158600, "start": 1586.0, "end": 1591.82, "text": " And the author provides the absolute sharpest, most painful example of this mathematical snag in action.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 264, 3793, 6417, 264, 8236, 8199, 377, 11, 881, 11697, 1365, 295, 341, 18894, 2406, 559, 294, 3069, 13, 50656], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09650824091455958, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.068026739437956e-12}, {"id": 397, "seek": 158600, "start": 1592.26, "end": 1596.16, "text": " The attempt to build mutually unbiased bases, or MUBs.", "tokens": [50678, 440, 5217, 281, 1322, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 11, 420, 17935, 33, 82, 13, 50873], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09650824091455958, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.068026739437956e-12}, {"id": 398, "seek": 158600, "start": 1596.44, "end": 1600.78, "text": " Mutually unbiased bases are a foundational concept in quantum information theory.", "tokens": [50887, 18517, 671, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 366, 257, 32195, 3410, 294, 13018, 1589, 5261, 13, 51104], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09650824091455958, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.068026739437956e-12}, {"id": 399, "seek": 158600, "start": 1601.3, "end": 1613.9, "text": " If two bases are mutually unbiased, it means that if you prepare a system perfectly in a state from the first basis and then measure it using the second basis, you have a perfectly uniform, totally random probability of getting any of the possible outcome.", "tokens": [51130, 759, 732, 17949, 366, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 11, 309, 1355, 300, 498, 291, 5940, 257, 1185, 6239, 294, 257, 1785, 490, 264, 700, 5143, 293, 550, 3481, 309, 1228, 264, 1150, 5143, 11, 291, 362, 257, 6239, 9452, 11, 3879, 4974, 8482, 295, 1242, 604, 295, 264, 1944, 9700, 13, 51760], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09650824091455958, "compression_ratio": 1.7231833910034602, "no_speech_prob": 2.068026739437956e-12}, {"id": 400, "seek": 161390, "start": 1613.9, "end": 1620.26, "text": " It's the ultimate uncertainty. If I know exactly how fast a particle is moving and I measure its position, the result is completely random.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 311, 264, 9705, 15697, 13, 759, 286, 458, 2293, 577, 2370, 257, 12359, 307, 2684, 293, 286, 3481, 1080, 2535, 11, 264, 1874, 307, 2584, 4974, 13, 50683], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09023421689083702, "compression_ratio": 1.5245098039215685, "no_speech_prob": 2.307393208791919e-12}, {"id": 401, "seek": 161390, "start": 1620.52, "end": 1621.74, "text": " They are mutually unbiased.", "tokens": [50696, 814, 366, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 13, 50757], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09023421689083702, "compression_ratio": 1.5245098039215685, "no_speech_prob": 2.307393208791919e-12}, {"id": 402, "seek": 161390, "start": 1621.8000000000002, "end": 1629.38, "text": " Right. Now, take that concept and imagine trying to construct three measurement contexts, Z, X, and Y, that are all pairwise mutually unbiased.", "tokens": [50760, 1779, 13, 823, 11, 747, 300, 3410, 293, 3811, 1382, 281, 7690, 1045, 13160, 30628, 11, 1176, 11, 1783, 11, 293, 398, 11, 300, 366, 439, 6119, 3711, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 13, 51139], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09023421689083702, "compression_ratio": 1.5245098039215685, "no_speech_prob": 2.307393208791919e-12}, {"id": 403, "seek": 162938, "start": 1629.38, "end": 1634.6000000000001, "text": " Z is unbiased relative to X, X is unbiased to Y, and Y is unbiased back to Z.", "tokens": [50365, 1176, 307, 517, 5614, 1937, 4972, 281, 1783, 11, 1783, 307, 517, 5614, 1937, 281, 398, 11, 293, 398, 307, 517, 5614, 1937, 646, 281, 1176, 13, 50626], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06914040289427105, "compression_ratio": 1.5126903553299493, "no_speech_prob": 1.7900142089063986e-12}, {"id": 404, "seek": 162938, "start": 1634.8200000000002, "end": 1649.96, "text": " If you attempt to mathematically build this triad using only real numbers in a two-dimensional space R2, the triangle gluing theorem demands that a highly specific geometric equation must hold true for the loop to close.", "tokens": [50637, 759, 291, 5217, 281, 44003, 1322, 341, 1376, 345, 1228, 787, 957, 3547, 294, 257, 732, 12, 18759, 1901, 497, 17, 11, 264, 13369, 1563, 9635, 20904, 15107, 300, 257, 5405, 2685, 33246, 5367, 1633, 1797, 2074, 337, 264, 6367, 281, 1998, 13, 51394], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.06914040289427105, "compression_ratio": 1.5126903553299493, "no_speech_prob": 1.7900142089063986e-12}, {"id": 405, "seek": 164996, "start": 1649.96, "end": 1660.78, "text": " I remember staring at this equation in the text. It forces the real number of math into a corner where it has to mathematically declare that the angle pi over 4 is congruent to 0.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 1604, 18043, 412, 341, 5367, 294, 264, 2487, 13, 467, 5874, 264, 957, 1230, 295, 5221, 666, 257, 4538, 689, 309, 575, 281, 44003, 19710, 300, 264, 5802, 3895, 670, 1017, 307, 8882, 19226, 281, 1958, 13, 50906], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0740829143884047, "compression_ratio": 1.647940074906367, "no_speech_prob": 1.898022537749622e-12}, {"id": 406, "seek": 164996, "start": 1661.16, "end": 1666.3, "text": " Which is, of course, a complete absurdity. Pi over 4 is 45 degrees. It does not equal 0.", "tokens": [50925, 3013, 307, 11, 295, 1164, 11, 257, 3566, 19774, 507, 13, 17741, 670, 1017, 307, 6905, 5310, 13, 467, 775, 406, 2681, 1958, 13, 51182], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0740829143884047, "compression_ratio": 1.647940074906367, "no_speech_prob": 1.898022537749622e-12}, {"id": 407, "seek": 164996, "start": 1666.4, "end": 1667.1200000000001, "text": " Obviously not.", "tokens": [51187, 7580, 406, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0740829143884047, "compression_ratio": 1.647940074906367, "no_speech_prob": 1.898022537749622e-12}, {"id": 408, "seek": 164996, "start": 1667.3600000000001, "end": 1676.68, "text": " The real plane R2 is too rigid, too flat. It simply cannot hold three mutually unbiased bases simultaneously. The mathematical loop physically cannot close.", "tokens": [51235, 440, 957, 5720, 497, 17, 307, 886, 22195, 11, 886, 4962, 13, 467, 2935, 2644, 1797, 1045, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 16561, 13, 440, 18894, 6367, 9762, 2644, 1998, 13, 51701], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0740829143884047, "compression_ratio": 1.647940074906367, "no_speech_prob": 1.898022537749622e-12}, {"id": 409, "seek": 167668, "start": 1676.68, "end": 1686.1200000000001, "text": " But going back to the woodshop, if you lift one of those pieces of wood slightly off the flat table, if you allow it to move into the third dimension, you can easily bend it so the corners touch perfectly.", "tokens": [50365, 583, 516, 646, 281, 264, 4576, 29431, 11, 498, 291, 5533, 472, 295, 729, 3755, 295, 4576, 4748, 766, 264, 4962, 3199, 11, 498, 291, 2089, 309, 281, 1286, 666, 264, 2636, 10139, 11, 291, 393, 3612, 11229, 309, 370, 264, 12413, 2557, 6239, 13, 50837], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09121679551530593, "compression_ratio": 1.6920152091254752, "no_speech_prob": 1.9432211915970665e-12}, {"id": 410, "seek": 167668, "start": 1686.28, "end": 1695.0800000000002, "text": " And in our mathematical framework, lifting it off the table corresponds directly to moving from the restrictive real numbers R2 to the more expansive complex number C2.", "tokens": [50845, 400, 294, 527, 18894, 8388, 11, 15798, 309, 766, 264, 3199, 23249, 3838, 281, 2684, 490, 264, 43220, 957, 3547, 497, 17, 281, 264, 544, 46949, 3997, 1230, 383, 17, 13, 51285], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09121679551530593, "compression_ratio": 1.6920152091254752, "no_speech_prob": 1.9432211915970665e-12}, {"id": 411, "seek": 167668, "start": 1695.26, "end": 1696.0800000000002, "text": " Ah, I see.", "tokens": [51294, 2438, 11, 286, 536, 13, 51335], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09121679551530593, "compression_ratio": 1.6920152091254752, "no_speech_prob": 1.9432211915970665e-12}, {"id": 412, "seek": 167668, "start": 1696.28, "end": 1699.26, "text": " The complex numbers provide those extra degrees of freedom.", "tokens": [51345, 440, 3997, 3547, 2893, 729, 2857, 5310, 295, 5645, 13, 51494], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09121679551530593, "compression_ratio": 1.6920152091254752, "no_speech_prob": 1.9432211915970665e-12}, {"id": 413, "seek": 169926, "start": 1699.26, "end": 1702.92, "text": " The imaginary phase angles, which act as that third dimension.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 26164, 5574, 14708, 11, 597, 605, 382, 300, 2636, 10139, 13, 50548], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17329537868499756, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920832360794002e-12}, {"id": 414, "seek": 169926, "start": 1703.36, "end": 1707.02, "text": " They allow the math to twist the pieces and seamlessly close the loop.", "tokens": [50570, 814, 2089, 264, 5221, 281, 8203, 264, 3755, 293, 38083, 1998, 264, 6367, 13, 50753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17329537868499756, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920832360794002e-12}, {"id": 415, "seek": 169926, "start": 1707.6, "end": 1714.66, "text": " The complex theory handles those exact same three mutually unbiased bases flawlessly without any absurdities.", "tokens": [50782, 440, 3997, 5261, 18722, 729, 1900, 912, 1045, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 13717, 12048, 1553, 604, 19774, 1088, 13, 51135], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17329537868499756, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920832360794002e-12}, {"id": 416, "seek": 169926, "start": 1715.2, "end": 1720.58, "text": " Chapter 7 wraps up this entire exploration by formally calling this triangle the minimal witness.", "tokens": [51162, 18874, 1614, 25831, 493, 341, 2302, 16197, 538, 25983, 5141, 341, 13369, 264, 13206, 7286, 13, 51431], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17329537868499756, "compression_ratio": 1.5570776255707763, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920832360794002e-12}, {"id": 417, "seek": 172058, "start": 1720.58, "end": 1724.3, "text": " In graph theory terms, the triangle represents a cycle rank of 1.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 4295, 5261, 2115, 11, 264, 13369, 8855, 257, 6586, 6181, 295, 502, 13, 50551], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10186013830713479, "compression_ratio": 1.613733905579399, "no_speech_prob": 1.7077804014692388e-12}, {"id": 418, "seek": 172058, "start": 1725.0, "end": 1735.96, "text": " It is the absolute smallest possible structure, the minimal witness, where real numbers definitively fail to map physical reality and complex numbers elegantly succeed.", "tokens": [50586, 467, 307, 264, 8236, 16998, 1944, 3877, 11, 264, 13206, 7286, 11, 689, 957, 3547, 28152, 356, 3061, 281, 4471, 4001, 4103, 293, 3997, 3547, 14459, 3627, 7754, 13, 51134], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10186013830713479, "compression_ratio": 1.613733905579399, "no_speech_prob": 1.7077804014692388e-12}, {"id": 419, "seek": 172058, "start": 1736.28, "end": 1744.1999999999998, "text": " So if we pause and take stock, we've seen a single isolated system get twisted into a logical knot if we try to force it to use real numbers.", "tokens": [51150, 407, 498, 321, 10465, 293, 747, 4127, 11, 321, 600, 1612, 257, 2167, 14621, 1185, 483, 23057, 666, 257, 14978, 16966, 498, 321, 853, 281, 3464, 309, 281, 764, 957, 3547, 13, 51546], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10186013830713479, "compression_ratio": 1.613733905579399, "no_speech_prob": 1.7077804014692388e-12}, {"id": 420, "seek": 174420, "start": 1744.2, "end": 1752.26, "text": " It fails structurally at n equals 3 with a single matrix, and it fails at n equals 2 if we attempt to chain three different contexts together in a closed loop.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 18199, 6594, 6512, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 365, 257, 2167, 8141, 11, 293, 309, 18199, 412, 297, 6915, 568, 498, 321, 5217, 281, 5021, 1045, 819, 30628, 1214, 294, 257, 5395, 6367, 13, 50768], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 421, "seek": 174420, "start": 1752.46, "end": 1755.32, "text": " Real numbers just can't hack the geometry of the universe.", "tokens": [50778, 8467, 3547, 445, 393, 380, 10339, 264, 18426, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 50921], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 422, "seek": 174420, "start": 1755.46, "end": 1763.4, "text": " But the author is not even close to finish putting the constraints on trial, because there's a whole other axis to physical reality that we haven't even touched yet.", "tokens": [50928, 583, 264, 3793, 307, 406, 754, 1998, 281, 2413, 3372, 264, 18491, 322, 7308, 11, 570, 456, 311, 257, 1379, 661, 10298, 281, 4001, 4103, 300, 321, 2378, 380, 754, 9828, 1939, 13, 51325], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 423, "seek": 174420, "start": 1763.42, "end": 1763.5800000000002, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51326, 1779, 13, 51334], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 424, "seek": 174420, "start": 1763.8, "end": 1767.28, "text": " The universe isn't just one isolated system sitting alone in a void.", "tokens": [51345, 440, 6445, 1943, 380, 445, 472, 14621, 1185, 3798, 3312, 294, 257, 22009, 13, 51519], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 425, "seek": 174420, "start": 1767.82, "end": 1768.7, "text": " Things interact.", "tokens": [51546, 9514, 4648, 13, 51590], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 426, "seek": 174420, "start": 1769.28, "end": 1773.56, "text": " What happens when you bring a second entirely separate system into the mix?", "tokens": [51619, 708, 2314, 562, 291, 1565, 257, 1150, 7696, 4994, 1185, 666, 264, 2890, 30, 51833], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12267111241817474, "compression_ratio": 1.6507462686567165, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214531666462363e-12}, {"id": 427, "seek": 177356, "start": 1773.56, "end": 1779.98, "text": " This takes us to section 4 of our deep dive, the dimensions of reality and system composition.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 2516, 505, 281, 3541, 1017, 295, 527, 2452, 9192, 11, 264, 12819, 295, 4103, 293, 1185, 12686, 13, 50686], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11400315284729004, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.846838654229188e-12}, {"id": 428, "seek": 177356, "start": 1780.54, "end": 1782.5, "text": " This is where chapter 8 opens up.", "tokens": [50714, 639, 307, 689, 7187, 1649, 9870, 493, 13, 50812], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11400315284729004, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.846838654229188e-12}, {"id": 429, "seek": 177356, "start": 1783.1399999999999, "end": 1790.9199999999998, "text": " Imagine you have system A, let's say an electron, sitting in a laboratory in New York, and you have system B, another electron, sitting in a laboratory in Tokyo.", "tokens": [50844, 11739, 291, 362, 1185, 316, 11, 718, 311, 584, 364, 6084, 11, 3798, 294, 257, 16523, 294, 1873, 3609, 11, 293, 291, 362, 1185, 363, 11, 1071, 6084, 11, 3798, 294, 257, 16523, 294, 15147, 13, 51233], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11400315284729004, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.846838654229188e-12}, {"id": 430, "seek": 177356, "start": 1791.12, "end": 1791.36, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [51243, 1033, 13, 51255], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11400315284729004, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.846838654229188e-12}, {"id": 431, "seek": 177356, "start": 1791.94, "end": 1800.56, "text": " The fundamental question is how do their individual mathematical descriptions combine to describe the joint overarching system of A and B together?", "tokens": [51284, 440, 8088, 1168, 307, 577, 360, 641, 2609, 18894, 24406, 10432, 281, 6786, 264, 7225, 45501, 1185, 295, 316, 293, 363, 1214, 30, 51715], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11400315284729004, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.846838654229188e-12}, {"id": 432, "seek": 180056, "start": 1800.56, "end": 1807.36, "text": " Now, any standard physics textbook would just scream, use the tensor product and move on to the next page without a second thought.", "tokens": [50365, 823, 11, 604, 3832, 10649, 25591, 576, 445, 7291, 11, 764, 264, 40863, 1674, 293, 1286, 322, 281, 264, 958, 3028, 1553, 257, 1150, 1194, 13, 50705], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11339302062988281, "compression_ratio": 1.5887445887445888, "no_speech_prob": 1.5193204767199875e-12}, {"id": 433, "seek": 180056, "start": 1807.44, "end": 1808.84, "text": " That's standard operating procedure.", "tokens": [50709, 663, 311, 3832, 7447, 10747, 13, 50779], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11339302062988281, "compression_ratio": 1.5887445887445888, "no_speech_prob": 1.5193204767199875e-12}, {"id": 434, "seek": 180056, "start": 1809.36, "end": 1812.8799999999999, "text": " But remember our guiding motto, constraint before content.", "tokens": [50805, 583, 1604, 527, 25061, 32680, 11, 25534, 949, 2701, 13, 50981], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11339302062988281, "compression_ratio": 1.5887445887445888, "no_speech_prob": 1.5193204767199875e-12}, {"id": 435, "seek": 180056, "start": 1813.06, "end": 1817.0, "text": " We're intentionally not assuming the complex tensor product right out of the gate.", "tokens": [50990, 492, 434, 22062, 406, 11926, 264, 3997, 40863, 1674, 558, 484, 295, 264, 8539, 13, 51187], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11339302062988281, "compression_ratio": 1.5887445887445888, "no_speech_prob": 1.5193204767199875e-12}, {"id": 436, "seek": 180056, "start": 1817.2, "end": 1820.1599999999999, "text": " We are rigorously testing the candidate's scalar fields.", "tokens": [51197, 492, 366, 42191, 5098, 4997, 264, 11532, 311, 39684, 7909, 13, 51345], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11339302062988281, "compression_ratio": 1.5887445887445888, "no_speech_prob": 1.5193204767199875e-12}, {"id": 437, "seek": 182016, "start": 1820.16, "end": 1827.5, "text": " So we are going to put the real numbers, the complex numbers, and even the quaternions on trial and see how they handle the stress of composite systems.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 321, 366, 516, 281, 829, 264, 957, 3547, 11, 264, 3997, 3547, 11, 293, 754, 264, 421, 771, 77, 626, 322, 7308, 293, 536, 577, 436, 4813, 264, 4244, 295, 25557, 3652, 13, 50732], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 438, "seek": 182016, "start": 1827.7, "end": 1828.0600000000002, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50742, 1033, 13, 50760], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 439, "seek": 182016, "start": 1828.38, "end": 1828.8200000000002, "text": " Quaternions.", "tokens": [50776, 2326, 771, 77, 626, 13, 50798], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 440, "seek": 182016, "start": 1828.94, "end": 1829.96, "text": " We haven't even mentioned them yet.", "tokens": [50804, 492, 2378, 380, 754, 2835, 552, 1939, 13, 50855], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 441, "seek": 182016, "start": 1830.3400000000001, "end": 1835.64, "text": " Those are the exotic math structures that are like complex numbers on steroids, right?", "tokens": [50874, 3950, 366, 264, 27063, 5221, 9227, 300, 366, 411, 3997, 3547, 322, 45717, 11, 558, 30, 51139], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 442, "seek": 182016, "start": 1835.68, "end": 1839.6200000000001, "text": " Instead of just one imaginary part, they have three different imaginary parts, I, J, and K.", "tokens": [51141, 7156, 295, 445, 472, 26164, 644, 11, 436, 362, 1045, 819, 26164, 3166, 11, 286, 11, 508, 11, 293, 591, 13, 51338], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11657780647277832, "compression_ratio": 1.670995670995671, "no_speech_prob": 1.727929865164013e-12}, {"id": 443, "seek": 183962, "start": 1839.62, "end": 1840.3799999999999, "text": " That's a good summary.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 311, 257, 665, 12691, 13, 50403], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 444, "seek": 183962, "start": 1840.78, "end": 1849.1999999999998, "text": " They are a perfectly valid, robust mathematical field, and they are often considered by physicists as a candidate for building exotic physical theories.", "tokens": [50423, 814, 366, 257, 6239, 7363, 11, 13956, 18894, 2519, 11, 293, 436, 366, 2049, 4888, 538, 48716, 382, 257, 11532, 337, 2390, 27063, 4001, 13667, 13, 50844], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 445, "seek": 183962, "start": 1849.4199999999998, "end": 1853.54, "text": " So the author tests all three, real complex and quaternionic.", "tokens": [50855, 407, 264, 3793, 6921, 439, 1045, 11, 957, 3997, 293, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 13, 51061], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 446, "seek": 183962, "start": 1853.62, "end": 1858.4199999999998, "text": " And the strict constraint we apply to test them is a principle called local tomography?", "tokens": [51065, 400, 264, 10910, 25534, 321, 3079, 281, 1500, 552, 307, 257, 8665, 1219, 2654, 2916, 5820, 30, 51305], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 447, "seek": 183962, "start": 1858.76, "end": 1859.1, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [51322, 1079, 13, 51339], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 448, "seek": 183962, "start": 1859.3999999999999, "end": 1868.52, "text": " The text defines this as the strict rule that a joint state should be exactly as informative as the sum of all complete local measurements on its individual parts.", "tokens": [51354, 440, 2487, 23122, 341, 382, 264, 10910, 4978, 300, 257, 7225, 1785, 820, 312, 2293, 382, 27759, 382, 264, 2408, 295, 439, 3566, 2654, 15383, 322, 1080, 2609, 3166, 13, 51810], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10286788253096847, "compression_ratio": 1.6689189189189189, "no_speech_prob": 2.0122588491322535e-12}, {"id": 449, "seek": 186852, "start": 1868.52, "end": 1883.44, "text": " Meaning, if I completely measure every single thing there is to know about System A in New York, and you completely measure every single thing there is to know about System B in Tokyo, and we get on the phone and pool our data, we should have the complete 100% picture of the joint system A-B.", "tokens": [50365, 19948, 11, 498, 286, 2584, 3481, 633, 2167, 551, 456, 307, 281, 458, 466, 8910, 316, 294, 1873, 3609, 11, 293, 291, 2584, 3481, 633, 2167, 551, 456, 307, 281, 458, 466, 8910, 363, 294, 15147, 11, 293, 321, 483, 322, 264, 2593, 293, 7005, 527, 1412, 11, 321, 820, 362, 264, 3566, 2319, 4, 3036, 295, 264, 7225, 1185, 316, 12, 33, 13, 51111], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0868471349988665, "compression_ratio": 1.8297101449275361, "no_speech_prob": 2.2103321777044993e-12}, {"id": 450, "seek": 186852, "start": 1883.68, "end": 1893.02, "text": " There should be no holistic parameters, no spooky hidden variables that exist only in the joint system that we can't see by looking really closely at the individual parts.", "tokens": [51123, 821, 820, 312, 572, 30334, 9834, 11, 572, 30510, 7633, 9102, 300, 2514, 787, 294, 264, 7225, 1185, 300, 321, 393, 380, 536, 538, 1237, 534, 8185, 412, 264, 2609, 3166, 13, 51590], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0868471349988665, "compression_ratio": 1.8297101449275361, "no_speech_prob": 2.2103321777044993e-12}, {"id": 451, "seek": 186852, "start": 1893.3799999999999, "end": 1895.74, "text": " The parts must fully explain the whole.", "tokens": [51608, 440, 3166, 1633, 4498, 2903, 264, 1379, 13, 51726], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.0868471349988665, "compression_ratio": 1.8297101449275361, "no_speech_prob": 2.2103321777044993e-12}, {"id": 452, "seek": 189574, "start": 1895.74, "end": 1906.06, "text": " To test if this holds true, the author uses a stunning piece of dimension-counting mathematics, often referred to in the literature as the Iraqi Woodard's Hardy Argument.", "tokens": [50365, 1407, 1500, 498, 341, 9190, 2074, 11, 264, 3793, 4960, 257, 18550, 2522, 295, 10139, 12, 26050, 278, 18666, 11, 2049, 10839, 281, 294, 264, 10394, 382, 264, 35149, 11558, 515, 311, 43930, 40081, 2206, 13, 50881], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09283458376393734, "compression_ratio": 1.652014652014652, "no_speech_prob": 1.4051468322229255e-12}, {"id": 453, "seek": 189574, "start": 1906.84, "end": 1914.38, "text": " We're literally going to count the degrees of freedom, the available dimensions of the state spaces for these different mathematical fields when they compose.", "tokens": [50920, 492, 434, 3736, 516, 281, 1207, 264, 5310, 295, 5645, 11, 264, 2435, 12819, 295, 264, 1785, 7673, 337, 613, 819, 18894, 7909, 562, 436, 35925, 13, 51297], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09283458376393734, "compression_ratio": 1.652014652014652, "no_speech_prob": 1.4051468322229255e-12}, {"id": 454, "seek": 189574, "start": 1914.64, "end": 1916.4, "text": " Let's do the math out loud for the listener.", "tokens": [51310, 961, 311, 360, 264, 5221, 484, 6588, 337, 264, 31569, 13, 51398], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09283458376393734, "compression_ratio": 1.652014652014652, "no_speech_prob": 1.4051468322229255e-12}, {"id": 455, "seek": 189574, "start": 1916.54, "end": 1920.1200000000001, "text": " Let's start with what turns out to be the Goldilocks case, complex number C.", "tokens": [51405, 961, 311, 722, 365, 437, 4523, 484, 281, 312, 264, 6731, 388, 35507, 1389, 11, 3997, 1230, 383, 13, 51584], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09283458376393734, "compression_ratio": 1.652014652014652, "no_speech_prob": 1.4051468322229255e-12}, {"id": 456, "seek": 192012, "start": 1920.12, "end": 1926.76, "text": " In a complex mathematical theory, the dimension of the state space for a system of size D is exactly D-squared.", "tokens": [50365, 682, 257, 3997, 18894, 5261, 11, 264, 10139, 295, 264, 1785, 1901, 337, 257, 1185, 295, 2744, 413, 307, 2293, 413, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 50697], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 457, "seek": 192012, "start": 1927.2199999999998, "end": 1929.78, "text": " So System A in New York has a dimension of D-A-squared.", "tokens": [50720, 407, 8910, 316, 294, 1873, 3609, 575, 257, 10139, 295, 413, 12, 32, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 50848], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 458, "seek": 192012, "start": 1930.28, "end": 1933.34, "text": " System B in Tokyo has a dimension of D-B-squared.", "tokens": [50873, 8910, 363, 294, 15147, 575, 257, 10139, 295, 413, 12, 33, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 51026], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 459, "seek": 192012, "start": 1933.4399999999998, "end": 1933.6399999999999, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [51031, 1033, 13, 51041], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 460, "seek": 192012, "start": 1933.9799999999998, "end": 1940.34, "text": " If the principle of local tomography holds true, multiplying the information of the parts should give us the exact information of the whole.", "tokens": [51058, 759, 264, 8665, 295, 2654, 2916, 5820, 9190, 2074, 11, 30955, 264, 1589, 295, 264, 3166, 820, 976, 505, 264, 1900, 1589, 295, 264, 1379, 13, 51376], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 461, "seek": 192012, "start": 1940.78, "end": 1943.3999999999999, "text": " So we multiply D-A-squared times D-B-squared.", "tokens": [51398, 407, 321, 12972, 413, 12, 32, 12, 33292, 1642, 1413, 413, 12, 33, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 51529], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11293232029881971, "compression_ratio": 1.8222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 1.3562773950800966e-12}, {"id": 462, "seek": 194340, "start": 1943.4, "end": 1946.2800000000002, "text": " Which gives us D-A-times D-B-squared.", "tokens": [50365, 3013, 2709, 505, 413, 12, 32, 12, 83, 1532, 413, 12, 33, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 50509], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 463, "seek": 194340, "start": 1946.7, "end": 1956.52, "text": " And if we build a brand new complex system from scratch with the size of D-A-times D-B, its state space dimension formula tells us it is also D-A-times D-B-squared.", "tokens": [50530, 400, 498, 321, 1322, 257, 3360, 777, 3997, 1185, 490, 8459, 365, 264, 2744, 295, 413, 12, 32, 12, 83, 1532, 413, 12, 33, 11, 1080, 1785, 1901, 10139, 8513, 5112, 505, 309, 307, 611, 413, 12, 32, 12, 83, 1532, 413, 12, 33, 12, 33292, 1642, 13, 51021], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 464, "seek": 194340, "start": 1956.7, "end": 1957.9, "text": " It perfectly balances.", "tokens": [51030, 467, 6239, 33993, 13, 51090], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 465, "seek": 194340, "start": 1958.14, "end": 1958.5400000000002, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [51102, 7587, 13, 51122], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 466, "seek": 194340, "start": 1958.8400000000001, "end": 1961.72, "text": " The parts multiplied together exactly equal the dimensions of the whole.", "tokens": [51137, 440, 3166, 17207, 1214, 2293, 2681, 264, 12819, 295, 264, 1379, 13, 51281], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 467, "seek": 194340, "start": 1962.3200000000002, "end": 1965.5800000000002, "text": " Complex numbers satisfy the constraint of local tomography flawlessly.", "tokens": [51311, 41184, 3547, 19319, 264, 25534, 295, 2654, 2916, 5820, 13717, 12048, 13, 51474], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 468, "seek": 194340, "start": 1966.0400000000002, "end": 1967.5800000000002, "text": " The ledger is perfectly balanced.", "tokens": [51497, 440, 4684, 1321, 307, 6239, 13902, 13, 51574], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 469, "seek": 194340, "start": 1967.8600000000001, "end": 1970.66, "text": " But what happens when we force quaternions to compose?", "tokens": [51588, 583, 437, 2314, 562, 321, 3464, 421, 771, 77, 626, 281, 35925, 30, 51728], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13888818567449396, "compression_ratio": 1.6859205776173285, "no_speech_prob": 1.5920810676750552e-12}, {"id": 470, "seek": 197066, "start": 1970.66, "end": 1974.1200000000001, "text": " For quaternions H, the state space dimension formula is different.", "tokens": [50365, 1171, 421, 771, 77, 626, 389, 11, 264, 1785, 1901, 10139, 8513, 307, 819, 13, 50538], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 471, "seek": 197066, "start": 1974.26, "end": 1976.44, "text": " It is D-times-2-D-minus-1.", "tokens": [50545, 467, 307, 413, 12, 83, 1532, 12, 17, 12, 35, 12, 2367, 301, 12, 16, 13, 50654], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 472, "seek": 197066, "start": 1976.6200000000001, "end": 1980.3400000000001, "text": " Let's test this by composing two basic quibits, so our size D equals 2.", "tokens": [50663, 961, 311, 1500, 341, 538, 715, 6110, 732, 3875, 421, 897, 1208, 11, 370, 527, 2744, 413, 6915, 568, 13, 50849], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 473, "seek": 197066, "start": 1980.44, "end": 1980.66, "text": " Okay.", "tokens": [50854, 1033, 13, 50865], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 474, "seek": 197066, "start": 1980.8600000000001, "end": 1987.4, "text": " The local dimension for one system is 2 times 4 minus 1, which is 2 times 3, giving us 6 dimensions of data.", "tokens": [50875, 440, 2654, 10139, 337, 472, 1185, 307, 568, 1413, 1017, 3175, 502, 11, 597, 307, 568, 1413, 805, 11, 2902, 505, 1386, 12819, 295, 1412, 13, 51202], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 475, "seek": 197066, "start": 1987.5600000000002, "end": 1989.64, "text": " So System A in New York has 6 dimensions.", "tokens": [51210, 407, 8910, 316, 294, 1873, 3609, 575, 1386, 12819, 13, 51314], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 476, "seek": 197066, "start": 1989.9, "end": 1991.5400000000002, "text": " System B in Tokyo has 6.", "tokens": [51327, 8910, 363, 294, 15147, 575, 1386, 13, 51409], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 477, "seek": 197066, "start": 1991.92, "end": 1994.7, "text": " If we multiply them together, 6 times 6 equals 36.", "tokens": [51428, 759, 321, 12972, 552, 1214, 11, 1386, 1413, 1386, 6915, 8652, 13, 51567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 478, "seek": 197066, "start": 1995.2, "end": 1999.8200000000002, "text": " Local tomography predicts that the joint overarching system should have exactly 36 dimensions.", "tokens": [51592, 22755, 2916, 5820, 6069, 82, 300, 264, 7225, 45501, 1185, 820, 362, 2293, 8652, 12819, 13, 51823], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14079748259650338, "compression_ratio": 1.6217105263157894, "no_speech_prob": 1.3509890905635813e-12}, {"id": 479, "seek": 199982, "start": 1999.82, "end": 2005.4399999999998, "text": " But if we look at the actual quaternionic state space formula for a joint system of size 4,", "tokens": [50365, 583, 498, 321, 574, 412, 264, 3539, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 1785, 1901, 8513, 337, 257, 7225, 1185, 295, 2744, 1017, 11, 50646], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13679190604917466, "compression_ratio": 1.6222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 2.389576383987624e-12}, {"id": 480, "seek": 199982, "start": 2005.98, "end": 2012.6, "text": " since 2 times 2 equals 4 outcomes total, the dimension formula is 4 times 8 minus 1,", "tokens": [50673, 1670, 568, 1413, 568, 6915, 1017, 10070, 3217, 11, 264, 10139, 8513, 307, 1017, 1413, 1649, 3175, 502, 11, 51004], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13679190604917466, "compression_ratio": 1.6222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 2.389576383987624e-12}, {"id": 481, "seek": 199982, "start": 2013.02, "end": 2015.76, "text": " which is 4 times 7, resulting in 28 dimensions.", "tokens": [51025, 597, 307, 1017, 1413, 1614, 11, 16505, 294, 7562, 12819, 13, 51162], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13679190604917466, "compression_ratio": 1.6222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 2.389576383987624e-12}, {"id": 482, "seek": 199982, "start": 2016.06, "end": 2018.46, "text": " 36 predicted versus 28 actual.", "tokens": [51177, 8652, 19147, 5717, 7562, 3539, 13, 51297], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13679190604917466, "compression_ratio": 1.6222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 2.389576383987624e-12}, {"id": 483, "seek": 199982, "start": 2019.0, "end": 2025.3, "text": " The local parts give you 36 dimensions of data, but the joint system mathematically only has room to hold 28.", "tokens": [51324, 440, 2654, 3166, 976, 291, 8652, 12819, 295, 1412, 11, 457, 264, 7225, 1185, 44003, 787, 575, 1808, 281, 1797, 7562, 13, 51639], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13679190604917466, "compression_ratio": 1.6222222222222222, "no_speech_prob": 2.389576383987624e-12}, {"id": 484, "seek": 202530, "start": 2025.3, "end": 2027.8, "text": " The composite space has too few dimensions.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 25557, 1901, 575, 886, 1326, 12819, 13, 50490], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 485, "seek": 202530, "start": 2028.48, "end": 2031.1399999999999, "text": " The local parts mathematically over-determine the whole.", "tokens": [50524, 440, 2654, 3166, 44003, 670, 12, 49136, 533, 264, 1379, 13, 50657], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 486, "seek": 202530, "start": 2031.68, "end": 2036.7, "text": " The quaternionic tensor product is a famously messy, over-constrained mathematical object,", "tokens": [50684, 440, 421, 771, 77, 313, 299, 40863, 1674, 307, 257, 34360, 16191, 11, 670, 12, 1671, 19639, 2001, 18894, 2657, 11, 50935], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 487, "seek": 202530, "start": 2036.98, "end": 2042.6, "text": " and this simple dimension counting exercise shows exactly why it fails the test of physical reality.", "tokens": [50949, 293, 341, 2199, 10139, 13251, 5380, 3110, 2293, 983, 309, 18199, 264, 1500, 295, 4001, 4103, 13, 51230], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 488, "seek": 202530, "start": 2042.68, "end": 2045.02, "text": " Okay, so quaternions are violently thrown out.", "tokens": [51234, 1033, 11, 370, 421, 771, 77, 626, 366, 46728, 11732, 484, 13, 51351], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 489, "seek": 202530, "start": 2045.1599999999999, "end": 2047.26, "text": " What about our old frenemy, the real numbers?", "tokens": [51358, 708, 466, 527, 1331, 33596, 3633, 11, 264, 957, 3547, 30, 51463], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1387459470870647, "compression_ratio": 1.5975103734439835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6165637621926021e-12}, {"id": 490, "seek": 204726, "start": 2047.26, "end": 2052.28, "text": " For real numbers are, the state space dimension formula is d times d plus 1 divided by 2.", "tokens": [50365, 1171, 957, 3547, 366, 11, 264, 1785, 1901, 10139, 8513, 307, 274, 1413, 274, 1804, 502, 6666, 538, 568, 13, 50616], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 491, "seek": 204726, "start": 2052.72, "end": 2055.62, "text": " Let's test composing two real quavats, so again, d equals 2.", "tokens": [50638, 961, 311, 1500, 715, 6110, 732, 957, 421, 706, 1720, 11, 370, 797, 11, 274, 6915, 568, 13, 50783], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 492, "seek": 204726, "start": 2056.08, "end": 2060.86, "text": " The local dimension for New York is 2 times 3 divided by 2, which simplifies to 3 dimensions.", "tokens": [50806, 440, 2654, 10139, 337, 1873, 3609, 307, 568, 1413, 805, 6666, 538, 568, 11, 597, 6883, 11221, 281, 805, 12819, 13, 51045], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 493, "seek": 204726, "start": 2061.24, "end": 2063.44, "text": " System A provides 3 dimension of data.", "tokens": [51064, 8910, 316, 6417, 805, 10139, 295, 1412, 13, 51174], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 494, "seek": 204726, "start": 2063.86, "end": 2065.08, "text": " System B provides 3.", "tokens": [51195, 8910, 363, 6417, 805, 13, 51256], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 495, "seek": 204726, "start": 2065.56, "end": 2066.52, "text": " Multiply them together.", "tokens": [51280, 31150, 356, 552, 1214, 13, 51328], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 496, "seek": 204726, "start": 2066.66, "end": 2068.26, "text": " 3 times 3 equals 9.", "tokens": [51335, 805, 1413, 805, 6915, 1722, 13, 51415], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 497, "seek": 204726, "start": 2069.0, "end": 2073.36, "text": " Local tomography predicts exactly 9 dimensions for the joint real system.", "tokens": [51452, 22755, 2916, 5820, 6069, 82, 2293, 1722, 12819, 337, 264, 7225, 957, 1185, 13, 51670], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14386928392493206, "compression_ratio": 1.688, "no_speech_prob": 1.846842991037878e-12}, {"id": 498, "seek": 207336, "start": 2073.36, "end": 2077.92, "text": " Now, calculate the actual real state space for a joint system of size 4.", "tokens": [50365, 823, 11, 8873, 264, 3539, 957, 1785, 1901, 337, 257, 7225, 1185, 295, 2744, 1017, 13, 50593], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 499, "seek": 207336, "start": 2078.56, "end": 2083.0, "text": " The formula is 4 times 5 divided by 2, which equals 10.", "tokens": [50625, 440, 8513, 307, 1017, 1413, 1025, 6666, 538, 568, 11, 597, 6915, 1266, 13, 50847], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 500, "seek": 207336, "start": 2083.1400000000003, "end": 2084.84, "text": " 9 predicted versus 10 actual.", "tokens": [50854, 1722, 19147, 5717, 1266, 3539, 13, 50939], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 501, "seek": 207336, "start": 2085.1600000000003, "end": 2087.76, "text": " The real composite space has too many dimensions.", "tokens": [50955, 440, 957, 25557, 1901, 575, 886, 867, 12819, 13, 51085], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 502, "seek": 207336, "start": 2087.94, "end": 2089.04, "text": " That's the fatal flaw.", "tokens": [51094, 663, 311, 264, 24069, 13717, 13, 51149], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 503, "seek": 207336, "start": 2089.52, "end": 2093.92, "text": " A real composite system carries extra non-locally accessible parameters.", "tokens": [51173, 316, 957, 25557, 1185, 16402, 2857, 2107, 12, 5842, 379, 9515, 9834, 13, 51393], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 504, "seek": 207336, "start": 2094.2400000000002, "end": 2097.42, "text": " Okay, we need a really solid analogy here because this is wild.", "tokens": [51409, 1033, 11, 321, 643, 257, 534, 5100, 21663, 510, 570, 341, 307, 4868, 13, 51568], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 505, "seek": 207336, "start": 2097.6600000000003, "end": 2100.3, "text": " The last time I tried to explain this with bank accounts, it fell apart.", "tokens": [51580, 440, 1036, 565, 286, 3031, 281, 2903, 341, 365, 3765, 9402, 11, 309, 5696, 4936, 13, 51712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 506, "seek": 207336, "start": 2100.76, "end": 2102.04, "text": " Let's think about data storage.", "tokens": [51735, 961, 311, 519, 466, 1412, 6725, 13, 51799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14935015843919486, "compression_ratio": 1.5925925925925926, "no_speech_prob": 2.849196139437238e-12}, {"id": 507, "seek": 210204, "start": 2102.04, "end": 2108.16, "text": " Imagine New York has a hard drive holding system A, and Tokyo has a hard drive holding system B.", "tokens": [50365, 11739, 1873, 3609, 575, 257, 1152, 3332, 5061, 1185, 316, 11, 293, 15147, 575, 257, 1152, 3332, 5061, 1185, 363, 13, 50671], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 508, "seek": 210204, "start": 2108.4, "end": 2108.98, "text": " I like this better.", "tokens": [50683, 286, 411, 341, 1101, 13, 50712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 509, "seek": 210204, "start": 2109.08, "end": 2109.36, "text": " Go on.", "tokens": [50717, 1037, 322, 13, 50731], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 510, "seek": 210204, "start": 2109.74, "end": 2113.12, "text": " With complex numbers, New York has a drive with 10 gigabytes of data.", "tokens": [50750, 2022, 3997, 3547, 11, 1873, 3609, 575, 257, 3332, 365, 1266, 42741, 295, 1412, 13, 50919], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 511, "seek": 210204, "start": 2113.64, "end": 2115.36, "text": " Tokyo has a drive with 10 gigabytes.", "tokens": [50945, 15147, 575, 257, 3332, 365, 1266, 42741, 13, 51031], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 512, "seek": 210204, "start": 2115.86, "end": 2121.7, "text": " When you network them together, the network administrator sees exactly 100 gigabytes of possible combined states.", "tokens": [51056, 1133, 291, 3209, 552, 1214, 11, 264, 3209, 25529, 8194, 2293, 2319, 42741, 295, 1944, 9354, 4368, 13, 51348], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 513, "seek": 210204, "start": 2122.06, "end": 2122.72, "text": " It balances.", "tokens": [51366, 467, 33993, 13, 51399], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 514, "seek": 210204, "start": 2123.2, "end": 2125.9, "text": " But with real numbers, New York has a 3 gigabyte drive.", "tokens": [51423, 583, 365, 957, 3547, 11, 1873, 3609, 575, 257, 805, 8741, 34529, 3332, 13, 51558], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 515, "seek": 210204, "start": 2126.38, "end": 2127.7599999999998, "text": " Tokyo has a 3 gigabyte drive.", "tokens": [51582, 15147, 575, 257, 805, 8741, 34529, 3332, 13, 51651], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16922982257345448, "compression_ratio": 1.9776785714285714, "no_speech_prob": 2.3160735482252726e-12}, {"id": 516, "seek": 212776, "start": 2127.76, "end": 2134.3, "text": " When they network them together, the network administrator suddenly sees 10 gigabytes of data space available.", "tokens": [50365, 1133, 436, 3209, 552, 1214, 11, 264, 3209, 25529, 5800, 8194, 1266, 42741, 295, 1412, 1901, 2435, 13, 50692], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13804288705190024, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283440725632484e-12}, {"id": 517, "seek": 212776, "start": 2134.8, "end": 2139.42, "text": " There is an entire phantom gigabyte of data that physically exists in the network.", "tokens": [50717, 821, 307, 364, 2302, 903, 25796, 8741, 34529, 295, 1412, 300, 9762, 8198, 294, 264, 3209, 13, 50948], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13804288705190024, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283440725632484e-12}, {"id": 518, "seek": 212776, "start": 2139.76, "end": 2144.0200000000004, "text": " But if you inspect the New York drive or the Tokyo drive individually, it is nowhere to be found.", "tokens": [50965, 583, 498, 291, 15018, 264, 1873, 3609, 3332, 420, 264, 15147, 3332, 16652, 11, 309, 307, 11159, 281, 312, 1352, 13, 51178], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13804288705190024, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283440725632484e-12}, {"id": 519, "seek": 212776, "start": 2144.2000000000003, "end": 2145.6800000000003, "text": " That analogy holds up perfectly.", "tokens": [51187, 663, 21663, 9190, 493, 6239, 13, 51261], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13804288705190024, "compression_ratio": 1.565217391304348, "no_speech_prob": 2.0283440725632484e-12}, {"id": 520, "seek": 214568, "start": 2145.68, "end": 2153.8999999999996, "text": " The local observers in New York and Tokyo literally cannot access or see the extra parameter that exists in the joint reality.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2654, 48090, 294, 1873, 3609, 293, 15147, 3736, 2644, 2105, 420, 536, 264, 2857, 13075, 300, 8198, 294, 264, 7225, 4103, 13, 50776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 521, "seek": 214568, "start": 2154.3599999999997, "end": 2156.3199999999997, "text": " The parts do not fully explain the whole.", "tokens": [50799, 440, 3166, 360, 406, 4498, 2903, 264, 1379, 13, 50897], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 522, "seek": 214568, "start": 2156.72, "end": 2161.2599999999998, "text": " So real numbers actually saddle us with more hidden dimensions than complex numbers do.", "tokens": [50917, 407, 957, 3547, 767, 30459, 505, 365, 544, 7633, 12819, 813, 3997, 3547, 360, 13, 51144], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 523, "seek": 214568, "start": 2161.5, "end": 2165.3999999999996, "text": " That feels entirely backward to how we normally discuss quantum weirdness.", "tokens": [51156, 663, 3417, 7696, 23897, 281, 577, 321, 5646, 2248, 13018, 3657, 1287, 13, 51351], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 524, "seek": 214568, "start": 2165.62, "end": 2168.8999999999996, "text": " We usually think of complex numbers as the exotic, complicated ones.", "tokens": [51362, 492, 2673, 519, 295, 3997, 3547, 382, 264, 27063, 11, 6179, 2306, 13, 51526], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 525, "seek": 214568, "start": 2169.04, "end": 2172.14, "text": " What exactly is this invisible 10th dimension in the real numbers?", "tokens": [51533, 708, 2293, 307, 341, 14603, 1266, 392, 10139, 294, 264, 957, 3547, 30, 51688], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 526, "seek": 214568, "start": 2172.3399999999997, "end": 2174.3999999999996, "text": " Where is the hidden gigabyte of data hiding?", "tokens": [51698, 2305, 307, 264, 7633, 8741, 34529, 295, 1412, 10596, 30, 51801], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12690117529460362, "compression_ratio": 1.7066666666666668, "no_speech_prob": 1.5674041925484161e-12}, {"id": 527, "seek": 217440, "start": 2174.4, "end": 2181.1800000000003, "text": " To answer what this phantom dimension actually is, we have to isolate the exact matrix where real numbers hide their secrets.", "tokens": [50365, 1407, 1867, 437, 341, 903, 25796, 10139, 767, 307, 11, 321, 362, 281, 25660, 264, 1900, 8141, 689, 957, 3547, 6479, 641, 14093, 13, 50704], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 528, "seek": 217440, "start": 2181.6, "end": 2185.2400000000002, "text": " And that brings us to section 5 of our dive, the invisible thread.", "tokens": [50725, 400, 300, 5607, 505, 281, 3541, 1025, 295, 527, 9192, 11, 264, 14603, 7207, 13, 50907], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 529, "seek": 217440, "start": 2185.56, "end": 2187.1600000000003, "text": " This covers chapters 9 through 12.", "tokens": [50923, 639, 10538, 20013, 1722, 807, 2272, 13, 51003], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 530, "seek": 217440, "start": 2187.62, "end": 2189.96, "text": " And this is where the text gets incredibly concrete.", "tokens": [51026, 400, 341, 307, 689, 264, 2487, 2170, 6252, 9859, 13, 51143], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 531, "seek": 217440, "start": 2190.36, "end": 2194.52, "text": " It doesn't just wave its hands and say there is a 10th dimension somewhere in the math.", "tokens": [51163, 467, 1177, 380, 445, 5772, 1080, 2377, 293, 584, 456, 307, 257, 1266, 392, 10139, 4079, 294, 264, 5221, 13, 51371], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 532, "seek": 217440, "start": 2194.7200000000003, "end": 2200.1, "text": " It literally hunts down and isolates the exact matrix responsible for it.", "tokens": [51381, 467, 3736, 7396, 1373, 760, 293, 7381, 1024, 264, 1900, 8141, 6250, 337, 309, 13, 51650], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13734913780575708, "compression_ratio": 1.6492537313432836, "no_speech_prob": 2.271636654824216e-12}, {"id": 533, "seek": 220010, "start": 2200.1, "end": 2200.6, "text": " It does.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 775, 13, 50390], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16441474958907726, "compression_ratio": 1.7572815533980584, "no_speech_prob": 1.9969091484550727e-12}, {"id": 534, "seek": 220010, "start": 2201.12, "end": 2206.5, "text": " And the culprit responsible for the missing dimension is an antisymmetric matrix conventionally called J.", "tokens": [50416, 400, 264, 39220, 6250, 337, 264, 5361, 10139, 307, 364, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 8141, 10286, 379, 1219, 508, 13, 50685], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16441474958907726, "compression_ratio": 1.7572815533980584, "no_speech_prob": 1.9969091484550727e-12}, {"id": 535, "seek": 220010, "start": 2206.7999999999997, "end": 2208.7799999999997, "text": " Let's define antisymmetric for the listener.", "tokens": [50700, 961, 311, 6964, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 337, 264, 31569, 13, 50799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16441474958907726, "compression_ratio": 1.7572815533980584, "no_speech_prob": 1.9969091484550727e-12}, {"id": 536, "seek": 220010, "start": 2209.1, "end": 2213.12, "text": " If you have a square grid of numbers and you flip it across its diagonal axis,", "tokens": [50815, 759, 291, 362, 257, 3732, 10748, 295, 3547, 293, 291, 7929, 309, 2108, 1080, 21539, 10298, 11, 51016], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16441474958907726, "compression_ratio": 1.7572815533980584, "no_speech_prob": 1.9969091484550727e-12}, {"id": 537, "seek": 220010, "start": 2213.52, "end": 2219.58, "text": " an antisymmetric matrix is one where all the positive numbers become negative and all the negative numbers become positive.", "tokens": [51036, 364, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 8141, 307, 472, 689, 439, 264, 3353, 3547, 1813, 3671, 293, 439, 264, 3671, 3547, 1813, 3353, 13, 51339], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16441474958907726, "compression_ratio": 1.7572815533980584, "no_speech_prob": 1.9969091484550727e-12}, {"id": 538, "seek": 221958, "start": 2219.58, "end": 2220.9, "text": " That is the exact definition.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 264, 1900, 7123, 13, 50431], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 539, "seek": 221958, "start": 2220.9, "end": 2228.5, "text": " Now, in the real number theory, an observable property of a system, meaning something you can actually build a physical machine in a lab to measure,", "tokens": [50431, 823, 11, 294, 264, 957, 1230, 5261, 11, 364, 9951, 712, 4707, 295, 257, 1185, 11, 3620, 746, 291, 393, 767, 1322, 257, 4001, 3479, 294, 257, 2715, 281, 3481, 11, 50811], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 540, "seek": 221958, "start": 2228.86, "end": 2231.9, "text": " must mathematically be represented by a symmetric matrix.", "tokens": [50829, 1633, 44003, 312, 10379, 538, 257, 32330, 8141, 13, 50981], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 541, "seek": 221958, "start": 2232.0, "end": 2232.24, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [50986, 1779, 13, 50998], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 542, "seek": 221958, "start": 2233.34, "end": 2239.06, "text": " Because J is antisymmetric, it fundamentally is not a valid observable on its own.", "tokens": [51053, 1436, 508, 307, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 11, 309, 17879, 307, 406, 257, 7363, 9951, 712, 322, 1080, 1065, 13, 51339], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 543, "seek": 221958, "start": 2239.36, "end": 2243.08, "text": " You cannot build a detector in New York to measure J on system A.", "tokens": [51354, 509, 2644, 1322, 257, 25712, 294, 1873, 3609, 281, 3481, 508, 322, 1185, 316, 13, 51540], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 544, "seek": 221958, "start": 2243.2799999999997, "end": 2245.58, "text": " It is completely invisible to local measurement.", "tokens": [51550, 467, 307, 2584, 14603, 281, 2654, 13160, 13, 51665], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15727438882132558, "compression_ratio": 1.6333333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.674768505301183e-12}, {"id": 545, "seek": 224558, "start": 2245.58, "end": 2248.4, "text": " Okay, so J doesn't exist locally, but...", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 370, 508, 1177, 380, 2514, 16143, 11, 457, 485, 50506], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 546, "seek": 224558, "start": 2248.4, "end": 2252.02, "text": " Well, what happens mathematically when you multiply two antisymmetric matrices together?", "tokens": [50506, 1042, 11, 437, 2314, 44003, 562, 291, 12972, 732, 2511, 14169, 2174, 17475, 32284, 1214, 30, 50687], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 547, "seek": 224558, "start": 2252.4, "end": 2254.2599999999998, "text": " A negative times a negative is a positive.", "tokens": [50706, 316, 3671, 1413, 257, 3671, 307, 257, 3353, 13, 50799], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 548, "seek": 224558, "start": 2254.46, "end": 2254.98, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [50809, 48746, 736, 13, 50835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 549, "seek": 224558, "start": 2255.34, "end": 2259.66, "text": " The text proves that when you take the tensor product of the invisible J matrix on system A", "tokens": [50853, 440, 2487, 25019, 300, 562, 291, 747, 264, 40863, 1674, 295, 264, 14603, 508, 8141, 322, 1185, 316, 51069], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 550, "seek": 224558, "start": 2259.66, "end": 2265.2799999999997, "text": " and multiply it by the invisible J matrix on system B, creating the joint matrix J tensor J,", "tokens": [51069, 293, 12972, 309, 538, 264, 14603, 508, 8141, 322, 1185, 363, 11, 4084, 264, 7225, 8141, 508, 40863, 508, 11, 51350], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 551, "seek": 224558, "start": 2265.98, "end": 2268.96, "text": " the antisymmetry of the two parts perfectly cancels out.", "tokens": [51385, 264, 2511, 14169, 2174, 9889, 295, 264, 732, 3166, 6239, 393, 66, 1625, 484, 13, 51534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 552, "seek": 224558, "start": 2269.22, "end": 2272.56, "text": " The resulting joint matrix is completely mathematically symmetric.", "tokens": [51547, 440, 16505, 7225, 8141, 307, 2584, 44003, 32330, 13, 51714], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12178584507533483, "compression_ratio": 1.835820895522388, "no_speech_prob": 1.7214525161249328e-12}, {"id": 553, "seek": 227256, "start": 2272.56, "end": 2280.2, "text": " Oh, wow. Because it is now symmetric J tensor, J becomes a real, valid, observable property of the joint overarching system.", "tokens": [50365, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 1436, 309, 307, 586, 32330, 508, 40863, 11, 508, 3643, 257, 957, 11, 7363, 11, 9951, 712, 4707, 295, 264, 7225, 45501, 1185, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 554, "seek": 227256, "start": 2280.42, "end": 2285.38, "text": " But because you can only build it by multiplying two things that don't locally exist as observables...", "tokens": [50758, 583, 570, 291, 393, 787, 1322, 309, 538, 30955, 732, 721, 300, 500, 380, 16143, 2514, 382, 9951, 2965, 485, 51006], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 555, "seek": 227256, "start": 2285.38, "end": 2288.2599999999998, "text": " No local product-based measurement can ever possibly see it.", "tokens": [51006, 883, 2654, 1674, 12, 6032, 13160, 393, 1562, 6264, 536, 309, 13, 51150], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 556, "seek": 227256, "start": 2288.72, "end": 2292.16, "text": " The local scientists in New York and Tokyo are completely blind to its existence,", "tokens": [51173, 440, 2654, 7708, 294, 1873, 3609, 293, 15147, 366, 2584, 6865, 281, 1080, 9123, 11, 51345], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 557, "seek": 227256, "start": 2292.54, "end": 2294.94, "text": " but it physically exists in the joint reality.", "tokens": [51364, 457, 309, 9762, 8198, 294, 264, 7225, 4103, 13, 51484], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 558, "seek": 227256, "start": 2295.6, "end": 2299.7, "text": " That J tensor J matrix is exactly your phantom tenth dimension.", "tokens": [51517, 663, 508, 40863, 508, 8141, 307, 2293, 428, 903, 25796, 27269, 10139, 13, 51722], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10140821955225489, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6949138490277016e-12}, {"id": 559, "seek": 229970, "start": 2299.7, "end": 2303.64, "text": " That is deeply unsettling. If this parameter exists, does it have bounds?", "tokens": [50365, 663, 307, 8760, 43964, 1688, 13, 759, 341, 13075, 8198, 11, 775, 309, 362, 29905, 30, 50562], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 560, "seek": 229970, "start": 2303.9199999999996, "end": 2306.2799999999997, "text": " Can this invisible dimension be cranked up to infinity?", "tokens": [50576, 1664, 341, 14603, 10139, 312, 21263, 292, 493, 281, 13202, 30, 50694], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 561, "seek": 229970, "start": 2306.7799999999997, "end": 2310.0, "text": " It cannot, and that boundary is covered in Chapter 10, Positivity.", "tokens": [50719, 467, 2644, 11, 293, 300, 12866, 307, 5343, 294, 18874, 1266, 11, 430, 9598, 4253, 13, 50880], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 562, "seek": 229970, "start": 2310.74, "end": 2317.12, "text": " The fundamental mathematics of probability strictly requires that all probabilities must be greater than or equal to zero.", "tokens": [50917, 440, 8088, 18666, 295, 8482, 20792, 7029, 300, 439, 33783, 1633, 312, 5044, 813, 420, 2681, 281, 4018, 13, 51236], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 563, "seek": 229970, "start": 2317.48, "end": 2319.96, "text": " You can't have a negative 10% chance of something happening.", "tokens": [51254, 509, 393, 380, 362, 257, 3671, 1266, 4, 2931, 295, 746, 2737, 13, 51378], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 564, "seek": 229970, "start": 2320.24, "end": 2320.54, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51392, 1779, 13, 51407], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14701023730602894, "compression_ratio": 1.494208494208494, "no_speech_prob": 1.776246250778657e-12}, {"id": 565, "seek": 232054, "start": 2320.54, "end": 2324.9, "text": " This positivity constraint places a strict leech on our hidden dimension.", "tokens": [50365, 639, 35198, 25534, 3190, 257, 10910, 476, 5023, 322, 527, 7633, 10139, 13, 50583], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18364322368915265, "compression_ratio": 1.5869565217391304, "no_speech_prob": 1.687534226940679e-12}, {"id": 566, "seek": 232054, "start": 2325.62, "end": 2332.2799999999997, "text": " If we add a variable amount of this J tensor J matrix to our overall state, let's call the amount cc,", "tokens": [50619, 759, 321, 909, 257, 7006, 2372, 295, 341, 508, 40863, 508, 8141, 281, 527, 4787, 1785, 11, 718, 311, 818, 264, 2372, 269, 66, 11, 50952], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18364322368915265, "compression_ratio": 1.5869565217391304, "no_speech_prob": 1.687534226940679e-12}, {"id": 567, "seek": 232054, "start": 2332.68, "end": 2339.9, "text": " the positivity constraint proves mathematically that the parameter c must be strictly bounded between minus 1 and 1.", "tokens": [50972, 264, 35198, 25534, 25019, 44003, 300, 264, 13075, 269, 1633, 312, 20792, 37498, 1296, 3175, 502, 293, 502, 13, 51333], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.18364322368915265, "compression_ratio": 1.5869565217391304, "no_speech_prob": 1.687534226940679e-12}, {"id": 568, "seek": 233990, "start": 2339.9, "end": 2342.1600000000003, "text": " So it's a genuine physical sliding scale.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 309, 311, 257, 16699, 4001, 21169, 4373, 13, 50478], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 569, "seek": 233990, "start": 2342.28, "end": 2349.4, "text": " You can have a universe where the state is c equals 0.5 or a universe where c equals minus 0.8,", "tokens": [50484, 509, 393, 362, 257, 6445, 689, 264, 1785, 307, 269, 6915, 1958, 13, 20, 420, 257, 6445, 689, 269, 6915, 3175, 1958, 13, 23, 11, 50840], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 570, "seek": 233990, "start": 2349.54, "end": 2353.4, "text": " and they are genuinely, measurably different global states of reality.", "tokens": [50847, 293, 436, 366, 17839, 11, 5731, 374, 1188, 819, 4338, 4368, 295, 4103, 13, 51040], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 571, "seek": 233990, "start": 2353.52, "end": 2355.6600000000003, "text": " They are completely distinct global states, yes.", "tokens": [51046, 814, 366, 2584, 10644, 4338, 4368, 11, 2086, 13, 51153], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 572, "seek": 233990, "start": 2356.2200000000003, "end": 2360.0, "text": " But look at what happens in Chapter 11 when we introduce the partial trace.", "tokens": [51181, 583, 574, 412, 437, 2314, 294, 18874, 2975, 562, 321, 5366, 264, 14641, 13508, 13, 51370], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 573, "seek": 233990, "start": 2360.2400000000002, "end": 2365.2000000000003, "text": " The partial trace is the mathematical tool physicists use for ignoring one part of a system, right?", "tokens": [51382, 440, 14641, 13508, 307, 264, 18894, 2290, 48716, 764, 337, 26258, 472, 644, 295, 257, 1185, 11, 558, 30, 51630], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09954309917631604, "compression_ratio": 1.678294573643411, "no_speech_prob": 1.714109865331992e-12}, {"id": 574, "seek": 236520, "start": 2365.2, "end": 2368.52, "text": " If I'm sitting in New York and I want to predict what my local instruments will do,", "tokens": [50365, 759, 286, 478, 3798, 294, 1873, 3609, 293, 286, 528, 281, 6069, 437, 452, 2654, 12190, 486, 360, 11, 50531], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 575, "seek": 236520, "start": 2368.9399999999996, "end": 2372.62, "text": " I use the partial trace to mathematically erase Tokyo from the equations.", "tokens": [50552, 286, 764, 264, 14641, 13508, 281, 44003, 23525, 15147, 490, 264, 11787, 13, 50736], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 576, "seek": 236520, "start": 2373.22, "end": 2378.2599999999998, "text": " And when you apply that partial trace operation to our global state containing the hidden c parameter,", "tokens": [50766, 400, 562, 291, 3079, 300, 14641, 13508, 6916, 281, 527, 4338, 1785, 19273, 264, 7633, 269, 13075, 11, 51018], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 577, "seek": 236520, "start": 2378.8599999999997, "end": 2382.0, "text": " the mathematics completely annihilates the J tensor J term.", "tokens": [51048, 264, 18666, 2584, 40430, 388, 1024, 264, 508, 40863, 508, 1433, 13, 51205], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 578, "seek": 236520, "start": 2382.14, "end": 2384.8999999999996, "text": " The c parameter vanishes entirely from the equation.", "tokens": [51212, 440, 269, 13075, 3161, 16423, 7696, 490, 264, 5367, 13, 51350], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 579, "seek": 236520, "start": 2385.16, "end": 2389.22, "text": " So no matter what the global value of c actually is,", "tokens": [51363, 407, 572, 1871, 437, 264, 4338, 2158, 295, 269, 767, 307, 11, 51566], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 580, "seek": 236520, "start": 2389.62, "end": 2392.66, "text": " whether the universe is sitting at 0.9 or minus 0.5,", "tokens": [51586, 1968, 264, 6445, 307, 3798, 412, 1958, 13, 24, 420, 3175, 1958, 13, 20, 11, 51738], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11406483857528023, "compression_ratio": 1.6807017543859648, "no_speech_prob": 1.7417470460703854e-12}, {"id": 581, "seek": 239266, "start": 2392.66, "end": 2397.44, "text": " the state looks totally, completely mixed and boring to the local observer in New York.", "tokens": [50365, 264, 1785, 1542, 3879, 11, 2584, 7467, 293, 9989, 281, 264, 2654, 27878, 294, 1873, 3609, 13, 50604], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 582, "seek": 239266, "start": 2397.7, "end": 2399.22, "text": " It just looks like random static.", "tokens": [50617, 467, 445, 1542, 411, 4974, 13437, 13, 50693], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 583, "seek": 239266, "start": 2399.3799999999997, "end": 2399.8199999999997, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50701, 7587, 13, 50723], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 584, "seek": 239266, "start": 2400.12, "end": 2404.8999999999996, "text": " The entire physical interval of states between minus 1 and 1 is marginally invisible.", "tokens": [50738, 440, 2302, 4001, 15035, 295, 4368, 1296, 3175, 502, 293, 502, 307, 10270, 379, 14603, 13, 50977], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 585, "seek": 239266, "start": 2405.08, "end": 2406.52, "text": " You cannot tell them apart locally.", "tokens": [50986, 509, 2644, 980, 552, 4936, 16143, 13, 51058], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 586, "seek": 239266, "start": 2407.0, "end": 2411.18, "text": " Now this realization sets up the absolute biggest twist in the entire text.", "tokens": [51082, 823, 341, 25138, 6352, 493, 264, 8236, 3880, 8203, 294, 264, 2302, 2487, 13, 51291], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 587, "seek": 239266, "start": 2411.2799999999997, "end": 2411.46, "text": " Yeah.", "tokens": [51296, 865, 13, 51305], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 588, "seek": 239266, "start": 2411.7, "end": 2413.7599999999998, "text": " Chapter 12, the mind bender.", "tokens": [51317, 18874, 2272, 11, 264, 1575, 272, 3216, 13, 51420], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 589, "seek": 239266, "start": 2413.96, "end": 2414.46, "text": " Oh, I'm ready.", "tokens": [51430, 876, 11, 286, 478, 1919, 13, 51455], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 590, "seek": 239266, "start": 2414.7999999999997, "end": 2419.5, "text": " We have this state, let's call it row of c, containing this invisible parameter c.", "tokens": [51472, 492, 362, 341, 1785, 11, 718, 311, 818, 309, 5386, 295, 269, 11, 19273, 341, 14603, 13075, 269, 13, 51707], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 591, "seek": 239266, "start": 2419.94, "end": 2422.1, "text": " We have to ask the ultimate quantum question.", "tokens": [51729, 492, 362, 281, 1029, 264, 9705, 13018, 1168, 13, 51837], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16643899815683147, "compression_ratio": 1.6044303797468353, "no_speech_prob": 1.6815726328750502e-12}, {"id": 592, "seek": 242210, "start": 2422.1, "end": 2424.02, "text": " Is this state entangled?", "tokens": [50365, 1119, 341, 1785, 948, 39101, 30, 50461], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 593, "seek": 242210, "start": 2424.46, "end": 2424.9, "text": " Entangled.", "tokens": [50483, 3951, 39101, 13, 50505], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 594, "seek": 242210, "start": 2425.36, "end": 2427.2999999999997, "text": " The signature feature of quantum mechanics,", "tokens": [50528, 440, 13397, 4111, 295, 13018, 12939, 11, 50625], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 595, "seek": 242210, "start": 2427.7599999999998, "end": 2433.06, "text": " the spooky non-local correlation that cannot possibly be explained by classical shared information.", "tokens": [50648, 264, 30510, 2107, 12, 5842, 304, 20009, 300, 2644, 6264, 312, 8825, 538, 13735, 5507, 1589, 13, 50913], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 596, "seek": 242210, "start": 2433.08, "end": 2433.56, "text": " That's the one.", "tokens": [50914, 663, 311, 264, 472, 13, 50938], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 597, "seek": 242210, "start": 2433.66, "end": 2436.56, "text": " So we ask, the math is the state row of c, entangled.", "tokens": [50943, 407, 321, 1029, 11, 264, 5221, 307, 264, 1785, 5386, 295, 269, 11, 948, 39101, 13, 51088], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 598, "seek": 242210, "start": 2436.8199999999997, "end": 2439.7, "text": " And the text delivers a spectacular unexpected answer.", "tokens": [51101, 400, 264, 2487, 24860, 257, 18149, 13106, 1867, 13, 51245], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 599, "seek": 242210, "start": 2440.12, "end": 2444.18, "text": " It proves that the answer depends entirely on which mathematical field you're using to look at it.", "tokens": [51266, 467, 25019, 300, 264, 1867, 5946, 7696, 322, 597, 18894, 2519, 291, 434, 1228, 281, 574, 412, 309, 13, 51469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 600, "seek": 242210, "start": 2444.2599999999998, "end": 2444.62, "text": " Wait, what?", "tokens": [51473, 3802, 11, 437, 30, 51491], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 601, "seek": 242210, "start": 2445.04, "end": 2446.64, "text": " Entanglement is a physical reality.", "tokens": [51512, 3951, 656, 3054, 307, 257, 4001, 4103, 13, 51592], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 602, "seek": 242210, "start": 2446.74, "end": 2447.96, "text": " It shouldn't depend on the math.", "tokens": [51597, 467, 4659, 380, 5672, 322, 264, 5221, 13, 51658], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 603, "seek": 242210, "start": 2448.2, "end": 2449.04, "text": " Let's look at the proof.", "tokens": [51670, 961, 311, 574, 412, 264, 8177, 13, 51712], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.16514228538230613, "compression_ratio": 1.6633986928104576, "no_speech_prob": 1.6947593502181224e-12}, {"id": 604, "seek": 244904, "start": 2449.04, "end": 2453.62, "text": " If you are operating strictly within the theory of real numbers,", "tokens": [50365, 759, 291, 366, 7447, 20792, 1951, 264, 5261, 295, 957, 3547, 11, 50594], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19008217917548287, "compression_ratio": 1.5445544554455446, "no_speech_prob": 1.4607671626129526e-12}, {"id": 605, "seek": 244904, "start": 2454.5, "end": 2461.2599999999998, "text": " the text proves definitively that the state is entangled for any value of c other than exactly zero.", "tokens": [50638, 264, 2487, 25019, 28152, 356, 300, 264, 1785, 307, 948, 39101, 337, 604, 2158, 295, 269, 661, 813, 2293, 4018, 13, 50976], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19008217917548287, "compression_ratio": 1.5445544554455446, "no_speech_prob": 1.4607671626129526e-12}, {"id": 606, "seek": 244904, "start": 2461.96, "end": 2466.04, "text": " The J tensor J matrix literally acts as an entanglement witness.", "tokens": [51011, 440, 508, 40863, 508, 8141, 3736, 10672, 382, 364, 948, 656, 3054, 7286, 13, 51215], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19008217917548287, "compression_ratio": 1.5445544554455446, "no_speech_prob": 1.4607671626129526e-12}, {"id": 607, "seek": 244904, "start": 2466.66, "end": 2471.02, "text": " The correlations it produces absolutely cannot be built out of local real states.", "tokens": [51246, 440, 13983, 763, 309, 14725, 3122, 2644, 312, 3094, 484, 295, 2654, 957, 4368, 13, 51464], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19008217917548287, "compression_ratio": 1.5445544554455446, "no_speech_prob": 1.4607671626129526e-12}, {"id": 608, "seek": 247102, "start": 2471.02, "end": 2475.3, "text": " Okay, so if we live in a real number universe, the state is undeniably entangled.", "tokens": [50365, 1033, 11, 370, 498, 321, 1621, 294, 257, 957, 1230, 6445, 11, 264, 1785, 307, 674, 15711, 1188, 948, 39101, 13, 50579], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09903924259138697, "compression_ratio": 1.6540284360189574, "no_speech_prob": 1.4552897732375558e-12}, {"id": 609, "seek": 247102, "start": 2475.42, "end": 2482.58, "text": " But if you take that exact same physical matrix, that exact same grid of numbers describing the exact same physical correlations,", "tokens": [50585, 583, 498, 291, 747, 300, 1900, 912, 4001, 8141, 11, 300, 1900, 912, 10748, 295, 3547, 16141, 264, 1900, 912, 4001, 13983, 763, 11, 50943], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09903924259138697, "compression_ratio": 1.6540284360189574, "no_speech_prob": 1.4552897732375558e-12}, {"id": 610, "seek": 247102, "start": 2482.92, "end": 2485.5, "text": " and you look at it from within the theory of complex numbers.", "tokens": [50960, 293, 291, 574, 412, 309, 490, 1951, 264, 5261, 295, 3997, 3547, 13, 51089], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09903924259138697, "compression_ratio": 1.6540284360189574, "no_speech_prob": 1.4552897732375558e-12}, {"id": 611, "seek": 247102, "start": 2485.6, "end": 2489.36, "text": " Where complex phases are allowed to exist locally on the individual drives.", "tokens": [51094, 2305, 3997, 18764, 366, 4350, 281, 2514, 16143, 322, 264, 2609, 11754, 13, 51282], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09903924259138697, "compression_ratio": 1.6540284360189574, "no_speech_prob": 1.4552897732375558e-12}, {"id": 612, "seek": 248936, "start": 2489.36, "end": 2495.84, "text": " The text proves mathematically that the state can be written perfectly as a convex combination of local product states.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 2487, 25019, 44003, 300, 264, 1785, 393, 312, 3720, 6239, 382, 257, 42432, 6562, 295, 2654, 1674, 4368, 13, 50689], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 613, "seek": 248936, "start": 2496.48, "end": 2499.2400000000002, "text": " Meaning, in the complex theory, it is entirely separable.", "tokens": [50721, 19948, 11, 294, 264, 3997, 5261, 11, 309, 307, 7696, 3128, 712, 13, 50859], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 614, "seek": 248936, "start": 2499.6, "end": 2500.78, "text": " It is not entangled at all.", "tokens": [50877, 467, 307, 406, 948, 39101, 412, 439, 13, 50936], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 615, "seek": 248936, "start": 2500.92, "end": 2501.88, "text": " So what does this all mean?", "tokens": [50943, 407, 437, 775, 341, 439, 914, 30, 50991], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 616, "seek": 248936, "start": 2502.42, "end": 2506.52, "text": " It means that entanglement is not an intrinsic fundamental physical property of a matrix.", "tokens": [51018, 467, 1355, 300, 948, 656, 3054, 307, 406, 364, 35698, 8088, 4001, 4707, 295, 257, 8141, 13, 51223], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 617, "seek": 248936, "start": 2507.2200000000003, "end": 2511.28, "text": " It is not some glowing magical aura that a state either has or doesn't have in a vacuum.", "tokens": [51258, 467, 307, 406, 512, 27064, 12066, 18355, 300, 257, 1785, 2139, 575, 420, 1177, 380, 362, 294, 257, 14224, 13, 51461], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 618, "seek": 248936, "start": 2511.82, "end": 2513.1600000000003, "text": " Entanglement is field relative.", "tokens": [51488, 3951, 656, 3054, 307, 2519, 4972, 13, 51555], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1441113860518844, "compression_ratio": 1.6754716981132076, "no_speech_prob": 1.4782064461371647e-12}, {"id": 619, "seek": 251316, "start": 2513.16, "end": 2520.92, "text": " A state is only classified as entangled relative to the mathematical vocabulary you are allowed to use to describe the local parts.", "tokens": [50365, 316, 1785, 307, 787, 20627, 382, 948, 39101, 4972, 281, 264, 18894, 19864, 291, 366, 4350, 281, 764, 281, 6786, 264, 2654, 3166, 13, 50753], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 620, "seek": 251316, "start": 2521.2999999999997, "end": 2522.96, "text": " You've grasped the core revelation.", "tokens": [50772, 509, 600, 29444, 3452, 264, 4965, 23456, 13, 50855], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 621, "seek": 251316, "start": 2523.7799999999997, "end": 2527.7599999999998, "text": " Because the complex theory allows for a much richer, more expansive set of local states,", "tokens": [50896, 1436, 264, 3997, 5261, 4045, 337, 257, 709, 29021, 11, 544, 46949, 992, 295, 2654, 4368, 11, 51095], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 622, "seek": 251316, "start": 2528.2, "end": 2530.6, "text": " states utilizing complex rotating phases,", "tokens": [51117, 4368, 26775, 3997, 19627, 18764, 11, 51237], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 623, "seek": 251316, "start": 2531.12, "end": 2536.7, "text": " it can locally explain correlations that the impoverished real theory has to throw its hands up", "tokens": [51263, 309, 393, 16143, 2903, 13983, 763, 300, 264, 704, 3570, 4729, 957, 5261, 575, 281, 3507, 1080, 2377, 493, 51542], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 624, "seek": 251316, "start": 2536.7, "end": 2540.12, "text": " and classify as spooky non-local entanglement.", "tokens": [51542, 293, 33872, 382, 30510, 2107, 12, 5842, 304, 948, 656, 3054, 13, 51713], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 625, "seek": 251316, "start": 2540.2, "end": 2541.06, "text": " Oh, wow.", "tokens": [51717, 876, 11, 6076, 13, 51760], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13478271016534768, "compression_ratio": 1.7045454545454546, "no_speech_prob": 1.8182007548855905e-12}, {"id": 626, "seek": 254106, "start": 2541.06, "end": 2546.56, "text": " The real theory is forced to call it entanglement simply because its local vocabulary is too poor", "tokens": [50365, 440, 957, 5261, 307, 7579, 281, 818, 309, 948, 656, 3054, 2935, 570, 1080, 2654, 19864, 307, 886, 4716, 50640], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 627, "seek": 254106, "start": 2546.56, "end": 2548.54, "text": " to describe the correlation any other way.", "tokens": [50640, 281, 6786, 264, 20009, 604, 661, 636, 13, 50739], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 628, "seek": 254106, "start": 2548.66, "end": 2550.86, "text": " That just, that completely breaks my brain a little bit.", "tokens": [50745, 663, 445, 11, 300, 2584, 9857, 452, 3567, 257, 707, 857, 13, 50855], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 629, "seek": 254106, "start": 2550.94, "end": 2554.66, "text": " It turns entanglement into a feature of the mathematical framework's limitations,", "tokens": [50859, 467, 4523, 948, 656, 3054, 666, 257, 4111, 295, 264, 18894, 8388, 311, 15705, 11, 51045], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 630, "seek": 254106, "start": 2554.9, "end": 2557.44, "text": " not just a standalone physical fact of the universe.", "tokens": [51057, 406, 445, 257, 37454, 4001, 1186, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 51184], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 631, "seek": 254106, "start": 2557.7799999999997, "end": 2559.88, "text": " It brilliantly highlights why the author's motto,", "tokens": [51201, 467, 8695, 42580, 14254, 983, 264, 3793, 311, 32680, 11, 51306], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 632, "seek": 254106, "start": 2560.0, "end": 2563.68, "text": " Constraint Before Content, is such a powerful and necessary lens.", "tokens": [51312, 8574, 424, 686, 4546, 30078, 11, 307, 1270, 257, 4005, 293, 4818, 6765, 13, 51496], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 633, "seek": 254106, "start": 2564.22, "end": 2568.5, "text": " It shows us exactly where our deeply held physical concepts actually originate.", "tokens": [51523, 467, 3110, 505, 2293, 689, 527, 8760, 5167, 4001, 10392, 767, 4957, 473, 13, 51737], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 634, "seek": 254106, "start": 2568.5, "end": 2570.88, "text": " They originate from the geometry of the math.", "tokens": [51737, 814, 4957, 473, 490, 264, 18426, 295, 264, 5221, 13, 51856], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12979229351946417, "compression_ratio": 1.7083333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 2.2539524500292396e-12}, {"id": 635, "seek": 257106, "start": 2571.06, "end": 2575.92, "text": " So the text has thoroughly and systematically dismantled real numbers.", "tokens": [50365, 407, 264, 2487, 575, 17987, 293, 39531, 30506, 1493, 957, 3547, 13, 50608], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 636, "seek": 257106, "start": 2576.48, "end": 2579.1, "text": " It showed they fail structurally at n equals 3.", "tokens": [50636, 467, 4712, 436, 3061, 6594, 6512, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 13, 50767], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 637, "seek": 257106, "start": 2579.6, "end": 2582.5, "text": " They physically fail to close triangular loops,", "tokens": [50792, 814, 9762, 3061, 281, 1998, 38190, 16121, 11, 50937], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 638, "seek": 257106, "start": 2582.62, "end": 2587.6, "text": " and they spawn invisible dimensions that lead to massive field-relative entanglement confusion.", "tokens": [50943, 293, 436, 17088, 14603, 12819, 300, 1477, 281, 5994, 2519, 12, 4419, 1166, 948, 656, 3054, 15075, 13, 51192], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 639, "seek": 257106, "start": 2588.4, "end": 2591.56, "text": " Complex numbers navigate all of these constraints perfectly, but...", "tokens": [51232, 41184, 3547, 12350, 439, 295, 613, 18491, 6239, 11, 457, 485, 51390], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 640, "seek": 257106, "start": 2591.56, "end": 2592.84, "text": " But we have a lingering problem.", "tokens": [51390, 583, 321, 362, 257, 49542, 1154, 13, 51454], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17125713678053867, "compression_ratio": 1.5125, "no_speech_prob": 1.7554118965715237e-12}, {"id": 641, "seek": 259284, "start": 2592.84, "end": 2595.1600000000003, "text": " We still don't have actual physics.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 920, 500, 380, 362, 3539, 10649, 13, 50481], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1771227993182282, "compression_ratio": 1.5080213903743316, "no_speech_prob": 1.7616343496798526e-12}, {"id": 642, "seek": 259284, "start": 2595.1600000000003, "end": 2600.26, "text": " We have spent all this time talking about abstract vector spaces, simplices, and polytopes.", "tokens": [50481, 492, 362, 4418, 439, 341, 565, 1417, 466, 12649, 8062, 7673, 11, 6883, 1473, 11, 293, 6754, 19337, 279, 13, 50736], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1771227993182282, "compression_ratio": 1.5080213903743316, "no_speech_prob": 1.7616343496798526e-12}, {"id": 643, "seek": 259284, "start": 2600.6400000000003, "end": 2604.0, "text": " How do we get the actual textbook physics equations back?", "tokens": [50755, 1012, 360, 321, 483, 264, 3539, 25591, 10649, 11787, 646, 30, 50923], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1771227993182282, "compression_ratio": 1.5080213903743316, "no_speech_prob": 1.7616343496798526e-12}, {"id": 644, "seek": 259284, "start": 2604.28, "end": 2609.54, "text": " How do we bridge the gap from geometric constraints to the things I actually learned in college?", "tokens": [50937, 1012, 360, 321, 7283, 264, 7417, 490, 33246, 18491, 281, 264, 721, 286, 767, 3264, 294, 3859, 30, 51200], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1771227993182282, "compression_ratio": 1.5080213903743316, "no_speech_prob": 1.7616343496798526e-12}, {"id": 645, "seek": 260954, "start": 2609.54, "end": 2612.8, "text": " That bridge is the final stage of our journey today.", "tokens": [50365, 663, 7283, 307, 264, 2572, 3233, 295, 527, 4671, 965, 13, 50528], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 646, "seek": 260954, "start": 2613.08, "end": 2614.82, "text": " Section 6, earning the textbook.", "tokens": [50542, 21804, 1386, 11, 12353, 264, 25591, 13, 50629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 647, "seek": 260954, "start": 2615.2799999999997, "end": 2619.06, "text": " And we cross this bridge through what the author calls the assumption audit.", "tokens": [50652, 400, 321, 3278, 341, 7283, 807, 437, 264, 3793, 5498, 264, 15302, 17748, 13, 50841], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 648, "seek": 260954, "start": 2619.2599999999998, "end": 2621.58, "text": " Part 3 of the text, recovered structure.", "tokens": [50851, 4100, 805, 295, 264, 2487, 11, 19542, 3877, 13, 50967], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 649, "seek": 260954, "start": 2621.82, "end": 2623.7, "text": " I genuinely loved this part of the document.", "tokens": [50979, 286, 17839, 4333, 341, 644, 295, 264, 4166, 13, 51073], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 650, "seek": 260954, "start": 2624.0, "end": 2627.4, "text": " The author is incredibly strict and intellectually honest with themselves.", "tokens": [51088, 440, 3793, 307, 6252, 10910, 293, 46481, 3245, 365, 2969, 13, 51258], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19503775278727215, "compression_ratio": 1.516431924882629, "no_speech_prob": 1.6783058233091341e-12}, {"id": 651, "seek": 262740, "start": 2627.4, "end": 2629.88, "text": " They refuse to just wave their hands at the end and say,", "tokens": [50365, 814, 16791, 281, 445, 5772, 641, 2377, 412, 264, 917, 293, 584, 11, 50489], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 652, "seek": 262740, "start": 2630.04, "end": 2631.7000000000003, "text": " and therefore quantum mechanics is true.", "tokens": [50497, 293, 4412, 13018, 12939, 307, 2074, 13, 50580], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 653, "seek": 262740, "start": 2632.2400000000002, "end": 2635.92, "text": " They painstakingly go through an assumption ledger in chapters 13 and 18", "tokens": [50607, 814, 1822, 372, 2456, 356, 352, 807, 364, 15302, 4684, 1321, 294, 20013, 3705, 293, 2443, 50791], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 654, "seek": 262740, "start": 2635.92, "end": 2641.04, "text": " to prove exactly what mathematical rules it costs to rebuild the textbook from scratch.", "tokens": [50791, 281, 7081, 2293, 437, 18894, 4474, 309, 5497, 281, 16877, 264, 25591, 490, 8459, 13, 51047], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 655, "seek": 262740, "start": 2641.1600000000003, "end": 2643.2400000000002, "text": " It's a master class in intellectual honesty.", "tokens": [51053, 467, 311, 257, 4505, 1508, 294, 12576, 26839, 13, 51157], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 656, "seek": 262740, "start": 2644.1, "end": 2647.54, "text": " To rebuild the entirety of finite dimensional quantum mechanics,", "tokens": [51200, 1407, 16877, 264, 31557, 295, 19362, 18795, 13018, 12939, 11, 51372], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 657, "seek": 262740, "start": 2647.98, "end": 2653.46, "text": " the text demonstrates that we only need to explicitly flag four basic operational assumptions.", "tokens": [51394, 264, 2487, 31034, 300, 321, 787, 643, 281, 20803, 7166, 1451, 3875, 16607, 17695, 13, 51668], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 658, "seek": 262740, "start": 2653.9, "end": 2654.28, "text": " Four.", "tokens": [51690, 7451, 13, 51709], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14791080838158016, "compression_ratio": 1.6284722222222223, "no_speech_prob": 1.9354706639468366e-12}, {"id": 659, "seek": 265428, "start": 2654.28, "end": 2656.6800000000003, "text": " Just four logical rules to build the universe.", "tokens": [50365, 1449, 1451, 14978, 4474, 281, 1322, 264, 6445, 13, 50485], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 660, "seek": 265428, "start": 2657.0400000000004, "end": 2658.2200000000003, "text": " Let's walk the listener through them.", "tokens": [50503, 961, 311, 1792, 264, 31569, 807, 552, 13, 50562], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 661, "seek": 265428, "start": 2658.46, "end": 2659.1200000000003, "text": " Assumption one.", "tokens": [50574, 6281, 449, 1695, 472, 13, 50607], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 662, "seek": 265428, "start": 2660.1400000000003, "end": 2660.98, "text": " Local tomography.", "tokens": [50658, 22755, 2916, 5820, 13, 50700], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 663, "seek": 265428, "start": 2661.6000000000004, "end": 2663.84, "text": " We explored this heavily just a moment ago.", "tokens": [50731, 492, 24016, 341, 10950, 445, 257, 1623, 2057, 13, 50843], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 664, "seek": 265428, "start": 2664.26, "end": 2669.1800000000003, "text": " We assume that the joint state of a composite system should be fully 100% explainable", "tokens": [50864, 492, 6552, 300, 264, 7225, 1785, 295, 257, 25557, 1185, 820, 312, 4498, 2319, 4, 2903, 712, 51110], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 665, "seek": 265428, "start": 2669.1800000000003, "end": 2672.42, "text": " by making local measurements on its individual parts.", "tokens": [51110, 538, 1455, 2654, 15383, 322, 1080, 2609, 3166, 13, 51272], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 666, "seek": 265428, "start": 2672.6200000000003, "end": 2672.88, "text": " Right.", "tokens": [51282, 1779, 13, 51295], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 667, "seek": 265428, "start": 2673.0400000000004, "end": 2674.34, "text": " As we saw with the dimension counting,", "tokens": [51303, 1018, 321, 1866, 365, 264, 10139, 13251, 11, 51368], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 668, "seek": 265428, "start": 2674.6000000000004, "end": 2678.8, "text": " insisting on this constraint is what fundamentally forces us into the arms of the complex numbers.", "tokens": [51381, 13466, 278, 322, 341, 25534, 307, 437, 17879, 5874, 505, 666, 264, 5812, 295, 264, 3997, 3547, 13, 51591], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 669, "seek": 265428, "start": 2678.92, "end": 2683.0600000000004, "text": " Because complex numbers balance the data ledger perfectly without creating phantom dimensions,", "tokens": [51597, 1436, 3997, 3547, 4772, 264, 1412, 4684, 1321, 6239, 1553, 4084, 903, 25796, 12819, 11, 51804], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1562396138906479, "compression_ratio": 1.6374622356495467, "no_speech_prob": 1.7832059613642759e-12}, {"id": 670, "seek": 268306, "start": 2683.06, "end": 2686.6, "text": " so we assume the physical universe prefers a balanced ledger.", "tokens": [50365, 370, 321, 6552, 264, 4001, 6445, 44334, 257, 13902, 4684, 1321, 13, 50542], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 671, "seek": 268306, "start": 2687.0, "end": 2688.34, "text": " Let's move to assumption two.", "tokens": [50562, 961, 311, 1286, 281, 15302, 732, 13, 50629], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 672, "seek": 268306, "start": 2688.84, "end": 2689.56, "text": " Assumption two.", "tokens": [50654, 6281, 449, 1695, 732, 13, 50690], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 673, "seek": 268306, "start": 2690.68, "end": 2694.36, "text": " Pure states are operationally represented as frame-completable vectors.", "tokens": [50746, 29474, 4368, 366, 6916, 379, 10379, 382, 3920, 12, 1112, 14657, 712, 18875, 13, 50930], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 674, "seek": 268306, "start": 2694.82, "end": 2695.7, "text": " Frame-completable.", "tokens": [50953, 31628, 12, 1112, 14657, 712, 13, 50997], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 675, "seek": 268306, "start": 2695.98, "end": 2697.1, "text": " Let me try to translate that.", "tokens": [51011, 961, 385, 853, 281, 13799, 300, 13, 51067], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 676, "seek": 268306, "start": 2697.24, "end": 2700.68, "text": " It means if I have a state of maximal knowledge, a pure state,", "tokens": [51074, 467, 1355, 498, 286, 362, 257, 1785, 295, 49336, 3601, 11, 257, 6075, 1785, 11, 51246], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19450815712533345, "compression_ratio": 1.564516129032258, "no_speech_prob": 1.905455611003748e-12}, {"id": 677, "seek": 270068, "start": 2700.68, "end": 2707.14, "text": " I can mathematically treat that state as one single axis of a complete orthogonal coordinate system.", "tokens": [50365, 286, 393, 44003, 2387, 300, 1785, 382, 472, 2167, 10298, 295, 257, 3566, 41488, 15670, 1185, 13, 50688], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19627484031345532, "compression_ratio": 1.554054054054054, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489160518425416e-12}, {"id": 678, "seek": 270068, "start": 2707.14, "end": 2708.48, "text": " That's a perfect translation.", "tokens": [50688, 663, 311, 257, 2176, 12853, 13, 50755], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19627484031345532, "compression_ratio": 1.554054054054054, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489160518425416e-12}, {"id": 679, "seek": 270068, "start": 2709.24, "end": 2715.04, "text": " It mathematically identifies a state with a distinguished coordinate axis of a measurement context.", "tokens": [50793, 467, 44003, 34597, 257, 1785, 365, 257, 21702, 15670, 10298, 295, 257, 13160, 4319, 13, 51083], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19627484031345532, "compression_ratio": 1.554054054054054, "no_speech_prob": 1.5489160518425416e-12}, {"id": 680, "seek": 271504, "start": 2715.04, "end": 2722.68, "text": " And this assumption is incredibly vital because it leads directly to the derivation of the Bourne rule in chapter 14.", "tokens": [50365, 400, 341, 15302, 307, 6252, 11707, 570, 309, 6689, 3838, 281, 264, 10151, 399, 295, 264, 35866, 716, 4978, 294, 7187, 3499, 13, 50747], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 681, "seek": 271504, "start": 2722.9, "end": 2723.74, "text": " The famous Bourne rule.", "tokens": [50758, 440, 4618, 35866, 716, 4978, 13, 50800], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 682, "seek": 271504, "start": 2724.5, "end": 2727.96, "text": " The eclosion P equals the absolute value of psi squared.", "tokens": [50838, 440, 308, 3474, 19996, 430, 6915, 264, 8236, 2158, 295, 20304, 8889, 13, 51011], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 683, "seek": 271504, "start": 2728.42, "end": 2732.64, "text": " The fundamental rule of quantum mechanics that says probability is the square of the amplitude.", "tokens": [51034, 440, 8088, 4978, 295, 13018, 12939, 300, 1619, 8482, 307, 264, 3732, 295, 264, 27433, 13, 51245], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 684, "seek": 271504, "start": 2732.98, "end": 2734.7799999999997, "text": " I was always taught that was just a raw postulate.", "tokens": [51262, 286, 390, 1009, 5928, 300, 390, 445, 257, 8936, 2183, 5256, 13, 51352], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 685, "seek": 271504, "start": 2734.98, "end": 2737.08, "text": " You're saying the author doesn't just assume it.", "tokens": [51362, 509, 434, 1566, 264, 3793, 1177, 380, 445, 6552, 309, 13, 51467], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 686, "seek": 271504, "start": 2737.2599999999998, "end": 2737.98, "text": " They do not assume it.", "tokens": [51476, 814, 360, 406, 6552, 309, 13, 51512], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 687, "seek": 271504, "start": 2738.02, "end": 2738.54, "text": " They derive it.", "tokens": [51514, 814, 28446, 309, 13, 51540], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15901906774678362, "compression_ratio": 1.69140625, "no_speech_prob": 2.048508498248003e-12}, {"id": 688, "seek": 273854, "start": 2738.54, "end": 2742.58, "text": " Remember the context transition identity we talked about earlier with the context graphs?", "tokens": [50365, 5459, 264, 4319, 6034, 6575, 321, 2825, 466, 3071, 365, 264, 4319, 24877, 30, 50567], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 689, "seek": 273854, "start": 2742.8, "end": 2747.04, "text": " The math that governs how one orthogonal coordinate frame rotates into another?", "tokens": [50578, 440, 5221, 300, 1980, 82, 577, 472, 41488, 15670, 3920, 42133, 666, 1071, 30, 50790], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 690, "seek": 273854, "start": 2747.16, "end": 2752.32, "text": " Yes, where squaring the unitary transition matrix gives you the bistochastic probability matrix.", "tokens": [50796, 1079, 11, 689, 2339, 1921, 264, 517, 4109, 6034, 8141, 2709, 291, 264, 18209, 8997, 2750, 8482, 8141, 13, 51054], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 691, "seek": 273854, "start": 2752.32, "end": 2758.18, "text": " If you accept assumption two, the idea that a pure state is just one vector of a coordinate frame,", "tokens": [51054, 759, 291, 3241, 15302, 732, 11, 264, 1558, 300, 257, 6075, 1785, 307, 445, 472, 8062, 295, 257, 15670, 3920, 11, 51347], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 692, "seek": 273854, "start": 2758.86, "end": 2763.08, "text": " then the Bourne rule is literally nothing more than reading off one single column", "tokens": [51381, 550, 264, 35866, 716, 4978, 307, 3736, 1825, 544, 813, 3760, 766, 472, 2167, 7738, 51592], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 693, "seek": 273854, "start": 2763.08, "end": 2766.7599999999998, "text": " of that already proven geometric context transition matrix.", "tokens": [51592, 295, 300, 1217, 12785, 33246, 4319, 6034, 8141, 13, 51776], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 694, "seek": 273854, "start": 2767.06, "end": 2767.36, "text": " Wow.", "tokens": [51791, 3153, 13, 51806], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12215950705788352, "compression_ratio": 1.7534246575342465, "no_speech_prob": 1.294165187341878e-12}, {"id": 695, "seek": 276736, "start": 2767.36, "end": 2772.52, "text": " The probability of an outcome is just the squared inner product of the state vector and the measurement vector.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 8482, 295, 364, 9700, 307, 445, 264, 8889, 7284, 1674, 295, 264, 1785, 8062, 293, 264, 13160, 8062, 13, 50623], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 696, "seek": 276736, "start": 2772.98, "end": 2775.1, "text": " It falls out of the geometry completely for free.", "tokens": [50646, 467, 8804, 484, 295, 264, 18426, 2584, 337, 1737, 13, 50752], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 697, "seek": 276736, "start": 2775.34, "end": 2777.1400000000003, "text": " That is breathtakingly elegant.", "tokens": [50764, 663, 307, 48393, 356, 21117, 13, 50854], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 698, "seek": 276736, "start": 2777.86, "end": 2781.7400000000002, "text": " No positing the probability shall be the absolute square from on high.", "tokens": [50890, 883, 1366, 1748, 264, 8482, 4393, 312, 264, 8236, 3732, 490, 322, 1090, 13, 51084], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 699, "seek": 276736, "start": 2781.94, "end": 2783.86, "text": " It's just geometry dictating the terms.", "tokens": [51094, 467, 311, 445, 18426, 12569, 990, 264, 2115, 13, 51190], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 700, "seek": 276736, "start": 2784.46, "end": 2785.7400000000002, "text": " Okay, let's look at assumption three.", "tokens": [51220, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 574, 412, 15302, 1045, 13, 51284], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 701, "seek": 276736, "start": 2786.0, "end": 2786.6400000000003, "text": " Assumption three.", "tokens": [51297, 6281, 449, 1695, 1045, 13, 51329], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 702, "seek": 276736, "start": 2787.0, "end": 2789.1800000000003, "text": " Measurement outcomes are labeled by real numbers.", "tokens": [51347, 41436, 518, 10070, 366, 21335, 538, 957, 3547, 13, 51456], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 703, "seek": 276736, "start": 2789.52, "end": 2791.98, "text": " Meaning when my physical detector clicks in the lab,", "tokens": [51473, 19948, 562, 452, 4001, 25712, 18521, 294, 264, 2715, 11, 51596], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 704, "seek": 276736, "start": 2792.34, "end": 2796.4, "text": " it spits out a standard real number on the screen like five volts or spin plus one.", "tokens": [51614, 309, 637, 1208, 484, 257, 3832, 957, 1230, 322, 264, 2568, 411, 1732, 22322, 420, 6060, 1804, 472, 13, 51817], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12279183523995536, "compression_ratio": 1.781758957654723, "no_speech_prob": 1.7622695837327118e-12}, {"id": 705, "seek": 279640, "start": 2796.4, "end": 2799.56, "text": " It doesn't spit out a complex number with an imaginary phase.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 1177, 380, 22127, 484, 257, 3997, 1230, 365, 364, 26164, 5574, 13, 50523], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.177689790725708, "compression_ratio": 1.4093264248704662, "no_speech_prob": 1.4834491057422183e-12}, {"id": 706, "seek": 279640, "start": 2799.6800000000003, "end": 2800.08, "text": " Exactly.", "tokens": [50529, 7587, 13, 50549], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.177689790725708, "compression_ratio": 1.4093264248704662, "no_speech_prob": 1.4834491057422183e-12}, {"id": 707, "seek": 279640, "start": 2800.26, "end": 2805.38, "text": " It's a very basic, pragmatic modeling choice about how human beings record data in a laboratory.", "tokens": [50558, 467, 311, 257, 588, 3875, 11, 46904, 15983, 3922, 466, 577, 1952, 8958, 2136, 1412, 294, 257, 16523, 13, 50814], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.177689790725708, "compression_ratio": 1.4093264248704662, "no_speech_prob": 1.4834491057422183e-12}, {"id": 708, "seek": 279640, "start": 2806.1800000000003, "end": 2812.46, "text": " But this incredibly simple assumption is all it takes to derive the nature of observables in chapter 15.", "tokens": [50854, 583, 341, 6252, 2199, 15302, 307, 439, 309, 2516, 281, 28446, 264, 3687, 295, 9951, 2965, 294, 7187, 2119, 13, 51168], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.177689790725708, "compression_ratio": 1.4093264248704662, "no_speech_prob": 1.4834491057422183e-12}, {"id": 709, "seek": 281246, "start": 2812.46, "end": 2816.92, "text": " In physics class, we are taught that observables are represented by Hermitian operators,", "tokens": [50365, 682, 10649, 1508, 11, 321, 366, 5928, 300, 9951, 2965, 366, 10379, 538, 21842, 270, 952, 19077, 11, 50588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15561124165852866, "compression_ratio": 1.569377990430622, "no_speech_prob": 1.421062594854361e-12}, {"id": 710, "seek": 281246, "start": 2817.7400000000002, "end": 2819.8, "text": " which frankly always sounded like dark magic to me.", "tokens": [50629, 597, 11939, 1009, 17714, 411, 2877, 5585, 281, 385, 13, 50732], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15561124165852866, "compression_ratio": 1.569377990430622, "no_speech_prob": 1.421062594854361e-12}, {"id": 711, "seek": 281246, "start": 2820.04, "end": 2822.92, "text": " It sounds like magic until you see the derivation.", "tokens": [50744, 467, 3263, 411, 5585, 1826, 291, 536, 264, 10151, 399, 13, 50888], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15561124165852866, "compression_ratio": 1.569377990430622, "no_speech_prob": 1.421062594854361e-12}, {"id": 712, "seek": 281246, "start": 2823.6, "end": 2827.18, "text": " The author shows that if you have a set of orthogonal projectors,", "tokens": [50922, 440, 3793, 3110, 300, 498, 291, 362, 257, 992, 295, 41488, 1716, 830, 11, 51101], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15561124165852866, "compression_ratio": 1.569377990430622, "no_speech_prob": 1.421062594854361e-12}, {"id": 713, "seek": 281246, "start": 2827.54, "end": 2830.92, "text": " which geometrically represent mutually exclusive measurement outcomes,", "tokens": [51119, 597, 12956, 81, 984, 2906, 39144, 13005, 13160, 10070, 11, 51288], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15561124165852866, "compression_ratio": 1.569377990430622, "no_speech_prob": 1.421062594854361e-12}, {"id": 714, "seek": 283092, "start": 2830.92, "end": 2834.38, "text": " and you assign a simple real number to label each one.", "tokens": [50365, 293, 291, 6269, 257, 2199, 957, 1230, 281, 7645, 1184, 472, 13, 50538], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15314260579771916, "compression_ratio": 1.4689265536723164, "no_speech_prob": 2.0243999619001807e-12}, {"id": 715, "seek": 283092, "start": 2834.7000000000003, "end": 2835.58, "text": " And you sum them all up.", "tokens": [50554, 400, 291, 2408, 552, 439, 493, 13, 50598], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15314260579771916, "compression_ratio": 1.4689265536723164, "no_speech_prob": 2.0243999619001807e-12}, {"id": 716, "seek": 283092, "start": 2835.78, "end": 2842.1800000000003, "text": " The resulting combined matrix is mathematically guaranteed to be Hermitian by pure construction.", "tokens": [50608, 440, 16505, 9354, 8141, 307, 44003, 18031, 281, 312, 21842, 270, 952, 538, 6075, 6435, 13, 50928], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15314260579771916, "compression_ratio": 1.4689265536723164, "no_speech_prob": 2.0243999619001807e-12}, {"id": 717, "seek": 283092, "start": 2842.34, "end": 2846.98, "text": " Because it's built out of real numbers multiplied by symmetric projection matrices.", "tokens": [50936, 1436, 309, 311, 3094, 484, 295, 957, 3547, 17207, 538, 32330, 22743, 32284, 13, 51168], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15314260579771916, "compression_ratio": 1.4689265536723164, "no_speech_prob": 2.0243999619001807e-12}, {"id": 718, "seek": 284698, "start": 2846.98, "end": 2847.5, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50365, 1079, 13, 50391], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 719, "seek": 284698, "start": 2847.76, "end": 2849.2400000000002, "text": " The statement observables are,", "tokens": [50404, 440, 5629, 9951, 2965, 366, 11, 50478], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 720, "seek": 284698, "start": 2849.36, "end": 2852.7400000000002, "text": " Hermitian isn't some deep new physical law of the universe.", "tokens": [50484, 21842, 270, 952, 1943, 380, 512, 2452, 777, 4001, 2101, 295, 264, 6445, 13, 50653], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 721, "seek": 284698, "start": 2853.14, "end": 2854.64, "text": " It's a mathematical equivalence.", "tokens": [50673, 467, 311, 257, 18894, 9052, 655, 13, 50748], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 722, "seek": 284698, "start": 2855.46, "end": 2859.92, "text": " Real valued sharp measurements and Hermitian operators are the exact same raw data", "tokens": [50789, 8467, 22608, 8199, 15383, 293, 21842, 270, 952, 19077, 366, 264, 1900, 912, 8936, 1412, 51012], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 723, "seek": 284698, "start": 2859.92, "end": 2862.52, "text": " just packaged in two different mathematical wrappers.", "tokens": [51012, 445, 38162, 294, 732, 819, 18894, 7843, 15226, 13, 51142], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 724, "seek": 284698, "start": 2863.12, "end": 2867.4, "text": " The spectral theorem from linear algebra guarantees they translate perfectly back and forth.", "tokens": [51172, 440, 42761, 20904, 490, 8213, 21989, 32567, 436, 13799, 6239, 646, 293, 5220, 13, 51386], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 725, "seek": 284698, "start": 2867.54, "end": 2869.64, "text": " Again, it's just so clean when you strip it down.", "tokens": [51393, 3764, 11, 309, 311, 445, 370, 2541, 562, 291, 12828, 309, 760, 13, 51498], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 726, "seek": 284698, "start": 2869.96, "end": 2871.22, "text": " Okay, the final constraint.", "tokens": [51514, 1033, 11, 264, 2572, 25534, 13, 51577], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 727, "seek": 284698, "start": 2871.46, "end": 2872.18, "text": " Assumption four.", "tokens": [51589, 6281, 449, 1695, 1451, 13, 51625], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.20072128498448735, "compression_ratio": 1.578397212543554, "no_speech_prob": 1.5191844093473406e-12}, {"id": 728, "seek": 287218, "start": 2872.18, "end": 2873.14, "text": " Assumption four.", "tokens": [50365, 6281, 449, 1695, 1451, 13, 50413], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 729, "seek": 287218, "start": 2873.62, "end": 2877.16, "text": " Time evolution is a strongly continuous unitary group.", "tokens": [50437, 6161, 9303, 307, 257, 10613, 10957, 517, 4109, 1594, 13, 50614], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 730, "seek": 287218, "start": 2877.5, "end": 2881.6, "text": " Breaking that down, things evolve continuously in time without jumping,", "tokens": [50631, 36715, 300, 760, 11, 721, 16693, 15684, 294, 565, 1553, 11233, 11, 50836], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 731, "seek": 287218, "start": 2881.8799999999997, "end": 2883.7799999999997, "text": " and the evolution is fundamentally reversible.", "tokens": [50850, 293, 264, 9303, 307, 17879, 44788, 13, 50945], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 732, "seek": 287218, "start": 2884.16, "end": 2888.16, "text": " If you accept that premise of continuous reversible composition in time,", "tokens": [50964, 759, 291, 3241, 300, 22045, 295, 10957, 44788, 12686, 294, 565, 11, 51164], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 733, "seek": 287218, "start": 2888.8999999999996, "end": 2891.9199999999996, "text": " chapter 16 proves that you can derive Schr\u00f6dinger's equation", "tokens": [51201, 7187, 3165, 25019, 300, 291, 393, 28446, 46191, 973, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 51352], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 734, "seek": 287218, "start": 2891.9199999999996, "end": 2897.9199999999996, "text": " directly from a finite dimensional matrix ODE, an ordinary differential equation.", "tokens": [51352, 3838, 490, 257, 19362, 18795, 8141, 422, 22296, 11, 364, 10547, 15756, 5367, 13, 51652], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 735, "seek": 287218, "start": 2898.16, "end": 2899.48, "text": " Wait, Schr\u00f6dinger's equation?", "tokens": [51664, 3802, 11, 46191, 973, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 30, 51730], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.15452621459960938, "compression_ratio": 1.6466165413533835, "no_speech_prob": 1.6427744581326564e-12}, {"id": 736, "seek": 289948, "start": 2899.48, "end": 2902.86, "text": " The absolute most famous equation in all of quantum mechanics,", "tokens": [50365, 440, 8236, 881, 4618, 5367, 294, 439, 295, 13018, 12939, 11, 50534], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 737, "seek": 289948, "start": 2902.98, "end": 2907.3, "text": " the one with the wave function and the Hamiltonian, is just a basic ODE.", "tokens": [50540, 264, 472, 365, 264, 5772, 2445, 293, 264, 18484, 952, 11, 307, 445, 257, 3875, 422, 22296, 13, 50756], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 738, "seek": 289948, "start": 2907.68, "end": 2909.62, "text": " In this framework, it is inescapable.", "tokens": [50775, 682, 341, 8388, 11, 309, 307, 294, 279, 9485, 712, 13, 50872], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 739, "seek": 289948, "start": 2910.2, "end": 2914.14, "text": " There is a mathematical theorem called Stone's theorem on one parameter unitary groups.", "tokens": [50901, 821, 307, 257, 18894, 20904, 1219, 15012, 311, 20904, 322, 472, 13075, 517, 4109, 3935, 13, 51098], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 740, "seek": 289948, "start": 2914.84, "end": 2919.04, "text": " It shows that any continuous unitary evolution can be mathematically written", "tokens": [51133, 467, 3110, 300, 604, 10957, 517, 4109, 9303, 393, 312, 44003, 3720, 51343], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 741, "seek": 289948, "start": 2919.04, "end": 2923.16, "text": " as the exponential of an imaginary number multiplied by a Hermitian matrix.", "tokens": [51343, 382, 264, 21510, 295, 364, 26164, 1230, 17207, 538, 257, 21842, 270, 952, 8141, 13, 51549], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 742, "seek": 289948, "start": 2923.62, "end": 2927.44, "text": " That Hermitian matrix acts as the geometric generator of the evolution.", "tokens": [51572, 663, 21842, 270, 952, 8141, 10672, 382, 264, 33246, 19265, 295, 264, 9303, 13, 51763], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11869697233216953, "compression_ratio": 1.6875, "no_speech_prob": 2.5042728307722584e-12}, {"id": 743, "seek": 292744, "start": 2927.44, "end": 2930.42, "text": " Which is exactly what physicists call the Hamiltonian.", "tokens": [50365, 3013, 307, 2293, 437, 48716, 818, 264, 18484, 952, 13, 50514], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 744, "seek": 292744, "start": 2930.62, "end": 2930.84, "text": " Yes.", "tokens": [50524, 1079, 13, 50535], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 745, "seek": 292744, "start": 2931.46, "end": 2936.2400000000002, "text": " Schr\u00f6dinger's equation drops out purely as the infinitesimal form of this continuous composition", "tokens": [50566, 46191, 973, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 11438, 484, 17491, 382, 264, 7193, 3324, 10650, 1254, 295, 341, 10957, 12686, 50805], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 746, "seek": 292744, "start": 2936.2400000000002, "end": 2936.84, "text": " over time.", "tokens": [50805, 670, 565, 13, 50835], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 747, "seek": 292744, "start": 2937.2000000000003, "end": 2940.04, "text": " Now, the text is very careful to note a limitation here.", "tokens": [50853, 823, 11, 264, 2487, 307, 588, 5026, 281, 3637, 257, 27432, 510, 13, 50995], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 748, "seek": 292744, "start": 2940.58, "end": 2944.42, "text": " This math doesn't tell us why the Hamiltonian represents the physical concept of energy,", "tokens": [51022, 639, 5221, 1177, 380, 980, 505, 983, 264, 18484, 952, 8855, 264, 4001, 3410, 295, 2281, 11, 51214], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 749, "seek": 292744, "start": 2944.62, "end": 2947.32, "text": " or why Planck's constant exists in the real world.", "tokens": [51224, 420, 983, 8112, 547, 311, 5754, 8198, 294, 264, 957, 1002, 13, 51359], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 750, "seek": 292744, "start": 2947.44, "end": 2949.46, "text": " Because that requires actual physical mapping.", "tokens": [51365, 1436, 300, 7029, 3539, 4001, 18350, 13, 51466], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 751, "seek": 292744, "start": 2949.64, "end": 2952.4, "text": " You have to measure the real world to attach the units.", "tokens": [51475, 509, 362, 281, 3481, 264, 957, 1002, 281, 5085, 264, 6815, 13, 51613], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14265670776367187, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 1.8250479252857055e-12}, {"id": 752, "seek": 295240, "start": 2952.4, "end": 2953.26, "text": " Precisely.", "tokens": [50365, 48746, 736, 13, 50408], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 753, "seek": 295240, "start": 2953.78, "end": 2960.54, "text": " But the structure of the equation itself, the famous I h-bar d by dt of Csi equals H-Psi,", "tokens": [50434, 583, 264, 3877, 295, 264, 5367, 2564, 11, 264, 4618, 286, 276, 12, 5356, 274, 538, 36423, 295, 383, 7691, 6915, 389, 12, 47, 7691, 11, 50772], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 754, "seek": 295240, "start": 2961.04, "end": 2966.6800000000003, "text": " is mathematically forced into existence by the simple assumption of continuous reversible time.", "tokens": [50797, 307, 44003, 7579, 666, 9123, 538, 264, 2199, 15302, 295, 10957, 44788, 565, 13, 51079], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 755, "seek": 295240, "start": 2967.2200000000003, "end": 2972.36, "text": " And finally, chapter 17 cleans up the last messy part of the textbook, generalized measurements.", "tokens": [51106, 400, 2721, 11, 7187, 3282, 16912, 493, 264, 1036, 16191, 644, 295, 264, 25591, 11, 44498, 15383, 13, 51363], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 756, "seek": 295240, "start": 2972.82, "end": 2973.6, "text": " The POVMs.", "tokens": [51386, 440, 22299, 53, 26386, 13, 51425], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 757, "seek": 295240, "start": 2974.14, "end": 2977.82, "text": " These are the fuzzy measurements that aren't perfectly sharp or mutually exclusive.", "tokens": [51452, 1981, 366, 264, 34710, 15383, 300, 3212, 380, 6239, 8199, 420, 39144, 13005, 13, 51636], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.22558557226302775, "compression_ratio": 1.515625, "no_speech_prob": 1.6751679253815266e-12}, {"id": 758, "seek": 297782, "start": 2977.82, "end": 2983.2400000000002, "text": " The author handles these elegantly, using a mathematical technique called Nymarck dilation.", "tokens": [50365, 440, 3793, 18722, 613, 14459, 3627, 11, 1228, 257, 18894, 6532, 1219, 426, 4199, 289, 547, 11504, 399, 13, 50636], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 759, "seek": 297782, "start": 2983.42, "end": 2986.92, "text": " Let's unpack how Nymarck dilation actually works mechanics-wise.", "tokens": [50645, 961, 311, 26699, 577, 426, 4199, 289, 547, 11504, 399, 767, 1985, 12939, 12, 3711, 13, 50820], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 760, "seek": 297782, "start": 2987.32, "end": 2990.78, "text": " Because just saying it's identical doesn't explain the how.", "tokens": [50840, 1436, 445, 1566, 309, 311, 14800, 1177, 380, 2903, 264, 577, 13, 51013], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 761, "seek": 297782, "start": 2991.02, "end": 2991.8, "text": " Think of it this way.", "tokens": [51025, 6557, 295, 309, 341, 636, 13, 51064], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 762, "seek": 297782, "start": 2992.42, "end": 2995.76, "text": " Suppose you want to make a fuzzy, imperfect measurement on your quantum system.", "tokens": [51095, 21360, 291, 528, 281, 652, 257, 34710, 11, 26714, 13160, 322, 428, 13018, 1185, 13, 51262], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 763, "seek": 297782, "start": 2996.38, "end": 3000.38, "text": " Nymarck dilation proves that you can achieve this by bringing in a completely separate,", "tokens": [51293, 426, 4199, 289, 547, 11504, 399, 25019, 300, 291, 393, 4584, 341, 538, 5062, 294, 257, 2584, 4994, 11, 51493], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 764, "seek": 297782, "start": 3000.54, "end": 3002.38, "text": " clean reference system called an ancilla.", "tokens": [51501, 2541, 6408, 1185, 1219, 364, 9789, 5291, 13, 51593], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 765, "seek": 297782, "start": 3002.94, "end": 3006.84, "text": " You mathematically couple your main system to this ancilla, allowing them to interact.", "tokens": [51621, 509, 44003, 1916, 428, 2135, 1185, 281, 341, 9789, 5291, 11, 8293, 552, 281, 4648, 13, 51816], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.14783971623856892, "compression_ratio": 1.7038216560509554, "no_speech_prob": 1.8396330465908495e-12}, {"id": 766, "seek": 300684, "start": 3006.84, "end": 3010.84, "text": " Then you perform a perfectly sharp, crisp measurement on the joint system.", "tokens": [50365, 1396, 291, 2042, 257, 6239, 8199, 11, 22952, 13160, 322, 264, 7225, 1185, 13, 50565], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 767, "seek": 300684, "start": 3010.94, "end": 3011.3, "text": " Ah!", "tokens": [50570, 2438, 0, 50588], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 768, "seek": 300684, "start": 3011.8, "end": 3015.26, "text": " So the act of measuring the joint system sharply mathematically acts as a fuzzy,", "tokens": [50613, 407, 264, 605, 295, 13389, 264, 7225, 1185, 42893, 44003, 10672, 382, 257, 34710, 11, 50786], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 769, "seek": 300684, "start": 3015.42, "end": 3018.1200000000003, "text": " imprecise measurement when you look only at the original subsystem.", "tokens": [50794, 704, 13867, 908, 13160, 562, 291, 574, 787, 412, 264, 3380, 2090, 9321, 13, 50929], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 770, "seek": 300684, "start": 3018.42, "end": 3019.94, "text": " That is the exact mechanism.", "tokens": [50944, 663, 307, 264, 1900, 7513, 13, 51020], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 771, "seek": 300684, "start": 3020.7400000000002, "end": 3026.28, "text": " By zooming out and looking at a larger geometric space, the fuzzy measurement resolves into a sharp one.", "tokens": [51060, 3146, 48226, 484, 293, 1237, 412, 257, 4833, 33246, 1901, 11, 264, 34710, 13160, 7923, 977, 666, 257, 8199, 472, 13, 51337], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 772, "seek": 300684, "start": 3026.9, "end": 3032.26, "text": " Even the weird generalized measurements are just sharp geometry if you expand your perspective enough.", "tokens": [51368, 2754, 264, 3657, 44498, 15383, 366, 445, 8199, 18426, 498, 291, 5268, 428, 4585, 1547, 13, 51636], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 773, "seek": 300684, "start": 3032.58, "end": 3034.78, "text": " It all comes back to geometry and constraints.", "tokens": [51652, 467, 439, 1487, 646, 281, 18426, 293, 18491, 13, 51762], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 774, "seek": 300684, "start": 3035.1200000000003, "end": 3036.46, "text": " Okay, let's take a deep breath.", "tokens": [51779, 1033, 11, 718, 311, 747, 257, 2452, 6045, 13, 51846], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.17527194213867187, "compression_ratio": 1.7403846153846154, "no_speech_prob": 1.5918425431971084e-12}, {"id": 775, "seek": 303684, "start": 3036.84, "end": 3042.58, "text": " We have journeyed through some incredibly dense, beautiful, and mind-expanding territory today.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 362, 4671, 292, 807, 512, 6252, 18011, 11, 2238, 11, 293, 1575, 12, 15952, 42389, 11360, 965, 13, 50652], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13691650202244887, "compression_ratio": 1.5766129032258065, "no_speech_prob": 2.417731162843162e-12}, {"id": 776, "seek": 303684, "start": 3042.94, "end": 3047.1600000000003, "text": " We started with the classical probabilities of flipping coins on the simplex.", "tokens": [50670, 492, 1409, 365, 264, 13735, 33783, 295, 26886, 13561, 322, 264, 2199, 87, 13, 50881], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13691650202244887, "compression_ratio": 1.5766129032258065, "no_speech_prob": 2.417731162843162e-12}, {"id": 777, "seek": 303684, "start": 3047.1600000000003, "end": 3053.98, "text": " We discovered that real numbers, while they can handle a simple, isolated coin completely and utterly break down at n equals 3,", "tokens": [50881, 492, 6941, 300, 957, 3547, 11, 1339, 436, 393, 4813, 257, 2199, 11, 14621, 11464, 2584, 293, 30251, 1821, 760, 412, 297, 6915, 805, 11, 51222], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13691650202244887, "compression_ratio": 1.5766129032258065, "no_speech_prob": 2.417731162843162e-12}, {"id": 778, "seek": 303684, "start": 3054.42, "end": 3059.1200000000003, "text": " unable to form the basic orthogonal structures required to distribute probability evenly.", "tokens": [51244, 11299, 281, 1254, 264, 3875, 41488, 9227, 4739, 281, 20594, 8482, 17658, 13, 51479], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13691650202244887, "compression_ratio": 1.5766129032258065, "no_speech_prob": 2.417731162843162e-12}, {"id": 779, "seek": 305912, "start": 3059.12, "end": 3069.44, "text": " We saw how they structurally failed the triangle gluing theorem, physically unable to close a loop of three mutually unbiased bases without declaring that 45 degrees equals zero.", "tokens": [50365, 492, 1866, 577, 436, 6594, 6512, 7612, 264, 13369, 1563, 9635, 20904, 11, 9762, 11299, 281, 1998, 257, 6367, 295, 1045, 39144, 517, 5614, 1937, 17949, 1553, 40374, 300, 6905, 5310, 6915, 4018, 13, 50881], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.09419984566537958, "compression_ratio": 1.3484848484848484, "no_speech_prob": 1.95798325469676e-12}, {"id": 780, "seek": 306944, "start": 3069.44, "end": 3075.64, "text": " We watched real numbers spawn a bizarre, invisible 10th dimension when we tried to compose two systems together,", "tokens": [50365, 492, 6337, 957, 3547, 17088, 257, 18265, 11, 14603, 1266, 392, 10139, 562, 321, 3031, 281, 35925, 732, 3652, 1214, 11, 50675], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1538601077970911, "compression_ratio": 1.5052083333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.8040635174979003e-12}, {"id": 781, "seek": 306944, "start": 3075.96, "end": 3081.08, "text": " leading to the absolute mind-bending realization that entanglement is not an inherent physical property,", "tokens": [50691, 5775, 281, 264, 8236, 1575, 12, 65, 2029, 25138, 300, 948, 656, 3054, 307, 406, 364, 26387, 4001, 4707, 11, 50947], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1538601077970911, "compression_ratio": 1.5052083333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.8040635174979003e-12}, {"id": 782, "seek": 306944, "start": 3081.44, "end": 3084.98, "text": " but is entirely relative to the mathematical vocabulary you were using.", "tokens": [50965, 457, 307, 7696, 4972, 281, 264, 18894, 19864, 291, 645, 1228, 13, 51142], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1538601077970911, "compression_ratio": 1.5052083333333333, "no_speech_prob": 1.8040635174979003e-12}, {"id": 783, "seek": 308498, "start": 3084.98, "end": 3090.38, "text": " And finally, we watched the text rebuild the towering achievements of 20th century physics,", "tokens": [50365, 400, 2721, 11, 321, 6337, 264, 2487, 16877, 264, 10567, 278, 21420, 295, 945, 392, 4901, 10649, 11, 50635], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19181213044283682, "compression_ratio": 1.379120879120879, "no_speech_prob": 2.7503635219999767e-12}, {"id": 784, "seek": 308498, "start": 3090.96, "end": 3094.92, "text": " the Born rule, Hermitian observables, and Schrodinger's equation,", "tokens": [50664, 264, 29808, 4978, 11, 21842, 270, 952, 9951, 2965, 11, 293, 2065, 340, 3584, 260, 311, 5367, 11, 50862], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19181213044283682, "compression_ratio": 1.379120879120879, "no_speech_prob": 2.7503635219999767e-12}, {"id": 785, "seek": 308498, "start": 3095.3, "end": 3100.54, "text": " using nothing more than probability geometry and four simple logical operational assumptions.", "tokens": [50881, 1228, 1825, 544, 813, 8482, 18426, 293, 1451, 2199, 14978, 16607, 17695, 13, 51143], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.19181213044283682, "compression_ratio": 1.379120879120879, "no_speech_prob": 2.7503635219999767e-12}, {"id": 786, "seek": 310054, "start": 3100.54, "end": 3105.38, "text": " It is a monumental achievement in changing how we conceptualize the subject.", "tokens": [50365, 467, 307, 257, 43105, 15838, 294, 4473, 577, 321, 24106, 1125, 264, 3983, 13, 50607], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12524944439268948, "compression_ratio": 1.5981012658227849, "no_speech_prob": 2.1340069468467338e-12}, {"id": 787, "seek": 310054, "start": 3105.82, "end": 3110.72, "text": " It transforms quantum mechanics from a dictatorial list of demands the universe makes of us", "tokens": [50629, 467, 35592, 13018, 12939, 490, 257, 42852, 831, 1329, 295, 15107, 264, 6445, 1669, 295, 505, 50874], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.12524944439268948, "compression_ratio": 1.5981012658227849, "no_speech_prob": 2.1340069468467338e-12}, {"id": 788, "seek": 310054, "start": 3110.